var tafsirData_7 = {
  "1": "“Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad” is the opening of this chapter with letters whose precise meaning Allah has kept to Himself. This surah begins, like several others, with separated letters pronounced by name, not as a word. The Quran signals from its very first sound that its language is made of the same Arabic letters that the Arabs used in daily speech and poetry, yet its composition remains beyond their ability to imitate, even though they are masters of eloquence. Saying these letters and then immediately presenting a clear Book is a quiet challenge to those who called revelation ancient fables or claimed the Prophet had studied previous scriptures. If the Quran were merely learned, any poet could replicate it from the same letters, yet none could.\n\nClassical commentators note that Allah knows best the intended wisdom, while affirming that the letters are not meaningless. They function as an attention marker, like a sound that makes a listener pause. For the Meccan audience accustomed to measured poetry, hearing disjointed letters followed by divine address would arrest attention and declare that what follows is weighty. Similar openings appear before passages about the Quran being a guide, a light, or a decisive criterion, linking the mysterious letters to the miraculous nature of the message that follows.\n\nThe combination “Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad” is unique to this surah, while “Alif-Lam-Mim” opens the second chapter that began with certainty of the meeting, and “Alif-Lam-Ra” opens others. The addition of “Sad” here has been linked by some scholars to the name of the surah, Al-A‘raf, and to the emphasis on storytelling that will follow, including the stories of Adam, Noah, and others, though such associations remain reflective rather than definitive.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to force a fixed translation onto these letters as if they were an ordinary word with a dictionary meaning. The respectful method transmitted from the companions is to recite them as revealed, affirm that their exact intent is with Allah, and focus on the clear guidance that follows them. For the listener who seeks straight guidance and whose heart is meant to expand to Islam, the letters become a humble reminder that human knowledge has limits, that the One whose knowledge encompasses every leaf and grain retains knowledge of some matters entirely with Himself, and that the proper response is to listen attentively to the reminder that comes after, which offers the light by which the straight path is walked toward the Home of Peace.",
  "2": "“˹This is˺ a Book sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺— do not let anxiety into your heart regarding it—so with it you may warn ˹the disbelievers˺, and as a reminder to the believers” follows immediately after the mysterious letters, explaining their purpose. The Book indicated is this Quran, whose blessed arrival was celebrated at the close of the previous surah as a detailed, perfected Word in truth and justice. The phrase “sent down to you O Prophet,” or “unzila ilaika,” personalizes the address to Muhammad, the one earlier commanded to follow only what is revealed and to convey without seeking reward. The next instruction is tenderly protective: “do not let anxiety into your heart regarding it,” or “fa-la yakun fi sadrika harajun minhu.” The word “haraj” means tightness, constriction, distress, the same tightness described for those whose chest is made narrow as if climbing into the sky when left astray. For the Prophet, tightness could mean hesitation to warn because the disbelievers mock the verses, call them fables, or intimidate. Allah says, let your chest remain spacious as it was opened to Islam; do not narrow with anxiety.\n\nThe purpose of the Book is then given in two directions. First, “so with it you may warn the disbelievers,” or “li-tunthira bihi,” using it as a warner, just as earlier the Book was sent to warn the Mother of Cities and around it and to warn of the Day of Gathering. Second, “and as a reminder to the believers,” or “wa thikra lil-mu’mineen,” a remembrance that revives what the heart already knows by fitrah, the innate call to the Originator. The same Book thus functions differently according to the recipient: for the heedless it is a warning that saves from dreadful punishment for turning away, for the mindful it is a gentle reminder that strengthens the certainty of the meeting.\n\nThis verse links to the repeated assurances that the Prophet is not a keeper over people and that messengers are only bringers of good news and warners. His duty is clear conveyance with a spacious heart; the outcome belongs to the One who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing. A common misunderstanding is to think that anxiety about delivering truth indicates weakness of faith. This verse normalizes the human feeling of tightness when facing opposition while correcting it with divine reassurance. For believers today, the verse teaches to hold the Book with confidence, to use it both to warn with wisdom and to remind with kindness, trusting that the One who sent it down and who knows best where to place His message will support the open heart and not leave the sincere invoker constricted.",
  "3": "“Follow what has been sent down to you from your Lord, and do not take others as guardians besides Him. How seldom are you mindful!” is the command to the entire community that follows the consolation to the Prophet about the Book sent down to warn and remind. After telling the Prophet not to let anxiety tighten his chest regarding the Book, Allah turns to all who hear: “Follow what has been sent down to you from your Lord,” or “ittabi‘oo ma unzila ilaikum min Rabbikum.” The verb “ittabi‘oo” means to walk behind, to trace the steps of, to stay upon the path, echoing the repeated call to follow the straight path and the blessed Book so you may be shown mercy. The source is specified as “from your Lord,” Rabbakum, the same nurturing Lord who provides for all, originates from a single soul, and maintains everything. Following therefore is not following a tribal custom but following the Nurturer who knows what benefits His servants.\n\nThe second command guards that following from dilution: “and do not take others as guardians besides Him,” or “wa la tattabi‘oo min doonihi awliya’.” “Awliya” are protectors, allies, guardians taken as authorities to be obeyed alongside Allah. This mirrors earlier warnings not to seek a judge besides Allah when the detailed Book has come, not to seek a guardian other than the Originator who provides for all, and not to obey those who fabricate lies about lawful and forbidden in Allah’s name. Taking guardians besides Him includes following invented food taboos, treating elegant deception as proof, and dividing faith into sects.\n\nThe verse then remarks with gentle reproach: “How seldom are you mindful!” or “qaleelan ma tathakkaroon,” how little you remember. This mirrors earlier refrains about seldom giving thanks and seldom being mindful, showing that forgetfulness is the root that lets beautified misdeeds remain appealing. Mindfulness, or “tadhakkur,” means connecting reminder to action, remembering that the Day you will be gathered together is real and that the Home of Peace awaits the guided.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think following revelation restricts freedom. The verse presents the opposite: guardians besides Him lead away from His Way and scatter the traveler into diverging paths. True freedom stays with the One whose Word is perfected and whose knowledge encompasses every leaf. Another mistake is to treat remembrance as occasional. The Book was sent as a continuous reminder precisely because heedlessness is recurrent, so following must be renewed daily through establishing prayer, paying dues without waste, and speaking justly, keeping the heart open rather than tight.",
  "4": "“˹Imagine˺ how many societies We have destroyed! Our torment took them by surprise ˹while sleeping˺ at night or midday” follows the call to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians, showing what happens when that call is ignored. The previous surah concluded with promises that what you are promised will certainly come and you will have no escape, and after varied signs and warnings, the immediate reminder is that many before did not escape. The phrase “how many societies We have destroyed,” or “wa kam min qaryatin ahlaknaha,” shows the pattern is not rare. “Qaryah” means a town or society, a settled community with leaders, markets, and customs, as earlier “We placed in every society the most wicked to conspire.” Many such societies, from ‘Ad and Thamud to the flourishing peoples before Mecca who were more established in the land, were destroyed for persisting in denial after messengers warned and proof was made distinct.\n\nThe detail “Our torment took them by surprise while sleeping at night or midday,” or “fa-ja’aha ba’suna bayatan aw hum qa’iloona,” captures both the timing and the suddenness. “Bayatan” is night attack, when people sleep vulnerable after a day of activity; “qa’iloona” is midday rest, the siesta in heat when vigilance relaxes. In both cases, the punishment, or “ba’suna,” arrived “baghtatan,” suddenly, as earlier promised for the Hour that takes losers by surprise, and for those showered with everything they desired and became prideful and were seized instantly.\n\nThis timing teaches that security in worldly routine is not protection. Night sleep and midday rest are times of trust, when a person feels safest, yet if hearts have been hardened and misdeeds made appealing, safety is illusory. The earlier warning that torment can come from above or below and split into factions finds illustration here: a specific society’s end came from above or below in its appointed moment, visible history confirming the unseen promise.\n\nA common misconception is to think societies are destroyed only gradually. This verse shows destruction can be sudden at the very hour of ease. For the mindful reader, it revives the opening call to follow what was sent down. Following with a guarded, remembering heart turns heedlessness into preparedness, so midday rest and nighttime sleep remain gifts of the One who causes dawn to break and made night for rest, rather than moments when surprise overtakes the heedless whose deeds were beautified until the term arrived.",
  "5": "“Their only cry—when overwhelmed by Our torment—was, ‘We have indeed been wrongdoers’” captures the moment after the surprising torment just described seized a society at night or midday. The previous verse said how many societies were destroyed when Our punishment came as a sudden visit. Now we hear the human sound that followed. The phrase “Their only cry,” or “fa-ma kana da‘wahum,” means their call, their plea, their declaration, when the punishment overwhelmed was nothing but this: “We have indeed been wrongdoers,” or “inna kunna zalimeen.” The doubling “inna” indeed we were shows forced admission, like those detained before the Fire who beg to return or detained before their Lord who cry “Absolutely, by our Lord!”\n\nThe word “zalimeen,” wrongdoers, is the same character description used throughout the previous surah for fabricators of lies, those who forbid what Allah provided, and those who will never succeed. In this life they called revelation ancient fables and followed elegant deception; under surprise punishment they recognize the label they had denied. Their confession is true but late, paralleling the earlier warning that on the Day signs arrive, belief will not benefit those who did not believe earlier. The tormented society, like individuals bearing burdens on backs, finds all eloquent excuses vanished, leaving only self-incrimination.\n\nThe verse also connects to the covenant of justice that no society is destroyed while its people are unaware. They had been warned, signs were varied, messengers endured, the Book was sent down to warn and to remind, but they preferred guardians besides Him and made deeds appealing until the appointed term.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think a last-moment admission necessarily saves. The Quran distinguishes between timely repentance, which the Lord who took mercy upon Himself welcomes with forgiveness and straightening, and compelled confession under chastisement, which arrives after the door for “mending ways” has closed. For the living, the lesson is to make this cry voluntarily and early, before surprise, like those who say “peace be upon you” to returning believers and who are told their Lord has taken mercy. Saying now, we were among the heedless, seeking openness of chest to Islam, transforms the cry from a condemned epitaph into a living turning that the All-Hearing, All-Knowing answers before the societies of today taste what earlier societies tasted when they persisted in scattered ways.",
  "6": "“We will surely question those who received messengers and We will question the messengers ˹themselves˺” broadens the accounting from destroyed societies who cried we were wrongdoers to the universal questioning that awaits every generation. After warning how many towns were seized at night or midday and how their confession came too late, the Quran now describes the procedure that ensures precise justice at the final gathering. The phrase “We will surely question those who received messengers,” or “fa-la-nas’alanna allatheena ursila ilaihim,” means those to whom messengers were sent, every community given insights from their Lord, from the Mother of Cities and around it to peoples who received Moses and others, will be asked: did the message reach you, how did you respond, did you follow what was sent down or take other guardians, did you dismiss the poor believers or guard orphan wealth and full measure? No claim of “we were unaware of their teachings” will remain, because a blessed, detailed Book was sent to remove that excuse.\n\nThe second questioning balances the first: “And We will question the messengers themselves,” or “wa la-nas’alanna al-mursaleen.” Even the noble messengers, who were told they are not keepers and not maintainers, who said “I only follow what is revealed to me” and “my prayer and worship and life and death are for Allah,” will be asked did they convey faithfully, did they warn and remind as commanded, did they turn away from polytheists as instructed. This is not accusation against them but establishment of perfect testimony, honoring their role as witnesses and removing any doubt about delivery.\n\nThis double questioning guarantees the two prerequisites stated earlier: your Lord would never destroy a society while its people are unaware, and your Lord is never unaware of what they do. Everyone questioned will be given a full account with sure knowledge, because He was never absent, as the next verse announces. A common misunderstanding is to think messengers are exempt from review or that communities can claim they were never told. The verse shows both sender and receiver are examined, making the conclusive argument truly conclusive. For those alive today, it encourages taking followership seriously, seeking the detailed Word, embodying it before testimony is demanded, and remembering that a spontaneous good word of justice or a just weight today will be remembered in a questioning where the Most Subtle, All-Aware will not miss a leaf’s fall or a whisper’s elegance.",
  "7": "“Then We will give them a full account with sure knowledge—for We were never absent” completes the scene of universal questioning that began with the promise to question both those who received messengers and the messengers themselves. The previous verse said We will surely question both groups, establishing testimony. This verse explains what follows: “We will give them a full account,” or “fa-la-naqussanna alaihim bi-ilm,” We will narrate, recount in detail, upon them with knowledge. “Qass” means to relate step by step, story after story, without omitting. The recounting is “with sure knowledge,” or “bi-ilm,” with complete, certain knowledge that misses nothing, and “for We were never absent,” or “wa ma kunna gha’ibeen,” We were never elsewhere while they acted. The Lord who knows what is in land and sea, who knows not a leaf falls without writing it, who calls back souls by night and knows what is done by day, and who sends guardian angels who never neglect, is never absent from any moment.\n\nThe wording “We will give them a full account” implies the account will be so detailed it leaves no gaps for false oaths like “By Allah, we were never polytheists” to stand. Earlier it was said even those who swore falsely would be exposed when mouths are sealed and limbs testify. The full account provides the true narrative against which every claim will be measured, fulfilling the earlier law that messengers are only bringers of good news and warners and that your Lord would never destroy a society while its people remain unaware. Awareness having been delivered, the account now rehearses it with sureness.\n\nThis verse also balances the scale metaphor that immediately follows: the weighing on that Day will be just, heavy scales for the successful, light for those who doomed themselves. Recounting with sure knowledge ensures weighing is not guessed.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to imagine the questioning is like a human interrogation where the questioner learns something new. The phrase “We were never absent” corrects that: questioning is for establishing proof to the questioned, not for acquiring information. Another misunderstanding is to think meticulous narration means harshness. For those who invoked morning and evening seeking His Face, walked with a light among people, established prayer and guarded measure, the full account with knowledge will be a comforting validation, preserving every overlooked kindness. For the heedless who took faith as play, the same detail ensures no wrong is missed. Remembering that He is never absent encourages living as if watched, which is the essence of being mindful.",
  "8": "“The weighing on that Day will be just. As for those whose scale will be heavy ˹with good deeds˺, ˹only˺ they will be successful” describes the measure that follows the full account with sure knowledge. After saying We were never absent while deeds were done, the Quran now says the evaluation of those deeds will be by weighing, or “wazn.” The phrase “The weighing on that Day will be just,” or “wal-waznu yawma’ithin al-haqq,” means the weighing itself is truth, accurate, upright, like the weight that is to be fulfilled with justice earlier commanded for markets. No adding, no subtracting, no confusion between ranks according to deeds will occur, because your Lord is never unaware and is the Swiftest Reckoner.\n\nThe result is then stated: “As for those whose scale will be heavy with good deeds, only they will be successful,” or “fa-man thaqulat mawaazeenuhu fa-ula’ika hum al-muflihoon.” Heavy scales mean abundant goods on the balance, the tenfold multiplied deeds, the clothing of righteousness, the mindful following of the detailed Book. “Mawaazeen” can be the scales themselves or the weighted deeds; scholars say deeds will have weight, given form, so even sincere words will count. Success, or “falah,” is the same triumph described as absolute triumph for whoever is spared torment, and as the end that belongs to the guided who walk the straight path to the Home of Peace.\n\nThis image links to the previous surah’s contrast between play and amusement and the better Home for the mindful, showing that many scattered ways ultimately converge at one balance. Earlier the polytheists were told they will soon know who will fare best in the end; this is how that knowing arrives, by weight.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think weighing is physical like market weighing only. The Quran presents it as precise moral weighing that includes intentions, as earlier the heart’s openness versus its constriction as if climbing into the sky already measured inward states. Another mistake is to think a heavy scale requires extraordinary acts. Even if many deeds are small, the ten-to-one multiplication and Allah’s mercy mean persistence in establishing prayer, remembering morning and evening, eating with His Name, and shunning sin can weight the scale more than a single grand act mixed with shirk. The verse thus encourages steadily accumulating heavy goods while the chance to earn remains, before the Day when belief after signs will no longer benefit those who did no good through faith.",
  "9": "“But those whose scale is light, they have doomed themselves for wrongfully denying Our signs” gives the counterpart to the heavy scale, completing the balance of just weighing. The previous verse said the weighing on that Day will be just and the heavy will be successful. Now the light scale is described. The phrase “those whose scale is light,” or “wa man khaffat mawaazeenuhu,” depicts a balance where good deeds are few, weightless, or nullified, as earlier said had they associated others with Him their good deeds would have been wasted. “Thaqulat” and “khaffat” form a clear pair: heavy versus light, like the expansive chest opened to Islam versus the tight constricted chest. Those light in good are not merely unlucky; the verse says “they have doomed themselves,” or “fa-ula’ika allatheena khasiroo anfusahum,” they ruined, lost themselves, a phrase that repeatedly described deniers who called revelation fables, who demanded shares for idols, and who arrived alone leaving behind everything they were given. Self-dooming shows responsibility; the light scale is the result of their own earning, acquiring sin open and secret, following elegant deception, and persisting in turning others away.\n\nThe cause is named precisely: “for wrongfully denying Our signs,” or “bima kanu bi-ayatina yazlimoon.” “Yazlimoon” indicates continuous injustice, placing signs where they do not belong, denying insights that had come from their Lord, beautifying misdeeds, and insisting revelation never came. This connects to the opening call not to be anxious about the Book sent to warn and remind, and to the command to follow what was sent down rather than take other guardians. Those who denied the signs denied the very light that would have heavy-weighted their scale.\n\nThe verse does not say they are doomed because scales were miscalibrated; it says they wrongfully denied signs, tying weightlessness to denial. This matches the earlier groups who denied the meeting until the Hour surprised them and now bear burdens on backs; light scales and heavy burdens are two images of the same loss.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think a light scale means a person was inherently bad. The Quran shows the path to lightness involves choices: refusing to see with insights, leaning toward decorated lies because the Hereafter was denied, and waiting for angels or final signs while doing no good through faith. The lesson is to make the scale heavy while heaviness still matters, by adding multiplied goods, shunning association even in small forms, and remembering that the Lord who established you on earth and provided livelihood, yet is seldom thanked, will weigh with perfect truth on the Day He was never absent.",
  "10": "“We have indeed established you on earth and provided you with a means of livelihood. ˹Yet˺ you seldom give any thanks” shifts from the scene of weighing to the present bounty that should have made the scales heavy. After describing heavy success and light self-dooming for denying signs, the Quran reminds humanity of the baseline favor that makes denial inexcusable. The phrase “We have indeed established you on earth,” or “wa laqad makkannakum fil-ard,” means We set you firmly, gave you ability, authority, and a settled place to live, much as earlier He placed successors on earth and made night for rest and day for seeking, establishing people and providing means of livelihood. This establishment includes physical stability, homes on firm soil, tools to cultivate gardens and manage cattle fit for labour, all by the One who produces gardens trellised and untrellised and who sends down rain producing clustered grain.\n\nThe next phrase details sustenance: “and provided you with a means of livelihood,” or “wa ja‘alna lakum fiha ma‘ayish,” We made for you therein livings, plural, suggesting many forms: crops of different flavours, olives and pomegranates, meat slaughtered with His Name, trades measured with justice, clothing to cover nakedness and as adornment, and the very breath revived each morning after souls are called back by night. Every leaf and grain recorded is part of that provision system maintained by the Trustee of all things.\n\nThen comes gentle reproach: “Yet you seldom give any thanks,” or “qaleelan ma tashkuroon.” This echoes earlier refrains, “how seldom are you mindful” and “how seldom do they comprehend.” Gratitude, or “shukr,” means recognizing the Giver, mentioning His Name, using provision within explained limits, paying dues at harvest, giving full measure, and turning the face to the Originator. Little thanks means most people use the established earth and its livelihoods while attributing them to other guardians, idols, or chance, as those who made deeds appealing and followed whispering devils.\n\nThe verse prepares for the Adam story that follows, showing that establishment on earth began with creation, shaping, angelic prostration, and provision in Paradise, yet ungratefulness led to deception there as well. A common mistake is to treat provision as self-earned entitlement, forgetting the Establisher. Remembering establishment and provision daily, even in the simple act of eating what Allah provided without following Satan’s footsteps, builds the heavy scale that leads to success rather than to self-dooming.",
  "11": "“Surely We created you, then shaped you, then said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Adam,’ so they all did—but not Iblis, who refused to prostrate with the others” returns to the very beginning of human establishment after the reminder that you were established on earth and given livelihood yet seldom give thanks. The verse compresses the origin story into three stages: “We created you,” or “khalaqnakum,” initial creation from clay as earlier said He created you from clay and appointed terms, and from a single soul; “then shaped you,” or “thumma sawwarnakum,” fashioning into proportion, giving hearing, sight, and hearts as earlier mentioned He could take away; and “then said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Adam,’” honoring the human in his full constitution. The command was to angels, high beings of light who obey without delay, to bow as a sign of honor and obedience to the Creator’s will, not worship of Adam himself. The Quran elsewhere clarifies this prostration was for Adam as a mark of his rank as successor on earth.\n\nThe obedience is noted: “so they all did,” or “fa-sajadoo,” showing the harmony of those who follow what is sent down without taking other guardians. The exception is named: “but not Iblis, who refused to prostrate with the others,” or “illa Iblees lam yakun min al-sajideen.” Iblis was of the jinn, created from fire, living among angels by worship, yet when pride was tested, he refused. “Ab a” means he was too proud to be among the prostrators, anticipating his claim “I am better than he is.” This refusal is the archetype of every later refusal to follow revelation, from those who said revelation is fables to those who divided faith into sects.\n\nThe link to the previous warning about seldom thanks is subtle: gratitude requires humility to recognize the source of establishment. Iblis’s ingratitude was to reject the Giver’s command out of self-comparison. A common misunderstanding is to view prostration as humiliation. In context, it was honor for humanity and a test of surrender to the Lord whose Word is perfected. Another mistake is to see Iblis’s act as minor. The Quran presents it as the first elegant deception turned into disobedience, the root of lying in wait on the Straight Path and approaching from front, back, right, and left to make most ungrateful. Remembering that all were created and shaped and then honored should make gratitude, not arrogance, the response, keeping the scale heavy rather than light through denying signs of the One who is never absent.",
  "12": "“What prevented you from prostrating when I commanded you? He replied, ‘I am better than he is: You created me from fire and him from clay’” records the interrogation that exposes the inner logic of the first rebellion, directly following the statement that Iblis refused to prostrate. The verse begins with Allah’s question: “What prevented you from prostrating when I commanded you?” or “ma mana‘aka alla tasjuda ith amartuk.” This is not seeking unknown information, since We were never absent and He knows what hearts conceal, but establishing proof against the disobedient so his reason is heard and judged. It mirrors later questionings on the Day when both recipients and messengers will be questioned.\n\nThe answer is the classic argument of pride: “I am better than he is: You created me from fire and him from clay,” or “ana khairun minhu khalaqtani min naarin wa khalaqtahu min teen.” Iblis compares origins, assuming fire, which rises, flames, and consumes, is superior to clay, which is low, molded, and still. The comparison assumes superiority is determined by material nobility rather than by obedience to the Lord of everything who placed successors on earth. Authentic tafsir notes Iblis made analogy, or “qiyas,” against a direct command, opposing a clear instruction with his own reasoning. That method, preferring analogy over command, became a pattern for later misguidance, like inventing divisions of cattle based on whims and then claiming revelation supports them.\n\nThe phrase “You created me … You created him” paradoxically affirms Allah as Creator while disobeying Him, as earlier those who associate still admitted Allah created heavens and earth. Iblis acknowledges the Creator yet refuses the Creator’s rank, echoing those who later set up equals with their Lord while knowing He is Lord of everything.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view Iblis’s logic as rational and therefore excusable. The Quran shows its flaw: worth with your Lord is by taqwa, mindful obedience, not by physical origin. Fire’s nature to rise does not grant moral superiority; clay’s ability to be shaped, to grow gardens and hold seeds that sprout, reflects the Subtle, All-Aware design praised earlier. For the listener, the lesson is to watch for the same prideful comparison today: “I am better than he” because of wealth, lineage, education, or followers, which hardens the heart, makes chest tight as if climbing into the sky, and leads to lying in ambush on the Straight Path to make most ungrateful. The cure is the opposite admission soon modelled by Adam, “We have wronged ourselves,” which opens the heart rather than constricting it.",
  "13": "“Then get down from Paradise! It is not for you to be arrogant here. So get out! You are truly one of the disgraced” pronounces the consequence of Iblis’s proud reasoning that fire is better than clay. The previous verse quoted his claim “I am better than he is.” Now the response is an expulsion. The command “Then get down from Paradise,” or “fa-ihbit minha,” is an order to descend from the lofty abode where angels prostrate and where Adam would soon dwell. “Ihbit” means to descend humbled, the opposite of the rising fire he boasted about. The reason is then given: “It is not for you to be arrogant here,” or “fa-ma yakoonu laka an tatakabbara fiha.” Arrogance, or “kibr,” is the very essence of claiming superiority while rejecting the command of the Lord who placed successors on earth and elevated some over others to test. Paradise is a place of purity where arrogance has no standing, just as earlier the verse warned arrogance makes a society ripe for destruction.\n\nThe command is reinforced: “So get out! You are truly one of the disgraced,” or “fa-ukhruj innaka min al-saghira.” “Saghir” means small, low, abased, the opposite of the superiority he claimed. The repetition shows the seriousness of considering oneself “khair” over Adam due to material. By refusing to prostrate when commanded, he claimed knowledge better than the All-Knowing who knows best where to place His message, and whose Word is perfected in truth and justice.\n\nThis expulsion sets the stage for his vow to lie in wait on the Straight Path and to approach from four sides to make most ungrateful, establishing the devils as allies of those who disbelieve and as whisperers with elegant deception. It also contrasts with the fate promised to the mindful who will have the Home of Peace with their Lord as Guardian; the arrogant are expelled from peace and reduced to smallness.\n\nA common mistake is to think arrogance is merely confidence. The Quran defines arrogance as rejecting truth and despising people, exactly what Iblis did: rejecting the command of truth and despising Adam as low clay. For listeners, the verse teaches that lofty rank cannot be claimed by origin but is given by Allah to the humble, and that Paradise is reserved for those who shun sin open and secret, honor orphan wealth, give full measure, and speak justly, all acts of humility before the Only True Master whom no vision can encompass.",
  "14": "“Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection” is the request Iblis makes immediately after being told to get down from Paradise as one of the disgraced for refusing to prostrate. The previous sentence left him expelled for arrogance. Now his words reveal strategic planning rather than remorse. The phrase “Then delay my end,” or “fa-anzirni,” means give me respite, postpone my death or punishment. Classical reports explain Iblis asked not for forgiveness but for time. The deadline he chooses is significant: “until the Day of their resurrection,” or “ila yawmi yub‘athoon,” the Day He will gather all together, the Day about which there is no doubt that opened this entire surah, the Day the trumpet will be blown and when ranks will be assigned.\n\nBy requesting delay until resurrection, Iblis seeks the longest possible extension, not reformation. He intends to use that interval to fulfill the vow he then states: to lie in ambush on the Straight Path and to approach from front, back, right, and left to make most ungrateful. This shows the quality of his enmity is not momentary anger but enduring, patient hostility, the same “aduwwun mubeen,” clear enemy, that believers are warned not to follow step by step.\n\nThe wording mirrors human procrastination that the surah has warned against: those who wait for angels or final signs and think belief after signs will benefit, and those who say we will believe if a sign comes but even if every sign came they would not believe unless Allah willed. Iblis asks for time that others waste, and he uses it to make them waste it too.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think Iblis’s request was denied. The next verse shows it is granted until the appointed Day, demonstrating the divine practice of granting respite and testing, as with nations who were showered with everything they desired after forgetting reminders, leading to sudden seizure. The grant does not indicate approval; it indicates that the world is a test where devils are allowed to whisper with elegant words to see whose hearts incline and whose remain mindful. For the listener, the lesson is to use the granted time oppositely to Iblis: to seek the insights that have come from the Lord, to follow what was sent down, to establish prayer and give full measure, turning respite into gratitude rather than into a stage for ambush.",
  "15": "“You are delayed ˹until the appointed Day˺” is the curt divine reply to Iblis’s request for delay until the Day they are resurrected. The previous verse quoted his plea, “Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection.” Now Allah says, “You are delayed,” or “innaka min al-munzareen,” you are among those given respite, granted time until a known term. The word “munzareen” means those allowed to wait, to remain, not immediately punished, matching earlier descriptions where wrongdoers are given rope, showered with everything after forgetting reminders, before being seized by surprise.\n\nThe phrase “until the appointed Day,” implied in classical recitation as “ila yawmi al-waqti al-ma‘loom,” the Day whose time is known to Allah, shows the respite is bounded. Iblis will not escape the final gathering before the True Master, the weighing that will be just, and the ranks assigned according to deeds. He imagined delay until resurrection would allow him to evade judgment entirely; the reply confirms delay but also confirms appointed accounting, as earlier promised, what you are promised will certainly come and you will have no escape.\n\nThis short exchange teaches a central principle reiterated through this surah: divine allowance is not endorsement. Just as Allah allowed devilish humans and jinn to whisper elegantly so that hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter would incline, and just as He placed the most wicked to conspire yet they plot only against themselves without perceiving, so He allows Iblis time to pursue his ambush. The purpose is to make the way of the wicked distinct and the Straight Path clear, letting each choose.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think that if Allah delays punishment, it means anger has subsided. The Quran shows delay is part of mercy that desires humbling, and part of justice that completes proof. Nations were given suffering to become humble, then abundance to test gratitude, then seizure when pride peaked. Similarly, individuals are given respite in which to repent, as the Lord took mercy upon Himself and said whoever repents after ignorance and mends ways, Allah is Forgiving, Merciful, yet His punishment will not be averted from the wicked who persist.\n\nFor believers, the implication is urgency. The enemy has been given time to ambush the Straight Path from every side; therefore the traveler should cling to guardianship of Allah, follow the detailed Book, invoke morning and evening seeking His Face, and not follow Satan’s footsteps, stepping toward the Home of Peace rather than toward the disgraced who will be filled with their followers in Hell.",
  "16": "“For leaving me to stray I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path” is Iblis’s first declaration of strategy after being granted delay until the appointed Day. The previous exchange gave him respite; now his intent, not repentance, emerges. The phrase “For leaving me to stray,” or “fa-bima aghwaitani,” has been read by scholars in two complementary ways. In one reading, Iblis attributes his straying to being led astray, deflecting blame as wrongdoers later deflect blame to jinn; in another reading, it means “Because You found me astray,” acknowledging his own choice. Both reflect the same evasion seen later when humans and their jinn allies will say “Our Lord, we benefited from each other,” each pointing elsewhere while testifying against themselves.\n\nThe vow itself is explicit: “I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path,” or “la-aq‘udanna lahum sirataka al-mustaqeem.” “Aq‘udanna” means to sit, to lie in wait, to occupy a position of surveillance like a robber on a road, not passing by but settling. The path he occupies is “Your Straight Path,” sirataka al-mustaqeem, the same path described as your Lord’s path perfectly straight made clear to the mindful, the path of Abraham the upright, the path to the Home of Peace. He does not ambush scattered ways; he targets the single straight route where success lies, because diverting from it yields greatest loss. This ambush takes forms already described: elegant words of deception whispered between human and jinn devils, beautifying misdeeds, turning people away from the Prophet, making them deem provision divided for idols fair, and making them say revelation is fables.\n\nThe word “ghawait” for straying connects to earlier straying groups who were deaf and dumb in darkness and to the principle that Allah leaves whom He wills to stray and guides whom He wills to the straight way. Iblis chooses to be the instrument of that straying for those who choose it, exploiting the hardness that follows refusing to believe at first and the chest that tightens as if climbing into the sky.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think Satan attacks only obvious evils. This vow shows he sits on the best path itself, beautifying deviations as righteousness, as later people will say “We found our forefathers doing it and Allah commanded it.” The defense is to follow what was sent down from your Lord and not take other guardians, to remember that no vision can encompass Him but He encompasses all vision, and to seek refuge with the Subtle, All-Aware who hears from every side the very approaches Iblis promises next, front, back, right, and left.",
  "17": "“I will approach them from their front, their back, their right, their left, and then You will find most of them ungrateful” details the method of the ambush promised on the Straight Path, completing Iblis’s plan after being given respite. The previous verse said he would sit in ambush on Your Straight Way; this verse says how he will execute that sitting: by approaching from every accessible angle. The phrase “from their front,” or “min baini aydeehim,” means from before them, blocking the future, making them despair of guidance or procrastinate toward the major signs, saying we will believe when angels come. “From their back,” or “min khalfihim,” means from behind, pulling through attachment to ancestors, as earlier people said “We found our forefathers doing it,” making past custom seem nobler than revelation. “From their right,” or “an aymanihim,” means from the side of goodness, beautifying excess or laxity in worship, making division of faith into sects seem piety, or making waste in harvest seem generosity. “From their left,” or “an shama’ilihim,” means from the side of evil, presenting shameful deeds as attractive, as with killing children made appealing and shameful deeds called commanded by Allah.\n\nThe goal is then stated: “and then You will find most of them ungrateful,” or “wa la tajidu aktharahum shakireen.” Gratitude, or “shukr,” is the response Allah best recognizes, as earlier asked does He not best know the grateful. Iblis’s four-direction assault aims precisely at smothering gratitude, replacing it with forgetfulness, hardness, and the seldom-thanks noted after establishment on earth and provision of livelihood. A heart that is ungrateful no longer invokes morning and evening seeking His Face, no longer pays dues at harvest without waste, and no longer weighs with justice.\n\nClassical commentators note that two directions are not mentioned: from above and from below. Above is the direction of divine mercy and supplication, and below is humility and prostration; mercy and humility remain the believer’s refuge. The Quran therefore teaches to counter four-sided approach with remembrance that rises and bows, with following what was sent down and taking no other guardians, which keeps the chest spacious.\n\nA common misconception is to treat temptation as only from one side. This verse warns that approach adapts, coming as doubt about truth, as nostalgia for tradition, as exaggeration in religion, or as invitation to sin, all to produce the same ungrateful heart whose scale will be light for denying signs. Vigilance from all sides, anchored in gratitude, preserves light to walk among people.",
  "18": "“Get out of Paradise! You are disgraced and rejected! I will certainly fill up Hell with you and your followers all together” is the renewed expulsion that answers Iblis’s vow to ambush the Straight Path from four sides to make most ungrateful. The previous verse detailed his method; this verse gives the divine reply and the final destination for that method’s success. The command repeats “Get out,” or “ukhruj,” but now with added attributes: “disgraced and rejected,” or “math’ooman madhhoora,” humiliated, blamed, banished, distanced from mercy, the same saghir, disgraced, announced earlier. “Math’oom” carries blameworthy abasement, “madhhoor” carries expulsion and driving away, combining inward contempt and outward banishment.\n\nThe sentence then sets the gathering that Iblis himself requested delay to exploit: “I will certainly fill up Hell with you and your followers all together,” or “la-amla’anna Jahannama minka wa mimman tabi‘aka minhum ajma‘een.” “La-amla’anna” is an emphatic oath: I will surely fill. Hell, or “Jahannam,” is named as the Fire that earlier was shown when some were detained before it begging to return, and where the alliance of jinn and humans will be told “The Fire is your home.” The filling includes Iblis and “whoever follows you among them,” or “mimman tabi‘aka,” linking to the alliance of wrongdoers made allies of one another because of misdeeds, and to those who obey devil-whispered arguments about slaughtered meat and thereby become polytheists. “All together,” or “ajma‘een,” stresses comprehensive gathering, none left out, just as all will be gathered before the True Master for questioning with sure knowledge.\n\nThis oath balances the earlier mercy that gave Iblis respite. Respite allows ambush; ambush allows choice; choice leads either to the Home of Peace or to a filled Hell. The severity assures that elegant deception, however beautiful, ends in disgrace. Authentic hadith note that Hell will indeed be filled, with space folding, while Paradise also will be filled with the grateful.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think that Satan’s threat dooms everyone. The Quran says he will find most ungrateful, but not all. Those who follow what was sent down, do not take other guardians, are mindful, and are among the few thankful, walk with a light that his four-sided approach cannot extinguish. Remembering that this weighing will be just and that ranks are according to deeds keeps the traveler from following the rejected one whose way, though widely followed by most who follow assumption, ends in being filled into disgrace together.",
  "19": "“O Adam! Live with your wife in Paradise and eat from wherever you please, but do not approach this tree, or else you will be wrongdoers” shifts the narrative from the expelled, disgraced enemy to the honored first human and his spouse, showing the immediate test that will illustrate both temptation and repentance. The previous verse left Iblis rejected with the oath that Hell will be filled with him and his followers. Now Adam and his wife are addressed directly in Paradise, the same Paradise from which arrogance was banished because it is not for the arrogant to be there. The call “O Adam! Live with your wife,” or “ya Adamu uskun anta wa zawjuka,” uses “uskun” meaning dwell, reside, find tranquility, indicating settlement, not a brief visit. His wife, later named as Hawa, is explicitly included, showing partnership in honor, trial, and later in repentance, where both say “We have wronged ourselves.”\n\nThe provision is generous: “eat from wherever you please,” or “fa-kula min haithu shi’tuma,” from whatever place you wish, in abundance, mirroring the earlier provision of livelihood on earth and the gardens of grapevines, olives, and pomegranates similar yet dissimilar. The abundance emphasizes mercy before command.\n\nThe single boundary is then set: “but do not approach this tree, or else you will be wrongdoers,” or “wa la taqrabaa hathihi al-shajarata fa-takoona min al-zalimeen.” “Do not approach,” or “la taqrabaa,” is stronger than “do not eat,” teaching distance from the occasion of sin, just as later commands say do not come near indecencies open or secret, do not come near the wealth of the orphan except to enhance, and shun all sin open and secret. The specific tree was not detailed in authentic revelation as a known species; its identity matters less than the test it represents: a single, clear, easy limit amid boundless permission, testing obedience, mindfulness, and gratitude.\n\nThe warning “or else you will be wrongdoers,” or “zalimeen,” links directly to the earlier principle that wrongdoers will never succeed and that fabricating lies is the greatest wrong. To cross a clear divine limit is to place desire where obedience belongs.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to read this as a harsh restriction in an otherwise merciful Paradise. The verse shows the opposite: the limit is tiny compared to the permission, a single tree among many, and the response to crossing it, as the next verses show, remains open to forgiveness through acknowledgment, unlike Iblis’s persistence in pride. The lesson for listeners is to recognize that divine limits, like just measure, covenant, and guarding prayer, are few compared to permitted provision, and approaching them invites the classification of wrongdoer that the straight path warns against.",
  "20": "“Then Satan tempted them in order to expose what was hidden of their nakedness. He said, ‘Your Lord has forbidden this tree to you only to prevent you from becoming angels or immortals’” narrates the first whispering that succeeds after the clear permission and single prohibition in Paradise. The previous verse allowed Adam and his wife to eat from wherever they pleased except one tree, warning they would become wrongdoers if they approached it. This verse explains the enemy’s motive and method. The motive, “in order to expose what was hidden of their nakedness,” or “li-yubdiya lahuma ma wooriya anhuma min saw’atihima,” shows Satan’s aim is to strip cover, to expose shame. “Saw’ah” means private parts and the shame that should remain covered. In Paradise, that shame was veiled; removing the cover was not merely physical but a removal of the clothing of righteousness that makes innocence secure, as later the best clothing is identified as righteousness. The enemy who vowed to make most ungrateful begins by making the honored pair lose their covering.\n\nHis method uses decorated deception: “He said, ‘Your Lord has forbidden this tree to you only to prevent you from becoming angels or immortals,’” or “ma nahakuma Rabbukuma an hathihi al-shajarati illa an takoona malakaini ow takoona min al-khalideen.” This is elegant words of deception in its purest form. He implies that the prohibition hides envy, that Allah withholds a higher rank from them, that disobedience is the secret path to angelic status or endless life. The claim flatters with promise of elevation, yet leads to disgrace, mirroring how later fabricators claimed “this portion is for Allah and this for our associates” and how whisperers argued about slaughtered meat.\n\nThe lie inverts reality: obedience to the Lord who established humanity and provides livelihood keeps rank, while following the clear enemy who is disgraced and rejected lowers it. Paradise, where arrogance is not allowed, was their spacious home; the whisper offered an invisible higher home that was illusion.\n\nA common misconception is to think temptation comes as obvious evil. This verse shows it comes as promised good, with a plausible story about divine motive. The defense taught repeatedly is to remember that Allah is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who took mercy upon Himself, that His command after detailed explanation is the guard of the straight path, and that approaching what is forbidden, even slightly, opens the covering. For the living, the passage encourages skepticism toward whispers that explain divine limits as divine stinginess and encourages seeking the blessed Book’s counsel that true immortality is not eaten from a forbidden tree but lived as gratitude for the Home of Peace.",
  "21": "“And he swore to them, ‘I am truly your sincere advisor’” follows the decorated promise that the forbidden tree would make them angels or immortals, adding the false guarantee that makes the whisper almost irresistible. The previous verse showed Satan’s motive to expose hidden nakedness and his method of implying Allah’s prohibition hid a better status. Now his method is strengthened by oath. The word “swore,” or “wa qasamahuma,” means he took an oath, he conjured with emphasis, calling upon Allah’s name while lying, to assure them. The phrase “I am truly your sincere advisor,” or “inni lakuma la-min al-nasiheen,” uses both “inni” indeed I am and “la-min al-nasiheen” surely among the sincere advisors, piling emphasis. The word “nasih” means one who gives “nasihah,” pure, unadulterated counsel that seeks only the good of the advised, like a guardian who seeks the Face of Allah.\n\nSatan adopts the very language of the guardians besides Him that the Quran warned against, the sincere-looking ally. He had just been expelled as disgraced and rejected for saying “I am better than he,” yet now he presents as better in counsel. This inversion is precisely the elegance in words of deception that tilts hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter, where falsehood is made pleasing and then persistent.\n\nThe story shows that the trick succeeded not because Adam and his wife were careless lovers of shame, but because they were trusting; they had known no lying in Paradise before, and a solemn oath in Allah’s name was unprecedented from a deceiver. Reports note they did not imagine anyone would swear falsely by Allah.\n\nFor listeners, this addition of an oath explains why following majority assumption and decorated speech is so dangerous: lies can wear the garment of sincerity and even scripture-like solemnity. The Quran later warns not to follow desires of those who deny proofs and set up equals while swearing solemnly that if a sign came they would believe, yet even if every sign came they would not believe. The defense is not to trust appearance of sincerity alone but to weigh counsel against the detailed, perfected Word whose insights have come and whose Book distinguishes truth from falsehood.\n\nA mistaken idea is that being sincere advisors is proven by intensity of oath alone. This verse shows oaths can be weaponized. The scale that will be heavy with good deeds will weigh not intensity of claim but conformity to the Lord who is never absent and who said the weighing on that Day will be just, so the path remains to follow what was sent down from your Lord and not take other guardians besides Him, however sincerely they swear.",
  "22": "“So he brought about their fall through deception. And when they tasted of the tree, their nakedness was exposed to them, prompting them to cover themselves with leaves from Paradise. Then their Lord called out to them, ‘Did I not forbid you from that tree and ˹did I not˺ tell you that Satan is your sworn enemy?’” traces the fall from oath to outcome and then to the divine call that opens the way back. The opening, “So he brought about their fall through deception,” or “fa-dallaahuma bi-ghuroor,” means he caused them to descend gradually, luring them down step by step with delusion, not by force. The step from graceful living with abundant eating, to approaching the tree, to tasting, was made to feel reasonable by decorated words and the false oath of sincerity.\n\nThe consequence is immediate and intimate: “And when they tasted of the tree, their nakedness was exposed to them,” or “fa-lamma thaqa al-shajarata badat lahuma saw’atuhuma.” Before, nakedness was hidden, clothed even without sewn garments by a covering from Allah that is later called the best clothing of righteousness. After tasting, the cover lifts, and shame appears. “Prompting them to cover themselves with leaves from Paradise,” or “wa tafiqo yakhsifani alaihima min waraqi al-jannah,” shows instinctive return to covering, layering leaves in haste, as humans naturally seek to cover what should remain private when heedlessness removes the veil.\n\nThen the merciful interrogation comes: “Then their Lord called out to them, ‘Did I not forbid you from that tree and did I not tell you that Satan is your sworn enemy?’” The question reminds them of two clear knowledges: the limit “do not approach this tree” and the warning “Satan is your sworn enemy, aduwwun mubeen,” a phrase repeated since the warning not to follow his footsteps in eating what Allah provided and since the story of his ambush on the Straight Path. The call is not to humiliate but to prompt the acknowledgment that Adam and his wife will immediately make.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think the story proves humans are doomed by one lapse as Iblis was. The contrast is central: Iblis persisted with “I am better,” while Adam and his wife. when called, will say “We have wronged ourselves.” One exalts through argument, the other humbles through confession, opening the door to mercy that the next verse shows is never locked for the repentant. For today, the sequence warns that tasting after approaching leads to exposure, and exposure should lead to covering again, not to shameless display, remembering that the best covering remains righteousness lived as gratitude for establishment on earth.",
  "23": "“Our Lord! We have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will certainly be losers” is the supplication Adam and his wife make immediately after being called about the tree and the sworn enemy. The previous verse left them covering with leaves after deception brought them down, and their Lord reminded them of the two clear warnings. Now their response models the opposite of Iblis’s response. Where Iblis said “I am better” and asked only for delay to mislead, they say “We have wronged ourselves,” or “Rabbana zalamna anfusana,” acknowledging that the wrong landed first upon their own selves, as later verses will say those whose scales are light have doomed themselves. The confession is mutual, both say it, both take responsibility without blaming only the deceiver, even though deception occurred.\n\nThe plea that follows shows complete dependence: “If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will certainly be losers,” or “wa illam taghfir lana wa tarhamna la-nakoonanna min al-khasireen.” Two divine acts are requested: forgiveness, or “maghfirah,” to cover and erase the sin, and mercy, or “rahmah,” to restore the former favor and add more. The condition “if You do not” shows they have no other guardian to turn to, echoing the earlier principle to take no guardian besides the Lord who provides for all and is never absent. Losers, or “khasireen,” is the same term used for those who deny the meeting until the Hour surprises them and who murder children foolishly, but here it is invoked as a fear to be averted through forgiveness.\n\nThis prayer was taught by Allah Himself in another place as words of repentance He inspired, fulfilling the principle that after ignorance one repents and mends, and then He is Forgiving, Merciful. It also establishes the pattern for humanity’s stay on earth announced next: residence and provision for an appointed term where living, dying, and resurrecting will happen, with the same choice between humble acknowledgment and proud persistence.\n\nA common misconception is to think repentance after a serious lapse is hopeless. This verse, placed after the story of the first human error, teaches immediately that loss is not final when confession, forgiveness, and mercy meet. For daily life, it becomes a frequent litany, turning each exposure of nakedness, whether literal or moral, into a return to covering with leaves of repentance and righteousness, remembering that the Lord whose weighing will be just also loves to forgive those who wronged themselves and who choose to see with insights before signs arrive when belief will no longer benefit.",
  "24": "“Descend as enemies to each other. You will find in the earth a residence and provision for your appointed stay” gives the sentence that follows Adam’s repentance, establishing the earth’s role as a place of testing rather than permanent punishment without hope. The previous verse quoted the Adam couple’s prayer acknowledging wrong and seeking forgiveness and mercy lest they be losers. This verse then says, “Descend,” or “ihbitoo,” addressing Adam, his wife, and Iblis together, indicating that enmity is now part of the world’s fabric. The phrase “as enemies to each other,” or “ba‘dukum li-ba‘din aduww,” depicts a triangle of hostility: humanity versus Satan and his soldiers who watch from where you cannot see, humanity versus whispering desires that beautify evil, and even among humans where devilish allies reinforce each other. This matches the ambush on the Straight Path from four sides to make most ungrateful.\n\nYet the sentence pairs descent with provision: “You will find in the earth a residence and provision for your appointed stay,” or “wa lakum fil-ardi mustaqarrun wa mata‘un ila heen.” “Mustaqarr” means a stable dwelling, a place to settle, echoing earlier assignment of a place to live and another to be laid to rest, and “mata‘” means enjoyment, goods, means of livelihood, the same ma‘ayish that were given on establishment yet seldom thanked. “Until an appointed term,” or “ila heen,” mirrors every matter having a set time and the term that had been delayed for Iblis and the term the polytheists will reach. Earth is therefore not merely exile; it is equipped residence with means to reach the Home of Peace if used rightly.\n\nThe immediate next verse adds the longer horizon: there you will live, there you will die, and from there be resurrected, confirming that earthly residence is bracketed between life and return to the True Master for informing.\n\nA misunderstanding is to read “Descend” as proof that Adam’s repentance was rejected. The Quran elsewhere states Adam’s Lord chose him again and guided him, showing forgiveness was granted before descent, and descent is deployment to the role of successor on earth foretold even before creation. For listeners, the message is to recognize the earth as both battlefield with a clear enemy and as a provisioned home where following what was sent down, not taking other guardians, and wearing the best clothing of righteousness guards against the enemy whose deception first exposed nakedness and whose ongoing aim is to strip that clothing again.",
  "25": "“There you will live, there you will die, and from there you will be resurrected” states the full lifecycle of the earthly descent announced just before, sealing the movement from Paradise to earth and back to gathering. The previous verse said descend as enemies to each other, with earth as residence and provision for an appointed stay. Now the three stages are plainly listed. “There you will live,” or “fiha tahyawna,” meaning earth is where the term appointed is completed, where the chest may be opened to Islam or tightened as if climbing into the sky, where the blessed Book is followed or neglected, where wealth of orphans is guarded or consumed, and where scales are made heavy or light.\n\n“there you will die,” or “wa fiha tamootona,” affirms that death on earth is inevitable, as earlier angels take the soul without neglecting and as the Hour comes suddenly while sleeping at night or midday. No descent from Paradise grants escape from that appointment; every successor is a traveler whose residence is temporary.\n\nThe culmination is “and from there you will be resurrected,” or “wa minha tukhrajoon,” and from it you will be brought forth, echoing earlier promises that from seeds Allah brings forth living and will bring forth living from dead on the Day He says Be and it will be. “Tukhrajoon” suggests being brought out, like plants emerging from soil after rain, matching earlier imagery of rain producing green stalks with clustered grain as proof of resurrection. This completes the circle opened at creation when you were created and shaped and honored, now confirming you will be shaped again for account.\n\nThis simple triad corrects the ancient and modern claim that there is nothing beyond this worldly life and we will never be resurrected, a claim earlier quoted from deniers and refuted by the power that splits dawn and knows every leaf. It also answers the waiting for angels or the Lord Himself: resurrection is the appointed exit from the earthly residence to the weighing that will be just and to the informing where He will recite with sure knowledge, never having been absent.\n\nFor living practice, the verse advises treating residence as preparation. Since provision and livelihood are given as a test, using them to follow what was sent down rather than taking other guardians, covering nakedness with righteousness rather than display, and guarding thanks in a world seldom grateful, builds the weight that leads to the Home of Peace. The descent is thus not an end but a timed stewardship that ends where it began, before the One who established you, questioned you, and whose Word is perfected, from whose earth you will indeed be brought forth.",
  "26": "“O children of Adam! We have provided for you clothing to cover your nakedness and as an adornment. However, the best clothing is righteousness. This is one of Allah’s bounties, so perhaps you will be mindful” addresses all descendants after recounting their parents’ descent, exposure, hasty covering with leaves, repentance, and appointment to live, die, and be resurrected from earth. The call “O children of Adam,” or “ya Bani Adam,” generalizes the lesson, showing the story is not distant history but family history. The first mercy noted is clothing: “We have provided for you clothing to cover your nakedness and as an adornment,” or “anzalna alaikum libasan yuwaree saw’atikum wa reesha.” “Anzalna” literally We sent down clothing, suggesting it comes from above like other provisions, whether through raw materials like cotton, wool, and hides sent down through creation, or through the knowledge to make it. Clothing serves two functions: to cover “saw’ah,” private shame, and as “reesh,” adornment, beauty, plumes, indicating that Islam does not forbid looking well, as gardens and cattle were given for use without waste.\n\nThen the verse lifts the gaze from fabric to character: “However, the best clothing is righteousness,” or “wa libasu al-taqwa thalika khair.” “Taqwa” is mindful consciousness, guarding against what angers Allah by living aware of Him, the same quality earlier said makes the Hereafter home better, that makes signs clear to those who remember, and that distinguishes the straight path. The clothing of taqwa is better because it covers the heart’s nakedness that elegance of deception seeks to expose and because it adorns inwardly with humility, gratitude, and justice that outlasts outward fabric.\n\nThe closing, “This is one of Allah’s bounties, so perhaps you will be mindful,” or “thalika min ayatillah la‘allahum yaththakkaroon,” ties this bounty to the bounties of establishment and livelihood seldom thanked. Mindfulness, or “tadhakkur,” means remembering the cover and not letting Satan strip it as he did the parents.\n\nA common error today is to treat clothing only as fashion or only as shame, swinging between display and neglect. The Quran teaches both covering and adornment, but ranks righteousness as the truest adornment that makes weighing heavy and residence on earth successful. Protecting that best clothing means following what was sent down, not taking other guardians, and not allowing four-sided approaches to make the heart ungrateful before the Day when full accounts are given with sure knowledge.",
  "27": "“O children of Adam! Do not let Satan deceive you as he tempted your parents out of Paradise and caused their cover to be removed in order to expose their nakedness” directly warns the descendants after their parents’ story of permission, deception, exposure, covering, and repentance. The call “O children of Adam” makes the warning personal; if your parents could be lured despite dwelling in Paradise, you, living amid the enmity announced as descent with residence and provision until an appointed time, should be even more vigilant. The phrase “Do not let Satan deceive you as he tempted your parents,” or “la yaftinannakum al-shaytanu kama akhraja abawaikum,” uses “fitnah” meaning to test, to seduce, to lead into trial by beautification. The manner is specified: he caused their cover to be removed in order to expose nakedness, the very aim already stated as exposing what was hidden, stripping the clothing of righteousness that was also called the best clothing. The tactic remains: promise of elevation, hidden good, and the gilded oath of sincere advice to make approach feel safe.\n\nThe verse then explains why vigilance is hard: “Surely he and his soldiers watch you from where you cannot see them,” or “innahu yarakum huwa wa qabeeluhu min haithu la tarawnahum.” “Qabeel” means his tribe, his soldiers, the devilish humans and jinn who whisper with elegant words. They observe without being observed, like an ambusher on the Straight Path waiting from four sides. Human eyes cannot detect them, but the All-Aware who encompasses all vision can, and the subtle Lord who knows the falling leaf can make them visible to the heart through remembrance.\n\nThe closing loops to a law: “We have made the devils allies of those who disbelieve,” or “inna ja‘alna al-shayateena awliya’a lillatheena la yu’minoonoon.” Alliance is not forced; it forms when people disbelieve in the Hereafter, incline to decorated lies, and persist in evil pursuits, allowing devils to become their awliya, guardians besides Him, while believers keep Allah as Guardian toward the Home of Peace.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think the story of Adam is ancient while modern deception is different. The verse says the method is identical: beautify approaching limits and expose what should stay covered, watching from unseen places. The defense taught earlier is to not come near indecencies open or secret, to wear taqwa as best clothing, and to follow what was sent down, remembering that the One whose weighing will be just is never absent from the unseen watchers’ gaze.",
  "28": "“Whenever they commit a shameful deed, they say, ‘We found our forefathers doing it and Allah has commanded us to do it.’ Say, ‘No! Allah never commands what is shameful. How can you attribute to Allah what you do not know?’” unmasks the next trick after warning not to be deceived as parents were. The phrase “Whenever they commit a shameful deed,” or “wa itha fa‘aloo fahishatan,” uses “fahishah,” an enormity openly foul, like the pre-Islamic practice of circumambulating the Kaaba naked claiming purity, or other indecencies excused as tradition. Two excuses are quoted together: “We found our forefathers doing it,” appealing to ancestral custom, and “and Allah has commanded us to do it,” attributing the shame to divine instruction. Both mimic the earlier fabrications where divisions of cattle were claimed to be from Allah while being lies without knowledge.\n\nThe commanded reply is sharp and clear: “Say, ‘No! Allah never commands what is shameful,’” or “qul inna Allaha la ya’muru bil-fahsha’.” Allah’s commands were just recited in the code: not associating partners, honoring parents, not killing children for fear of poverty, not coming near indecencies open or secret, guarding orphan wealth, fulfilling measure and covenant, all guided to the straight path that is your Lord’s Path. Nothing in that path is fahishah. To claim He commands shame is to invert His Word which is perfected in truth and justice.\n\nThe question that follows exposes ignorance: “How can you attribute to Allah what you do not know?” or “a-taqooloona ala Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon.” This mirrors earlier challenges: “Do you have any knowledge that you can produce for us? You follow only assumption,” and “Tell me with knowledge if you are truthful.” Attributing a command to Allah without knowledge is the same as fabricating lies against Him, one of the greatest wrongs that will never succeed and that earns dreadful punishment for turning away.\n\nA common error is to think ancestral continuity sanctifies a practice. The Quran separates heritage from authority; heritage must be measured against the detailed Book. Another error is to think divine permission for adornment, or eating and drinking without waste, contradicts modesty. The next verses will show adornment during worship and lawful provision are gifts from Allah, while shame is not. The safeguard is sincerity, uprightness, and calling upon Him alone, distinguishing what He truly commanded from what devils make appear as commanded while watching from where you cannot see them.",
  "29": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘My Lord has commanded uprightness and dedication ˹to Him alone˺ in worship, calling upon Him with sincere devotion. Just as He first brought you into being, you will be brought to life again’” sets the true command against the false claim that Allah orders shame. After the polytheists said “We found our forefathers doing it and Allah commanded it,” and were told He never commands fahishah, the Prophet is instructed to state what the Lord actually commands. The opening, “My Lord has commanded uprightness,” or “qul amara Rabbi bil-qist,” uses “qist” meaning justice, balance, equity, like giving full measure and weighing with justice, speaking justly even regarding relatives, and judging fairly. Uprightness is the straight path without crookedness, the Deenan Qiyaman perfect way.\n\nThe next command is “and dedication to Him alone in worship,” or “wa aqimoo wujoohakum inda kulli masjid,” literally establish your faces at every place of worship, meaning direct the entire face and being toward Him at every prostration, in every mosque, at every prayer, as earlier “I have turned my face to the Originator” and “Establish prayer and be mindful of Him” indicated. The phrase “calling upon Him with sincere devotion,” or “wa id‘oohu mukhliseena lahu al-deen,” emphasizes “ikhlas,” purity, making religion sincerely His alone, without seeking other guardians.\n\nThe verse then reminds of power that makes both uprightness and resurrection coherent: “Just as He first brought you into being, you will be brought to life again,” or “kama bada’akum ta‘oodoon.” As He created you from clay, shaped you, originated you from a single soul, assigned a place to live and to be laid to rest, gave life to a dead heart with a light to walk among people, and promised you will live, die, and be resurrected from the earth, so He will bring you back. This reasoning was used earlier to answer “How can you be deluded” when He brings living from dead.\n\nA misunderstanding is to view worship as occasional ritual detached from justice. This verse joins them: Qist in dealings and ikhlas on the prayer mat are both commanded by the same Lord, showing that social uprightness and devotional sincerity are one command. For practice, it means dressing properly for worship rather than the pre-Islamic naked circumambulation, eating from lawful adornments without waste, and remembering that the One who first created in truth will recreate with truth on the Day the weighing will be just, when guidance that was offered is weighed and ranks assigned.",
  "30": "“He has guided some, while others are destined to stray. They have taken devils as their masters instead of Allah— thinking they are ˹rightly˺ guided” explains the division that results from the upright command just stated, linking back to the earlier principle that guidance belongs to Allah who opens chests to Islam and tightens others as if climbing into the sky. The verse says “He has guided some,” or “fareeqan hada,” a group whom He guided, and “while others are destined to stray,” or “wa fareeqan haqqa alaihim al-dalalah,” another group upon whom straying has become justly established. The wording “haqqa” indicates straying became their due after choices: they turned away when insights came, refused to believe at first, leaned toward elegant deception because they disbelieved in the Hereafter, and made deeds seem appealing.\n\nThe reason is then given: “They have taken devils as their masters instead of Allah—thinking they are rightly guided,” or “innahum ittakhathoo al-shayateena awliya’a min doon Allahi wa yahsaboona annahum muhtadoon.” This is the alliance warned about where devils become allies of those who disbelieve, and where wrongdoers are made allies of one another because of misdeeds. “Awliya” here are guardians, protectors, authorities obeyed, exactly the guardians the Book warned not to take besides Him. The tragedy is self-deception, or “yahsaboona,” they reckon, suppose, think they are guided, like those earlier who followed assumption and beautified killing of children thinking it was commanded.\n\nThis self-perception shows that feeling guided is not proof of guidance. The true proof is conformity to what was sent down, to the Book perfectly explained whose Word is perfected in truth and justice. The verse links to the repeated note that most people who follow assumption lie, and that elegant words of deception tilt hearts that are already disbelieving.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to conclude that if guidance and straying are by Allah’s will, human effort is irrelevant. The Quran pairs will with reason: those who took devils as masters are destined to stray because they chose that guardianship. Another mistake is to think sincerity alone guarantees guidance while following devils. The verse shows sincerity misdirected to devils still counts as straying. The safeguard is to test guardianship against the Lord’s command of qist and ikhlas and against the prophetic warning not to be deceived as parents were when the enemy swore he was a sincere advisor. Choosing alliance with the All-Hearing, All-Knowing over the watching unseen soldiers keeps one among the guided who will have the Home of Peace rather than among those who only think they are guided while wandering blindly in defiance.",
  "31": "“O Children of Adam! Dress properly whenever you are at worship. Eat and drink, but do not waste. Surely He does not like the wasteful” gives practical instructions for honoring the body and bounty that follow the warning about mistaking devils for guides. After saying He has guided some and others are destined to stray because they took devils as masters thinking they are rightly guided, the Quran addresses all descendants with counsel that restores the two coverings introduced earlier: clothing that covers nakedness and adornment, and righteousness that is the best clothing. The call “O Children of Adam” recalls that parents were first given beautiful provision in Paradise and later leaves to cover after tasting, establishing dress as part of human dignity.\n\nThe first command, “Dress properly whenever you are at worship,” or “khuthoo zeenatakum inda kulli masjid,” means take your adornment at every place of prostration, every prayer and every act of worship. “Zeenah” is beauty, adornment, clean and presentable clothing that reflects giving thanks for establish-ment and livelihood. This corrects the shameful deed where pagans circumambulated naked claiming Allah commanded it, and whose claim was answered that He never commands shame. Proper dress at worship shows uprightness, or “qist,” and dedication with sincerity, turning the face to the Originator who is never absent.\n\nThe second command balances nourishment: “Eat and drink, but do not waste,” or “wa kuloo wa ishrabou wa la tusrifoo.” The earlier permissions to eat clustered grain, gardens, and lawful cattle with His Name, and to pay dues at harvest but not waste, are condensed here. Eating and drinking are permitted as part of the means of livelihood seldom thanked; waste, or “israf,” is exceeding the bound by excess, neglect, or showing off, whether in food, clothing, or worship itself.\n\nThe seal, “Surely He does not like the wasteful,” or “innahu la yuhibbu al-musrifeen,” is the same phrase used for harvest waste. The Lord whose establishment and livelihood should evoke gratitude does not love excess that forgets gratitude. A common error is to view ascetic nakedness as piety or wasteful indulgence as gratitude. The verse corrects both toward the middle path of the straight way: dress with dignity for worship, enjoy lawful provision thankfully, and avoid waste, wearing both the adornment of fabric and the better clothing of mindfulness that remains aware of the gathering before Him.",
  "32": "“Who has forbidden the adornments and lawful provisions Allah has brought forth for His servants? Say, ‘They are for the enjoyment of the believers in this worldly life, but they will be exclusively theirs on the Day of Judgment. This is how We make Our revelations clear for people of knowledge’” confronts the invented taboos that made some adornment and food seem unlawful. The previous verse taught to dress properly for worship, eat and drink without waste, and the earlier catalogue exposed those who said cattle and crops are reserved and who divided shares for idols. Now the Quran asks a rhetorical question that expects no human legislator: “Who has forbidden the adornments and lawful provisions Allah has brought forth,” or “man harrama zeenata Allahi allati akhraja li-ibadihi wat-tayyibati min al-rizq.” “Zeenah” includes lawful clothing and beauty He sent down for covering and adornment, and “tayyibat min al-rizq” means good, pure, wholesome provisions, like gardens trellised and untrellised, olives and pomegranates, cattle fit for labour and small graziers. “Akhraja” emphasizes He brought them forth, not devils or associates.\n\nThe answer is implied: no one has authority to forbid what Allah made lawful without knowledge, just as the greatest wrong is to fabricate lies against Him about lawful and forbidden, as asked earlier “Has He forbidden the two males or the two females?” The verse then announces the distribution: “Say, They are for the enjoyment of the believers in this worldly life, but they will be exclusively theirs on the Day of Judgment,” or “qul hiya lillatheena amanoo fil-hayati al-dunya khalisatan yawma al-qiyamah.” In this world, disbelievers may also enjoy adornment and provision by mercy, but in the Hereafter delight is purified, exclusive, unmixed with fear or reckoning, like the Home of Peace versus Hell’s boiling drink.\n\nThe closing, “This is how We make Our revelations clear for people of knowledge,” or “kathalika nufassilu al-ayat li-qawmin ya‘lamoon,” repeats the pattern of varying signs for those who know, comprehend, remember. Knowledge here means learning that the detailed Book is judge, not elegant deception.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think piety requires rejecting beauty. The Quran shows adornment and tayyibat are brought forth for servants to use gratefully, within justice and without waste, distinguishing true prohibitions like open and secret indecencies and associating partners, which the next verse lists, from invented fabrications. The informed person therefore enjoys gratefully now and hopes for exclusive joy later where no provision is left behind as when they came alone.",
  "33": "“My Lord has only forbidden open and secret indecencies, sinfulness, unjust aggression, associating ˹others˺ with Allah ˹in worship˺—a practice He has never authorized—and attributing to Allah what you do not know” lists the true prohibitions that answer the question who has forbidden adornment and lawful provision. After affirming that adornments and wholesome provisions are brought forth for believers to enjoy in this world and exclusively in the next, the Quran clarifies what is actually forbidden, contrasting the short, clear divine list with the pagans’ long invented divisions.\n\nThe first category, “open and secret indecencies,” or “al-fawahisha ma zahara minha wa ma batana,” covers shameful acts visible and hidden, as earlier commanded not to come near indecencies openly or secretly and not to follow Satan’s footsteps that led to stripping cover. “Sinfulness,” or “al-ithm,” is every act of disobedience that earns punishment, earlier commanded to shun all sin open and secret. “Unjust aggression,” or “al-baghyu bi-ghairi al-haqq,” means transgression without right, like spilling sacred life without legal right, usurping orphan wealth, or giving short measure, all violations of uprightness, or qist.\n\nThe list then reaches the gravest two: “associating others with Allah—a practice He has never authorized,” or “an tushrikoo billahi ma lam yunazzil bihi sultana,” the same “sultan,” authoritative proof, mentioned when Abraham asked how to fear associate-gods without authorization. Shirk is greatest because it nullifies good deeds and makes the scale light. Finally, “and attributing to Allah what you do not know,” or “wa an taqooloo ala Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon,” captures the method behind invented taboos: speaking about lawful and unlawful without knowledge, like dividing wombs between males and females or claiming shameful deeds are commanded.\n\nThis catalogue mirrors the earlier code: do not associate partners, do not kill children, do not approach indecencies, do not kill the sacred soul, do not come near orphan wealth except to enhance, give full measure, speak justly, fulfill covenant, all paths whose straightness leads to mindfulness. A common mistake is to think religion forbids the beautiful while allowing these core wrongs. The verse reverses the picture: beauty and tayyibat are permitted for thanksgiving, while the listed indecencies, sins, aggressions, shirk, and speaking without knowledge are the true fences that the All-Hearing, All-Knowing never authorized to cross.",
  "34": "“For each community there is an appointed term. When their time arrives, they can neither delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it” places the newly listed true prohibitions within the framework of time that has governed this surah’s warnings. After cataloguing what the Lord actually forbids, from open indecencies to speaking without knowledge, the Quran reminds that persistently crossing those lines as a community has a limit. The phrase “For each community there is an appointed term,” or “wa li-kulli ummatin ajal,” uses “ummah,” a community bound by shared life and belief, and “ajal,” a fixed term, like the appointed term earlier described for living, dying, and being resurrected from earth, and like the term human and jinn allies admitted they had reached. Just as individuals have a place to live and a place to be laid to rest, communities collectively have a span within which warnings are varied, signs are made distinct, and proof is made conclusive.\n\nThe precision is then stressed: “When their time arrives, they can neither delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it,” or “fa-itha ja’a ajaluhum la yasta’khiroona sa‘atan wa la yastaqdimoon.” Hour, or “sa‘ah,” here means the briefest moment. Human cleverness, large numbers, and elegant conspiracy, like the greatest wicked in every society who plot without perceiving they plot against themselves, cannot move the appointed hour by a heartbeat. This matches the law that your Lord is swift in punishment but also all-forgiving, and that He is never unaware of deeds.\n\nThis verse links directly to earlier warnings that torment took societies at night or midday suddenly, that their only cry was we were wrongdoers too late, and that what you are promised will certainly come with no escape. It also balances the delay given to Iblis until the appointed Day, showing his respite is itself bounded by an ajal.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat community terms as distant and irrelevant to personal conduct. The Quran shows collective terms are built from individual choices to follow what was sent down or to take other guardians, to eat the tayyibat thankfully or to forbid them without knowledge. Contemporary communities that beautify shameful deeds and attribute lies to Allah accumulate toward their term exactly as past communities did. Remembering the unmovable appointment encourages hastening toward the best clothing of righteousness, sincere worship, and gratitude for provision before the hour that can neither be delayed nor hastened renders belief after it useless for those who did not believe earlier.",
  "35": "“O children of Adam! When messengers from among yourselves come to you reciting My revelations—whoever shuns evil and mends their ways, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve” returns to the universal call after establishing that each community has an unmovable appointed term. The previous verse said when the time arrives it cannot be delayed or advanced; this verse explains the purpose of the time given. The call “O children of Adam,” as earlier with clothing and not letting Satan deceive, gathers all descendants without distinction, echoing the origin from a single soul. “When messengers from among yourselves come to you reciting My revelations,” or “ya’tiyannakum rusulun minkum yaqussoona alaikum ayati,” means messengers sharing your nature, language, and concerns, as earlier it was said messengers from among you proclaiming revelations and warning of the meeting. Their task is “qass,” recounting signs with sure knowledge, the same varied signs made clear for people of knowledge.\n\nThe condition for success after hearing is then stated: “whoever shuns evil and mends their ways,” or “fa-man ittaqa wa aslaha.” Like earlier “whoever believes and does good, there will be no fear for them,” and “whoever commits evil ignorantly then repents and mends,” this pairs guarding, or “taqwa,” shunning what the true prohibitions listed, with “islah,” repairing, making good after fault. It is not perfection without lapse but guarding and patching.\n\nThe promise is luminous: “there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve,” or “fa-la khawfun alaihim wa la hum yahzanoon,” repeated throughout the Book for the heavy-scaled, the guided, and the entrusted who never disbelieve. No fear of the appointed term’s sudden torment, no fear of the gathering before the True Master, and no grief for worldly play left behind, because the Home of Peace awaits.\n\nThe verse contrasts the plight of those who do not guard and mend, whose term ends with the cry we were wrongdoers and whose testimony will be they were disbelievers. A common mistake is to think messengers came only for ancient societies. The Quran frames their coming as recurring until the term, with the final messenger warning the Mother of Cities and around it with the blessed Book so no one can say we were unaware. The practical invitation is to respond while the term still allows: shun the listed indecencies and associations, mend what was approached near of sin open and secret, and hold fast to the straight path so fear and grief are replaced by the security earlier promised to those whose faith is not tarnished.",
  "36": "“But those who receive Our revelations with denial and arrogance will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever” gives the counterpart to the promise that whoever shuns evil and mends will have no fear nor grief. The previous verse opened hope for children of Adam when messengers come reciting revelations; this verse states the outcome for the opposite response. The phrase “receive Our revelations with denial and arrogance,” or “allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina wa-istakbaroo anha,” joins two attitudes already described at length: denying, or “taktheeb,” calling signs fables, attributing adornment laws to Allah without knowledge, and arrogantly deeming oneself above surrender, like Iblis who said I am better and like those whose hearts were hardened and veils cast.\n\nThe sentence uses intensified arrogance, “istakbaroo,” to consider oneself too great to submit, refusing to prostrate to the command, refusing to eat what Allah’s Name is mentioned upon, refusing to approach worship properly adorned. The recompense is “will be the residents of the Fire,” or “ula’ika as-haabu al-Nar,” companions, inhabitants who stay, abiding as earlier the alliance of jinn and humans said the Fire is your home to stay forever, and “they will be there forever,” or “hum fiha khalidoon,” lingering, not a passing visit. This matches the warning that belief will not benefit after your Lord’s major signs arrive for those who did not believe earlier or did no good through faith.\n\nThe verse also echoes the just weighing: light scales doom themselves for wrongfully denying signs, while heavy scales belong to the successful. Residency in Fire is the light-scale outcome made vivid.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think denial is limited to verbal rejection. The Quran’s pairing shows denial plus arrogance includes lived rejection, like taking devils as masters thinking they are rightly guided, forbidding adornments Allah brought forth, and committing shameful deeds while claiming forefathers and Allah commanded them. The alternative was earlier offered: follow what was sent down from your Lord and do not take other guardians; how seldom you are mindful. The door remains open to shun and mend before the term arrives that can neither be delayed nor advanced, before the day the account with sure knowledge leaves confession as the only word: we were disbelievers. Choosing humble reception now places one among those whose scales are heavy rather than among those whose arrogance lights the Fire’s companionship.",
  "37": "“Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His revelations? They will receive what is destined for them, until Our messenger-angels arrive to take their souls, asking them, ‘Where are those false gods you used to invoke besides Allah?’ They will cry, ‘They have failed us,’ and they will confess against themselves that they were indeed disbelievers” intensifies the verdict on denial with arrogance by naming it the greatest wrong and then depicting its boundary at death. The question “Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His revelations?” echoes the repeated refrain about the worst injustice, earlier tied to claiming false prophecy and attributing taboos without knowledge. Fabrication, or “iftara,” includes saying Allah commanded shame, dividing provision for idols unfairly, and claiming adornments are forbidden. Denial, or “taktheeb,” includes calling the blessed, detailed Book fables.\n\nThe phrase “They will receive what is destined for them,” or “ula’ika yanaluhum naseebuhum min al-kitab,” means their share written in the clear record that none can change will reach them exactly as decreed until the appointed term for their community arrives. The portion may be provision in this world, respite, or the recorded deeds, but it continues only until the threshold: “until Our messenger-angels arrive to take their souls,” or “hatta itha ja’at-hum rusuluna yatawaffawnahum,” the same angels earlier described as never neglecting, who take without delay and who will stretch hands to the fabricators saying give up your souls today you will be rewarded with disgrace.\n\nThe angels’ question exposes the lie: “Where are those false gods you used to invoke besides Allah?” or “aina ma kuntum tad‘oona min dooni Allah.” The question is the same as posed on the Day all are gathered, “Where are your partners you used to claim?” The expected rescue never appears. The answer is stripped: “They have failed us,” or “dalloo anna,” they have strayed from us, they led us astray and then vanished, as earlier said fabricated gods will fail and claims will stray. The closing, “and they will confess against themselves that they were indeed disbelievers,” or “wa shahidoo ala anfusihim annahum kanu kafireen,” mirrors the gathering where all testify against themselves that worldly life deluded them, confirming the earlier pattern that denial born of arrogance ends in forced confession at the moment scale weight is sealed.\n\nA common self-deception is to trust in naseeb, allotted share, without preparing for its end. The verse warns the share ends when the takers arrive, asking the unanswerable question that reveals who truly owns guidance and rescue.",
  "38": "“Enter the Fire along with the evil groups of jinn and humans that preceded you. Whenever a group enters Hell, it will curse the preceding one until they are all gathered inside, the followers will say about their leaders, ‘Our Lord! They have misled us, so multiply their torment in the Fire.’ He will answer, ‘It has already been multiplied for all, but you do not know’” describes the social agony that follows the solitary testimony at death, when fabricated lies meet their destination. The previous verse showed soul-takers asking where false gods are, and the dying confessing they failed us. Now the command is collective: “Enter the Fire along with the evil groups of jinn and humans that preceded you,” or “qala udkhuloo fin ar an qablakum min al-jinn wal-ins.” “Umam” means communities, groups that shared a way, like the “umam amthalukum” earlier for animal communities, but here grouped by misguidance: devilish humans and jinn who whispered, most wicked who conspired in every town, wrongdoers made allies of one another.\n\nInside, the social bond that beautified alliance shatters: “Whenever a group enters Hell, it will curse the preceding one,” or “kullama dakhalat ummatun la‘anat ukhtaha.” “La‘anat” means to curse, to ask for distance from mercy. Those who once thought they were rightly guided by taking devils as masters now see guidance as misguidance and love as enmity. The scene continues “until they are all gathered inside,” or “hatta itha iddarakoo fiha jamee‘an,” until they are piled together, followers and followed all caught in the same Fire whose filling with you and your followers was promised to Iblis.\n\nThe followers plead: “Our Lord! They have misled us, so multiply their torment in the Fire,” or “Rabbana ha’ula’i adalloona fa-atihim athaban di‘fan.” “Di‘fan” means doubled, multiplied. The answer is sobering: “It has already been multiplied for all, but you do not know,” or “li-kullin di‘fun wa lakin la ta‘lamoon.” Each group’s torment is already multiplied, followers for obeying, leaders for misleading, as wrongdoers plotted only against themselves yet also earned burdens for misguiding others.\n\nA common misunderstanding is that blame can be shifted to leaders as sincere excuse. The Quran shows leaders and followers both receive multiplied recompense because alliance was chosen when devils were taken as masters thinking they were guided. The next verse shows leaders retort “you were no better than us, taste what you committed,” sealing mutual responsibility before the Just weighing that never wrongs.",
  "39": "“Then the leaders will say to their followers, ‘You were no better than us! So taste the torment for what you used to commit’” continues the exchange inside the Fire after followers asked for doubled punishment for leaders who misled them and were told all already have doubled portions but you do not know. The previous scene showed groups cursing predecessors as they entered. Now the leaders answer. The phrase “You were no better than us,” or “fa-ma kana lakum alaina min fadl,” means you were not superior to us, you were not favored over us by guidance or innocence. This rebuts the followers’ implied claim that they were victims without choice. Leaders point out that followers willingly accepted elegant deception, followed assumptions, forbade adornments Allah brought forth, and said Allah commanded shame, while guidance had been sent down and insight had come with sure knowledge. Both sides turned away from the reminder, took devils as masters, and thought they were rightly guided.\n\nThe command “So taste the torment for what you used to commit,” or “fa-thooqoo al-athaba bima kuntum taksiboon,” uses “thooqoo,” taste, as earlier the punishment for disbelief was told to be tasted and as dwellers of Hell will taste boiling drink and flame cover. “Bima kuntum taksiboon” ties directly to ranks according to deeds and earning sin open and secret. Taste implies immediate, personal experience that leaves no doubt, just as exposure of nakedness was immediate when the tree was tasted.\n\nThis exchange dismantles the comforting illusion that wrong can be outsourced to charismatic figures, whether the greatest wicked in a town who conspired, or human and jinn whisperers who beautified killing children and dividing livestock. Alliance of wrongdoers made allies of one another becomes mutual accusation inside the Fire, completing the pattern where ties are broken and claims stray on the Day each arrives alone leaving behind what was given.\n\nA common self-deception is to wait for a leader to take responsibility before deciding. The Quran shows each soul will be told to taste for its own earning; following is itself an earning. The contrast is the Home of Peace where bitterness is removed from hearts and rivers flow beneath, awarded to those who chose to follow what was sent down from their Lord and not take other guardians, walking with a light among people rather than with a leader into cursing. The lesson is to examine allegiances now, before the question “Where are your gods?” becomes unanswerable.",
  "40": "“Surely those who receive our revelations with denial and arrogance, the gates of heaven will not be opened for them, nor will they enter Paradise until a camel passes through the eye of a needle. This is how We reward the wicked” intensifies the torment after the mutual cursing of leaders and followers, sealing the barrier between those who were heavy with belief and those who were light through denying signs. The opening repeats the formula “those who receive Our revelations with denial and arrogance,” or “allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina wa-istakbaroo anha,” the same pair that leads to abiding in Fire. The first consequence is given: “the gates of heaven will not be opened for them,” or “la tufattahu lahum abwabu al-sama’.” This has been understood in two united ways: their deeds and prayers do not ascend as good deeds via the gates through which provision is sent down, and at death their souls do not ascend through the gates as souls of believers do, but are cast down, as reported in the hadith of the soul’s ascent where angels do not open for a disbelieving soul. In both senses, ascent is blocked.\n\nThe second consequence uses the most graphic impossibility in everyday imagery: “nor will they enter Paradise until a camel passes through the eye of a needle,” or “hatta yalija al-jamalu fi sammi al-khiyat.” The camel is among the largest animals the Arabs knew, the needle’s eye among the smallest openings. The image says entrance is impossible by the ordinary norms of measure and justice that the Quran earlier commanded to keep and of which Allah perfects truth and justice. Some recitations read “jummal” as thick rope, sharpening the image of the impossible thread, but the message is the same: denial and arrogance make the scale light beyond remedy after the opportunity to shun evil and mend has passed.\n\nThe verse closes, “This is how We reward the wicked,” or “wa kathalika najzi al-mujrimeen,” those who cut themselves off, the same who were called wicked when the way was made distinct and whose reward is dreadful punishment for turning away.\n\nA common mistaken hope is that afterlife entry may be achieved through lineage, intercession without permission, or delayed profession when signs arrive. The verse shows the gate is conditioned on reception of revelations with submission and humility. For the living, it urges receiving revelations with attentive response, establishing prayer, dressing properly for worship, eating without waste, and guarding orphan wealth, thereby keeping deeds that ascend through open gates toward the Home of Peace rather than being rejected at the gate.",
  "41": "“Hell will be their bed; flames will be their cover. This is how We reward the wrongdoers” completes the description of the wicked’s abode after the impossible camel-and-needle barrier, giving a tactile image of entrapment after the gates of heaven are shut. The previous verse said those who received revelations with denial and arrogance will not have gates opened nor enter Paradise until an impossibility occurs. Now the Quran describes where they do reside. The phrase “Hell will be their bed,” or “lahum min Jahannama mihad,” uses “mihad,” a prepared bed, a mattress laid out beneath. But this is not restful night made for stillness; it is a bed that is punishment, where the surface itself is torment. The next phrase, “flames will be their cover,” or “wa min fawqihim ghawash,” means coverings, canopies, overlays from above, flames that blanket and press. Beneath is Hell, above is fire, enveloping from both sides as earlier torment could come from above or below and as societies were seized at night or midday while sleeping.\n\nThe image of bed and cover echoes the earlier gifts of establishment on earth with livelihood and clothing to cover nakedness and as adornment, where the best clothing is righteousness. Those who refused gratitude for establishment and rejected the best clothing, taking devils as masters thinking they are guided and committing shameful deeds while attributing them to Allah, find the opposite furnishing: a bed and cover that expose rather than adorn, that scorch rather than shelter.\n\nThe verse seals with the same formula: “This is how We reward the wrongdoers,” or “wa kathalika najzi al-zalimeen,” the unjust who placed things where they do not belong, who associated partners, forbade tayyibat, and attributed lies. It parallels the reward for the wicked in the previous verse, showing that “mujrim” and “zalim” converge on the same denial with arrogance that lightened the scale.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to imagine punishment as distant abstraction. The bed-and-cover image brings it close to daily experience of sleeping and dressing, reminding that the respite of night for rest and the mercy of clothing were signs that could have led to thankfulness. Choosing instead to follow elegant deception makes the once-merciful night and covering become agents of justice. The contrast with the believers’ reward, rivers beneath them with bitterness removed from hearts and the announcement that this Paradise is awarded for what they used to do, makes the urgency tangible: follow what was sent down from your Lord now, before the appointed term that can neither be delayed nor advanced arrives.",
  "42": "“As for those who believe and do good—We never require of any soul more than what it can afford—it is they who will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever” presents the counterpart to the bed of Hell and cover of flames, following the same pairing logic that contrasted heavy and light scales. The verse opens with the beloved Quranic pair “those who believe and do good,” or “allatheena amanoo wa ‘amiloo al-salihat,” belief, or “iman,” as trust in Allah alone without tarnishing faith with shirk, and good deeds, or “salihat,” as repairing and mending, like shunning evil, establishing prayer, guarding orphan wealth, giving full measure, speaking justly, and following the blessed Book. This echoes earlier promises: whoever believes and does good, there will be no fear for them nor will they grieve, and whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded tenfold.\n\nThe parenthesis, “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford,” or “la nukallifu nafsan illa wus‘aha,” is a merciful insertion that reassures. The same principle appeared after commands to fulfil measure and speak justly. No soul is burdened beyond capacity; the heavy scale does not demand angelic perfection but sincere, consistent effort within ability to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians. This prevents despair that the straight path is unattainable.\n\nThe recompense is then announced: “they will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever,” or “ula’ika as-haabu al-Jannah hum fiha khalidoon.” “As-haab,” companions, suggests intimacy and ownership, as earlier as-haab al-Nar for the wicked. The dwelling is the Home of Peace promised to those mindful, where bitterness is removed, rivers flow beneath, and satisfaction is exclusive beyond this world’s mixed enjoyment.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think Paradise requires perfection without error. The verse, by pairing belief and good with the capacity principle, shows that sincere, mindful effort, even with repentance after ignorance that mends, suffices with Allah’s mercy, which is infinite and outweighs punishment that is swift only for the persistent wicked. Another mistake imagines good deeds are worthless if small; the tenfold multiplication and the merciful standard ensure that steady small goods, like covering with proper adornment for worship, eating wholesome provision without waste, and wearing righteousness, accumulate toward eternal residence before the term arrives.",
  "43": "“We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts. Rivers will flow under their feet. And they will say, ‘Praise be to Allah for guiding us to this. We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us. The messengers of our Lord had certainly come with the truth’” describes the interior and exterior bliss of those residents of Paradise whose faith and good deeds, within capacity, made the scale heavy. The previous verse promised those believers eternal companionship with Paradise; now the experience of entry is unfolded.\n\nThe first mercy is inward: “We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts,” or “wa naza‘na ma fi sudoorihim min ghill.” “Ghill” means hidden ill will, rancour, envy, lingering grudge, the residue that even sincere believers might have carried from disputes in worldly life or from being divided by sects. The One who knows what breasts conceal and who encompasses every leaf and grain extracts it with subtlety, or “lutf,” leaving hearts spacious as they were opened to Islam, not tight as if climbing into the sky. This purified heart makes peace complete, as Paradise is the Home of Peace.\n\nThe outward panorama follows: “Rivers will flow under their feet,” or “tajri min tahtihim al-anhar,” beneath elevated dwellings, an image of effortless abundance contrasting with the desert’s search for water and with the affliction of thirst. The provision is continuous, without need to earn, unlike earthly livelihood seldom thanked.\n\nTheir own speech completes the scene: “Praise be to Allah for guiding us to this. We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us,” or “alhamdulillahi allathee hadana li-hatha wa ma kunna li-nahtadiya lawla an hadana Allah.” “Alhamdulillah” as first words in Paradise echo the opening of the previous surah’s praise for the Lord of all worlds and the closing praise after wrongdoers were uprooted. The acknowledgment that guidance itself was a gift matches the earlier principle that He has guided some while others are left to stray after taking devils as masters thinking they were guided. Here the guided confess the source.\n\nThe final witness seals proof: “The messengers of our Lord had certainly come with the truth,” or “laqad ja’at rusulu Rabbina bil-haqq,” confirming that the blessed, detailed Book and all earlier warnings were true. Then it will be announced, “This is Paradise awarded to you for what you used to do,” acknowledging both divine guidance and human deeds weighed tenfold, neither canceling the other, merging mercy and justice in the perfected Word.",
  "44": "“The residents of Paradise will call out to the residents of the Fire, ‘We have certainly found our Lord’s promise to be true. Have you too found your Lord’s promise to be true?’ They will reply, ‘Yes, we have!’ Then a caller will announce to both, ‘May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers’” dramatizes the moment of final verification that follows the removal of bitterness and the rivers flowing beneath. The previous verse showed the guided praising Allah for guidance after entry. Now communication stretches across the barrier between the two homes. The phrase “The residents of Paradise will call out to the residents of the Fire,” or “wa nada as-haabu al-Jannati as-haaba al-Nar,” shows proximity yet absolute separation, as seen earlier when groups entered Hell cursing predecessors and followers. The question is framed as shared discovery: “We have certainly found our Lord’s promise to be true. Have you too found your Lord’s promise to be true?” This mirrors the earlier dynamic of promise given to both sides: what you are promised will certainly come and you will have no escape, the gathering about which there is no doubt, the better Home for the mindful, the dreadful punishment for the wicked. On that Day, each finds the promise realized exactly as described by the Most Subtle, All-Aware, never absent.\n\nThe answer is unanimous and unwillingly sincere: “They will reply, ‘Yes, we have!’” or “qaloo na‘am.” Earlier they confessed “they have failed us” when asked where false gods are, and all confessed they were disbelievers; now they admit the promise’s truth with no decorated speech left to incline the heart.\n\nThen a formal announcement seals the divide: “Then a caller will announce to both, ‘May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers,’” or “fa-aththana mu’aththinun bainahum an la‘natu Allahi ala al-zalimeen.” “La‘nah” means expulsion from mercy, a curse that corresponds to “We have made the devils allies of those who disbelieve” and to the gates of heaven not being opened. The announcer identifies the wrongdoers further in the next verse as those who hindered from Allah’s Way, made it appear crooked, and disbelieved in the Hereafter.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think promises are metaphorical. This dialogue shows they are factual and will be acknowledged by both sides. For the living, it urges treating the Lord’s promise as already verified in advance, as the people of knowledge do when revelations are made clear, choosing the adornment of righteousness and the Book sent down to follow while the invitation still replaces condemnation.",
  "45": "“those who hindered ˹others˺ from Allah’s Way, strived to make it ˹appear˺ crooked, and disbelieved in the Hereafter” identifies the wrongdoers upon whom the caller’s condemnation just announced will rest, continuing the conversation between residents of Paradise and residents of the Fire. The previous verse had the announcer say “May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers.” This verse explains who those wrongdoers are with three traits that have been displayed throughout both surahs. First, “those who hindered others from Allah’s Way,” or “allatheena yasuddoona an sabili Allah,” as earlier those who turned others away from the Prophet and distance themselves, who said do not listen to this Quran, and who vigorously whispered with elegant deception to incline hearts. Hindering the Way is not limited to physical blocking; it includes mocking the Book so listeners doubt, making faith appear as play, and beautifying killing children as piety.\n\nSecond, “strived to make it appear crooked,” or “wa yabghoonaha iwaja,” sought to make the straight path seem bent, twisted, flawed. The straight path was described as “your Lord’s Path perfectly straight” and “My Path perfectly straight so follow it and do not follow other ways for they will divide you.” The hinderers work to make that straight path look crooked by presenting adornments brought forth for servants as if forbidden, by presenting shameless deeds as if commanded, and by presenting devils as if they were sincere advisors who swore they are. They invert the values, calling truth ancient fables and decorated falsehood wisdom.\n\nThird, “and disbelieved in the Hereafter,” or “wa hum bil-akhirati kafiroon,” the root of inclination toward elegant deception because, as earlier, hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter are receptive to it, pleased with it, and persistent in it. Disbelief in the Hereafter makes hindered, crooked-making attractive since the meeting that gives life weight is denied.\n\nThe sequence reveals a progression: disbelief in the meeting dulls the scale, beautified hindering hardens the heart, and making the straight seem crooked leads followers away until the appointed term arrives unmoved. A common mistake is to think hindering is only done by powerful tyrants. The Quran shows it is also done by anyone who spreads decorated lies that make the path look crooked, confirming that alliance of wrongdoers spreads. The next verse shows a barrier between the destinations, with people on the heights recognizing both sides, hoping to join Paradise while praying not to be joined with the wrongdoers, illustrating that awareness of these three traits is itself guidance toward not being among them.",
  "46": "“There will be a barrier between Paradise and Hell. And on the heights ˹of that barrier˺ will be people who will recognize ˹the residents of˺ both by their appearance. They will call out to the residents of Paradise, ‘Peace be upon you!’ They will have not yet entered Paradise, but eagerly hope to” depicts the striking middle place that follows the mutual call between the two homes and the caller’s condemnation upon wrongdoers who hindered and made the Way crooked while disbelieving in the Hereafter. The phrase “There will be a barrier between Paradise and Hell,” or “wa bainahuma hijab,” describes a partition, a veil that separates the two ultimate destinations so their conditions do not mix, just as in this world the Book separates the way of the wicked made distinct from the straight path. Upon the heights of that barrier, or “wa ala al-a‘raf rijal,” are people on the elevations, the “a‘raf,” plural of heights, from which they can see both sides. The Quran says they will recognize residents of both by their appearance, or “siymah,” marks. Believers’ faces shine with light given to walk among people and the ablution of prayer, while deniers are marked by dust and constriction.\n\nTheir greeting is telling: “They will call out to the residents of Paradise, ‘Peace be upon you!’” or “nadoo as-haaba al-Jannati an salamun alaikum.” “Salam” is the same greeting the believers in Our revelations are told to receive, “Peace be upon you! Your Lord has taken upon Himself to be Merciful.” Those on the heights therefore speak as well-wishers of the blessed, already inclined toward mercy, yet “They will have not yet entered Paradise, but eagerly hope to,” or “lam yadkhulooha wa hum yatma‘oon.” Hope, or “tama‘,” means longing expectation with good anticipation, reflecting that their scales may be balanced, with good and bad equal, or with uncertain weight, awaiting divine mercy that the next verses show will indeed admit them after acknowledging the humble believers the arrogant had scorned.\n\nClassical scholars differ on their precise identity, some saying they are people whose good and evil equalize, others that they are eminent just witnesses, but all agree they illustrate the theme of rank according to deeds and of the Word perfected in truth and justice where not even a leaf falls unknown.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to imagine the barrier means indifference. The scene shows active recognition, greeting, and hope, teaching that after death identities remain and recognition persists, as earlier the dead given a light walk among people recognizable by their illumination. For the living, the barrier encourages striving to be among those greeted with peace due to following what was sent down, not being deceived as parents were when cover was stripped, so the hope of the heights becomes entry into the Home of Peace where bitterness is removed.",
  "47": "“When their eyes will turn towards the residents of Hell, they will pray, ‘Our Lord! Do not join us with the wrongdoing people’” continues the scene on the heights that began with the barrier between Paradise and Hell. The previous verse said those on the heights recognize both groups by appearance and call to the residents of Paradise with “Peace be upon you” while eagerly hoping to enter. Now the gaze shifts. The Arabic says “wa itha surifat absaruhum tilqaa as-haab al-nar,” when their eyes are turned toward the companions of the Fire. The verb “surifat” suggests the eyes are turned, perhaps unwillingly or by divine direction, not that they seek to look at punishment. The sight is sobering: they see the same wrongdoers just described as hinderers who made the straight seem crooked, whose scales were light, whose bed is Hell with flames as cover. Immediately the people of the heights, who themselves have not yet entered Paradise and are still in a place of hope and suspense, make a supplication that reveals their awareness: “Our Lord! Do not join us with the wrongdoing people,” or “Rabbana la taj‘alna ma‘a al-qawmi al-zalimeen.” They ask not to be placed with the unjust, the associators, the deniers described earlier.\n\nThis prayer mirrors the earlier prayer of the righteous after being saved from the Fire, and it teaches that even those near Paradise remain humble, fearful of being counted among the wrongdoers. The phrase “wrongdoing people,” or “al-qawmi al-zalimeen,” connects to every earlier use: those who fabricated lies, divided faith, denied signs with arrogance, and took adornment as forbidden while permitting shame. The Heights people recognize that the only thing separating them from that company is mercy, not a claim of perfection.\n\nThe verse shows that sight on that Day is not neutral. Looking at Paradise brings greeting of peace; looking at Hell produces urgent seeking of refuge. The turning of the eyes becomes a lesson for the living: keep your gaze toward what you hope to join, and when you see the fate of arrogance and heedlessness, renew the same prayer now, before eyes are turned by compulsion. Classical commentators note that this supplication was preserved for believers to use in this world whenever they witness corruption, reminding them to ask Allah to keep them distinct from the unjust while they still can choose to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians.",
  "48": "“Those on the heights will call out to some ˹tyrants in the Fire˺, who they will recognize by their appearance, saying, ‘Your large numbers and arrogance are of no use ˹today˺!’” deepens the conversation across the barrier. The previous verse showed those on the heights praying when their eyes turned to Hell, asking not to be joined with wrongdoers. Now they actively address a specific group inside the Fire, recognized by marks that have become unmistakable. The Arabic says “wa nada as-haabu al-a‘rafi rijalan ya‘rifoonahum bi-seemahum,” they will call men they know by their marks, their faces, their bearing. In this context, the recognized figures are tyrannical leaders whose large followings and high status in worldly life were their boast, the same chiefs who earlier said of poor believers “Has Allah favoured these” and who forbade what Allah provided.\n\nTheir rebuke is concise: “Your large numbers and arrogance are of no use today!” or “ma aghna ankum jam‘ukum wa ma kuntum tastakbiroon.” “Jam‘ukum,” your gathering, your amassing, includes wealth, tribal numbers, armies, and crowds that once seemed to guarantee security. “Ma kuntum tastakbiroon,” what you used to be arrogant about, includes pride over lineage, refusal to bow like Iblis, rejection of the messenger as a liar or fool, and dismissing poor believers as unworthy. Today, on the weighing when gates of heaven do not open for the arrogant, none of that accumulation helps, just as earlier it was said those fabricated gods will fail them and their large numbers cursed each other inside the Fire.\n\nThe verse mirrors earlier worldly boasts. In Mecca, chiefs mocked the small circle of believers and trusted their multitude; here the small circle on the heights questions the multitude in the Fire. It also echoes the question to the Heights people about the humble believers they swore would never be shown mercy, about to be answered with entry to Paradise.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think that status and numbers guarantee success before Allah. This verse shows the opposite: what counts is not jam‘ but taqwa, the righteous clothing that is best, the heavy scale built from belief and good within capacity. For the living, the Heights’ call teaches to value humility over haughtiness, to follow the detailed Book rather than the consensus of the powerful, and to remember that appearance which seems impressive may on that Day be the very mark of loss, while the humble faces mocked may be the ones told “Enter Paradise, no fear for you.”",
  "49": "“Are these ˹humble believers˺ the ones you swore would never be shown Allah’s mercy?’ ˹Finally, those on the heights will be told:˺ ‘Enter Paradise! There will be no fear for you, nor will you grieve’” brings the Heights episode to its merciful climax after the prayer not to be joined with wrongdoers and the rebuke to tyrants whose numbers and arrogance proved useless. The Heights people now turn to address the inhabitants of the Fire about the very believers those tyrants had scorned in worldly life. The question, “Are these humble believers the ones you swore would never be shown Allah’s mercy?” or “a-ha’ula’i allatheena aqsumtum la yanaluhum Allahu bi-rahmah,” quotes the oaths of the disbelieving elite who, as earlier said, asked “Has Allah favoured these poor believers out of all of us?” and who swore the weak would never attain divine favor. Their oaths were solemn, public, and confident, asserting that mercy is distributed by worldly standards.\n\nThe verb “aqsumtum” shows they swore by Allah Himself that He would withhold mercy from those they deemed lowly, the same poor who invoked their Lord morning and evening seeking His Face. The Quran now exposes the vanity of such oaths. Mercy is not granted by tribal council; it is the Lord who took mercy upon Himself and whose mercy is always close to the good-doers. The worldly verdict is reversed: those sworn to be excluded are the ones now about to be welcomed.\n\nThe final announcement resolves the suspense of the Heights: “Enter Paradise! There will be no fear for you, nor will you grieve,” or “udkhuloo al-jannata la khawfun alaikum wa la antum tahzanoon.” This is the same promise given to those who shun evil and mend ways, who believe and do good within capacity, and whose scales are heavy. Addressed here to both the humble believers and, according to most commentators, to the people of the Heights themselves after their recognition and supplication, it declares that hope mixed with fear has turned into secure entry.\n\nThe verse teaches that swearing others out of mercy is a perilous act of presumption. The Heights’ question humbles the arrogant by making their own former oath the evidence against them, while merciful entry humiliates no humble believer but honors them. For listeners, the lesson is to refrain from declaring who is beyond mercy, to keep to the straight path of humility, to wear adornment and enjoy tayyibat without extravagance, and to trust that the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who never was absent will admit whom He wills to the Home of Peace where bitterness is removed and rivers flow beneath.",
  "50": "“The residents of the Fire will then cry out to the residents of Paradise, ‘Aid us with some water or any provision Allah has granted you.’ They will reply, ‘Allah has forbidden both to the disbelievers’” presents the desperate reversal of provision after the Heights have been told to enter Paradise without fear or grief. The previous verses showed the tyrants’ numbers and arrogance proved useless and the humble believers admitted to mercy. Now those in the Fire, whose bed is Hell and cover is flames, make a plea across the barrier that once separated them by choice. The cry, “Aid us with some water or any provision Allah has granted you,” or “afeedoo alaina min al-ma’i aw mimma razaqakum Allah,” is humble and specific. After a life where they forbade adornments Allah brought forth, divided crops for idols, and took faith as play, they now beg for the simplest tayyibat they had restricted in others and for any rizq that in Paradise flows as rivers beneath. The word “afeedoo” means pour, give abundantly, as if any amount would relieve scorching thirst.\n\nThe reply is firm and explanatory: “Allah has forbidden both to the disbelievers,” or “inna Allaha harramahuma ala al-kafireen.” The same verb “harrama” used for livestock taboos and for the true prohibitions of indecencies and shirk is now used for the final prohibition: water and provision of Paradise are forbidden to those who rejected the giver in the world. The prohibition is not arbitrary; it follows the life they chose when they were told “Eat and drink but do not waste” and instead they wasted gratitude, spread corruption after the land had been set in order, and called upon other guardians. The next verse defines those disbelievers as those who took faith as amusement and were deluded by worldly life, so today We will forget them as they forgot the meeting of this Day.\n\nThe exchange mirrors the earlier plea of the wretched detained before the Fire begging water, and the worldly situation where the grateful were denied by the powerful. Roles are reversed with perfect justice, weighed on scales that do not wrong. A common misunderstanding is to think that pleas at that stage can still change the barrier. The barrier, or “hijab,” is fixed after the appointed term that cannot be delayed or advanced, when belief after signs no longer benefits. For the living, the verse urges to give water and provision now while giving still builds the heavy scale, to remember gratitude for the One who sends winds ushering mercy and rain to a lifeless land, and to not treat faith as play, lest a day come when water itself is forbidden.",
  "51": "“those who took this faith ˹of Islam˺ as mere amusement and play and were deluded by ˹their˺ worldly life.’ ˹Allah will say,˺ ‘Today We will ignore them just as they ignored the coming of this Day of theirs and for rejecting Our revelations’” explains the reason for the prohibition of water and provision just announced to the disbelievers. After the Fire’s residents beg for water and are told Allah has forbidden both to disbelievers, this verse defines those disbelievers not by a single error but by a pattern of treating the serious as trivial. The phrase “took this faith as mere amusement and play,” or “allatheena ittakhadhoo deenahum lahwan wa la‘iban,” echoes earlier descriptions of those who take faith as play and amusement and are deluded by worldly life, and earlier that worldly life itself is play and amusement for the heedless. Here the faith itself was turned into entertainment, a subject for mocking revelations, debating with elegant deception, and varying stories to call the Quran ancient fables, while the real Home of Peace was treated as a joke.\n\nThe second trait, “were deluded by their worldly life,” or “wa gharrathum al-hayatu al-dunya,” shows the world’s nearer life tricked them, beautifying deeds and making accumulation seem meaningful, as earlier each people’s deeds were made appealing. The delusion closed their eyes to the scales that will be heavy or light and to the barrier now fixed between the two homes.\n\nThe divine response is then stated: “Today We will ignore them just as they ignored the coming of this Day of theirs and for rejecting Our revelations,” or “fa-alyawma nansahum kama nasoo liqaa yawmihim hatha wa ma kanu bi-ayatina yajhadoon.” “Nansahum” means We will forget them, not out of divine forgetfulness, since We were never absent and never unaware, but as recompense: We will leave them in their punishment as they left remembrance, ignoring them as they ignored the meeting whose knowledge would have made them mindful. The Day they ignored has arrived; the revelations they rejected now testify.\n\nA common misconception is that religion is a light cultural amusement. The verse shows treating the decisive Way as amusement is itself the hinge of loss, leading to being forgotten on the Day when being remembered is the only hope. The remedy is to treat the Book explained with knowledge as a serious guide and mercy, to follow what was sent down, to dress for worship, eat with gratitude without waste, and to keep the appointment in sight while the term still allows.",
  "52": "“We have certainly brought them a Book which We explained with knowledge—a guide and mercy for those who believe” follows the declaration that those who took faith as play were ignored as they ignored the meeting. The verse steps back to name the ignored gift itself. The pronoun “them” refers to humanity already addressed as children of Adam and as those who received messengers and who await fulfilment. The statement, “We have certainly brought them a Book,” or “wa laqad ji’nahum bi-kitab,” emphasizes arrival and divine agency. This is the same blessed Book sent down to warn and remind, the Book sent down from your Lord to follow and not take other guardians, whose Word is perfected in truth and justice and cannot be changed. The next phrase, “which We explained with knowledge,” or “fassalnahu ala ilmin,” mirrors earlier refrains “We explained with knowledge—a guide and mercy” and “We explained with knowledge for those who believe.” The explanation is not random variation but variation built upon knowledge, precise, detailed, covering worship, morality, livelihood, and the unseen, as earlier “We have left nothing out of the Record.”\n\nIts function is then stated: “a guide and mercy for those who believe,” or “hudan wa rahmah li-qawmin yu’minoonoon.” Guide, or “huda,” is the light that lets the dead-hearted walk among people and the straight path made clear to the mindful; mercy, or “rahmah,” is the closeness promised as “Allah’s mercy is always close to the good-doers” and the mercy taken upon Himself that welcomes the repentant who mend ways. For those who believe, the same Book that is a warning to the arrogant becomes guidance that expands the chest and mercy that pours like rain on a lifeless land.\n\nThis verse prepares for the next question, “Do they only await the fulfilment of its warning?” If the Book has already been brought and explained with knowledge, why wait for its threat to become sight? The waiting is portrayed as heedlessness, the same delay Iblis exploited lying in wait on the Straight Path.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think the Book’s detail is too difficult or too distant. The verse says it is explained with knowledge precisely to be a guide, not a puzzle, and its mercy is for the believing, not for the one who insists on associating others and beautifying shame. For the sincere, the way to receive its mercy is the same as opening the heart to Islam: follow what was sent down, call upon Him humbly and secretly, avoid corruption after order, and remember the best clothing of righteousness as the lens through which the Book’s lessons become clear.",
  "53": "“Do they only await the fulfilment ˹of its warning˺? The Day it will be fulfilled, those who ignored it before will say, ‘The messengers of our Lord certainly came with the truth. Are there any intercessors who can plead on our behalf? Or can we be sent back so we may do ˹good,˺ unlike what we used to do?’ They will have certainly ruined themselves, and whatever ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them” shows the end of waiting once the Book explained with knowledge has been brought. The opening question, “Do they only await the fulfilment of its warning?” or “hal yanzuroona illa ta’weelahu,” asks what the deniers expect if not the very fulfilment, or “ta’weel,” the ultimate outcome the Book warned about, the gathering, the weighing, the Fire whose residents beg for water and are refused. The same expectation appeared earlier as “Do they only await the fulfilment?” and as “Are they awaiting the coming of angels or your Lord or some signs,” when belief after signs would no longer benefit.\n\nThe scene of that Day is then quoted. Those who ignored it before, the same who took faith as play and were deluded by worldly life, will say, “The messengers of our Lord certainly came with the truth,” or “la-qad ja’at rusulu Rabbina bil-haqq,” the exact confession made by those in Paradise who said we would never have been guided if Allah had not guided us and the messengers came with truth, but here said too late. Then the plea bursts: “Are there any intercessors who can plead on our behalf? Or can we be sent back so we may do good, unlike what we used to do?” The first request recalls the begging of those in the Fire for water and the earlier detained before the Fire begging to be returned, promising to be believers. The second request is the same as “If only we could be sent back we would never deny signs.”\n\nThe verdict closes: “They will have certainly ruined themselves, and whatever gods they fabricated will fail them,” or “qad khasiroo anfusahum wa dalla anhum ma kanu yaftaroon.” Self-ruin, or “khasiroo anfusahum,” describes light scales who doomed themselves, and failing gods echoes the moment polytheists will say “They have failed us” when the soul-takers ask where false gods are.\n\nA common self-deception is to postpone response until warning becomes sight. The verse shows sight after waiting is a confession without benefit. The time to answer the guide-and-mercy Book is while intercession is still sought correctly through sincere devotion in this world, before the fulfilment leaves only self-ruin and vanished fabrications.",
  "54": "“Indeed your Lord is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne. He makes the day and night overlap in rapid succession. He created the sun, the moon, and the stars—all subjected by His command. The creation and the command belong to Him ˹alone˺. Blessed is Allah—Lord of all worlds!” returns to the grandeur of Lordship after the warning about awaiting fulfilment. The previous verses showed the Book brought with knowledge as guide and mercy and the ruin of those who waited until fulfilment to confess. Now the foundation for that Book’s authority is restated: your Lord is Allah, not a guardian besides Him, not a devil taken as master, the same Lord called Self-Sufficient, Full of Mercy, Lord of everything.\n\nThe first proof is creation: “Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne,” or “khalaqa al-samawati wal-arda fi sittati ayyam thumma istawa ala al-arsh.” Six Days indicates deliberate, wise staging, not need for time, showing the All-Wise who measures every matter. “Established Himself on the Throne” affirms His majesty and highness above creation, in a manner befitting Him without likening to creatures and without emptying it of meaning, as passed down from the earliest scholars who said the meaning is known while the modality is not asked about.\n\nThe next proof is governance: “He makes the day and night overlap in rapid succession,” or “yughshi al-laila al-nahara yatlubuhu hatheetha,” He covers night with day, each chasing the other swiftly, the same overlap praised when He causes dawn to break and makes night for rest. “He created the sun, the moon, and the stars—all subjected by His command,” or “wa al-shamsa wa al-qamara wa al-nujoomu musakhkharatin bi-amrihi,” the celestial guides earlier said to travel with precision and to guide through darkness, all subservient, not lords.\n\nThe principle is then distilled: “The creation and the command belong to Him alone,” or “ala lahu al-khalqu wal-amr.” Creation, or “khalq,” is bringing into being; command, or “amr,” is directing, legislating, governing. Both belong to the same Owner, joining Lordship and Law, so the prohibition list that forbad indecencies, shirk, and lying about Allah is not an extra burden but the command of the Creator. The exclamation “Blessed is Allah—Lord of all worlds!” or “tabaraka Allahu Rabbu al-alameen,” praises abundance of blessing that flows from that unified ownership, the same blessedness of the Book which taught to follow and be mindful so you may receive mercy. A common error is to split creation from command, accepting the Creator while rejecting His law. The verse unites them, calling to humble, secret supplication that the next verses enjoin.",
  "55": "“Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly. Surely He does not like the transgressors” follows the majestic affirmation that creation and command belong to Allah alone, blessed Lord of all worlds, who makes night and day overlap and subjects sun, moon, and stars. The previous verse established who your Lord is; this verse teaches how to turn to Him. The command “Call upon your Lord,” or “ud‘oo Rabbakum,” includes both supplication for needs and worship that calls with action, like dressing for worship and eating with thanks. The manner is defined twice: “humbly and secretly,” or “tadarru‘an wa khufyatan.” “Tadarru‘” means lowliness, showing need, softness, humility of heart and voice, as opposed to the arrogance for which the Heaven’s gates do not open. “Khufyah” means secretly, quietly, private, not for show. The pair matches the earlier description of those who invoke morning and evening seeking His Face, and contrasts with the noisy boasting of those whose numbers and arrogance proved useless.\n\nThe reason given is moral: “Surely He does not like the transgressors,” or “innahu la yuhibbu al-mu‘tadeen.” Transgression, or “i‘tida,” means crossing bounds, like spreading corruption after order, wasting provision, associating partners, or committing indecencies openly or secretly. Calling upon Him arrogantly, loudly to be seen, or while persisting in transgression, contradicts the humility the call requires. Earlier prohibitions listed indecencies, sin, aggression without right, shirk, and speaking about Allah without knowledge as true transgressions that block the straight path.\n\nThis verse also corrects a misunderstanding about secret supplication. Some imagine that hidden devotion is less rewarded than public display. The Quran praises secret humility precisely because it is sincere, the same sincerity, or “ikhlas,” commanded as “calling upon Him with sincere devotion” after uprightness. Humble secret prayer is the antidote to the four-sided ambush that aims to make most ungrateful, because gratitude grows in private remembrance.\n\nFor daily life, it encourages regular, quiet, needful calling, like the winds that softly usher mercy before heavy rain, rather than loud, theatrical claims. The next verse continues, “Do not spread corruption after order and call upon Him with hope and fear,” showing that the humble secret caller is also the one who keeps the earth set in order and whose hope-filled fear keeps mercy close to the good-doers.",
  "56": "“Do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order. And call upon Him with hope and fear. Indeed, Allah’s mercy is always close to the good-doers” balances the previous call for humble secret supplication with social and emotional dimensions. The first command, “Do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order,” or “wa la tufsidoo fil-ardi ba‘da islahiha,” addresses the earth that was earlier said to have been created in six Days and established on the Throne, where day and night overlap by gentle command. “Islah” means setting in order, rectifying, making sound, as the Creator did by providing livelihood, sending winds with mercy, and guiding through the detailed Book. Corruption, or “fasad,” undoes that order through indecencies open and secret, shirk, unjust aggression, forbidding tayyibat, and beautifying child killing, the exact deeds the fabricators spread. The prohibition shows that worship and social responsibility are inseparable.\n\nThe second command completes inward orientation: “And call upon Him with hope and fear,” or “wa id‘oohu khawfan wa tama‘a.” After teaching to call humbly and secretly, now the heart’s two wings are named. Fear, or “khawf,” remembers the Fire whose residents beg for water and are refused, the Fire whose bed and cover are prepared for the arrogant, and the term that cannot be delayed. Hope, or “tama‘,” remembers the Home of Peace, the removal of bitterness, and the promise that scales heavy will be successful. Together they keep supplication alive, as earlier the winds usher mercy with both promise of rain and need for rain. Calling with only fear despairs; with only hope grows careless; with both, the heart stays mindful.\n\nThe promise that follows is tender: “Indeed, Allah’s mercy is always close to the good-doers,” or “inna rahmata Allahi qareebun min al-muhsineen.” Good-doers, or “muhsineen,” are those who excel, who worship as if seeing Him, who give full measure, guard orphan wealth, and shun sin, the same people near whom mercy is close even while the Fire’s prohibited water is far from transgressors. The statement links back to “He does not like transgressors” just before; not liking transgression does not mean distant; mercy’s closeness to the good is the counterpart.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat hope and fear as contradictions. The verse shows they are companions that keep corruption from spreading and keep private humility from becoming hidden despair, ensuring the land remains ordered and provision travels from winds to fruit as the next verses describe.",
  "57": "“He is the One Who sends the winds ushering in His mercy. When they bear heavy clouds, We drive them to a lifeless land and then cause rain to fall, producing every type of fruit. Similarly, We will bring the dead to life, so perhaps you will be mindful” returns to creation’s mercy as proof of resurrection, linking the Book’s warning and the command to call with hope and fear to a visible natural mercy. The opening, “He is the One Who sends the winds ushering in His mercy,” or “wa huwa allathee yursilu al-riyaha bushran baina yadai rahmatihi,” describes winds, or “riyah,” sent as bearers of glad tidings before rain, the mercy, or “rahmah,” that is rain itself. The same winds earlier praised as designs of the Almighty, All-Knowing now become heralds, much as messengers are sent to warn before the Day.\n\nThe sequence is then detailed: “When they bear heavy clouds, We drive them to a lifeless land and then cause rain to fall, producing every type of fruit,” or “hatta itha aqallat sahaban thiqalan suqnahu li-baladin mayyitin fa-anzalna bihi al-ma’a fa-akhrajna bihi min kulli al-thamarat.” The clouds are heavy with water, driven by command, not chance, to a dead land where soil seemed hopeless, as earlier fertile and infertile lands were contrasted and as earlier seeds sprouted by the Splitter. The result is every type of fruit, the same diversity seen in gardens trellised and untrellised, olives and pomegranates similar yet dissimilar, a sign for the believing and a provision seldom thanked.\n\nThe spiritual parallel is then drawn: “Similarly, We will bring the dead to life, so perhaps you will be mindful,” or “kathalika nukhriju al-mawta la‘allakum tathakkaroon.” The bringing forth of fruit from dead land is the exact likeness of bringing forth humans from the earth where they live, die, and will be resurrected. As the earlier rain produced green stalks with clustered grain calling to look at fruit’s ripening, so this rain-like resurrection calls to be mindful, or “tathakkaroon,” remembering that the Lord whose creation and command are His will also fulfil the promise awaited by the doubters.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to separate nature’s mercy from the final mercy. The verse joins them: the winds’ mercy proves the greater mercy of raising the dead and weighing justly is possible. For the mindful, every wind before rain and every fruit after becomes a reminder to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, keeping the land from corruption and the heart open to the mercy always close to the good-doers.",
  "58": "“The fertile land produces abundantly by the Will of its Lord, whereas the infertile land hardly produces anything. This is how We vary ˹Our˺ lessons to those who are thankful” uses the agriculture just described to illustrate receptive and unreceptive hearts, directly after promising that the dead will be raised like fruit from dead land. The previous verse showed winds bearing heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit as a likeness of resurrection. Now two kinds of land are contrasted. The fertile land, or “al-baladu al-tayyib,” the good, pure soil, “produces abundantly by the Will of its Lord,” or “yakhruju nabatuhu bi-ithni Rabbihi,” its plants emerge easily, fully, beautifully, with Allah’s leave, matching the believer’s heart that receives the Book explained with knowledge and responds as the attentive do, whose chest is opened to Islam. The infertile land, or “al-khabeeth,” the bad, salty, hard soil, “hardly produces anything,” or “la yakhruju illa nakidan,” only with difficulty, stunted, bitter, and scanty, matching the heart that disbelieves in the Hereafter, takes devils as masters thinking itself guided, and remains tight as if climbing into the sky.\n\nThe same water descends on both, just as the same blessed Book is sent to all, but outcome differs by capacity prepared internally and by willingness to be thankful. The verse then states its method: “This is how We vary Our lessons to those who are thankful,” or “kathalika nusarrifu al-ayat li-qawmin yashkuroon.” “Nusarrifu” means We turn, vary, repeat lessons from different angles, as earlier We varied lessons for those who know, who comprehend, who remember, and who believe. Here the audience is “those who are thankful,” or “yashkuroon,” because thankfulness is the fertile soil. Gratitude recognizes the Sender behind the wind and rain, attributes provision to the One who provides for all, and guards measure, covenant, and dress, producing abundant good that tenfold multiplies on the scale.\n\nA common mistake is to think the difference in harvest proves water is lacking. The verse shows water, like revelation, is ample; the soil’s readiness matters. Another error is to think thankfulness is only words. The fertile metaphor shows thankfulness is fruition, putting lessons into practice as abundant produce, not withholding. For daily cultivation, it encourages softening the heart with remembrance, seeking humble secret supplication with hope and fear, and not spreading corruption after order, keeping the inner land fertile so that when the mercy that is close to the good-doers arrives, it produces the righteousness that is the best clothing and the weight that makes the scale heavy.",
  "59": "“Indeed, We sent Noah to his people. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous Day’” begins the series of prophetic stories that illustrate fertile and infertile response after the parable of good and bad land. The previous verses used winds and soils to explain how the same rain produces different harvests in thankful versus unthankful ground. Now the same truth is shown in human history, starting with the first major messenger after Adam’s descent. The opening, “Indeed, We sent Noah to his people,” or “laqad arsalna Noohan ila qawmihi,” places Noah, or “Nuh,” as a sent messenger like the later Hud, Salih, Lot, and Shu‘ayb, and earlier Abraham and Moses, confirming that your Lord would never destroy a community while its people are unaware. Noah’s call is then quoted: “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghairahu.” This is the core of uprightness, or “qist,” and sincere devotion, or “ikhlas,” defendingpure monotheism against taking other guardians, the same message that made the straight path clear.\n\nThe motive is fear, not hatred: “I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous Day,” or “inni akhafu alaikum athaba yawmin azeem.” The “tremendous Day,” or “yawmin azeem,” echoes the Day whose torment the Prophet earlier feared if he disobeyed and whose fulfilment is awaited by doubters, the tremendous Day when fulfilment arrives, intercessors are sought, and scales weigh justly. Noah fears that Day for his own people, much as the Heights people prayed not to be joined with wrongdoers.\n\nThe phrasing connects directly to the present audience. The polytheists of Mecca were also told to worship Allah alone, not to divide shares for associates, and to fear the same tremendous Day. Their chiefs’ reply in the next verse mirrors Mecca’s chiefs who called Muhammad a fool and liar.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view prophetic warning as harshness. The verse shows warning is mercy, like winds ushering rain, aiming to make the infertile land fertile before the time appointed for each community, which can neither be delayed nor advanced, arrives. Remembering that creation and command belong to Him alone, as dawn and night overlap by His design, makes the call to worship Him alone the only reasonable response for those who wish to be among the thankful whose lessons bear abundant fruit.",
  "60": "“But the chiefs of his people said, ‘We surely see that you are clearly misguided’” records the elite response to Noah’s compassionate warning to worship Allah alone and fear the tremendous Day. The previous verse quoted Noah’s sincere plea as a messenger from the Lord of all worlds. This verse shows who answered and how. The speakers are “the chiefs of his people,” or “al-mala’u min qawmihi,” the council of leaders, heads of clan and wealth, the same societal rank earlier described as the most wicked to conspire in a town and as the disbelieving chiefs who would later call Hud a fool and Salih and Lot’s opponents. Their verdict is delivered with emphasized certainty: “We surely see that you are clearly misguided,” or “inna la-naraka fi dalalin mubeen.” “Naraka” we see you, and “dalalin mubeen” clearly misguided, obvious error, as Abraham once saw his father and people in clear misguidance for taking idols.\n\nThe accusation reverses reality. Noah was on guidance that opens the chest to Islam, calling to the straight path made clear to the mindful; they were in darkness where misdeeds are made appealing, asking to await fulfilment while deluded by worldly life. Their “seeing” is blind, like the blind people described at the flood’s end, “They were certainly a blind people,” and like those whose hearts and eyes We turn away as they refused to believe at first.\n\nThe verse links to the broader theme of fertile versus infertile land. The same reminder produced abundant fruit in hearts prepared by thankfulness and little in hard ground that denied signs with arrogance. The chiefs’ response demonstrates barren soil: instead of weighing the message, they weigh the messenger’s status, as earlier they swore humble believers would never receive mercy.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat rejection by elites as proof of error. The Quran shows the opposite pattern: chiefs often reject first because guidance threatens their numbers and arrogance that will later be said to be of no use, while the humble who invoke morning and evening respond. For the listener today, the lesson is to judge the message, not the majority or the chiefs’ perception, and to recognize that being called misguided by those who hinder the Way and make it seem crooked is ironically a sign of being on the straight path that the Lord whose Word is perfected has blessed.",
  "61": "“He replied, ‘O my people! I am not misguided! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds’” gives Noah’s calm reply after being called clearly misguided by the chiefs. The previous verse quoted their scorn, “We surely see that you are clearly misguided,” an accusation that carried social weight because it came from the elite. Noah’s response mirrors the pattern of prophetic forbearance that will be echoed by Hud when called a fool. He begins with gentle belonging, “O my people,” or “ya qawmi,” retaining affection despite rejection, as Hud and Salih will do, showing the messenger is not a stranger seeking advantage but a brother fearing the tremendous Day for his own kin. Then comes a clear negation: “I am not misguided,” or “laisa bi dalalah,” directly denying the label “dalal,” misguidance, and implicitly placing misguidance back where the Book has located it, among those who take devils as masters thinking they are guided.\n\nThe correction follows with positive identity: “But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,” or “wa lakinni rasoolun min Rabbi al-alameen.” The phrase “Lord of all worlds,” or “Rabb al-alameen,” recalls the opening praise of this Quran’s second chapter and the closing praise after wrongdoers were uprooted, and the blessed Lord of all worlds who created heavens and earth in six Days. By invoking that title, Noah anchors his mission not in personal claim but in the universal nurturing Lord whose creation and command belong to Him, who subjects sun, moon, and stars, and whose mercy is close to the good-doers. A messenger from that Lord brings what was explained with knowledge, guide and mercy, not misguidance.\n\nThis exchange contrasts with Iblis’s “I am better than he,” which asserted superiority without proof. Noah asserts not superiority but mission, humility with authority, like the Prophet who said “I only follow what is revealed to me” and “I am not a keeper over you.”\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think answering accusations requires harsh counter-attack. Noah models dignified affirmation: remain among your people, deny false labels without mirroring arrogance, and restate the source. For the Meccan listener facing similar chief-voices calling the Prophet misguided, fool, and liar, the parallel would be unmistakable: the misguided are not those who call to worship Allah alone and fear the tremendous Day, but those who call such callers misguided while their deeds remain beautified.",
  "62": "“conveying to you my Lord’s messages and giving you sincere advice. And I know from Allah what you do not know” explains Noah’s role in detail after he denied being misguided and affirmed he is a messenger from the Lord of all worlds. The previous defense established identity; this verse establishes function and source. The first function, “conveying to you my Lord’s messages,” or “uballighukum risalati Rabbi,” presents him as a deliverer, like winds heralding mercy, accurately transmitting what was sent down, as earlier messengers were told they are only deliverers of good news and warners, not keepers. “Risalat” are messages that explain the straight path, true prohibitions, and the tremendous Day, the same messages varied for the thankful to produce abundant harvest.\n\nThe second function, “and giving you sincere advice,” or “wa ansahu lakum,” uses “nasihah,” pure, selfless counseling that seeks only the listener’s good, opposite to Satan’s false oath “I am your sincere advisor” that sought to expose nakedness. Noah’s advice is sincere because it warns of the torment he fears for them while hoping for their mercy, much as the heights people prayed not to be joined with wrongdoers. True nasihah weaves together fear and hope, calling with hope and fear as earlier taught.\n\nThe third element is knowledge: “And I know from Allah what you do not know,” or “wa a‘lamu mina Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon.” This knowledge is what Allah teaches His messengers of the unseen, not the keys of the unseen reserved to Him alone, but what He discloses of the meeting, the scales, the barrier, the winds, and the appointed term that the unmindful ignore. This explains why his warning should be trusted over the chiefs’ “seeing” that he is misguided; his sight is illuminated with a light to walk among people while theirs is veiled as blind.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat a messenger’s advice as ordinary opinion to be weighed against chiefly opinion. The verse distinguishes: one is sincere conveyance from the Lord of all worlds plus knowledge from Allah, the others are assumption and elegant deception that make the scale light. For the Meccan audience, the invitation was to accept Noah’s pattern in Muhammad, who also conveys messages and knows what they do not, and to become fertile land producing abundantly rather than hard ground yielding little, before the fulfilment they await arrives and intercessors are sought in vain.",
  "63": "“Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own, warning you, so you may beware and perhaps be shown mercy?” addresses the root astonishment behind the chiefs’ accusation of misguidance. After Noah described himself as a messenger conveying messages, giving sincere advice, and knowing from Allah what they do not, this verse reveals why they called him misguided: they were amazed that a reminder, or “dhikr,” would come through a man like themselves. The question, “Do you find it astonishing,” or “awa ajibtum an ja’akum thikrun min Rabbikum ala rajulin minkum,” echoes the same question Hud will ask, “Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own so he may warn you?” The phrasing shows consistent surprise across communities: expecting perhaps a mighty angel, as earlier requested “Why has no visible angel come,” or a heavenly display, not a brother from among the people, the humble kind that invokes morning and evening.\n\nThe reminder’s source is emphasized: “from your Lord,” Rabbikum, the same nurturing Lord who provides livelihood, sends winds, makes day and night overlap, and whose mercy is close to the good-doers, not a foreign power. The means, “through one of your own,” or “ala rajulin minkum,” is mercy’s wisdom: a human messenger can be understood, can share language, can model the best clothing of righteousness, can eat and drink without waste, can be the fertile soil that demonstrates itself. The purpose is double: “warning you, so you may beware,” or “li-yunthirakum wa li-tattaqoo,” to warn of the tremendous Day so that attentive fear develops, as earlier warning of the Day of gathering, and “perhaps be shown mercy,” or “wa la‘allakum turhamoon,” so that fear leads to guarding and mending and then to mercy pronounced as close and as taken upon Himself.\n\nThe chiefs’ astonishment was therefore misplaced. The true astonishment would be that the Lord who never destroys a community while its people are unaware would leave them without a reminder. Their dismissal as “clearly misguided” was itself the misguidance of taking devils as masters thinking they are guided.\n\nFor today’s listener, the verse teaches not to undervalue a reminder because its carrier is familiar, but to examine its content: does it call to worship Allah alone, forbid indecencies and shirk, command uprightness, and point to the scales and the barrier? If so, it is the dhikr meant to make the infertile heart mindful before the term that cannot be delayed arrives.",
  "64": "“But they rejected him, so We saved him and those with him in the Ark, and drowned those who rejected Our signs. They were certainly a blind people” concludes Noah’s episode with the outcome that validates the warning they found astonishing. The previous verses showed Noah inviting to worship Allah alone, acknowledging he is not misguided but a messenger with sincere advice and knowledge from Allah, and asking why they find it strange that a reminder comes through one of their own. Now the response is summarized: “But they rejected him,” or “fa-kathaboohu,” they called him a liar, denied his messages, the same verb used for denying revelations with arrogance, for which scales become light. The rejection was not casual doubt but persistent denial like those who ignored the fulfilment until they ruined themselves.\n\nThe divine response splits into rescue and consequence: “so We saved him and those with him in the Ark,” or “fa-anjainahu wa allatheena ma‘ahu fil-fulk.” Salvation includes Noah and the few fertile-soil responders who believed, boarded, and were established as successors, much like the earlier promise that those who shun evil and mend will have no fear. “And drowned those who rejected Our signs,” or “wa aghraqna allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina,” the same drowning by water that is mercy when sent as rain producing every fruit becomes torment when it overwhelms after order has been rejected and corruption spread. The water that was heralded by winds ushering mercy becomes the scale of justice for the defiant.\n\nThe moral assessment is then given: “They were certainly a blind people,” or “innahum kanoo qawman amineen,” blind in heart, not only in eyes, as earlier described those who deny signs are deaf and dumb lost in darkness and those whose hearts and eyes are turned away as they refused to believe at first. Their blindness was not lack of reminder but refusal to see the reminder through a man among them.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to read the flood as arbitrary harshness rather than as the appointed term that cannot be delayed for a community that chose to follow elegant deception instead of the Book explained with knowledge. The episode shows the pattern recurring with ’Ad and Hud next: reminder through a brother, accusation of misguidance or foolishness, persistence, then the term’s fulfilment, inviting the Meccan audience to choose the Ark of humble, secret calling with hope and fear before the warning’s fulfilment they claim to await arrives beyond intercession.",
  "65": "“And to the people of ’Ad We sent their brother Hud. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. Will you not then fear Him?’” opens the second major story after Noah, showing that the fertile-and-infertile lesson repeats with a new community and a new brother-messenger. The previous episode ended with saving the sincere in the Ark and drowning the blind who rejected signs, a group whose term arrived without delay. The next sentence, “And to the people of ’Ad We sent their brother Hud,” or “wa ila ‘Adin akhahum Hooda,” emphasizes kinship. Hud is not a stranger or an angel, but a brother in lineage, the same human closeness that made Noah’s reminder astonishing to chiefs. His call is identical in foundation: “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghairuhu,” the single worship demanded from the Lord whose creation and command belong to Him alone, who forbade only indecencies, sin, aggression, shirk, and speaking without knowledge, not adornment or tayyibat.\n\nThe appeal ends with an inviting question: “Will you not then fear Him?” or “afala tattaqoon,” will you not be mindful, guarded, wearing the best clothing of righteousness that earlier was called better than any adornment. Fear here is not terror but protective awareness that keeps one from calling adornment forbidden and from taking faith as play, just as earlier “so you may beware and perhaps be shown mercy” linked the reminder to taqwa.\n\nThe ’Ad were known for power, tall stature, and monumental buildings, a strong establishment on earth much like those given livelihood abundantly. Their establishment made them prone to think numbers and arrogance would avail, exactly the boast later rebuked on the Heights when tyrants’ large numbers and arrogance were said to be of no use. Sending a brother among the strong tests whether strength will submit to sincerity.\n\nA common error is to think each prophet brought a different core message. The repetition shows the core is one: worship the One whose winds herald mercy, who subjects sun and moon, who will bring the dead to life like fruit from dead land, and who will weigh with justice. Another error is to treat fear of Allah as mere fright. Hud’s question presents fear as the fertile soil’s openness that allows mercy to stay close to the good-doers, the mercy announced as always near those who call humbly and secretly while not spreading corruption after order.",
  "66": "“The disbelieving chiefs of his people responded, ‘We surely see you as a fool, and we certainly think you are a liar’” records the elite reply to Hud’s brotherly call to worship Allah alone and to be mindful. The speakers are again “the chiefs,” or “al-mala’u,” as with Noah’s people, the same social rank that hinders the Way, makes it appear crooked, and deludes by worldly life, the tyrants later recognized by their appearance on the Heights. Their characterization of Hud is a step beyond calling Noah misguided: “We surely see you as a fool,” or “inna la-naraka fi safahah,” safahah meaning light-mindedness, foolishness, lack of judgment, as if wisdom belonged to those who denied signs with arrogance. And “we certainly think you are a liar,” or “wa inna la-nazunnuka min al-kathibeen,” liar being one who fabricates lies against Allah, the very worst wrong for which every group will taste doubled torment. Ironically, the lying accusation came from those whose own fabrications about lawful and forbidden had been exposed as assumption and beautified lies.\n\nThe two labels, fool and liar, mirror the labels Meccan chiefs used against Muhammad, calling him fool, poet, and fabricator of ancient fables, expecting that prestige would settle truth. The pattern is now unmistakable: each community’s chiefs respond to the same worship-alone message with elite contempt, mistaking the straight path for folly. Their seeing, or “naraka,” is the same blind seeing that turned away hearts and eyes as they refused to believe at first, claiming to see misguidance where guidance was plain.\n\nThe next verses will show Hud’s forbearance matching Noah’s: he will say “O my people, I am not a fool but a messenger from the Lord of all worlds, conveying my Lord’s messages and I am your sincere advisor,” contrasting sincere advice with the deceit of the sincere-sounding advisor who swore to Adam. This forbearance reflects the earlier instruction not to let anxiety tighten the chest regarding the Book and to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear.\n\nFor contemporary listeners, the verse warns against measuring truth by social cachet. The accused fool was actually the wise one who knew what they did not, whose shelter was the Ark of guidance while the self-declared wise remained blind until drowning or being seized by surprise at night or midday. Keeping the earth from corruption after it was set in order means listening for content rather than for status.",
  "67": "“Hûd replied, ‘O my people! I am no fool! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds’” mirrors Noah’s dignified denial of misguidance, showing the consistent character of brothers who fear the tremendous Day for their people. The previous verse had the disbelieving chiefs call him a fool and a liar, using status to dismiss the call to worship Allah alone and to be mindful. Hud’s reply begins again with “O my people,” retaining kinship and concern, then directly negates the label: “I am no fool,” or “laisa bee safahah,” there is no foolishness in me. The same word “safahah” they used to diminish him is returned as a calm denial, without mirroring their arrogance. The correction follows: “But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,” or “wa lakinni rasoolun min Rabbi al-alameen,” the same Lord whose creation and command belong to Him alone, who made day and night overlap and who sends winds heralding mercy. This identity reframes foolishness: true folly would be to deny the One who created heavens and earth in six Days and to take devils as masters thinking oneself guided.\n\nThe reply connects to the broader theme of fertile soil versus infertile ground. Hud’s message, like Noah’s, was conveying his Lord’s messages with sincere advice, not a claim to possess treasuries or knowledge of the unseen. The chiefs’ “seeing” him as a fool was the blind seeing of those who deny signs with arrogance and whose scales become light.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think a firm denial of a false label is itself arrogance. Hud shows it is not; it is truthful self-defense paired with reaffirming mission, maintaining humility while refusing to accept false description. For the Meccan audience who heard Muhammad called poet, sorcerer, and liar, this echo would be unmistakable. The believer today learns to answer personal attacks similarly: do not accept false titles, do not attack in kind, re-anchor in the Lord of all worlds whose Word is perfected and whose Book was explained with knowledge as guide and mercy for the believing, keeping the best clothing of righteousness intact.",
  "68": "“conveying to you my Lord’s messages. And I am your sincere advisor” continues Hud’s self-description after denying foolishness, deepening the parallel with Noah’s “conveying my Lord’s messages and giving you sincere advice, and I know from Allah what you do not know.” The phrase “conveying to you my Lord’s messages,” or “uballighukum risalati Rabbi,” repeats the image of deliverance, like winds delivering mercy, where the messenger does not invent but transmits accurately, as earlier commanded to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians. The messages include worship of Allah alone, fearing Him, remembering favours to become successful, and recognizing that every community has an appointed term.\n\nThe second role, “And I am your sincere advisor,” or “wa ana lakum nasihun ameen,” adds trustworthiness. “Nasih” means sincere, pure counsel that seeks only the listeners’ welfare, opposite to the oath-bound “sincere advisor” claimed by Satan who wanted to expose nakedness. “Ameen” means trustworthy, faithful, not betraying the message. This pair was the hallmark of prophets who feared the tremendous Day for their people and whose chest was spacious with Islam, unlike the constricted chest of those who denied.\n\nThis description links to the warning not to spread corruption after order and to call with hope and fear. A sincere advisor does not corrupt the land with elegant deception but sets it in order with truth, exactly what Hud does by calling his people to remember they were made successors after Noah and increased in stature.\n\nA common mistake is to confuse eloquent speakers who claim sincerity with true advisors. The verse gives the test: does the advisor convey the Lord’s messages faithfully, without adding fabricated shares for associates, invented cattle taboos, or shameful deeds claimed as commanded? Hud’s trustworthiness is proven by his willingness to remain among his people despite being called fool and liar, continuing to advise even when it brings no personal gain. For modern listeners, the pattern helps distinguish fertile counsel that produces abundant fruit by the Will of the Lord from infertile advice that hardly produces anything, inviting them to be among the thankful who receive varied lessons and give thanks for establishment on earth rather than seldom giving thanks.",
  "69": "“Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own so he may warn you? Remember that He made you successors after the people of Noah and increased you greatly in stature. So remember Allah’s favours, so you may be successful” repeats the astonishment question that Noah asked, now tied to the specific history of ’Ad. The opening, “Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own so he may warn you?” or “awa ajibtum an ja’akum thikrun min Rabbikum ala rajulin minkum li-yunthirakum,” mirrors Noah’s “so you may beware and perhaps be shown mercy,” but here the purpose after warning is memory. Hud adds, “Remember that He made you successors after the people of Noah and increased you greatly in stature,” or “wa uthkuroo ith ja‘alakum khulafaa’a min ba‘di qawmi Nooh wa zadakum fil-khalqi bastah.” The ’Ad were known for physical greatness and monumental power after the flood, successors, or “khulafaa,” on earth like later generations described as successors, established with livelihood to test thankfulness.\n\nThe increase “in stature,” or “bastah,” is a visible favour, like the increase in number later noted for Midian and like the fertile land producing abundantly. Remembering, or “uthkuroo,” is the antidote to the seldom-thanks noted after establishment, and to the delusion of worldly life that makes faith seem like play. The call to “remember Allah’s favours, so you may be successful,” or “fa-uthkuroo aalaa Allahi la‘allakum tuflihoon,” links gratitude directly to success, or “falah,” the same heavy-scale success promised to those who shun evil and mend, who believe and do good within capacity, whose gates of heaven open.\n\nHud’s reminder shows the continuity of divine favours: after Noah’s flood, a new people were given a new chance, just as after each community’s term a successor arises. The infertile response would be to treat the reminder as foolishness, as the chiefs did, while the fertile response is to let remembrance soften the hard ground.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think reminders from familiar people are less impressive than angelic signs. This verse teaches that the most impressive sign is precisely a reminder through one of your own, whose stature you can see and whose sincerity you can test. For the Heights people who recognized humble believers by appearance, this memory of succession should have humbled the arrogant whose numbers and arrogance later proved of no use. Remembering favours as a community keeps the scale heavy before the Day the promise is fulfilled and intercessors are sought in vain.",
  "70": "“They said, ‘Have you come to us so that we would worship Allah alone and abandon what our forefathers used to worship? Then bring us what you threaten us with, if what you say is true!’” records the ’Ad people’s defiant reply after Hud asked them to remember they were successors after Noah and increased in stature. The previous verses showed Hud denying foolishness, affirming he is a trustworthy messenger conveying messages and advising sincerely, and asking why they find a reminder through one of their own astonishing. Now their answer exposes the heart of resistance: loyalty to forefathers over the Originator. The phrase “so that we would worship Allah alone and abandon what our forefathers used to worship,” or “li-na‘buda Allaha wahdahu wa nathara ma kana ya‘budu aba’una,” shows they heard the call clearly. Worshipping Allah alone, or “wahdahu,” the essence of uprightness and sincere devotion, would require abandoning the shares for associates, the she-camel taboos, and the elegant deception inherited as custom. Their question implies this is unthinkable.\n\nThe second sentence then issues the familiar challenge: “Then bring us what you threaten us with, if what you say is true!” or “fa’tina bima ta‘iduna in kunta min al-sadiqeen.” This demand for immediate punishment mirrors Noah’s people’s challenge and later Thamud’s “Bring us what you threaten if you are a messenger,” and Midian’s similar threats. It assumes that if threat does not materialize instantly, the warner is a liar. The same logic appeared in Mecca when deniers said “Bring the torment you threaten if you are truthful” and “If what you seek to hasten were within my power the matter would be settled,” forgetting that signs and punishment are only with Allah and appointed terms cannot be delayed or advanced.\n\nThis reply links to the earlier warning not to be deluded by worldly life and not to make the straight seem crooked. Their forefather argument and hastening challenge make the straight path look crooked while beautifying misdeeds.\n\nA common error today is to defend a practice solely because ancestors did it. The Quran honors parents, yet forbids associating partners or following whim without knowledge even if forefathers did. For the Meccan listener, the echo was direct: will you abandon what forefathers worshipped for the Creator who provides adornment and tayyibat, or will you demand hastened punishment and discover, as ’Ad did, that their successors after Noah were uprooted by the very winds that usher mercy turned into torment.",
  "71": "“You will certainly be subjected to your Lord’s torment and wrath. Do you dispute with me regarding the so-called gods which you and your forefathers have made up —a practice Allah has never authorized? Then wait! I too am waiting with you” is Hud’s firm answer after his people demanded he bring the threat if truthful and clung to forefather worship. The opening, “You will certainly be subjected to your Lord’s torment and wrath,” or “qad waqa‘a alaikum min Rabbikum rijsun wa ghadab,” announces that punishment and anger have already fallen in decree, like a cloud heavy with rain whose driving is only a matter of appointment. “Rijs” can mean filth, impurity, or punishment, and “ghadab” is wrath, the same wrath whose gates are not opened for the arrogant and whose torment is tasting. The certainty “qad waqa‘a,” has fallen, shows divine judgment, once justification is complete, is as sure as the overlapping of night and day.\n\nHe then exposes the cause of dispute: “Do you dispute with me regarding the so-called gods which you and your forefathers have made up —a practice Allah has never authorized?” or “a-tujadiloonanee fi asma’in sammaytumoha antum wa aba’ukum ma nazzala Allahu biha min sultan.” The names, or “asma’,” are mere labels invented without revelatory authority, like shares for associates and cattle taboos earlier exposed as “bi-za‘mihim,” by their claim, without knowledge. “Sultan,” authoritative proof, was the same warrant earlier demanded for lawful and forbidden. Their dispute is not about evidence but about protecting invented names from the detailed Book explained with knowledge.\n\nThe closing pairs waiting: “Then wait! I too am waiting with you,” or “fa-intaziroo inni ma‘akum min al-muntazireen.” Both sides await fulfilment, as earlier you will soon know who will fare best in the end and “Say, keep waiting, we too are waiting.” Hud’s waiting is with the mercy close to the good-doers; their waiting is toward the appointment they challenged.\n\nA common mistake is to think naming confers reality. The verse teaches that names without sultan are fabrication, however ancient. The wise wait is to remember favours, be thankful, and turn watchful waiting into humble secret calling with hope and fear, rather than hastening the torment already hovering while the winds that can bring both fruit and flood are sent.",
  "72": "“So We saved him and those with him by Our mercy and uprooted those who denied Our signs. They were not believers” closes Hud’s story with the twin outcomes seen in Noah’s flood. The previous verse left Hud and his people both waiting after the dispute over invented names. Now the term that cannot be delayed arrives. The phrase “So We saved him and those with him by Our mercy,” or “fa-anjainahu wa allatheena ma‘ahu bi-rahmatin minna,” emphasizes rescue is “by Our mercy,” not merely by their deeds, echoing that the Lord took mercy upon Himself, that rivers flow for the guided, and that the Heights hope is answered with entry. Those saved are the fertile land that produced abundantly, the few thankful who remembered favours and wore the best clothing of righteousness.\n\nThe counterpart, “and uprooted those who denied Our signs. They were not believers,” or “wa qata‘na dabira allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina wa ma kanu mu’mineen,” uses the same verb “qata‘na dabira,” cut the tail, uproot, as earlier the wrongdoers were utterly uprooted and all praise belongs to the Lord of all worlds. The cutting is complete, removing the community as a successor, just as fertile land’s harvest is complete. The reason, “They were not believers,” is stark after their question “Will you not then fear Him?” Their disbelief was not lack of reminder but choice to call the sincere advisor a fool and liar and to demand hastened punishment.\n\nThis pattern connects to larger cycles: each community has an appointed term; when it comes, water or wind, which in other contexts brings every fruit, becomes the instrument of uprooting. The ’Ad, increased in stature and made successors after Noah, had their stature increase become a source of arrogance whose numbers and arrogance later proved of no use to tyrants on the Heights.\n\nA misunderstanding is to view salvation as merely survival. Hud’s salvation is by mercy toward continued guidance and toward the path perfectly straight, while uprooting is the consequence of taking devils as masters thinking they are guided and making the Way seem crooked. For the Meccan audience, the message was immediate: the Book has come, the reminder through one of your own is here, the winds usher mercy before rain; will you be among the saved by mercy who remember favours, or among the uprooted who wait for the threat while disputing names without sultan until the Day its fulfilment is confessed too late.",
  "73": "“And to the people of Thamûd We sent their brother Ṣâliḥ. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. A clear proof has come to you from your Lord: this is Allah’s she-camel as a sign to you. So leave her to graze ˹freely˺ on Allah’s land and do not harm her, or else you will be overcome by a painful punishment’” opens the third story, showing the same fertile-hard-soil test with a unique sign. The previous story ended with Hud saved by mercy and deniers uprooted. Now “to Thamud We sent their brother Salih,” or “wa ila Thamooda akhahum Salihan,” again emphasizes brotherhood, the humane bridge. His call repeats the foundation: “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” the same worship-alone that made Noah fear the tremendous Day and Hud ask will you not fear Him.\n\nThe distinction for Thamud is a tangible proof: “A clear proof has come to you from your Lord: this is Allah’s she-camel as a sign,” or “qad ja’atkum bayyinatun min Rabbikum hathihi naqatu Allahi lakum ayah.” “Bayyinah” is the same clear proof Noah and Hud brought, but here it takes the form of a specific camel, described as Allah’s camel to honor it, as earlier winds and rains were driven to lifeless land and as the Book was brought as guide and mercy. The sign tests obedience in everyday stewardship: “So leave her to graze freely on Allah’s land and do not harm her,” or “fa-tharooha ta’kul fi ardi Allahi wa la tamassooha bi-soo’in.” The land is called Allah’s land, reminding them establishment and livelihood are His, and the she-camel is His creature to be respected, not idolized or abused. Thamud were successors after ’Ad, who built palaces on plains and carved homes into mountains, strong establishment that could lead to gratitude or to arrogance.\n\nThe warning, “or else you will be overcome by a painful punishment,” or “fa-yakhuthakum athabun aleem,” is the same painful torment awaited by earlier challengers who said bring what you threaten.\n\nA common error is to think signs are only abstract verses. The she-camel shows a sign can be a living creature entrusted to a community’s care, testing whether provision will be shared justly, as with paying dues at harvest without waste, or corrupted. Passing the test would have shown fertile thankfulness; failing, as next verses narrate with killing the camel and the earthquake, repeats the uprooting of those who hindered, made the Way crooked, and handled signs with arrogance.",
  "74": "“Remember when He made you successors after ’Âd and established you in the land—and you built palaces on its plains and carved homes into mountains. So remember Allah’s favours, and do not go about spreading corruption in the land” continues Salih’s address, deepening Hud’s earlier call to remember favours. The previous verse gave Thamud the sign of the she-camel not to be harmed; this verse gives the context that makes that test meaningful. The opening, “Remember when He made you successors after ’Ad,” or “wa uthkuroo ith ja‘alakum khulafaa’a min ba‘di ‘Adin,” places Thamud in the chain of succession after the uprooted ’Ad, just as ’Ad were successors after Noah’s drowned. Each succession is a fresh chance with the same Book’s message and the same land’s provision. The next phrase, “and established you in the land—and you built palaces on its plains and carved homes into mountains,” or “wa bawwa’akum fil-ardi tattakhithoona min suhooliha qusooran wa tanhitoona al-jibala buyoota,” describes remarkable civil achievement. Archaeology of northern Arabia shows precisely such Nabatean skill, confirming the Quran’s detail. Plain palaces and mountain homes signify mastery over both soft and hard terrain, a visible measure of establishment and stature.\n\nThe moral call follows: “So remember Allah’s favours, and do not go about spreading corruption in the land,” or “fa-uthkuroo aalaa Allahi wa la ta‘thaw fil-ardi mufsideen.” Remembering, or “uthkuroo,” again ties gratitude to success, as earlier with ’Ad. Corruption, or “fasad,” after order, or “islah,” is the same corruption forbidden after the land was set in order and linked to humble secret calling with hope and fear. For Thamud, corruption would be killing the she-camel, defrauding property, and beautifying indecency, which the next verses show.\n\nThis verse links aesthetic prowess to responsibility. Building skill is a favour, not proof of immunity from the term that cannot be delayed. A common misunderstanding is to treat civilization’s monuments as self-validating righteousness. The Quran praises the achievement but warns its purpose is remembrance, not arrogance, lest the heights’ rebuke “your large numbers and arrogance are of no use” become their own. The fertile land that remembers produces abundantly; the infertile, though built high in stone, hardly produces anything, until an earthquake strikes and the homes that were carved become the homes where they fall lifeless.",
  "75": "“The arrogant chiefs of his people asked the lowly who believed among them, ‘Are you certain that Ṣâliḥ has been sent by his Lord?’ They replied, ‘We certainly believe in what he has been sent with’” shows the social split within Thamud when Salih’s sign arrived. The previous verses showed Salih calling to worship Allah alone, honoring the she-camel as Allah’s sign, and reminding them they were successors after ’Ad with palaces and mountain homes. Now the community fractures along the same line seen in earlier stories: arrogant chiefs versus humble believers. The questioners are “the arrogant chiefs,” or “al-mala’u allatheena istakbaroo,” the same elite who called Noah misguided and Hud a fool, whose arrogance later makes the gates of heaven closed. They address not Salih directly but “the lowly who believed among them,” or “allatheena istud‘ifoo liman amana minhum,” those deemed weak, oppressed, the same poor who invoked their Lord morning and evening and were sworn to be denied mercy. Their question, “Are you certain that Salih has been sent by his Lord?” or “a-ta‘lamoona anna Salihan mursalun min Rabbihi,” is posed with skeptical tone, expecting hesitation, as if certainty about revelation were naive.\n\nThe lowly respond with unhesitant faith: “We certainly believe in what he has been sent with,” or “inna bima ursila bihi mu’minoon.” The phrase mirrors Noah’s trustworthy followers and the later “We certainly believe in what he has been sent with” in other contexts. Their faith is not in Salih’s person but in what he was sent with, the risalah, the bayyinah, the she-camel as sign, and the call to worship alone, the same messages that vary for the thankful.\n\nThe exchange illustrates the fertile-infertile divide: same water of reminder falls, but the weak soil produces little while the humbled heart, though socially low, produces abundant certainty.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates low social status with light thinking. This verse inverts it: the arrogant, not the lowly, are blind to the sign, because they assess truth by status, not by content. The lowly recognize their Lord as Subjector of sun and moon and as the One who sends winds with mercy, so a she-camel as His sign fits His lordship; the arrogant, whose stature made them forget, cannot. For the living, the lesson is to seek certainty with the lowly whose hearts are opened to Islam rather than with the chiefs whose chest is tight, remembering that success, or “falah,” comes from remembering favours, not from belonging to the chiefs.",
  "76": "“The arrogant said, ‘We surely reject what you believe in’” records the final rupture after the interrogation between the arrogant and the lowly. The previous verse showed arrogant chiefs questioning the weak believers whether they are certain Salih is sent, and the weak answering “We certainly believe in what he has been sent with.” Now the arrogant reply with absolute rejection: “We surely reject what you believe in,” or “inna bima amantum bihi kafiroon.” The phrasing is telling: they do not say we reject Salih alone, but what you believe in, making the lowly’s faith itself the target, just as Meccan chiefs mocked the poor believers as proof Muhammad could not be true.\n\nThis rejection mirrors earlier arrogant stances: Pharaoh’s people rejecting Moses and Aaron, and the later villages rejecting Shu‘ayb. The arrogant openly announce they are disbelievers in the message that commands uprightness, dedicates faces in worship, and avoids corruption after order. Their rejection is not presented as doubt but as defiance after proof. They had a visible she-camel sign grazing on Allah’s land, a clear bayyinah, yet they chose denial with arrogance for which gates of heaven do not open.\n\nThe brevity of the sentence, compared to the lowly’s affirming “We certainly believe,” shows the hardness that has become appealing. Earlier it was said misdeeds of disbelievers were made appealing, and that We made each people’s deeds appealing to them, leading to the barrier where the heights recognize by appearance. The arrogant find their rejection appealing, as Iblis found “I am better” appealing.\n\nA common error is to view intra-community disagreement as harmless. The Quran shows this split is the line between two harvests: one producing abundantly by the Lord’s leave, the other hardly producing. The arrogant’s rejection directly leads in the next verses to killing the she-camel and being struck lifeless in their carved homes, confirming that appointing a few weak believers as the criterion of truth is more reliable than following chiefs whose large numbers and arrogance will be said to be of no use when the Fire’s abode is prepared.",
  "77": "“Then they killed the she-camel —defying their Lord’s command—and challenged ˹Ṣâliḥ˺, ‘Bring us what you threaten us with, if you are ˹truly˺ one of the messengers’” brings Thamud’s rejection from words to act. The previous verses showed a verbal split: lowly believed, arrogant rejected what they believe in. Now the arrogant move to desecrate the sign itself. The phrase “Then they killed the she-camel —defying their Lord’s command,” or “fa-‘aqaroo al-naqata wa ‘ataw an amri Rabbihim,” combines slaughter with insolence. “‘Aqara” means hamstring, cut, kill cruelly, and “‘ataw” means to exceed bounds, rebel insolently against the command “Do not harm her or painful punishment will take you.” The command was the only limit amid vast provision, like the single tree for Adam, testing whether they would eat and graze freely without harming Allah’s sign. They failed, spreading corruption after order, as earlier warned not to spread corruption in the land.\n\nThe second act compounds the first: “and challenged Salih, ‘Bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truly one of the messengers,’” or “wa qaloo ya Salihu i’tina bima ta‘iduna in kunta min al-mursaleen.” This is the same hastening challenge Hud’s people made, “Bring us what you threaten if you are truthful,” and ’Ad and Thamud repeatedly made, treating punishment as a proof that must be delivered on demand, forgetting that punishment is only with Allah and that every community has a term that cannot be delayed or advanced. Their question “if you are truly one of the messengers” shows they no longer ask for evidence but for instant enforcement.\n\nThe killing of the she-camel is a vivid image of infertile land hardly producing anything despite abundance: the communities built palaces and carved mountains yet could not leave one she-camel to graze freely. The sign that could have softened the land was instead destroyed.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view the hamstringing as a mere animal incident. The Quran treats it as defying the Lord’s command and corrupting His land, the core sin whose wages is being overtaken by the same painful torment they dared. For listeners who were told to dress properly, eat without waste, and call humbly and secretly, this warns that defiance plus hastening closes the very mercy that causes winds to usher rain to lifeless land and that waits with hope and fear.",
  "78": "“Then an ˹overwhelming˺ earthquake struck them, and they fell lifeless in their homes” describes the response to the hamstringing and the challenge to bring the threat. After they defied the command about the she-camel and demanded Salih bring what he threatened if he is a messenger, the term that cannot be delayed arrives in the form already warned: painful punishment overtaking. The phrase “Then an overwhelming earthquake struck them,” or “fa-akhathathum al-rajfah,” means a violent shaking seized them. “Rajfah” is a tremor that shakes the ground and hearts, fitting for a people whose sin was striking the earth’s sign of mercy and whose homes were carved into mountains for imagined security. The earthquake, like the wind that destroyed ’Ad or the flood that drowned Noah’s people, is an instrument subjected by His command, the same Lord whose creation and command belong to Him alone, who makes day and night overlap.\n\nThe result, “and they fell lifeless in their homes,” or “fa-asbahoo fi darihim jathimeen,” means they became corpses kneeling, fallen, motionless in their dwellings. “Jathimeen” evokes bodies crouched, humbled, the opposite of the palaces and carved homes they boasted. Those homes, built to display favour, became the very place of seizure, as earlier torment took societies at night or midday while they were in their homes. For Thamud, midday sun over stone mountains did not protect; the earth beneath betrayed the arrogant.\n\nThe connection to the fertility parable is stark: fertile land produced abundantly, but Thamud’s infertile arrogance produced only an earthquake that left no growth. Their large numbers and carved numbers proved of no use, as later said to tyrants in the Fire, while the lowly believers, perhaps few, had been among those saved with Salih by mercy.\n\nA common misconception is to view natural disasters as random. The verse places the rajfah as “what you threaten,” the fulfilment awaited by the deniers, which they themselves hastened, after the Book had been explained with knowledge and the messenger’s sincere advice was given. It invites the living to hear the Messenger who warns with the Book, to remember favours after succession, and to leave the she-camels of signs to graze, which is to preserve mercy in the land, before the earth they carve is made to shake.",
  "79": "“So he turned away from them, saying, ‘O my people! Surely I conveyed to you my Lord’s message and gave you ˹sincere˺ advice, but you do not like ˹sincere˺ advisors’” shows Salih’s final address after the earthquake left the deniers lifeless in their homes. The previous verse described the rajfah, the shaking that cut the tail of the community. Now Salih physically and morally turns away, as Hud and other prophets did, after his people’s end. The phrase “So he turned away from them,” or “fa-tawalla anhum,” is the same turning observed with prophets whose people persisted, indicating he is not rejoicing but withdrawing from a now-judged people, as earlier the Prophet was told to turn away from mockers unless they change topics.\n\nHis words preserve the pattern of prophetic testimony before departure: “O my people! Surely I conveyed to you my Lord’s message and gave you sincere advice, but you do not like sincere advisors,” or “ya qawmi laqad ablaghtukum risalata Rabbi wa nasahtu lakum wa lakin la tuhibboona al-nasiheen.” He repeats the language used by Noah and Hud: conveying risalah, offering nasihah, confirming that the duty of messengers is to deliver and advise, not to keep or maintain. The accusation “you do not like sincere advisors” diagnoses the fertile-barren divide. Advice was not lacking; liking was. Their hearts, made appealing by misdeeds and guided by devils taken as masters, found sincere advice unappealing and hastened punishment instead, saying “Bring us what you threaten.”\n\nThe phrase links to the broader call to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians, to call humbly and secretly, and to be among the thankful who produce abundantly rather than the infertile who hardly produce. Salih’s sadness reflects the sorrow of Noah and Hud, yet also confirms that the conclusive argument has been made.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to blame the advisor for the community’s fall. Salih’s statement clarifies the fall was by choice: dislike of nasihah. For the Meccan and modern audience, whose prophets also convey and advise, the lesson is to love the nasih, to remember favours after succession, to leave divine signs to graze, and to keep the land from corruption, before the turning away becomes permanent and the earthquake of the appointed term leaves only the question “Where are the false gods you invoked besides Allah?” unanswered except by “They have failed us.”",
  "80": "“And ˹remember˺ when Lot scolded ˹the men of˺ his people, ˹saying,˺ ‘Do you commit a shameful deed that no man has ever done before?’” introduces the next community whose appointed term and corruption illustrate the same fertile-barren test, now centered on a shameful deed, or “fahishah.” The previous story ended with Salih turning away because his people did not like sincere advisors and were struck by an earthquake in their carved homes. Now the narrative shifts to Lot, or “Lut,” whose people’s corruption was not chiefly hamstringing a camel but violating the natural order of intimacy. The opening, “And remember when Lot scolded his people,” or “wa Lootan ith qala li-qawmihi,” calls to recollection as earlier calls to remember after Noah and ’Ad. Lot’s people were known for a deed said to have no precedent in community form: men lusting after men instead of women, combined with highway robbery and public indecency, as later details explain.\n\nThe rebuke, “Do you commit a shameful deed that no man has ever done before?” or “a-ta’toona al-fahishata ma sabaqakum biha min ahadin min al-alameen,” uses “fahishah,” the same term forbidden openly and secretly and whose command Allah never gives. The claim “no man has ever done before” emphasizes that this was not an isolated private slip but a communal normalization, a fabricated law, much like dividing shares for associates or killing the she-camel. They institutionalized what should be approached with chastity and within limits taught by “do not come near indecencies open or secret.”\n\nLot’s scolding, or “qala,” is the same brotherly warning given by Noah, Hud, and Salih, fearing the tremendous Day. His people’s establishment, like ’Ad’s stature and Thamud’s palaces, did not prevent moral collapse; establishment without gratefulness produces hardly anything, like infertile land.\n\nA common misconception is to treat such fahishah as a purely private matter with no communal term. The Quran places it among the deeds that cause an appointed term to arrive without delay, as rain of brimstone will show, linking sexual transgression, like forbidding tayyibat or corrupting land, to spreading corruption after order. The best clothing of righteousness that covers nakedness is the garment violated by such exposure, and the winds ushering mercy that bring every fruit stand in contrast to a rain of punishment. For listeners taught to dress for worship, eat without waste, and call humbly, Lot’s scolding invites mindful preservation of the order set in creation, where creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds.",
  "81": "“You lust after men instead of women! You are certainly transgressors” spells out the shameful deed Lot condemned, giving definition after the introductory rebuke. The previous verse asked, “Do you commit a shameful deed no man has ever done before?” Now the nature of that deed is named plainly: “You lust after men instead of women!” or “innakum la-ta’toona al-rijala shahwatan min doon al-nisa’.” The phrase “min doon al-nisa’” instead of women shows the replacement of what Allah made as proper pairing, echoing the broader pattern of taking guardians besides Him and dividing provision without sultan. Lust, or “shahwah,” is desire untethered from the lawful channel that produces families, livelihood, and remembrance. The people of Lot institutionalized this replacement, making it a public identity, as Thamud institutionalized killing the she-camel and ’Ad institutionalized arrogance.\n\nThe judgment that follows is universal: “You are certainly transgressors,” or “bal antum qawmun musrifoon.” “Musrifoon” means those who exceed bounds, extravagant, wasteful, the same word used for waste in eating and in harvest, “do not waste, He does not like the wasteful.” Excess here is not merely quantity but transgressing the limit set by the One whose creation and command are His, who made night for rest and day for seeking, who subjected sun, moon, and stars for order. The fertile land’s limit is to produce abundantly within its nature; the infertile transgresses by producing hardly anything while claiming it is production. This sexual transgression parallels that economic waste: both are israf.\n\nThe verse links to the earlier catalogue of true prohibitions: indecencies open and secret are forbidden, and associating without sultan and speaking without knowledge are forbidden. Lot’s people’s act falls under open fahishah, while their communal insistence that expelling the chaste is the answer will be quoted next, showing how israf defends itself.\n\nA common misunderstanding frames this prohibition as lacking wisdom. The Quran frames it as order after creation in six Days and establishment on the Throne, where day and night succeed each other in measured pursuit, an order whose violation invites the rain of brimstone as the next verses show, matching the pattern that every community has a term that when it comes cannot be delayed. The alternative, filling the land with grateful, righteous clothing that covers nakedness, keeps mercy close to the good-doers who call humbly and secretly.",
  "82": "“But his people’s only response was to say, ‘Expel them from your land! They are a people who wish to remain chaste!’” records the reply to Lot’s plain rebuke about lusting after men instead of women as transgression. The previous verse gave moral definition; this verse gives social reaction. The speakers are again the people, like the chiefs before, but here the consensus is shown by “their only response was to say,” or “wa ma kana jawaba qawmihi illa an qaloo.” The response contains not argument but expulsion: “Expel them from your land! They are a people who wish to remain chaste!” or “akhrijoohum min qaryatikum innahum unasun yatatahharoon.” The phrase “yatatahharoon” means they purify themselves, seek cleanliness, keep to chastity, the very quality earlier praised as the best clothing of righteousness and as the fertile land producing. The people’s slur treats purity as a flaw to be banished, just as earlier arrogant chiefs treated the lowly believers’ certainty as naivety and Hud’s sincerity as folly.\n\nThis inversion, where chastity is cause for exile, mirrors Iblis’s inversion where fire was deemed better than clay and where Satan swore he was a sincere advisor while seeking to expose nakedness. It shows that when a community normalizes fahishah, the scales of value reverse: corruption is called order and order is called corruption. The call to expel also shows the chiefs’ claim to ownership of the land, forgetting it is Allah’s land as Salih said leave her to graze freely on Allah’s land.\n\nThe response links to the barrier scene where the arrogant’s large numbers proved useless; here the community feels numerous enough to expel the chaste few, believing numbers guarantee survival. The term that cannot be delayed will prove otherwise, as a rain of brimstone soon falls and Lot’s family except his wife is saved.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think exile of the righteous solves a society’s tension. The Quran shows expulsion is often the last step before the appointed term strikes at night or midday, when the only cry is we were wrongdoers. For listeners who were told to dress for worship, eat without waste, and call with hope and fear, Lot’s people’s reply warns against mocking those who wish to remain chaste, whose healing is the community’s last mercy. Loving sincere advisors, as Salih said his people did not, is the fertile response that keeps mercy close.",
  "83": "“So We saved him and his family except his wife, who was one of the doomed” gives the division that follows the threat to expel the chaste. The previous verse quoted the people’s only answer, expel them for wanting to remain pure. Now the divine saving distinguishes within the family itself. The phrase “So We saved him and his family,” or “fa-anjainahu wa ahlahu,” is the same salvation by mercy given to Noah with his companions in the Ark and to Hud and Salih with theirs. Those saved are the fertile land that produced abundantly by the Lord’s leave, the thankful who followed what was sent down and kept the best clothing. Lot, his daughters, and the few who called humbly, were saved because they wore righteousness and did not take devils as masters thinking they were guided.\n\nThe exception, “except his wife, who was one of the doomed,” or “illa imra’atahu kanat min al-ghabireen,” is striking. She was of his household yet chose the side of the expellers, sympathizing with their fahishah and its normalization, much as earlier some heedless were deluded by worldly life and others followed devils’ elegant deception. “Ghabireen” means those who remain behind, left in punishment, the ones whose term arrived. Her example parallels Noah’s wife and shows that kinship to a messenger does not guarantee mercy; response does. It echoes the barrier where people are recognized by marks, not by lineage, and where the question “Are these the humble you swore would never receive mercy?” exposes worldly ranking.\n\nThe verse connects to the broader law that every community has a term and that the weighing will be just. Salvation is not by numbers, as tyrants’ large numbers proved useless, nor by association alone, but by the weight of belief and good within capacity.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to assume family ties secure intercession without faith. Lot’s wife shows the opposite: nearness without righteousness yields remaining behind, while lowly believers without noble lineage are told “Enter Paradise, no fear for you.” The lesson, after being taught to call humbly and secretly without transgressing and not to spread corruption after order, is to keep the household fertile by remembering favours, guarding chastity, and following the detailed Book, so that when winds usher mercy as rain that can also become brimstone, the family is among the saved rather than among the doomed who remain.",
  "84": "“We poured upon them a rain ˹of brimstone˺. See what was the end of the wicked!” describes the punishment that realized the term for Lot’s people after the threat to expel the chaste and the saving of Lot’s family except the doomed wife. The phrase “We poured upon them a rain of brimstone,” or “wa amtarna alaihim matara,” repeats the verb “amtarna” used earlier for rain that produces every type of fruit and is likened to raising the dead. Here the same verb that brought mercy as fruit now brings torment as stones, showing the same creation and command belonging to Him can be blessing or trial. The rain was not water but hardened clay stones, or “hijarah,” baked and marked, as detailed elsewhere, a rain that matches their hard-hearted persistence in israf, exceeding bounds.\n\nThe command that follows is reflective, not vengeful: “See what was the end of the wicked!” or “fa-unzur kaifa kana aqibatu al-mujrimeen.” “Unzur” is the same empirical command given at the opening of this surah’s warnings, “See how We destroyed many societies at night or midday,” and earlier “Travel through the land and see the end of deniers.” Looking is with the eye of the heart as well as the eye of travel, observing ruins near the Dead Sea where Lot’s people lived, as a lesson varied for the thankful.\n\nThe description “the wicked,” or “al-mujrimeen,” ties them to the broader category whose way was made distinct, who were warned would be inhabited with doubled torment, whose bed is Hell and cover is flames, whose deeds were made appealing until the Day fulfilment left only self-ruin. Their wickedness included hinderers who made the straight seem crooked and who disbelieved in the Hereafter, and now lust-driven transgression added to that record.\n\nA common error is to separate moral and physical order as unrelated. This rain shows they are linked: spreading corruption after order in the land leads the very rain that is mercy to become a scale of justice. The alternative, producing every fruit for the thankful, remains available. For listeners instructed to eat adornment without waste, to call with hope and fear, and to not lie in ambush on every road hindering the path, the sight of the wicked’s end invites mindfulness, which is to see the appointed term’s inevitability before it comes suddenly, so as not to be among the ghabireen left behind when the merciful wind turns into brimstone.",
  "85": "“And to the people of Midian We sent their brother Shu‘aib. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. A clear proof has already come to you from your Lord. So give just measure and weight, do not defraud people of their property, nor spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order. This is for your own good, if you are ˹truly˺ believers’” opens the fifth major story, again with a brother-messenger and a specific social corruption. The previous story ended with rain of brimstone for transgressive lust; now Midian’s disease is economic: cheating in measure and spreading corruption after order. The call, “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” is the constant foundation, identical to Noah, Hud, Salih, and Lot, the straight worship-alone that the Book explained with knowledge and that makes the scale heavy.\n\nThe next phrase, “A clear proof has already come to you from your Lord,” or “qad ja’atkum bayyinatun min Rabbikum,” indicates Shu‘ayb, like Salih with the she-camel, brought a bayyinah suited to his people, often understood as his eloquent guidance and the tangible measure itself. The application is immediate and practical: “So give just measure and weight, do not defraud people of their property, nor spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order,” or “fa-awfoo al-kaila wal-meezana wa la tabkhasoo al-nasa ashya’ahum wa la tufsidoo fil-ardi ba‘da islahiha.” This repeats the commands to give full measure and weigh with justice, not to come near orphan wealth except to enhance, and not to spread corruption after islah, now directed at a market town whose livelihood depended on trade routes. Defrauding by short measure was the public, normalized “fahishah” of Midian as lust was for Lot’s people.\n\nThe closing incentive, “This is for your own good, if you are truly believers,” or “thalikum khairun lakum in kuntum mu’mineen,” links ethics to self-interest, not exploitation. Goodness, or “khair,” here is the better harvest of fertile land producing abundantly, versus hardly producing.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat business fraud as minor compared with other sins. Shu‘ayb’s placement as a major prophet shows it is a community-term sin like disbelief and aggression, for which the appointed term cannot be delayed. For listeners who were told to dress for worship and eat without waste, Shu‘ayb’s call teaches that justice in commerce is worship, that mercy close to the good-doers includes market honesty, and that the winds of mercy that bring fruit also measure whether the scale in this world matches the Scale that will be just on the Day.",
  "86": "“And do not lie in ambush on every road—threatening and hindering those who believe in Allah from His Path and striving to make it ˹appear˺ crooked. Remember when you were few, then He increased you in number. And consider the fate of the corruptors!” continues Shu‘ayb’s counsel after commanding just measure, no defrauding, and no corruption after order. The opening, “And do not lie in ambush on every road—threatening and hindering those who believe,” or “wa la taq‘udoo bi-kulli siratin too‘idoona wa tasuddoona an sabili Allahi man amana bihi wa tabghoonaha iwaja,” directly names the Iblisian tactic announced on the Straight Path: “I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path” and “I will approach from front, back, right, left.” Midian’s chiefs literally sat on trade roads threatening believers, but the Quran’s wording generalizes to every path where the sincere are intimidated from following what was sent down. Hindering, or “sadd,” is the same hindering warned of for the wrongdoers upon whom condemnation was announced, and making the Way seem crooked, or “tabghoonaha iwaja,” is the same twisting for which that condemnation was due.\n\nThe moral memory that follows is personal: “Remember when you were few, then He increased you in number,” or “wa uthkuroo ith kuntum qaleelan fa-kaththarakum.” This mirrors the favours noted for ’Ad increased in stature and Thamud made successors after ’Ad and established with palaces, and it counters the arrogant use of large numbers. Increase is a favour to breed thankfulness, not a license to ambush, just as fertile rain increased fruit to be eaten without waste, not to be wasted.\n\nThe concluding command, “And consider the fate of the corruptors!” or “wa unzuroo kaifa kana aqibatu al-mufsideen,” repeats the empirical “see the end” that closed Noah’s flood, Hud’s uprooting, Salih’s earthquake, and Lot’s rain of brimstone, inviting Midian to look at those ends before joining them.\n\nA common error is to think hindering belief is mere personal preference. Shu‘ayb shows it is mufsida, corruption that undoes islah, the order after which corruption is forbidden, and that collective term that pours at night or midday. The alternative, given for believers, is patience until Allah judges, the best of judges, keeping hope and fear while calling humbly and secretly, so that increase in numbers becomes increase in grateful heavy scales rather than increase in ambush that makes the straight seem crooked.",
  "87": "“If some of you do believe in what I have been sent with while others do not, then be patient until Allah judges between us. He is the Best of Judges” continues Shu‘ayb’s final counsel after warning Midian not to lie in ambush on every road and to consider the fate of corruptors. The community was split, as in Thamud where lowly believed and arrogant rejected. Shu‘ayb acknowledges the split openly: “If some of you do believe in what I have been sent with while others do not,” or “wa in kana ta’ifatun minkum amanoo billathee ursiltu bihi wa ta’ifatun lam yu’minoo.” The division is not hidden; it is the reality after a clear proof, or bayyinah, has come and the reminder to give just measure was delivered. Instead of calling for violent confrontation, Shu‘ayb prescribes patience: “then be patient until Allah judges between us,” or “fa-isbiroo hatta yahkuma Allahu bainana.” “Isbiroo” here means to endure, to wait with steadfastness, keeping to the straight path while the term that cannot be delayed yet allows.\n\nThe reason patience is enjoined is the judge’s identity: “He is the Best of Judges,” or “wa huwa khairu al-hakimeen,” the same title given when Shu‘ayb later prays “You are the best of those who judge,” and earlier “Allah’s promise to help is never broken, and you have already received narratives of messengers.” The best judge judges with perfect knowledge, encompassing everything, as Shu‘ayb will say “Our Lord has encompassed everything in knowledge.” His judgment will be the weighing that is just, the barrier that separates, and the rivers that flow or the rain of brimstone that pours.\n\nThis instruction links to Noah’s saving in the Ark, Hud’s mercy, and Salih’s earthquake: in each case, the prophet left judgment to the Lord rather than forcing it. Midian is told to do the same. A common misunderstanding is to think patience means acceptance of corruption. Shu‘ayb’s patience is not silence; he already warned, advised, and reminded that they were few then increased. Patience here means to hold to belief, continue calling humbly with hope and fear, and avoid ambush, until the conclusive argument of the appointed term arrives. For the Meccan listener being told his people have divided faith into sects and his duty is not to be a keeper, Shu‘ayb’s patience teaches that the Hour that takes by surprise will itself be the most just division.",
  "88": "“The arrogant chiefs of his people threatened, ‘O Shu‘aib! We will certainly expel you and your fellow believers from our land, unless you return to our faith.’ He replied, ‘Even if we hate it?’” shows the arrogant response to Shu‘ayb’s call for patience until the best of judges decides. The speakers are again “the arrogant chiefs,” or “al-mala’u allatheena istakbaroo,” the same elite who questioned Salih’s lowly believers and who threatened Lot’s family with expulsion for wishing to remain chaste. Their threat is territorial and coercive: “We will certainly expel you and your fellow believers from our land, unless you return to our faith,” or “la-nukhrijannaka ya Shu‘aibu wa allatheena amanoo ma‘aka min qaryatina aw la-ta‘oodunna fi millatina.” They claim ownership of the land, forgetting it is Allah’s land as Salih said, and present faith as a tribal identity to which one can be returned by pressure, like cattle taboos imposed without sultan.\n\nShu‘ayb’s reply is astonished and principled: “Even if we hate it?” or “awa law kunna kariheen.” The question exposes the absurdity of forced conversion: would you compel us to return to a faith we detest, whose devils we have recognized as false masters and whose beautified killing and defrauding we have abandoned for uprightness and just measure? Hate here is not personal spite but wholesome detestation for shirk, the same detestation the poor believers showed when they refused to dismiss the weak for the powerful.\n\nThis exchange mirrors Lot’s people who said expel them for they wish to remain chaste, and it foreshadows the question he will expand in the next verse about fabricating a lie against Allah if they returned after being saved. The threat reveals infertile soil producing hardly anything despite increase in numbers, clinging to large numbers and arrogance that will be said to be of no use.\n\nA common error is to think faith can be restored by expulsion threats. Shu‘ayb’s question shows faith is conviction, not geography; returning despite hated disbelief would be lying against the Lord who saved them. For believers told not to insult what others invoke lest they insult Allah out of ignorance, and to follow what was sent down rather than take other guardians, the verse teaches to endure expulsion threats with the same spacious chest that was opened to Islam, trusting that Allah, who encompasses everything in knowledge and in whom they trust, will judge with truth between them and a people who spread corruption after order.",
  "89": "“We would surely be fabricating a lie against Allah if we were to return to your faith after Allah has saved us from it. It does not befit us to return to it unless it is the Will of Allah, our Lord. Our Lord has encompassed everything in ˹His˺ knowledge. In Allah we trust. Our Lord! Judge between us and our people with truth. You are the best of those who judge” expands Shu‘ayb’s astonished “Even if we hate it?” into a complete theological stance. The first sentence, “We would surely be fabricating a lie against Allah if we were to return to your faith after Allah has saved us from it,” or “qad iftaraina ala Allahi kathiban in ‘udna fi millatikum ba‘da ith najjana Allahu minha,” uses “iftara,” the worst wrong of inventing a lie against Allah, already named as dividing crops for associates, forbidding tayyibat, and claiming Allah commands shame. To return to shirk after being saved from it, after tasting the guidance that trustworthy messengers convey, would be to claim Allah approves what He has forbidden as open and secret indecencies and associating without sultan, a lie as grave as claiming revelation without being given it.\n\nThe qualification, “It does not befit us to return to it unless it is the Will of Allah, our Lord,” shows humility before divine will, acknowledging that human constancy itself depends on the Lord whose will could guide all if He willed, yet who leaves the tight-chested to wander when they refuse first belief. “Our Lord has encompassed everything in His knowledge,” or “wasi‘a Rabbuna kulla shay’in ilman,” recalls that Allah knows what is in land and sea, not a leaf falls without a record, and knows who is grateful versus who spreads corruption, so returning would not escape His notice.\n\nThe final turn is trust and supplication: “In Allah we trust,” or “ala Allahi tawakkalna,” the same reliance that kept Noah, Hud, and Salih firm when called fool or threatened, and “Our Lord! Judge between us and our people with truth. You are the best of those who judge,” or “Rabbana iftah bainana wa baina qawmina bil-haqq wa anta khairu al-fatiheen.” “Iftah” means open, decide, give victory of truth, like the opening of the Book. The best opener is the Lord of all worlds whose creation and command are His.\n\nA misunderstanding is that refusal to return is stubbornness. Shu‘ayb frames it as fidelity to being saved, refusal to lie, and trust in the encompassing knowledge that will weigh justly when patience until the best judge decides ends and the earthquake they are about to taste arrives.",
  "90": "“The disbelieving chiefs of his people threatened, ‘If you follow Shu‘aib, you will surely be losers!’” records the final propaganda of the arrogant after Shu‘ayb placed his trust in Allah and asked the best of judges to decide. The speakers are “the disbelieving chiefs,” or “al-mala’u allatheena kafaroo,” the same group who just threatened expulsion unless he returned to their faith. Now they target not Shu‘ayb but his followers, using the fear of loss to peel them away: “If you follow Shu‘aib, you will surely be losers!” or “la-in ittab‘tum Shu‘aiban innakum ithan la-khasiroon.” “Khasiroon,” losers, is the key Quranic term for those who deny the meeting until the Hour surprises them and they bear burdens on backs, those whose scales are light and who doom themselves. By labeling followers as losers, the chiefs invert reality, as earlier they swore humble believers would never receive mercy and as Iblis called sincere advice folly.\n\nThe tactic mirrors earlier elite speech: Salih’s arrogant questioning whether the lowly really believe, ’Ad’s chiefs calling Hud a fool, Noah’s chiefs calling him misguided, all using status to define success. Their definition of loss is worldly: loss of market share if they give just measure, loss of custom if they leave ambush on roads, loss of tribal cohesion if they abandon forefather gods. Shu‘ayb’s definition of loss is opposite: loss on the Day fulfilment, when intercessors are sought and failing gods stray, and when the barrier’s heights ask about those sworn to be denied mercy.\n\nThe verse’s placement before the earthquake is deliberate. The chiefs predict loss for followers; the next verses show who were the true losers when the shaking seized them and they were wiped out as if they had never lived there.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates following a trustworthy messenger with worldly loss. The Quran shows following Shu‘ayb, who conveyed the Lord’s messages and gave sincere advice, is precisely the fertile soil that produces abundantly and is saved by mercy, while following chiefs who lie in ambush and make the Way seem crooked is the path whose end is being among the ghabireen. The next two verses will confirm the reversal: those who rejected Shu‘ayb were the losers, not his followers, teaching listeners to judge loss by the just weighing, not by chiefs’ threat.",
  "91": "“Then an ˹overwhelming˺ earthquake struck them and they fell lifeless in their homes” is the divine answer to the threat that followers of Shu‘ayb would be losers and to the demand for hastened judgment. The previous verse left the disbelieving chiefs warning of loss and earlier threatening expulsion. Now the appointed term that cannot be delayed or advanced arrives. The phrase “Then an overwhelming earthquake struck them,” or “fa-akhathathum al-rajfah,” is the same rajfah that struck Thamud after killing the she-camel, a violent shaking that seized the established town of Midian, known for its trade and measure. For a people whose sin was shaking the market’s balance with short measure and shaking the roads with ambush, the earth’s shaking is fitting justice. The Lord whose creation and command are His, who makes night and day overlap, commands the earth as He subjects sun, moon, and stars.\n\nThe result, “and they fell lifeless in their homes,” or “fa-asbahoo fi darihim jathimeen,” repeats the wording for Thamud, corpses fallen, kneeling, humbled in the dwellings they claimed as “our land” from which they would expel believers. The homes where they built ambush and counted increase in numbers became the place of stillness, as earlier societies were seized at night or midday while asleep or at play, and as the drowned and the brimstone-rained were overtaken.\n\nThis outcome validates Shu‘ayb’s patient appeal to the best of judges and his trust in the Lord who encompasses everything in knowledge. The best judge judged with truth, distinguishing the fertile from the infertile, the thankful who varied lessons produce abundantly from those whose land hardly produces.\n\nA common error is to see such punishment as random nature. The verse links it to the term for each community and to spreading corruption after order, especially economic corruption and hindering the Path, for which the end of corruptors was to be considered. For the Meccan traders who also controlled measures and roads, the earthquake is a pointed warning: just as winds usher mercy that can become brimstone, the earth that is residence and provision for an appointed stay can be made to shake when just measure and non-corruption are persistently refused, leaving the threat of losers inverted onto the threateners.",
  "92": "“Those who rejected Shu‘aib were ˹wiped out˺ as if they had never lived there. Those who rejected Shu‘aib were the true losers” drives home the reversal of the chiefs’ claim that followers would be losers. The previous verse showed the earthquake left them lifeless in their homes; this verse reflects on what that lifelessness means historically. The phrase “as if they had never lived there,” or “ka-an lam yaghnaw fiha,” uses “yaghna,” to dwell richly, to flourish, to thrive. Midian had flourished: increased from few to many, established in land, busy markets, roads ambushed for gain. After the rajfah, it was as if they had never thrived, never built, never traded, their prosperity erased like a plant hardly produced. The same expression was used for Thamud elsewhere, and it echoes that the richly adorned societies destroyed at night or midday were wiped as if they had not been.\n\nThe verdict is then doubled for emphasis: “Those who rejected Shu‘aib were the true losers,” or “allatheena kathaboo Shu‘aiban kanoo hum al-khasireen,” not those who followed him as threatened, but the rejecters themselves. “Khasiroon” here is not mere bankruptcy but self-dooming for wrongfully denying signs, light scales that doom selves, the same loss threatened for those who would follow Shu‘ayb but now shown to belong to those who rejected him. The repetition, naming the rejecters twice, seals that the loser label belongs where the best judge placed it.\n\nThis double line connects to the earlier double story pattern: those who rejected Noah were blind and drowned, those who denied Hud were uprooted, Thamud were left lifeless, Lot’s wife remained among the doomed, and now Midian’s rejecters are as if never there, while the saved were those with him by mercy.\n\nA misunderstood idea is to equate present flourishing with immunity from being wiped. The verse shows flourishing itself is a favour whose gratitude is measured by just measure and non-ambush; without it, flourishing is precisely what is wiped, so no trace of “we were powerful traders” remains to protect. The next verse will show Shu‘ayb turning away, asking how he can grieve for a people who chose disbelief, confirming that losers are not pitiable as victims but accountable as choosers of the infertile refusal to be thankful.",
  "93": "“He turned away from them, saying, ‘O my people! Indeed, I have delivered to you the messages of my Lord and gave you ˹sincere˺ advice. How can I then grieve for those who chose to disbelieve?’” records Shu‘ayb’s departure after his people were wiped as if they never lived there and shown to be the true losers. The pattern matches Noah, Hud, and Salih, each delivering and advising, then turning away when the term arrives. The phrase “He turned away from them,” or “fa-tawalla anhum,” is the same withdrawal seen with Salih after the earthquake, indicating not hatred but completion of duty. He had already said “be patient until Allah judges” and prayed “Judge between us with truth, You are the best of judges,” and judgment has now come.\n\nHis words, “O my people! Indeed, I have delivered to you the messages of my Lord and gave you sincere advice,” or “ya qawmi laqad ablaghtu kum risalati Rabbi wa nasahtu lakum,” repeat the core prophetic resume: conveying, or “tabligh,” the messages explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, and offering nasihah, pure counsel, like the sincere advisor who seeks only the listeners’ good. The reminder is that the Book and the Bayyinah came, the straight path was made clear, the fertile-infertile parable was varied, yet the response was to reject the lowly believers, to threaten expulsion unless he returned to their faith, and to warn followers they would be losers.\n\nThe final question, “How can I then grieve for those who chose to disbelieve?” or “fa-kaifa asa ala qawmin kafireen,” is rhetorical. Grief, or “asa,” is natural for a brother-messenger, as earlier prophets grieved, but after clear delivery and sincere advice, persistent disbelief—kafiroon—makes grief misplaced, because the term was just, not delayed or advanced, and the weighing will be just. This matches Allah’s earlier comfort not to let anxiety tighten the chest regarding the Book; the messenger is not a keeper, and his reliance is on the One who encompasses everything.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to expect prophets to mourn the destroyed as if they were denied chance. Shu‘ayb’s words show chance was given through succession after former peoples, increase from few to many, and detailed reminders, but they loved not sincere advisors. For the living, the lesson is to cherish the advisor, give just measure, leave roads un-ambushed, and remember favours, so the turning away never becomes a farewell of judgment.",
  "94": "“Whenever We sent a prophet to a society, We afflicted its ˹disbelieving˺ people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled” extracts the general divine law behind the five stories just recounted from Noah to Shu‘ayb. The previous episodes each showed a people afflicted with a final seizing after rejecting their brother’s sincere advice. This verse steps back to explain the earlier, gentler phase that preceded final terms. The phrase “Whenever We sent a prophet to a society,” or “wa ma arsalna fi qaryatin min nabiyyin,” shows this is a recurring pattern, not an isolated reaction, linked to the principle that your Lord would never destroy a society while its people are unaware. The initial affliction, “We afflicted its disbelieving people with suffering and adversity,” or “akhathna ahlaha bil-ba’sa’i wa al-darra’i,” uses two terms already seen: “ba’sa,” severe hardship like famine, economic loss, defeat, and “darra,” distress like illness, bodily pain. These are the same sufferings that earlier were meant to make people humble, or “yata darra‘oon,” become low, suppliant, calling humbly and secretly.\n\nThe purpose is then stated with the same gentleness: “so perhaps they would be humbled,” or “la‘allahum yaddarra‘oon.” The particle “la‘alla” perhaps shows hope from mercy always close to the good-doers, the same mercy that sends winds ushering rain to lifeless land and that took mercy upon Himself to welcome the repentant who mend. Humbled calling, or “tadarru‘,” is the opposite of arrogance for which gates of heaven do not open.\n\nThis verse links to the later cycle where adversity is changed to prosperity until they flourish and then argue “our forefathers too had been visited by adversity and prosperity,” so We seized them by surprise while unaware. The first phase is therefore a merciful alarm, like day and night overlapping to remind of the Creator’s command.\n\nA common error is to view suffering as mere abandonment. The Quran shows even hardship is ordered, varied as lessons for those who are thankful, aiming to soften the hard soil so it might produce abundantly before the final weighing. For the Meccan audience who had enjoyed livelihood yet seldom gave thanks and who experienced drought before prosperity, the law was immediate. For any reader, the instruction remains to answer early humbling with humble secret calls, not with the hard-chested tightening that makes punishment come at night or midday while asleep or at play.",
  "95": "“Then We changed their adversity to prosperity until they flourished and argued ˹falsely˺, ‘Our forefathers ˹too˺ had been visited by adversity and prosperity.’ So We seized them by surprise, while they were unaware” continues the divine law after the first phase of suffering meant to humble. The previous verse said societies were afflicted with ba’sa and darra so perhaps they would become humbled. This verse describes the second phase when humbling did not occur. The phrase “Then We changed their adversity to prosperity,” or “thumma baddalna makana al-sayyi’ati al-hasanata,” means the evil, hardship, was replaced with good, ease, abundance. The word change is deliberate: the same hand that created and commands can swap states, as winds rotate and seasons turn. “Until they flourished,” or “hatta afaw,” indicates growth, increase in numbers, wealth, and produce, the same flourishing Midian enjoyed from few to many and ’Ad in stature, but without thankfulness the fertile land produces differently than the thankful.\n\nTheir interpretation was then twisted: “they argued falsely, ‘Our forefathers too had been visited by adversity and prosperity,’” or “qaloo qad massa aba’ana al-darra’u wa al-sarra’u.” “Massa” touch, meaning our state is just a natural cycle that touched ancestors too, not a sign from the All-Wise. This is assumption and beautified deception, the same reasoning that made the chiefs call Hud a fool and the Midian chiefs call followers losers. By naturalizing the sign, they neutralized its humbling purpose and made their deeds appealing again.\n\nThe consequence, “So We seized them by surprise, while they were unaware,” or “fa-akhathnahum baghtatan wa hum la yash‘uroon,” repeats the baghtah, suddenness, that took societies at night or midday, the Hour that takes losers by surprise with burdens on backs, and the Hour that when it comes belief no longer benefits. “While they were unaware,” or “la yash‘uroon,” shows heedlessness persisted despite varied lessons.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to attribute prosperity to approval and adversity to chance. The verse shows both are tests varied for the thankful, requiring humble secret calling with hope and fear after hardship and grateful stewardship after ease. The next verses declare had they been faithful and mindful We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth, but disbelief led to seizure for what they earned, proving that the rotation from adversity to prosperity is meant to lead to mindfulness, not to the argument that history merely cycles without a Judge.",
  "96": "“Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth. But they disbelieved, so We seized them for what they used to commit” states the alternate history that could have been for every destroyed society from Noah to Midian, revealing the full generosity behind the affliction-prosperity cycle. The condition, “Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful,” or “wa law anna ahla al-qura amanoo wa ittaqaw,” pairs belief, or “iman,” without tarnish, with taqwa, mindful guarding like the best clothing, the same pair that makes scales heavy and that earlier made the fertile land produce abundantly by the Lord’s leave. The promised result, “We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth,” or “la-fatahna alaihim barakatin mina al-sama’i wal-ardi,” uses “fatahna,” opened, pour, as rain once poured as mercy producing every fruit and as winds were driven to lifeless land. “Barakat,” blessings, means growth, continuity, increase that remains, unlike the fleeting play of deluding worldly life. From heaven, timely rain and protection; from earth, gardens trellised and untrellised, palms, olives, pomegranates, and cattle fit for labour.\n\nThe actual history is then given: “But they disbelieved, so We seized them for what they used to commit,” or “wa lakin kathaboo fa-akhathnahum bima kanu yaksiboon.” “Kathaboo” denied signs and messengers who had delivered and given sincere advice, and “bima kanu yaksiboon” for what they used to earn, linking to ranks according to deeds and earning sin open and secret.\n\nThis counterfactual shows that seizure was not inevitable but chosen by response. The same wind that can usher mercy can be left to bring barrenness when thankfulness is replaced by “our forefathers also had adversity and prosperity,” the assumption that neutralizes signs.\n\nA common error is to think divine blessings are random favor to the powerful. The verse teaches blessings are constrained to faith and mindfulness, which turn hard soil into fertile by thankfulness that varies lessons abundantly, while disbelief, even amid palaces carved into mountains, leads to being as if never there. The next three verses then ask, did societies feel secure that Our punishment would not come by night while asleep, by day while at play, or that they are safe from Allah’s planning, none feel secure except losers, urging the thankful to keep scales heavy before the term arrives.",
  "97": "“Did the people of those societies feel secure that Our punishment would not come upon them by night while they were asleep?” is the first of three probing questions that follow the counterfactual about blessings from heaven and earth. The previous verse said had those peoples been faithful and mindful We would have overwhelmed them with blessings, but they disbelieved and were seized. This question turns to present listeners who remain. The phrase “Did the people of those societies feel secure,” or “a-fa-amina ahlu al-qura,” asks whether they felt safe, guarded, free from fear, as earlier societies felt when they took faith as play and were deluded. The specific scenario, “that Our punishment would not come upon them by night while they were asleep,” or “an ya’tiyahum ba’suna bayatan wa hum na’imoon,” recalls exactly how overwhelming punishments were said to come: “Our torment took them at night or midday,” and Thamud and Midian were taken where they fell lifeless in their homes while sleeping. Night sleep is a time of trust, as night was made for rest and for the soul to be called back, a mercy seldom thanked. Feeling secure in that trust while persisting in denial, arrogance, defrauding measure, and ambushing roads mistakes mercy for immunity.\n\nThe question is rhetorical, expecting the answer “They should not feel secure,” and it links to the broader warning that every community has an appointed term that when it comes cannot be delayed or advanced, and that the fulfilment awaited by deniers, when it arrives, leaves only self-ruin and vanished fabrications.\n\nA common self-deception is to think punishment, if it comes, will give warning time to react. Night surprise while asleep shows no time is given after the door closes, just as belief after the sun rises from the west would not benefit. The next verses ask the same about coming by day while at play and about security from Allah’s planning, tightening the circle. For the living, the answer is to take the night itself as a reminder: each sleep is a minor death where souls are called back; waking is a revival to complete an appointed term, an opportunity to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear rather than to sleep securely while persisting in corruption after order, before the night’s mercy becomes night’s seizure.",
  "98": "“Or did they feel secure that Our punishment would not come upon them by day while they were at play?” continues the series of security questions after asking whether societies felt secure from punishment by night while asleep. The previous question painted nighttime vulnerability; this one paints daytime distraction. The phrase “by day while they were at play,” or “duhan wa hum yal‘aboon,” uses “duha,” forenoon, the bright part of day when markets are busy, caravans move, and people are absorbed in activity, and “yal‘aboon,” at play, engaged in amusements that treat serious faith as mere play, as earlier those who took faith as amusement and play were ignored as they ignored the meeting. The same root described worldly life as play and amusement and the midday “qa’iloona” rest that could also be seized.\n\nThe question, “Or did they feel secure,” or “awa amina,” again expects no; no people whose messengers conveyed and advised should feel secure while continuing to deny signs with arrogance, forbid what Allah permitted, or spread corruption after order. Daytime play is just as exposed as nighttime sleep. The wind that brings mercy and then produces every fruit can be commanded to bring shaking by day; the sun and moon subjected by His command mark time without protecting transgressors when the term arrives.\n\nThis pairing, night asleep and day at play, covers the full 24-hour cycle of human activity already described as “to Him belongs whatever exists in day and night” and “He knows what you do by day and calls back souls by night.” No hour is outside the creation and command that belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds. Feeling secure in either hour mistakes the provision of livelihood and the establishment on earth for entitlement, the hardly-thanks noted after establishment.\n\nA common illusion today is to think busy daytime achievement equals safety, that commerce at play can outpace accountability. The Quran says the same market whose measure must be given justly can be the scene of seizure if measure is defrauded and roads are ambushed, and the same plain where palaces are built can shake. The next verse asks the ultimate question, did they feel secure from Allah’s planning, none feels secure except losers, showing that security from punishment while persisting in taking devils as masters thinking they are guided is itself the mark of loss before the final barrier.",
  "99": "“Did they feel secure against Allah’s planning? None would feel secure from Allah’s planning except the losers” condenses the two previous night-and-day questions into their theological root and gives the verdict. After asking whether peoples felt secure from punishment by night while asleep or by day while at play, the Quran now names the deeper security they assumed: “against Allah’s planning,” or “makra Allah.” “Makr” here means subtle, intricate planning, the encompassing, all-aware arrangement that can shift adversity to prosperity, then seize by surprise while unaware, as with societies that flourished after hardship and said forefathers also saw cycles. It is the planning that made every prophet’s enemies devilish humans and jinn whispering elegant deception so hearts that disbelieve in the Hereafter incline, and that made the wicked allies of one another because of misdeeds, yet allowed the winds to usher mercy before rain that also proves resurrection. Allah’s planning is not deceit as human makr can be; it is the perfected, unseen governance that brings every matter to its mustaqarr, its set place.\n\nThe answer is stark: “None would feel secure from Allah’s planning except the losers,” or “fa-la ya’manu makra Allahi illa al-qawmu al-khasiroon.” “Khasiroon” are the losers earlier defined as those who denied the meeting until the Hour surprised them with burdens on backs, whose scales are light and who doom themselves, who were wiped as if never there. To feel secure while taking faith as play, hindering the Way, and making it seem crooked, is itself the sign of loss, as earlier said none feel secure from Allah’s planning except losers.\n\nThis verse balances the earlier security question about Iblis’s delayed end and the best judge’s patience. Security from planning while persisting in corruption after order mistakes patience for approval, a mistake the next verse warns successors not to make: is it not clear to those who take over the land after its former residents that if We will We can punish them too and seal hearts so they will not hear?\n\nA common misunderstanding is to read “fear Allah’s planning” as despair. The Quran joins it with the opposite virtue from earlier, calling humbly with hope and fear and knowing mercy is close to good-doers. Feeling secure while spreading corruption is loser’s logic; fearing the subtle plan while doing good is the fertile soil’s wisdom that remembers favours after succession, varies lessons thankfully, and stays mindful before the day when fulfilment leaves only self-ruin and strayed fabrications.",
  "100": "“Is it not clear to those who take over the land after ˹the destruction of˺ its former residents that—if We will—We can punish them ˹too˺ for their sins and seal their hearts so they will not hear ˹the truth˺?” turns from the destroyed to their successors, making the land itself a lesson, after warning that only losers feel secure from Allah’s planning. The previous episodes ended with societies seized at night or midday and the question whether successors feel secure; this verse answers by making succession the proof. The phrase “Is it not clear to those who take over the land after its former residents,” or “awa lam yahdi lillatheena yarithoona al-arda min ba‘di ahliha,” means has guidance, or “huda,” not reached, not become plain, to the heirs who inherit, or “yarithoona,” the earth after the destroyed? They see empty palaces, carved homes where the fallen became lifeless, markets where measure was cheated now silent, just as Midian and Thamud and ’Ad and Noah’s people left traces. Succession, like being made successors after Noah and after ’Ad, should teach, as fertile land remembers, that the same establishment can be the next term.\n\nThe threat is twofold: “if We will, We can punish them too for their sins,” or “an law nasha’u asabnahum bi-thunoobihim,” punishment for sins, like forebears’ seizure by earthquake, flood, or brimstone, and “and seal their hearts so they will not hear the truth,” or “wa natba‘u ala quloobihim fa-hum la yasma‘oon.” Sealing, or “tab‘,” is the consequence of refusing to believe at first, as earlier We turned hearts and eyes as they refused to believe at first, leaving them to wander, and as earlier gates of heaven do not open for the arrogant. A sealed heart cannot hear the reminder though it comes through one of your own, though the Book is a guide and mercy, though winds herald rain.\n\nThis verse links creation-and-command to succession-and-seal. The same Lord who established on the Throne and subjects sun and moon controls inheritance of land and openness of hearts. The fertile-infertile metaphor appears again: inheritors can be thankful and produce abundantly, or remain infertile, hardly producing, until punishment comes by night or day.\n\nA common error among successors is to think new possession equals new immunity, that because we built anew we will not be shaken. The verse says succession is not immunity but test, as earlier increase from few to many was a test. Is it not clear, then, to look at the ruins, to give just measure, not to lie in ambush, to call humbly and secretly, before the seal makes the varied lessons no longer heard.",
  "101": "“We have narrated to you ˹O Prophet˺ some of the stories of those societies. Surely, their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but still they would not believe in what they had already denied. This is how Allah seals the hearts of the disbelievers” summarizes the five narratives from Noah to Shu‘ayb and gives their theological conclusion, after asking whether successors are guided by the ruins. The opening, “We have narrated to you some of the stories of those societies,” or “tilka al-qura naqussu alaika min anba’iha,” shows the stories, or “anba’,” tidings, are selection, not exhaustive, told to the Prophet who was told not to let anxiety tighten his chest regarding the Book. The stories are the empirical “see the end” command fulfilled through revelation when travel is not possible, allowing the blind heart to see with sure knowledge though We were never absent.\n\nThe next sentence explains their stubbornness: “Surely, their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but still they would not believe in what they had already denied,” or “wa laqad ja’athum rusuluhum bil-bayyinati fa-ma kanoo li-yu’minoo bima kathaboo min qabl.” Their denial was not for lack of bayyinah; Salih had the she-camel, Shu‘ayb had just measure, Lot had scolding, Hud had reminder of stature, Noah had Ark, yet they would not believe in what they had already denied, meaning their earlier refusal hardened into inability, like the infertile land that hardly produces. The phrase connects to the previous sealing threat: after refusing at first, hearts and eyes are turned, leaving them to wander.\n\nThe verdict then states the law: “This is how Allah seals the hearts of the disbelievers,” or “kathalika yatba‘u Allahu ala quloobi al-kafireen.” Sealing is not arbitrary but the consequence of persistent denial with arrogance for which gates do not open, taking devils as masters thinking they are guided, and making misdeeds appealing. It is the same sealing mentioned for successors who will not hear if they follow forefathers’ cycle argument.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view sealing as preventing belief despite desire. The verse shows sequence: clear proofs came, they refused what they had already denied, then sealing followed. The next verses will note We did not find most of them true to covenant and most were rebellious, and then will send Moses to Pharaoh as a new, larger-scale example of the same pattern, inviting successors not to repeat the hard-soil that, despite winds heralding mercy, produced only the rain of punishment.",
  "102": "“We did not find most of them true to their covenant. Rather, We found most of them truly rebellious” gives the divine assessment after summarizing the societies whose stories were narrated and whose hearts were sealed like the disbelievers. The previous verse said messengers came with clear proofs but they would not believe in what they had already denied. This verse explains why that refusal was deep, not momentary. The phrase “We did not find most of them true to their covenant,” or “wa ma wajadna li-aktharihim min ahdin,” refers to the primordial covenant taken from children of Adam, the pledge to worship Allah alone and to follow His command, and to the specific covenants each community made with its messenger when warned, like promising not to harm the she-camel or not to defraud measure. “Ahd” means binding promise. Not finding them true to it means their practice did not match their pledge, as earlier they took faith as play, hindered the Way, and attributed to Allah what they do not know.\n\nThe counterpart, “Rather, We found most of them truly rebellious,” or “wa in wajadna aktharahum la-fasiqeen,” uses “fasiqeen,” those who step out, break out, as earlier “those who deny Our signs will be afflicted for rebelliousness.” Rebellion here is persisting in corruption after order, like lying in ambush on every road and striving to make the path seem crooked, like expelling the chaste, like defrauding property.\n\nThis diagnosis links to the fertile-barren parable: most soils proved infertile, hardly producing, despite winds and rains varied for the thankful. It also links to the security questions: most felt secure from Allah’s planning as losers do, instead of calling humbly with hope and fear. The statement “most of them” shows the prophetic pattern where saving is by mercy for a few and uprooting for the denying majority, yet each community’s term still showcases mercy close to good-doers.\n\nA common error is to think covenant is only historical. The Quran shows covenant is renewed with each reminder through one of your own, each time the Book explained with knowledge arrives as guide and mercy. Breaking it by following elegant deception makes hearts sealed, as earlier We turned hearts as they refused first, ensuring they will not hear. The text therefore prepares for Moses and Pharaoh, where the clash between covenant-keeping and rebellion becomes an empire-wide manifestation, and invites the successor who inherits land after its former residents to be among the few who remain true to covenant, giving just measure and not spreading corruption, before the appointed term that cannot be delayed arrives.",
  "103": "“Then after them We sent Moses with Our signs to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they wrongfully rejected them. See what was the end of the corruptors!” begins the transition from the five Arabian societies to the archetypal empire, continuing the covenant-rebellion theme that just concluded with most found rebellious. The phrase “Then after them,” or “thumma ba‘athna min ba‘dihim Moosa,” places Moses chronologically after Noah, Hud, Salih, Lot, and Shu‘ayb, showing the straight message persists across regions. The mission, “with Our signs to Pharaoh and his chiefs,” or “bi-ayatina ila Fir‘awna wa mala’ihi,” uses “ayat,” signs, not merely words, because Moses came with staff and shining hand from the Lord of all worlds, tangible bayyinah like the she-camel, but on a scale that confronted a throne. Pharaoh and his chiefs, or “mala’,” are the ultimate chiefs, whose large numbers and arrogance earlier proved useless and whose gates will remain closed for arrogance.\n\nThe response, “but they wrongfully rejected them,” or “fa-zalamo biha,” means they did wrong with the signs, treated them unjustly, as earlier “zalimoo” for denying signs made scales light. Their wrong included calling skilled magic what was clear truth, seeking to drive Moses from the land, and later threatening losers who follow him, mirroring earlier chiefs who called Hud a fool and threatened expulsion.\n\nThe command that follows, “See what was the end of the corruptors!” or “fa-unzur kaifa kana aqibatu al-mufsideen,” repeats the empirical call given after Lot’s rain and after Shu‘ayb’s ambush warning. Here the corruptors, or “mufsideen,” are those who spread corruption after order, the exact title given to Midian and to the warning not to spread corruption after the land was set in order. Seeing the end of Pharaoh’s corruptors will be the Red Sea, the drowned army as with Noah’s flood, confirming that every community’s term, empires included, cannot be delayed.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think Pharaoh’s story is merely political. The verse frames it as the same spiritual test: a clear proof from the Lord who created heavens and earth in six Days and subjected sun, moon, and stars arrives; chiefs assess by status; the wrongfully rejected sign becomes the measure of being among the wicked whose bed is Hell or among those saved by mercy whose rivers flow. For successors inheriting land, the sign-herald’s call is to look and choose the fertile response before the sea of fulfilment pours.",
  "104": "“And Moses said, ‘O Pharaoh! I am truly a messenger from the Lord of all worlds’” records Moses’ opening address to the ultimate chief, echoing the brothers’ openings to their peoples and lifting the claim to the highest universal authority. The previous verse said Moses was sent with Our signs to Pharaoh and his chiefs but they wrongfully rejected them. Now the dialogue begins. Moses says, “O Pharaoh! I am truly a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,” or “ya Fir‘awna inni rasoolun min Rabbi al-alameen.” The title “Lord of all worlds,” or “Rabb al-alameen,” recalls Noah’s “I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,” Hud’s same, and the opening praise “Blessed is Allah—Lord of all worlds” after the creation verse, and the throne verse. It asserts that his authority outranks Pharaoh’s claimed lordship over Egypt, because the One who created heavens and earth in six Days and subjects sun and moon is Lord over the very throne Pharaoh sits on and over the rivers he boasts.\n\nThe opening “O Pharaoh” is direct, respectful in address yet firm in hierarchy, as brother-messengers said “O my people,” but here the brotherhood is human rather than tribal, since Moses was raised in Pharaoh’s house yet stands as warner. His self-identification mirrors Noah, Hud, and Salih’s “I am a messenger,” but adds “truly,” or “inna,” emphasizing truthfulness, needed because Pharaoh will immediately call him a skilled magician and a liar, the same liar label chiefs gave Hud.\n\nThe statement also sets up the obligation in the next verse: to say nothing about Allah except the truth and to have come with clear proof so let the Children of Israel go. The pairing shows that being Lord of all worlds is not abstract theology but entails that creation and command belong to Him alone, so Pharaoh’s command that enslaves and corrupts after order is illegitimate.\n\nA common error is to view Moses’ claim as political rebellion alone. The Quran frames it as the same worship-alone message: let His people worship their true Lord instead of the self-declared lord who spreads corruption. For the Meccan audience who heard “Will you not then fear Him?” and “Are you certain Salih is sent,” Moses’ universal Lord claim re-centers fear where it belongs: not before numbers and arrogance that are of no use, but before the Lord whose punishment comes by night while asleep or by day while at play and whose mercy is close to the good-doers who call humbly and secretly.",
  "105": "“obliged to say nothing about Allah except the truth. Indeed, I have come to you with clear proof from your Lord, so let the children of Israel go with me” continues Moses’ address, defining the messenger’s duty and demand. The previous verse gave his identity as messenger from the Lord of all worlds. Now he describes the ethic of that office: “obliged to say nothing about Allah except the truth,” or “haqeequn ala an la aqoola ala Allahi illa al-haqq.” “Haqeeq” means it is incumbent, proper, necessary for one carrying signs from the Lord who perfected His Word in truth and justice to speak only truth. This contrasts with fabricating lies against Allah, attributing shame, taking other guardians, and speaking without knowledge, the core wrong for which every torn community was seized. Moses’ fidelity is the same as Noah, Hud, Salih, and Shu‘ayb conveying messages and giving sincere advice, knowing from Allah what others do not.\n\nThe proof is then presented: “Indeed, I have come to you with clear proof from your Lord,” or “qad ji’tukum bi-bayyinatin min Rabbikum,” the same bayyinah sent to Thamud as a she-camel and to Midian as just measure, but here staff and shining hand suited to a magician-proud court. “From your Lord” emphasizes the Lord is Pharaoh’s Lord too, though Pharaoh claims lordship, reinforcing that creation and command belong to the blessed Lord, not to the throne’s occupant.\n\nThe demand follows, “so let the children of Israel go with me,” or “fa-arsil ma‘iya Bani Isra’eel.” The Children of Israel, the successors after former residents, were enslaved, the same oppression theme as the poor believers dismissed and Salih’s lowly questioned. Releasing them corrects corruption after order and defrauding of people’s property on a national scale, merging social justice with monotheism.\n\nThe verse links to earlier release patterns: Noah’s Ark saved the believing few, Lot’s family were saved except the wife among the doomed, Shu‘ayb’s followers were threatened with expulsion, all now distilled into freeing an entire people.\n\nA common misunderstanding separates theology from liberation. Moses’ statement shows saying truth about Allah includes demanding justice for the oppressed, because the Lord whose mercy is close to good-doers does not like transgressors who spread corruption. The next verse will show Pharaoh’s reply, “If you have come with a sign, bring it if you are truthful,” mirroring the hastening challenge of earlier deniers who wanted signs on demand, setting the stage for the staff to become a snake that the chiefs will call magic.",
  "106": "“Pharaoh said, ‘If you have come with a sign, then bring it if what you say is true’” records Pharaoh’s response after Moses claimed to be a messenger obliged to say nothing but truth and to have come with clear proof so the Children of Israel should be released. The demand, “If you have come with a sign, then bring it if what you say is true,” or “in kunta ji’ta bi-ayatin fa’ti biha in kunta min al-sadiqeen,” mirrors exactly the challenge of ’Ad and Thamud, “Bring us what you threaten if you are truthful,” and the Meccan demand “If you have come with a sign bring it,” all treating a sign as a performance to be produced on cue rather than as a bayyinah to be recognized with humble calling. The conditional “if you are truthful” uses the same word “sadiqeen” previously denied to Hud and Salih as “liar,” flipping the burden: the truthful messenger must prove by spectacle before Pharaoh will acknowledge the Lord of all worlds.\n\nPharaoh’s request links to the broader theme of awaiting fulfilment as validation. Earlier the deniers said “We will not believe until we receive what messengers received,” and awaited angels or the Lord’s signs in a way that belief after signs would not benefit. Pharaoh now embodies that stance at imperial scale, assuming the throne can judge the heavens’ signs.\n\nThe next two verses will show the response: staff becomes a snake and hand shines white, signs subjected by His command like sun, moon, and stars, the creation and command that belong to Him alone. The chiefs’ immediate reinterpretation as “skilled magician” shows that even when the demanded sign arrives, the infertile eye that hardly produces calls it magic, as earlier they called messengers fool and misguided, and as devils made misdeeds appealing.\n\nA common error is to think Pharaoh’s demand is reasonable skepticism. The Quran frames it as wrongful rejection, or “zalamo biha,” after signs were wrongfully rejected, because a sign had already been implied in Moses’ truthful character, his knowledge from Allah, and the suffering of the Israelites that should have humbled. For listeners taught not to lie in ambush on every road hindering those who believe from His Path and making it seem crooked, Pharaoh’s challenge warns that demanding proof as a condition for justice and then dismissing it as trick is the crooked-making that earns being among the corruptors whose end the watcher is told to see.",
  "107": "“So Moses threw down his staff and—behold!—it became a real snake” answers Pharaoh’s demand for a sign with a sign that cannot be mistaken for trickery. The previous verse recorded Pharaoh’s challenge, “If you have come with a sign, bring it if you are truthful,” the same hastening that earlier communities used to demand punishment. Now Moses acts not by his own will but as inspired: “So Moses threw down his staff,” or “fa-alqa asahu,” the same staff he used to tend and lean upon, an ordinary object known to the court. The result, “it became a real snake,” or “fa-itha hiya thu‘banun mubeen,” stresses clarity, “mubeen,” evident, not illusion. This is not the deceptive eye-trick the magicians will later perform; it is creation and command from the One who created heavens and earth in six Days and who subjects sun, moon, and stars. The stick’s transformation into a living serpent demonstrates that the Lord who makes winds bring heavy clouds and makes dead land produce every fruit can also bring life from wood in an instant.\n\nThe link to earlier signs is direct. Thamud’s she-camel was a living sign not to be harmed; here a living serpent becomes the sign to be witnessed, both from Allah’s land and by His permission. Like the staff, the fertile land that produces abundantly versus the infertile that hardly produces will now be seen in the audience: some will see a snake and recognize truth, others will see it and call it skilled magic, as the chiefs do next.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat this as mere magic surpassing magicians. The Quran distinguishes: magicians deceived eyes and stunned people with great display, while Moses’ staff devoured the objects of their illusion, showing truth prevailing over deception. For the living, the staff-snake is also a reminder that the same hand that was forbidden to harm the she-camel and commanded to give just measure is the hand that can, by revelation, manifest what disbelievers call impossible, calling attention back to worship Allah alone and not to spread corruption after order, before the final rain that will not be a snake but a sea.",
  "108": "“Then he drew his hand ˹out of his collar˺ and it was ˹shining˺ white for all to see” follows the staff-snake with a second sign that Pharaoh’s court demanded, showing that the proof is not a single spectacle but a pair that covers different senses. The previous verse showed the staff become a real snake that moved and threatened; this verse shows Moses’ hand drawn from his garment become luminous. The Arabic says “wa naza‘a yadahu fa-itha hiya bayda’u li-nazireen,” he pulled his hand and it was white, shining for beholders. “Bayda’” means bright white, radiant without disease, opposite to the leprosy the onlookers might have suspected, and “li-nazireen” for all to see means the light was public, not hidden, like the sun’s shining that guides and the stars that all can use. Together, staff and hand cover transformation outside the body and illumination of the body itself, both subjected by His command.\n\nThe pairing recalls the creation-and-command principle: He who makes night and day overlap and subjects sun, moon, and stars can subject a staff to become a serpent and a hand to shine. Both occur by the Lord of all worlds, the same Lord Moses claimed when he said he is a messenger who says nothing but truth and who has come with clear proof so let the Children of Israel go.\n\nImmediate reaction, recorded next, is again elite dismissal: the chiefs will say “He is indeed a skilled magician who seeks to drive you from your land,” re-labeling revelation as trickery, as earlier they called Hud a fool and Salih’s sign a threat. Their seeing remains blind, like the blind people drowned with Noah, because they evaluate by assumption and elegant deception rather than by the humble secret calling that keeps mercy close to the good-doers.\n\nA common error is to view these signs as unrelated miracles. The Quran places them as back-to-back proofs of the two domains Pharaoh controlled: land and people. The staff strikes the earth’s symbols of power, the hand shows the person’s own impurity cannot be hidden. For the Israelites who were told to be patient until the best of judges judges and to seek Allah’s help, the shining hand would later contrast with the gates of heaven not opening for the arrogant, whose faces will be dust. The two signs therefore invite the same choice offered to every successor after former residents: will you be fertile land producing thankfully or infertile hardly producing when the light shines for all to see.",
  "109": "“The chiefs of Pharaoh’s people said, ‘He is indeed a skilled magician’” records the instant elite reinterpretation of the staff-snake and shining hand. The previous two verses showed Moses throwing down a staff that became a real snake and drawing a hand that shone white for all to see, both signs from the Lord who created heavens and earth and subjected celestial bodies. The response comes not from Pharaoh alone but from “the chiefs of Pharaoh’s people,” or “al-mala’u min qawmi Fir‘awn,” the same social layer that called Noah misguided, Hud a fool, Salih’s sign a threat, and Shu‘ayb’s followers losers. Their label, “He is indeed a skilled magician,” or “inna hatha la-sahirun aleem,” is the familiar strategy of renaming revelation as sorcery, as earlier they called eloquence ancient fables and later will call Moses’ staff a great display of magic that can be matched. “Aleen” implies knowledgeable, adept, implying Moses’ knowledge is mere craft, not knowledge from Allah as he claimed “I know from Allah what you do not know.”\n\nThis renaming protects their position. If Moses is a magician, there is no need to let the Children of Israel go or to give just measure after corruption, because the challenge remains within the palace’s expertise. The label also prepares the next strategy: mobilizing magicians from all cities to counter him, turning a sign of Lordship into a contest of skill.\n\nThe verse links to the larger law that those who deny signs with arrogance have gates of heaven closed until a camel passes through a needle, and that most people follow assumption and lie. Calling a prophet a skilled magician is both assumption and lie, the same pair that led societies to feel secure from Allah’s planning until punishment came at night or midday.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think calling truth magic is a neutral opinion. The Quran shows it is wrongful rejection, or “zalamo biha,” that invites being among the corruptors whose end the watcher is told to see. For the living, the test is to recognize that the Creator who sends winds ushering mercy and makes fertile land produce abundantly does not need magic to transform a staff; He whose creation and command belong to Him alone can make a lifeless land produce every fruit and can make the dead live again, so the proper response is not to label but to beware and perhaps be shown mercy, as Noah asked.",
  "110": "“who seeks to drive you from your land.’ ˹So Pharaoh asked,˺ ‘What do you propose?’” completes the chiefs’ warning about Moses and shows Pharaoh’s political turn. The previous verse quoted them calling him a skilled magician; this verse adds their motive accusation: “who seeks to drive you from your land,” or “yureedu an yukhrijakum min ardikum.” The claim is cleverly crafted to trigger tribal defense. They frame Moses’ demand to let the Children of Israel go with him not as liberation of the oppressed but as expulsion of the hosts, echoing the earlier threat to expel Shu‘ayb and his believers and the threat to expel Lot’s people for desiring chastity. Land, or “ard,” here is Allah’s land as Salih said, yet the chiefs present it as theirs, the same “our land” from which they threatened expulsion. By making the sign a land issue, they shift attention from worshipping Allah alone to protecting possessions, the same shift that made Midian’s chiefs prefer defrauding measure over just weight.\n\nThe phrase “So Pharaoh asked, ‘What do you propose?’” or “fa-matha ta’muroon,” shows Pharaoh consulting his council, the “mala’,” despite his claim to be lord. The consultation reveals dependency beneath boasts of dominance, much as his later claim to kill sons and keep women shows fear masquerading as control. This consulting also reveals the planning, or “makr,” that will gather magicians from all cities, a plan the weak may think secure but which the Quran says only losers feel secure from.\n\nThe land accusation also connects to the theme of succession: those who take over land after its former residents should see they can be punished for sins, yet here successors accuse the warner of seeking to drive them out, inverting the truth that it is their corruption that drives societies to ruin as if they had never lived there.\n\nA common error is to accept the opposition’s framing that prophets are political opportunists. The Quran shows Moses sought no dominion for himself; his demand was to free a people to worship their true Lord, the Lord of all worlds whose throne is above Pharaoh’s. For readers taught to remember when they were few then increased and to consider the fate of corruptors, the lesson is to discern when “defending the land” is actually defending corruption that should be left, and to choose the fertile path of letting the oppressed go rather than ambushing on every road to hinder them.",
  "111": "“They replied, ‘Let him and his brother wait and send mobilizers to all cities’” records the council’s counter-plan after Pharaoh asked what they propose about the skilled magician who seeks to drive them from the land. The previous verse showed chiefs reframing Moses’ liberation call as expulsion. Now they answer, “Let him and his brother wait,” or “arjih wa akhahu,” delay him and Aaron, give respite, not immediate decision, and “send mobilizers to all cities,” or “wa arsil fil-mada’ini hashireen,” dispatch gatherers to collect every clever magician. The word “hashireen” means those who gather, mobilize, assemble, the same gathering language used for the Day He will gather them all together, but here gathering is for illusion rather than truth.\n\nThis strategy reveals both fear and confidence. Fear, because they delay direct confrontation with the staff-snake and shining hand, hoping to dilute the sign’s impact with a larger spectacle. Confidence, because they trust that the state’s network can produce a “clever magician,” or “sahir aleem,” to match Moses, as earlier they called him that. The plan mirrors earlier collective strategies: Thamud’s arrogant killing the she-camel to defy the sign, Midian’s chiefs threatening loss to followers, and Pharaoh’s later claim to completely dominate by killing sons.\n\nIt also links to the theme of succession and appointed terms. Instead of giving just measure and leaving corruption, they invest in a show that will prove to be the great display that truth will devour. The delay they propose, or “arjih,” is itself a respite like the respite given to Iblis until the appointed Day, a time in which guidance is still possible but which they use to engineer hindering.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view institutional mobilization as proof of truth. The Quran shows the opposite: the truth that prevailed and illusions that failed were not decided by which side could gather more specialists from more cities. For the Meccan audience who saw Quraysh mobilize to mock the Quran as ancient fables and to call Muhammad a poet, the parallel would be clear: gathering many to embellish falsehood does not make falsehood true, and the best judge remains the One who subjected sun, moon, and stars and whose mercy is close to good-doers who call humbly and secretly rather than gathering to deceive eyes and stun people.",
  "112": "“to bring you every clever magician” is the purpose clause for the mobilizers sent to all cities. The chiefs had said let him and his brother wait and send gatherers; this verse completes the order with the target: “to bring you every clever magician,” or “ya’tooka bi-kulli sahirin aleem.” The word “sahir” is magician, one who practices “sihr,” illusion that deceives eyes, and “aleem” clever, learned, skilled, the same pair used to describe Moses as a skilled magician by his opponents. The doubling of “every” and “clever” shows they do not want mediocre tricks; they want the most adept, the ones whose great display stuns crowds.\n\nThe plan to bring every clever magician to Pharaoh’s court places the confrontation on Pharaoh’s terms, in the city, before his people, with reward promised. It echoes the gathering of devilish humans and jinn who whisper elegant words to each other to incline hearts, a gathering of deceivers to defend deception. Yet the Quran has already shown that staff and hand were not sihr but “thu‘ban mubeen,” a clear living snake, and “bayda’ li-nazireen,” shining for all to see, signs from the Lord of all worlds whose creation and command are His, not tricks that merely stun.\n\nThis verse sets up the contrast that will climax when Moses is told “You first” and their great magic is devoured by his staff. The same contrast appeared in the fertile-barren land parable: the same water, but one produces abundantly by Allah’s leave, the other hardly produces; here the same stage, but one brings truth that prevails, the other brings illusions that fail.\n\nA common error is to think truth can be outnumbered by gathering experts. The Quran shows the gatherers’ success in bringing many does not change the weighing that will be just or the hearts that will be sealed for refusing what they already denied. The next verses show the magicians’ own initial incentive is reward and closeness to Pharaoh, a worldly motive that later transforms when they see truth, illustrating that even among the gathered clever, the fertile heart can still produce abundant faith when it sees the devoured illusions and chooses the Lord of Moses and Aaron over the lord of the city.",
  "113": "“The magicians came to Pharaoh, saying, ‘Shall we receive a ˹suitable˺ reward if we prevail?’” brings the gathered clever magicians face to face with the throne, revealing their initial motive. The mobilizers brought every clever magician from all cities; now they arrive and negotiate. The question, “Shall we receive a suitable reward if we prevail?” or “inna lana la-ajran in kunna nahnu al-ghalibeen,” is frank. “Ajr” means reward, wage, payment, and their condition “if we prevail” shows confidence that reward depends on victory, like the earlier opposition that measured success by numbers and closeness to chiefs. This worldly bargaining contrasts with Moses, who said “I ask no reward, my reward is with the Lord of all worlds,” conveying Lord’s messages and giving sincere advice for no wage, and with the prophets who turned away from societies without asking for land or dominion.\n\nThe magicians’ logic mirrors Midian’s defrauding measure: seek gain, then define truth accordingly. They have not yet seen the staff or the hand; they already frame their participation as a transaction. The phrase also echoes the affluent who took faith as amusement and were deluded by worldly life, treating signs as contests where reward matters more than remembrance.\n\nThe verse connects to the broader economy of reward promised by the Quran: the good deed multiplied tenfold, the rivers beneath Paradise, the removal of bitterness, versus the bed of Hell and the rain of brimstone for the doomed. The magicians presently seek the lesser, perishable ajr from Pharaoh, whose large numbers and arrogance will soon be said to be of no use, while the greater ajr lies with the Lord whose mercy is close to good-doers.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to judge participants by skill alone, ignoring motive. The Quran highlights motive because motive explains the sudden transformation about to occur: once they see truth devour illusion, they will abandon the Pharaonic ajr for the Lord’s ajr, understanding that no earthly payment compensates for the Fire whose gates do not open for the arrogant and whose cover is flames. Their initial question therefore sets the baseline from which the later prostration and perseverance under threats of amputation and crucifixion will shine as true fertile production after being merely hired to deceive eyes.",
  "114": "“He replied, ‘Yes, and you will certainly be among those closest to me’” is Pharaoh’s answer to the magicians’ request for reward if they prevail. The previous verse showed them asking about ajr, wage; now Pharaoh adds status to wage. He says, “Yes,” confirming reward, and “you will certainly be among those closest to me,” or “wa innakum la-min al-muqarrabin.” “Muqarrabin” means those brought near, intimates with access, the inner circle that earlier surrounded chiefs who felt secure from Allah’s planning. Closeness to Pharaoh meant political proximity, protection, and prestige in a land where saying “our land” was their boast.\n\nThe offer reveals Pharaoh’s strategy for dealing with a sign from the Lord of all worlds. Instead of letting the Children of Israel go, he binds talent to the throne with double incentive: payment and proximity, much as earlier chiefs offered expulsion or called followers losers to keep them from Shu‘ayb. The closeness promised is the same closeness that later the magicians will reject when they believe in the Lord of Moses and Aaron, showing that nearness to a throne that claims “I am your lord most high” is worthless when the true Throne has been established after creating heavens and earth in six Days.\n\nThe verse also foreshadows the reversal of closeness. Currently the magicians seek closeness to Pharaoh; after the staff devours illusions, they will fall prostrating and seek closeness to Allah, whose mercy is close to good-doers who call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, and whose Paradise is close to the mindful where rivers flow.\n\nA common error is to equate proximity to power with success. The Quran repeatedly shows large numbers and arrogance are of no use when the term arrives at night while asleep or by day while at play. Pharaoh’s muqarrabin will soon be threatened with having hands and feet cut off on opposite sides and then crucified, proving that the Pharaonic closeness he offered was itself a path to being among the doomed, while the true closeness is to the One whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds, whose weighing will be just and whose gates open only to the not-arrogant.",
  "115": "“They asked, ‘O Moses! Will you cast, or shall we be the first to cast?’” opens the contest proper after negotiations about reward and closeness. The magicians, gathered from all cities and promised wage and proximity, now address Moses with courtesy that contrasts with Pharaoh’s threats. The question, “O Moses! Will you cast, or shall we be the first to cast?” or “ya Moosa imma an tulqiya wa imma an nakoona nahnu al-mulqeen,” offers choice of order, a formal politeness that appears in the court where skill is displayed. The verb “alqaa,” to throw, to cast, links directly to Moses’ earlier throwing of the staff that became a real snake and to the magicians’ upcoming throwing that will deceive eyes.\n\nThe choice matters because it establishes fairness and witness. Had Moses been forced to go first, the later claim that he merely copied could arise. By letting them go first, the deception is displayed fully before truth devours it, ensuring the distinction is unmistakable, as earlier truth prevailed and illusions failed. The same fairness was shown when Hud said “Then wait, I too am waiting,” and Shu‘ayb said “be patient until Allah judges,” letting the appointed term clarify.\n\nThe magicians’ deference also hints at their professionalism: they want orderly display, confident that their great magic that stunned people and deceived eyes will prevail, like fertile land expected to produce. Their confidence mirrors Midian’s flourishing before saying forefathers also saw adversity and prosperity and being seized while unaware.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view courtesy as faith. At this moment courtesy is etiquette, not belief; belief will come only after seeing the staff devour illusions and recognizing the Lord of all worlds behind it. For listeners taught to call humbly and secretly without transgressing and to give just measure without defrauding, the verse teaches that even contests framed politely can be theatres where truth and illusion are tested, and that the best response, as Moses will show by saying “You first,” is confidence in the Lord whose day and night succeed rapidly and whose mercy waits to be shown to those who, when the illusion is exposed, will fall prostrating rather than persist in arrogance.",
  "116": "“Moses said, ‘You first.’ So when they did, they deceived the eyes of the people, stunned them, and made a great display of magic” shows the choice Moses makes and the effect of the magicians’ throw. The previous verse offered Moses the option to cast first; this verse records his answer: “Moses said, ‘You first,’” or “qala alqoo,” he said cast. The verb “alqoo” matches the magicians’ and Moses’ throwing, emphasizing parallel actions with opposite natures. By letting them begin, Moses permits the full weight of human illusion to be felt before divine inspiration, “Throw down your staff and it devoured objects of illusion,” demonstrates the difference between deceiving eyes and devouring illusions, much as earlier the cloud heavy with mercy made the dead land fruitful while the same rain could become brimstone for the corrupt.\n\nThe impact is described in three stages: “they deceived the eyes of the people,” or “saharo a‘yuna al-nas,” they bewitched eyes, making a rope appear as a snake through sleight; “stunned them,” or “wa istarhaboohum,” they struck awe, fear, overwhelming spectacle, as later the people will be stunned; and “made a great display of magic,” or “wa ja’oo bi-sihrin azeem,” a mighty sihr, the same term earlier used for calling Moses a skilled magician. The phrase “great” acknowledges capability without conceding truth. Their magic was indeed great as deception, as the land’s infertile produce can seem great until compared with abundant harvest by the Lord’s leave.\n\nThis triple effect explains why many would be inclined to feel secure from Allah’s planning as losers do, trusting the stun as proof. Yet the next verses will show the same crowd, when truth prevails, sees illusions fail and the deceivers themselves fall prostrate, proving that eye-deception cannot withstand the encompassing knowledge that was never absent and that knows what breasts conceal.\n\nA common error equates being stunned with being convinced. The crowd was stunned, but stun is not guidance; guidance comes from following what was sent down with knowledge and not taking other guardians. The verse therefore prepares the reversal: the great magic that deceived eyes will be devoured, the stunned will be replaced by those who prostrate, and the wages sought from Pharaoh will be exchanged for perseverance in faith under threat of cutting hands and feet, showing that truth’s humble secret calling with hope and fear ultimately outlasts the great display.",
  "117": "“Then We inspired Moses, ‘Throw down your staff,’ and— behold!—it devoured the objects of their illusion!” is the turning point where inspiration answers deception. The previous verse showed the magicians deceiving eyes, stunning people, and making a great display. Now divine command intervenes: “Then We inspired Moses, ‘Throw down your staff,’” or “wa awhaina ila Moosa an alqi asaka.” “Awha” means inspired, revealed, spoken to the heart, the same wahi that conveys Lord’s messages and sincere advice through Moses and earlier prophets. Moses had already thrown his staff once to show Pharaoh a real snake and a shining hand; now the same staff is commanded again, but this time its purpose is not to present a sign for belief but to annihilate the great magic just displayed.\n\nThe result is immediate and decisive: “it devoured the objects of their illusion,” or “fa-itha hiya talqafu ma ya’fikoon.” “Talqafu” means to swallow quickly, to gulp down, and “ma ya’fikoon” what they fabricate, what they falsely turn from truth, the same root as “ifk,” lie, used for fabricating lies against Allah, the greatest wrong whose bed is Hell. The staff that was once an ordinary stick becomes an active devourer, not merely a competing snake, showing the difference between creation and deceit: one is subjected by His command and truly eats, the other merely appears to crawl for eyes.\n\nThis devouring mirrors larger patterns: the fertile land devouring hardly-produced weeds, the wind-driven rain producing every fruit devouring barrenness, the truth prevailing and illusions failing as the next verse states. It also echoes the later promise that if people had been faithful We would have opened blessings from heaven and earth, but disbelief led to seizure; here lies are seized and devoured.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to see Moses’ staff as equal magic, only stronger. The Quran stresses difference in source: their objects are “ifk,” lies, while his staff acts by wahi from the Lord whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds. For those taught to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear and to consider the end of corruptors, this devouring is the visible end of corruption’s claim, inviting the stunned eyes to see with the heart, as the magicians immediately will, falling prostrate to the Lord of all worlds despite threats of cutting and crucifixion.",
  "118": "“So the truth prevailed and their illusions failed” states the verdict after the staff devoured the fabrications, distilling the contest into a timeless principle. The previous verse described inspiration to throw the staff that swallowed what they fabricated; this verse gives the assessment: “So the truth prevailed,” or “fa-waqa‘a al-haqq,” the truth occurred, established, fell into place as something firm, like the day and night that overlap in rapid succession by His command. “Al-haqq” is not merely correct opinion but the Real that corresponds to the Lord whose Word is perfected, the same Truth whose creation and command are His and who established Himself on the Throne. “And their illusions failed,” or “wa batala ma kanoo ya‘maloon,” their deeds became batil, null, void, as earlier “illusions failed” will repeat and as earlier deeds beautified became self-ruin. “Ma kanoo ya‘maloon,” what they were doing, covers the deception of eyes, the stunning, the great display, and the expectation of reward and closeness to Pharaoh.\n\nThe pairing captures the hour’s weighing: heavy truth versus light illusions, fertile land producing abundantly versus infertile hardly producing. The same phrasing will soon be used to describe the basket of the defeated: they were defeated there and put to shame, the truth’s prevalence bringing humiliation to the hinders.\n\nThis verse links to the broader history of varied lessons. Just as Noah’s Ark truth prevailed over the flood of denial, Hud’s mercy over ’Ad’s uprooting, Salih’s she-camel truth over the earthquake, Lot’s chastity over the rain of brimstone, and Shu‘ayb’s just measure over the shaking, so here Moses’ truth prevails over the great magic that made crowds feel secure. The pattern is that every community has an appointed term where truth’s occurrence and nullification of falsehood become visible, though the term for each differs in form.\n\nA common mistake is to think prevailing is temporary. The Quran presents it as established, “waqa‘a,” having occurred and remaining, just as the bliss of Paradise where bitterness is removed and rivers flow remains. For the listener who awaits fulfilment, the verse assures that when the Book explained with knowledge arrives, its truth will similarly occur and all elegant deception that inclined hearts will become batil, inviting the wise to side with the prevailing truth now through humble secret calling before the Day fulfilment leaves only self-ruin.",
  "119": "“So Pharaoh and his people were defeated right there and put to shame” describes the immediate humiliation of the throne after truth prevailed and illusions failed. The contest had been arranged by mobilizers from all cities, with every clever magician gathered, rewarded, and brought near, deceiving eyes and stunning people with great magic, then devoured by the staff. Now the assessment is political and moral: “So Pharaoh and his people were defeated right there,” or “fa-ghuliboo hunalika,” they were overcome on that spot, in that very assembly where they expected to drive Moses from the land and be declared victors. The word “ghuliboo” means overpowered, vanquished, the same root as the cheese of victory promised to magicians if they prevailed but now applied to the throne itself. “And put to shame,” or “wa inqalaboo saghireen,” they returned as “saghiroon,” small, low, disgraced, the same “saghira” used for Iblis expelled as one of the disgraced and for the arrogant whose gates would not open. The defeat was not delayed to the appointed term by night or day; it was instant shame in the place of pride.\n\nThe verse connects to earlier shaming scenes: tyrants on the Heights told their numbers and arrogance are of no use, the arrogant chiefs of Thamud and Midian told their large numbers and palaces were as if never there, all examples of large gatherings made small. It also echoes that creation and command belong to Him, so Pharaoh’s command to dominate by killing sons and keeping women is now shown as hollow before the true Command.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates defeat with mere loss of contest. Here defeat is transformation of status: from those who felt secure from Allah’s planning to those exposed as losers who feel that security, from those who hindered on every road and made the Way seem crooked to those whose hindering is publicly shamed.\n\nFor the audience taught to seek Allah’s help and be patient, the land belongs to Allah and the ultimate outcome belongs to the righteous, the scene confirms that patience until the best of judges judges does indeed bring victory, but victory in this world for Moses is not yet complete until the sea, just as for Noah was the Ark and for Lot was the rain. The immediate shame foreshadows the greater drowning to come, inviting the onlookers to consider, as told to consider the fate of corruptors, before the final night surprise arrives.",
  "120": "“And the magicians fell down, prostrating” captures the most unexpected outcome of the contest, more telling than the defeat of Pharaoh. The previous verse said Pharaoh and his people were defeated right there and put to shame after the staff devoured illusions and truth prevailed. Now those hired to display the greatest magic enact the opposite of what they were hired to do. The phrase “And the magicians fell down, prostrating,” or “wa ulqiya al-saharatu sajideen,” uses the same verb “alqaa,” to throw down, as Moses threw his staff and the magicians threw their illusions. Now the magicians themselves are thrown, not by hands but by truth, falling, or “ulqiya,” into prostration, “sajideen,” the posture refused by Iblis and commanded to angels for Adam, and the posture of sincere devotion taught as “establish your faces at every place of worship.” Their fall is the human echo of the staff’s fall that devoured.\n\nThe prostration shows immediate recognition of the Lord of all worlds behind the sign. They had asked for ajr if they prevailed and were promised closeness to Pharaoh; now they abandon both for closeness to the Lord whose mercy is close to good-doers who call humbly with hope and fear. Their fall also mirrors the fertile land producing abundantly after receiving the same water that left the infertile hardly producing: the same great display that stunned crowds produced, in them, faith.\n\nThe verse links to the broader theme of guidance versus sealing. They were among those gathered to deceive, yet guidance opened their chests to Islam while Pharaoh’s remained tight as if climbing into the sky. It parallels the lowly who believed in Salih while arrogant chiefs rejected, and the humble who invoked morning and evening versus chiefs who swore they would never receive mercy.\n\nA common error is to think skill protects from submission. Here the most skilled in illusion are the first to submit, recognizing difference between eye-deception and true devouring, demonstrating that knowledge can lead to humility when not blocked by arrogance. For listeners taught that the Book was explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the magicians’ prostration models the correct response: when truth prevails, fall to it, even if the throne threatens to cut hands and feet, as Pharaoh will, because the earth belongs to Allah and the ultimate outcome belongs to the righteous who seek His help and are patient.",
  "121": "“They declared, ‘We ˹now˺ believe in the Lord of all worlds—’” is the verbal testimony that explains the prostration just described. The magicians fell down prostrating; now they speak. The declaration, “We now believe in the Lord of all worlds,” or “qaloo amanna bi-Rabbi al-alameen,” repeats the title Moses used when he said “I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds, obliged to say nothing but truth, with clear proof.” By using the same title, they affirm Moses’ claim and reject Pharaoh’s claim to be lord. “Rabbi al-alameen,” Lord of all worlds, was earlier blessed after creation and command were said to belong to Him alone, and after the winds ushered mercy, making it the name that unites worship and provision. Their “now,” or the immediate “amanna,” shows conversion is instant, not gradual, as truth’s swallowing left no room for doubt.\n\nThe declaration also answers the chiefs’ accusation that Moses sought to drive them from the land. The magicians do not speak of land but of Lordship, shifting loyalty from territorial chief to universal nurturer, much as the lowly believers in Thamud believed in what Salih was sent with rather than in clan pride.\n\nThe verse sets up the next specification, “the Lord of Moses and Aaron,” which clarifies they do not mean a vague lord but the God who inspired the staff. This precision preempts Pharaoh’s accusation of conspiracy hatched in the city to drive out its people, showing their belief is in the proven sign’s source, not in a political plot.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think faith expressed under pressure of a public contest is coerced. The magicians’ faith was under pressure, but it was pressure against interest: they had just been promised reward and closeness, and the crowd still stunned was an audience for Pharaoh. Declaring belief therefore risked the very reward they sought and invited the threatened amputation and crucifixion recorded next. Their faith is thus the fertile land’s abundant production at the cost of immediate loss, illustrating that true belief, once the scales are seen to be just and the barrier clear, chooses the Lord of all worlds whose mercy after the staff’s victory is to remove bitterness and make rivers flow, over the lord whose gates remain closed for the arrogant and whose bed is Hell with flames as cover.",
  "122": "“the Lord of Moses and Aaron’” specifies the Lord of all worlds just confessed, making the magicians’ belief unmistakable and unshareable with Pharaoh. The previous verse had them say “We believe in the Lord of all worlds,” a title Pharaoh could still claim to share as he claimed divinity; this verse narrows it: “the Lord of Moses and Aaron,” or “Rabbi Moosa wa Haroon.” Aaron, or “Haroon,” is Moses’ brother whom they had been told to let wait and whose wait now ends with shared vindication. By naming both brothers, the magicians tie their faith to the historical, visible bearers of the staff and shining hand, the messengers who conveyed the Lord’s messages with sincere advice and who know from Allah what others do not.\n\nThis specification also protects against later reinterpretation that belief was in some abstract force. It is belief in the One who created heavens and earth in six Days, established Himself on the Throne, sends winds ushering mercy, and will bring the dead to life like fruit from dead land, the same One whose creation and command belong to Him alone. The phrase parallels earlier precise identifications: Salih’s Lord of the she-camel, Shu‘ayb’s Lord who encompasses everything in knowledge.\n\nImmediate context shows why precision matters. Pharaoh will accuse them of conspiracy devised in the city to drive out its people, as if belief were a plot rather than a response to truth prevailing. By naming Moses and Aaron, they declare the plot is not theirs with Moses but Moses’ plot is from Allah, as earlier We inspired Moses to throw his staff, not from a city council.\n\nA common error today is to speak of faith vaguely to avoid cost. The magicians model precise confession despite the cost of being called conspirators and being threatened with cutting hands and feet on opposite sides and crucifixion. Precision is itself worship: it honors the sincere advisor over the deceiver who swore “I am your sincere advisor” to Adam to expose nakedness. For the listener told to call upon your Lord humbly and secretly without transgressing and to remember Allah’s favours as successors after former residents, the magicians’ specific Lord invites similar specificity: not just belief in a lord, but belief in the Lord who sent Moses and Aaron with bayyinah, whose mercy is close to good-doers and whose punishment, while patient, is not to be awaited as play.",
  "123": "“Pharaoh threatened, ‘How dare you believe in him before I give you permission? This must be a conspiracy you devised in the city to drive out its people, but soon you will see’” records the throne’s response to the magicians’ precise confession in the Lord of Moses and Aaron. The previous verse showed the magicians prostrating after truth devoured illusion; now Pharaoh attempts to reclaim narrative control with two accusations. First, “How dare you believe in him before I give you permission?” or “amantum bihi qabla an athana lakum,” shows Pharaoh’s claim to regulate belief itself, as if faith were a wage needing palace approval, like the reward and closeness he promised if they prevailed. This mirrors earlier chiefly control: expelling Shu‘ayb unless he returned to their faith, expelling Lot for desiring chastity, and calling Hud a fool by chiefly verdict. It exposes the arrogance for which gates of heaven do not open.\n\nSecond, “This must be a conspiracy you devised in the city to drive out its people,” or “inna hatha la-makrun makartumoohu fil-madeenati li-tukhrijoo minha ahlaha,” flips the earlier chiefs’ accusation that Moses seeks to drive you from your land. Now belief itself is framed as a plot hatched with Moses before the contest, a city-wide conspiracy to displace the population. “Makr” here is scheming, the same term for Allah’s perfected planning and for Iblis’s ambush, but Pharaoh uses it to make sincere persuasion look like covert treachery, as earlier elegant words were used to make misguidance look like guidance.\n\nThe closing threat, “but soon you will see,” or “fa-sawfa ta‘lamoon,” is the same “you will soon know” told to Shu‘ayb’s rejecters about who are losers and to those awaiting fulfilment about who ruined themselves, here wielded as intimidation before the specific mutilation threat that follows.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates authority’s permission with legitimacy. The Quran shows permission to believe belongs to the Lord of all worlds, not to Pharaoh, and that the magicians’ belief, given without permission, is precisely the fertile production that makes the land thrive, while Pharaoh’s permission-based faith would leave the land hardly producing. The next verses show the cost of unpermitted belief: threats to cut hands and feet and crucifixion, met with “Surely to our Lord we will all return” and a prayer for perseverance until death while submitting, modeling that true closeness is not among Pharaoh’s muqarrabin but with the Lord whose mercy is close to the good-doers.",
  "124": "“I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, then crucify you all’” is Pharaoh’s detailed sentence after accusing the believing magicians of believing without permission and conspiring to drive out the city’s people. The threat, “I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides,” or “la-uqatti‘anna aidiyakum wa arjulakum min khilafin,” describes a cruel, exemplary mutilation, left hand and right foot or vice versa, designed to maximize pain and to make the body a public warning, as Pharaoh earlier boasted “We will completely dominate them” by killing sons and keeping women. The addition, “then crucify you all,” or “thumma la-usallibannakum ajma‘een,” promises collective execution on display, the ultimate display of a throne that felt defeated and put to shame after its great magic was devoured and truth prevailed.\n\nThe severity contrasts sharply with the magicians’ previous expectation of reward and closeness if they prevailed; the throne’s ajr has turned to severing. It contrasts even more with the reward now sought from the Lord of Moses and Aaron, who gives tenfold, removes bitterness, and makes rivers flow. Pharaoh’s sentence attempts to make belief appear as loser’s path, as his chiefs warned Midian that following Shu‘ayb would make them losers.\n\nThe verse also mirrors other threatened expulsions and mutilations in the prophetic cycle, yet stands out for its explicit cruelty, marking Pharaoh as archetype of the tyrant whose large numbers and arrogance on the Heights are of no use and whose bed is Hell. His threat fulfills the pattern of hindering on every road: not only blocking the Way but punishing those who leave ambush.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view such threats as proof that faith is dangerous and should be hidden. The magicians’ next reply, “Surely to our Lord we will all return” and their prayer for perseverance and dying while submitting, shows that severe threat does not cancel the fertile response; it purifies it, turning the infertile fear of a court that deceives eyes into the thankful harvest that varies lessons for the good. For listeners instructed not to spread corruption after order and to call with hope and fear, the verse teaches that the hope that fears only Allah can withstand the fear that a tyrant tries to instill by threatening limbs and cross, because the earth belongs to Allah and the ultimate outcome belongs to the righteous who seek His help and are patient.",
  "125": "“They responded, ‘Surely to our Lord we will ˹all˺ return’” is the magicians’ calm reply to Pharaoh’s savage threat to cut hands and feet on opposite sides and crucify. The previous verse left the newly believing magicians under a sentence that would mutilate and display them, the cost of believing in the Lord of Moses and Aaron after seeing the staff devour great magic. Their answer, “Surely to our Lord we will all return,” or “inna ila Rabbina munqaliboon,” reorients return. Pharaoh promised to cut and crucify to display his dominance, but the magicians remind that the true return, or “inqilab,” turning with ultimate outcome, is to their Lord, the Lord of all worlds who created heavens and earth in six Days, subjected sun, moon, and stars, sends winds with mercy, and will bring the dead to life like fruit from dead land. This mirrors earlier returns: “to your Lord is your return and He will inform you of differences,” and “there you will live, there you will die, from there you will be resurrected.” Return to Allah dwarfs return to Pharaoh’s pole.\n\nThe word “inna” surely, with certainty, shows spacious chest opened to Islam replacing tight chest that fears the throne. They had just been defeated in worldly terms, put to shame as Pharaoh’s people were defeated, yet they claim the greater victory: return to the best of judges, the One whose weighing will be just and whose mercy is close to good-doers. This mirrors Shu‘ayb’s trust, “In Allah we trust, our Lord has encompassed everything in knowledge,” and Moses’ reassurance, “Seek Allah’s help and be patient, the earth belongs to Allah.”\n\nThe statement also dissolves Pharaoh’s threat of closeness. He promised to make them among the muqarrabin close to him; they answer closeness to Pharaoh is temporary, return to the Lord is permanent, and there they hope for the forgiveness they will request next: “Shower us with perseverance and let us die while submitting.” A common misunderstanding is to equate being cut off with being ruined. The magicians show that severed limbs do not doom the soul that returns to its Lord, while the intact palace doomed its occupants earlier as if they had never lived there. Their reply models the fertile land that produces abundantly even when cut, trusting that the One who made the night for rest and the day for work will gather the soul beyond the throne’s reach, where rivers flow and bitterness is removed for those who believed and did good within capacity, forever companions of Paradise.",
  "126": "“Your rage towards us is only because we believed in the signs of our Lord when they came to us. Our Lord! Shower us with perseverance, and let us die while submitting ˹to You˺” completes the magicians’ reply after affirming return to their Lord, adding diagnosis of Pharaoh’s rage and a prayer for the end they now desire. The diagnosis, “Your rage towards us is only because we believed in the signs of our Lord when they came to us,” or “wa ma tanqimu minna illa an amanna bi-ayati Rabbina lamma ja’atna,” names the sole cause of wrath: belief in ayat, signs, when they arrived. “Tanqimu” means to resent, to take revenge, the same resent that made ’Ad’s chiefs call Hud a fool, Thamud kill the she-camel, Lot’s people expel the chaste, and Midian’s chiefs call Shu‘ayb losers. The timing, “when they came to us,” emphasizes they believed at first sight, unlike those who would not believe in what they had already denied and whose hearts were sealed. Their belief was immediate, fertile, taught by the devouring staff that truth prevailed.\n\nThe prayer that follows is the proper response to that diagnosis: “Our Lord! Shower us with perseverance, and let us die while submitting,” or “Rabbana afrigh alaina sabran wa tawaffana muslimeen.” “Afrigh” means pour abundantly, like rain producing every fruit or winds bearing heavy clouds driven to lifeless land, asking for sabr, perseverance, the same patience enjoined on Shu‘ayb until Allah judges and on Moses’ people to seek help and be patient, for the earth belongs to Allah. “Waffana muslimeen” means cause us to die while submitting, in full submission as the prophets said prayer, worship, life, and death are for Allah, Lord of all worlds, without partner, first to submit. This prayer contrasts with Iblis’s request to be delayed to mislead; they ask to be taken in submission, not delayed.\n\nThe verse links to the Book brought as guide and mercy for those who believe and to the hope-fear calling humbly and secretly without transgressing. Perseverance poured and death as muslims is the seal that no cutting or crucifixion can undo, as earlier the bed of Hell and flames as cover cannot harm the forgiven whose scales are heavy. A common misunderstanding is that such prayer is despair under torture. The Quran presents it as confident trust that the return they just affirmed will be to the Lord whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed, whose mercy is close to good-doers, ensuring that even if limbs are severed, the soul’s submission makes it among those who will be told “Enter Paradise, no fear for you.”",
  "127": "“The chiefs of Pharaoh’s people protested, ‘Are you going to leave Moses and his people free to spread corruption in the land and abandon you and your gods?’ He responded, ‘We will kill their sons and keep their women. We will completely dominate them’” shows the counter-attack after the magicians’ belief and the shaming of the throne. The chiefs, or “al-mala’u min qawmi Fir‘awn,” the same elite who called Moses a magician and sought to drive him from the land, now pressure Pharaoh directly. Their protest, “Are you going to leave Moses and his people free to spread corruption in the land and abandon you and your gods?” or “a-tatharu Moosa wa qawmahu li-yufsidoo fil-ardi wa yatharaka wa alihataka,” inverts names. They label Moses’ call to worship Allah alone, give just measure, and not lie in ambush as corruption, or “yufsidoo,” while their own spreading of corruption after order, their throne’s defrauding and expulsion threats, is presented as order. Abandoning Pharaoh and his gods is framed as betrayal, as if the fertile call were the infertile.\n\nPharaoh’s response doubles oppression: “We will kill their sons and keep their women. We will completely dominate them,” or “sa-nuqqattilu abna’ahum wa nastahyee nisa’ahum wa inna fawqahum qahiroon.” This is the same policy used at Moses’ birth and later remembered as dreadful torment and severe test, echoing Pharaoh’s earlier harness and the later remembrance “We rescued you from the people of Pharaoh who afflicted you with dreadful torment—killing your sons and keeping your women.” The phrase “We will completely dominate them,” or “qahiroon,” claims the “qahr,” irresistible dominance that earlier belonged only to Allah, the Irresistible Subduer above His servants who is All-Wise, All-Aware.\n\nThe exchange shows how elite insecurity uses language of corruption to defend corruption, making the straight seem crooked. It also sets the stage for Moses’ next counsel to seek help and be patient, because the earth belongs to Allah and the ultimate outcome belongs to the righteous, not to those who feel secure from planning.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to read this as mere politics detached from the earlier worship debate. The Quran joins them: the contest over who is Lord of all worlds expresses itself in who controls sons and women and land, and the answer will come through famine, shortage, floods, locusts, lice, frogs, blood, and finally drowning, all while the appointed term for each community cannot be delayed, teaching that domination claimed by a throne is itself the most fragile corruption.",
  "128": "“Moses reassured his people, ‘Seek Allah’s help and be patient. Indeed, the earth belongs to Allah ˹alone˺. He grants it to whoever He chooses of His servants. The ultimate outcome belongs ˹only˺ to the righteous’” is the prophetic answer to Pharaoh’s renewed threat to kill sons and keep women and to dominate. After the chiefs protested that Moses would spread corruption and abandon Pharaoh’s gods, and Pharaoh promised complete domination, Moses turns to the oppressed, not to the throne, with two commands that have anchored every story from Noah to Shu‘ayb: “Seek Allah’s help and be patient,” or “ista‘eeno billahi wa isbiroo.” “Ista‘eeno” is to seek help, or “isti‘anah,” the same help the Quran said to ask from Allah alone whom you call humbly and secretly, while “isbiroo” is to endure with steadfastness until the best of judges judges, as Shu‘ayb said be patient until Allah judges and as earlier messengers endured rejection until help came. The couplet joins reliance and perseverance, the two wings after winds usher mercy.\n\nThe reasoning that follows reframes ownership: “Indeed, the earth belongs to Allah alone. He grants it to whoever He chooses of His servants,” or “inna al-arda lillahi yoorithuha man yashaa’u min ibadihi.” This counters Pharaoh’s “our land” and the chiefs’ fear of being driven from the land. Land, like creation and command that belong to Him alone, is Allah’s property to inherit, or “yoorithu,” as earlier He placed successors after former residents and will make the oppressed successors of eastern and western blessed lands. The earth is not Pharaoh’s estate; it is Allah’s trust lent to test, and He grants it by wisdom, not by Pharaoh’s “qahiroon.”\n\nThe seal, “The ultimate outcome belongs only to the righteous,” or “wa al-aqibatu lil-muttaqeen,” uses “aqibah,” final outcome, the same Home of Peace promised to the mindful and the Paradise where rivers flow, and “muttaqeen,” those who wear the best clothing of righteousness and who are mindful. This mirrors earlier “You will soon know who will fare best in the end” and “The good end belongs to the God-fearing.”\n\nA common error equates outcome with immediate escape from oppression. Moses teaches the immediate step is help and patience while acknowledging true ownership, trusting that the ultimate outcome, though it may pass through famine and sea, belongs to the righteous, not to those who feel secure while asleep or at play and only losers feel secure from Allah’s planning.",
  "129": "“They complained, ‘We have always been oppressed—before and after you came to us ˹with the message˺.’ He replied, ‘Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land to see what you will do’” captures the understandable exhaustion of the oppressed after Moses’ call to seek help and be patient with the earth belonging to Allah. The people’s complaint, “We have always been oppressed—before and after you came to us,” or “oodheena min qablu an ta’tiyana wa min ba‘di ma ji’tana,” voices the weariness of those who endured killing of sons and keeping of women under Pharaoh and who now face renewed threat to kill sons again. Their words imply that the messenger’s arrival has not yet brought immediate relief, a feeling also seen when prophets’ peoples said we seldom give thanks and when the chiefs called messengers misguided.\n\nMoses’ reply does not deny the hardship nor promise instant ease; it points to divine agency and test: “Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land to see what you will do,” or “asa Rabbukum an yuhlika aduwwakum wa yastakhlifakum fil-ardi fa-yanzura kaifa ta‘maloon.” “Asa” perhaps expresses hope and divine will, not guarantee to be presumed upon, as earlier “perhaps you will be mindful” and “perhaps you will be successful” used the same hopeful particle. “Destroy your enemy,” or “yuhlika aduwwakum,” parallels the uprooting of those who denied and the wiping of Midian as if never there, while “make you successors in the land,” or “yastakhlifakum fil-ardi,” echoes being made successors after Noah and after ’Ad and Thamud, and anticipates being made successors of eastern and western blessed lands after Pharaoh. The purpose clause, “to see what you will do,” or “li-yanzura kaifa ta‘maloon,” reveals that succession is itself a test, not a final reward, like the fertile land producing abundantly versus infertile hardly producing, with the same wind-driven rain.\n\nThis exchange links to the next verses where Pharaoh’s people are afflicted with famine and shortage so they might return, and where prosperity later makes them claim “This is what we deserve.” The oppressed are reminded that inheritance does not equal immunity; it is a stage where thankfulness is varied and measured.\n\nA common misunderstanding expects immediate prosperity after turning to faith. Moses teaches that help and patience precede inheritance, and inheritance precedes examination, as earlier each community has a term and the weighing will be just, inviting the oppressed to transform complaint into patient, helped reliance before the sea parts.",
  "130": "“Indeed, We afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine and shortage of crops so they might come back ˹to their senses˺” describes the first merciful blow after Moses’ promise that perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors to see what you will do. The previous exchange left the Israelites weary from oppression before and after the message, and Moses pointing to possible destruction of the enemy and succession as a test. Now the mechanism begins, not with immediate drowning but with measured affliction: “Indeed, We afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine and shortage of crops,” or “wa laqad akhathna ala Fir‘awna bi-sineena wa naqsin mina al-thamaraat.” “Sineen” are years of drought, famine, the same “ba’sa” and “darra” earlier sent to societies so perhaps they would be humbled, and “naqs min al-thamarat” shortage of fruits, the very clustered grain and garden produce that the winds produce from lifeless land. Taking away the produce tests their earlier boast that increase in stature and number proves favour.\n\nThe purpose is stated tenderly: “so they might come back to their senses,” or “la‘allahum yaththakkaroon,” so perhaps they will remember, be mindful, the same mindfulness, or “tathakkur,” that makes lessons vary for the thankful and that makes worship proper at every mosque and eating without waste. Remembering here means connecting famine to the Creator who sends winds, makes day and night overlap, and whose creation and command belong to Him alone, rather than attributing it to chance as they did when they said forefathers also had adversity and prosperity.\n\nThis verse links to the broader pattern where hardship precedes prosperity as a cycle upto the surprise seizure while unaware. For Pharaoh’s people, famine is the first gentle call after the staff-snake and shining hand, after the magicians’ prostration and the threat to cut limbs, and after the renewed threat to kill sons. It parallels the drought sent to earlier societies and to Mecca itself, where livelihood was given yet seldom thanks was offered.\n\nA common error treats famine as purely natural without a caller. The Quran names the afflictor as “We” and the purpose as remembrance, showing the same hand that brings every type of fruit can withhold fruit to soften hard soil. For the successor-to-be, the Israelites, the lesson is that succession is not merely escape from famine’s agents but succession into the role of the grateful who remember and whose land becomes blessed from heaven and earth, if they remain faithful and mindful when their own test of increase arrives.",
  "131": "“In times of prosperity, they said, ‘This is what we deserve,’ but in adversity, they blamed it on Moses and those with him. Surely all is destined by Allah. Yet most of them did not know” unmasks the double standard that famine and shortage were meant to cure. The previous verse said We afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine and shortage so they might remember. This verse shows the remembrance failed and was replaced by self-praise and scapegoating. In prosperity, or “itha ja’athum al-hasanatu,” when good, abundance, ease came, they said “This is what we deserve,” or “la-na hathihi,” this is ours, due to our merit, our planning, our gods, our numbers, a claim like the arrogant who took faith as play and were deluded by worldly increase, and like those who said “We have certainly been favoured” when rain produced fruit for the fertile. In adversity, or “wa in tusibhum sayyi’ah,” when evil, harm, bad harvest touched them, “they blamed it on Moses and those with him,” or “yatayyaroo bi-Moosa wa man ma‘ahu,” they saw bad omens in Moses, attributing famine to his ill omen, the same “tatayyur” that made chiefs call Hud a fool and Salih’s people demand she-camel harm.\n\nThe divine correction, “Surely all is destined by Allah,” or “ala innama ta’iruhum inda Allah,” means their omen, their fortune and misfortune, is with Allah, determined by His creation and command, not by Moses, who is merely a conveyor of messages and sincere advisor who knows what they do not. The same Lord who created heavens and earth in six Days and subjects sun, moon, and stars decrees adversity and prosperity as tests varied for the thankful.\n\nThe closing, “Yet most of them did not know,” or “wa lakinna aktharahum la ya‘lamoon,” echoes that most follow assumption and lie, most are rebellious, most are not true to covenant, explaining why they missed the humbling purpose.\n\nA common misunderstanding attributes prosperity to self and adversity to the pious, making the messenger a scapegoat for the community’s hard soil that hardly produces. The verse teaches that both states are from Allah to test whether the land will remember and produce thankfully or, as Pharaoh’s people did next, say “No matter what sign you bring to deceive us we will never believe,” persisting in arrogance until floods, locusts, lice, frogs, and blood arrive as clear signs and still fail to soften, leading to drowning while heedless.",
  "132": "“They said, ‘No matter what sign you may bring to deceive us, we will never believe in you’” captures Pharaoh’s people’s final verbal obstinacy after blaming Moses for adversity and claiming prosperity as deserved. The previous verse exposed their omen logic, that all is destined by Allah yet most do not know. Now they declare a blanket refusal: “No matter what sign you may bring to deceive us, we will never believe in you,” or “wa qaloo mahma ta’tina bihi min ayatin li-tasharana biha fa-ma nahnu laka bi-mu’mineen.” “Mahma,” whatever, “ta’tina,” you bring, “min ayah,” any sign, “li-tasharana,” to bewitch us, to deceive us, shows they have already categorized any bayyinah as sihr, magic, as earlier chiefs called Moses a skilled magician who seeks to drive you from the land. The word “tasharana” links to “sahar” that deceived eyes in the contest, but now they claim even any sign from the Lord of all worlds would be that same bewitchment, closing the door that earlier signs like staff-snake and shining hand had opened for the magicians.\n\nThe closing “we will never believe in you” echoes the earlier obstinacy “Even if We sent angels and the dead spoke they would not believe unless Allah willed,” and the earlier threat that even if every sign came they still would not believe as they refused at first and hearts were turned. This is the infertile declaration: same rain, no produce, the soil that hardly produces anything by the Will of its Lord.\n\nThis refusal sets up the plagues that follow: floods, locusts, lice, frogs, and blood, all as clear signs, but they persisted in arrogance and were a wicked people. Their “whatever sign” is answered with a succession of signs, each more pervasive than a staff in a court, touching water, food, health, and sleep.\n\nA common error is to think such absolute refusal is not possible today. The Quran shows refusal hardens gradually: calling the warner misguided, then fool, then liar, then expelling the chaste, then defrauding measure, then attributing adversity to the warner, then sealing by saying whatever sign you bring we will never believe. At each stage the Book explained with knowledge was available as guide and mercy, the winds still ushered rain, and calling humbly and secretly with hope and fear was still possible. The verse thus warns successors not to repeat the “never” that made Pharaoh’s people remain among those whose hearts are sealed so they will not hear, before the inevitable drowning while heedless.",
  "133": "“So We plagued them with floods, locusts, lice, frogs, and blood—all as clear signs, but they persisted in arrogance and were a wicked people” lists the successive clear signs that answered the blanket refusal “whatever sign you bring we will never believe.” The previous verse showed Pharaoh’s people closing the door to any bayyinah. Now Allah, whose creation and command belong to Him alone and who subjects winds and rain, sends signs that touch the whole society, not only the court. The list, “floods, locusts, lice, frogs, and blood,” or “al-toofana wa al-jarada wa al-qummala wa al-dafadi‘a wa al-dama,” uses each plague as a bayyinah, a distinct lesson. Floods, or “toofan,” overwhelmed the Nile’s order that made them feel secure by day at play; locusts devoured the shortage-of-crops that had already been a warning and the produce they claimed as deserved; lice and vermin infested bodies and stores; frogs filled waters and homes, invading the palaces carved with pride; blood turned the Nile and waters they drank, the source they attributed to other gods, into the fluid of life made undrinkable, echoing the pollution of their own measures.\n\nEach is described as “ayat mufassalat,” signs distinct, detailed, varied, the same “mufassal” used for the Book explained with knowledge. Yet “they persisted in arrogance and were a wicked people,” or “fa-istakbaroo wa kanoo qawman mujrimeen.” “Istakbaroo” is the arrogance for which gates of heaven do not open and for which the chiefs earlier were called arrogant who asked the lowly if they are certain Salih is sent. Mujrimeen, the wicked who cut off truth, are the same whose end the watcher is told to see and who were warned that the weighing will be just.\n\nThe verse links to the cycle of affliction so they might be humbled versus the prosperity that made them say “This is ours.” Each plague was a chance to remember that the Lord who established you after former residents can punish for sins and seal hearts so they will not hear. Pharaoh’s people remained infertile, hardly producing, despite varied waters.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats plagues as random disasters. The Quran frames them as mufassalat, each teaching a distinct lesson about Lordship over the very substance the elite trusted: river, crop, body, home, water, all within the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who is never absent, inviting the mindful to call upon Him humbly and secretly before the final sea, which will also be a sign that swallows arrogance at night while asleep.",
  "134": "“When tormented, they pleaded, ‘O Moses! Pray to your Lord on our behalf, by virtue of the covenant He made with you. If you help remove this torment from us, we will certainly believe in you and let the Children of Israel go with you’” shows the momentary softening that plagued, arrogant persistence momentarily interrupted. The previous verse showed floods, locusts, lice, frogs, and blood as clear signs, but they persisted in arrogance as a wicked people. Now, under pressure, they turn to the same Moses they called a magician who seeks to drive them from the land and whom they vowed never to believe. The phrase “When tormented, they pleaded, ‘O Moses! Pray to your Lord on our behalf, by virtue of the covenant He made with you,’” or “wa lamma waqa‘a alaihim al-rijzu qaloo ya Moosa ud‘u lana Rabbaka bima ahida indak,” reveals that even Pharaoh’s people recognize Moses has a covenant, or “ahd,” a special nearness to the Lord of all worlds, the same covenant the Children of Israel were told to fulfil and the same covenant most people were found not true to. They ask him to invoke by that covenant, acknowledging its reality while previously calling it magic.\n\nThe promise that follows, “If you help remove this torment from us, we will certainly believe in you and let the Children of Israel go with you,” or “la-in kashafta anna al-rijza la-nu’minanna laka wa la-nursilanna ma‘aka Bani Isra’eel,” mirrors the pledges made by earlier societies when overwhelmed and the later plea of the Fire’s residents for water, conditional faith under compulsion, like detaining before the Fire and begging to be sent back. It also mirrors Pharaoh’s own people’s earlier famine where they blamed Moses but now beg him.\n\nThis oscillation, arrogant in ease, pleading in torment, is the same as “there are some who pretend to listen but veils are cast” and “if Allah rescues us from this we will be ever grateful, then they associate.” The true nature of the plea is revealed in the next verse: as soon as torment is removed until they meet their fate, they break the promise.\n\nA common error is to mistake forced pleading for true humbling, or “tadarru‘,” that earlier afflictions sought. The Quran distinguishes: tadarru‘ is humble, secret, hopeful calling without condition before seizure; this conditional bargaining is not it. For successors, the lesson is to honor the covenant while the torment is still a sign to be read, not a whip to bargain with, so that belief is fertile production by the Lord’s leave rather than a fleeting promise broken when waters clear, before the final drowning while heedless overtakes those who persisted.",
  "135": "“But as soon as We removed the torment from them—until they met their inevitable fate—they broke their promise” exposes the fragility of conditional pledges made under plagues. The previous verse quoted their plea to Moses to pray by his covenant so they would believe and release the Israelites if torment was lifted. This verse records the aftermath: “But as soon as We removed the torment from them—until they met their inevitable fate—they broke their promise,” or “fa-lamma kashafna anhum al-rijza ila ajalin hum balighoohu itha hum yankuthoon.” “Kashafna” means We uncovered, removed, like a cloud lifted; “ila ajalin hum balighoohu” until a term they must reach, the appointed term that for each community cannot be delayed or advanced, here the term that ends at the sea. “Yankuthoon” means they break, violate, undo a firm knot, the same breaking of covenant found in most people not true to covenant and truly rebellious.\n\nThe pattern is the same as the broader cycle: afflicted with suffering so perhaps they would be humbled, then changed from adversity to prosperity until they flourished and said forefathers also had cycles, so We seized them by surprise while unaware. Each plague removal was a prosperity after adversity that tested whether humility would remain. Instead, breaking the promise showed the land remained infertile, hardly producing anything, despite varied lessons for the thankful.\n\nThe verse also connects to the promise-threat of Midian, where chiefs warned followers they would be losers if they followed Shu‘ayb; here the breakers prove they are the losers as if never there. Their breaking prepares the final punishment, drowning for denying signs and being heedless, as the next verse states.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think a removed torment proves approval. The removal here was respite until the ajal, a test of whether the brief humbling would become steady mindfulness, the best clothing, the humble secret calling with hope and fear. Breaking shows the earlier pleading was not the tadarru‘ sought but bargaining, like those who ignore the meeting as mere play. For successors who inherit the eastern and western blessed lands, the verse warns that every relief is a test to keep promise, give just measure, and not lie in ambush, before the inevitable fate that is met after promise-breaking leaves the heart sealed so it will not hear.",
  "136": "“So We inflicted punishment upon them, drowning them in the sea for denying Our signs and being heedless of them” gives the final term for Pharaoh’s people after the cycle of plagues, pleadings, and breakings. The previous verse showed that as soon as torment was removed until their inevitable fate, they broke the promise, the same breaking that made most not true to covenant and rebellious. Now the inevitable fate arrives. The phrase “So We inflicted punishment upon them,” or “fa-intaqamna minhum,” means We exacted recompense, showed that the torment they challenged Moses to bring, the rijs and wrath said to have fallen, and the vengeance warned as close to the good-doers, has a day. The instrument, “drowning them in the sea,” or “fa-aghraqnahum fil-yamm,” returns to water as both mercy and might. The same sea whose winds usher heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit, and whose Nile was first made famine-short, then blood, now opens and closes by the Lord who created heavens and earth in six Days and established Himself on the Throne, whose command subjects winds and waters. The sea that carried Moses’ Ark-like deliverance now carries Pharaoh’s army to its bed as Hell will be their bed with flames as cover.\n\nThe reason is doubled: “for denying Our signs and being heedless of them,” or “bi-annahum kathaboo bi-ayatina wa kanoo anha ghafileen.” “Kathaboo” denied with arrogance, the same denial that closed gates of heaven until a camel passes through a needle; “ghafileen,” heedless, inattentive, the same heedlessness for which societies were seized at night while asleep or by day while at play, feeling secure from planning as losers do.\n\nThis drowning fulfills Moses’ hope that perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors to see what you will do, and the earlier promise that if those societies had been faithful We would have opened blessings from heaven and earth. Instead, denial and heedlessness led to being overwhelmed.\n\nA common error separates natural sea from moral order. The Quran joins them: the sea’s obedience to the One whose creation and command are His makes it a sign and a scale, just as the staff’s snake and the hand’s whiteness were signs. For successors who will soon cross that sea and then demand a god like the idol worshippers they encounter, and who will make a calf that lows, the drowning is the empirical “See what was the end of the corruptors” that should keep the fertile soil thankful and the clothing of righteousness intact, before the next trial of golden ornaments.",
  "137": "“And ˹so˺ We made the oppressed people successors of the eastern and western lands, which We had showered with blessings. ˹In this way˺ the noble Word of your Lord was fulfilled for the Children of Israel for what they had endured. And We destroyed what Pharaoh and his people constructed and what they established” shows the merciful side of the same sea that drowned the heedless, fulfilling Moses’ perhaps for successors. The previous verse closed the enemy’s term with drowning for denying signs with heedlessness. This verse opens the successor’s term. The phrase “We made the oppressed people successors of the eastern and western lands,” or “wa awrathna al-qawma allatheena kanoo yustad‘afoona mashariqa al-ardi wa magharibaha,” refers to the Children of Israel, long exploited, killed sons kept women, the weak whose faith the arrogant swore would never receive mercy. They are made heirs, or “awrathna,” as earlier the earth belongs to Allah who grants it to whom He wills and as successors after Noah, ’Ad, Thamud, and Midian. The lands are the blessed eastern and western expanses, often understood as the Levant’s fertile tracts whose olives, pomegranates, and gardens were showered, or “barakna,” with increasing blessing, the same barakah as the blessed Book.\n\nThe fulfilment is then noted: “In this way the noble Word of your Lord was fulfilled for the Children of Israel for what they had endured,” or “wa tammat kalimatu Rabbika al-husna ala Bani Isra’eela bima sabaroo.” “Kalimatu al-husna,” the beautiful, noble word, is the promise of succession for the patient, as earlier “Seek Allah’s help and be patient.” “Bima sabaroo” for what they patiently endured ties inheritance to perseverance, not to entitlement, mirroring the tenfold reward and the removal of bitterness for the patient who call humbly.\n\nThe counterpart, “And We destroyed what Pharaoh and his people constructed and what they established,” or “wa dammarna ma kana yasna‘u Fir‘awnu wa qawmuhu wa ma kanu ya‘rishoon,” wipes the monuments, palaces, and trellises they erected, as Midian’s flourishing became as if never there and Thamud’s carved homes became lifeless dwellings.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view succession as guaranteed permanence. The phrasing “to see what you will do” already warned that inheritance is a test. Within verses they will demand a god like the idol worshippers they meet and make a calf that lows, showing the same infertile risk remains for successors, needing the Tablets’ fundamentals and best-of-it counsel to stay fertile and not be among those turned away from signs for arrogance.",
  "138": "“We brought the Children of Israel across the sea and they came upon a people devoted to idols. They demanded, ‘O Moses! Make for us a god like their gods.’ He replied, ‘Indeed, you are a people acting ignorantly!’” follows the succession to blessed lands with an immediate test of that succession’s heart. The previous verse said the oppressed were made heirs of eastern and western blessed lands and Pharaoh’s constructions were destroyed. Now the new heirs, fresh from drowning of the enemy they had feared before and after Moses came, cross the sea that was both road and grave. The phrase “We brought the Children of Israel across the sea,” or “wa jawazna bi-Bani Isra’eela al-bahra,” recalls the crossing as divine act, like the ark that saved Noah and the mercy that saved Hud and Salih, not human engineering. Immediately, “they came upon a people devoted to idols,” or “fa-ataw ala qawmin ya‘kifoona ala asnam lahum,” they encounter a settled community whose devotion, or “ya‘kifoona,” is to remain at, cling to, idols, the same “asnam” Abraham asked his father about, taking idols as gods while being in clear error.\n\nThe successors’ demand is startling: “Make for us a god like their gods,” or “ij‘al lana ilahan kama lahum alihah,” seeking a tangible god to see and touch, forgetting that your Lord is Allah who created heavens and earth in six Days, established on the Throne, makes night and day overlap, subjects sun, moon, stars, and whose creation and command are His alone, blessed Lord of all worlds, whom no vision can encompass but He encompasses all vision. The demand mirrors the earlier “Who has forbidden adornment” error, attributing divinity where none belongs, and the earlier fabricated shares for associates.\n\nMoses’ reply is diagnostic: “Indeed, you are a people acting ignorantly!” or “innakum qawmun tajhaloon,” ignorance, or “jahl,” not merely lack of data but acting without the knowledge that was explained with knowledge in the Book, the same ignorance for which most do not know and for which successors may have hearts sealed so they will not hear.\n\nA common misconception is to think succession guarantees mindfulness. This episode shows that inheritance without continued remembrance can quickly request the very associate-gods whose makers were uprooted, explaining why the Tablets will soon detail fundamentals and advise taking the best of it, and why the story is told to the Arabian successors after former residents, to warn them not to repeat “Make us a god like theirs” in new forms, like calf-making from golden jewellery that neither speaks nor guides.",
  "139": "“What they follow is certainly doomed to destruction and their deeds are in vain” is Moses’ further answer after calling his people ignorant for demanding a god like the idol devotees they encountered after crossing the sea. The previous verse showed the demand “Make for us a god like their gods” and the rebuke “You are a people acting ignorantly.” Now Moses explains why such following is futile. The phrase “What they follow is certainly doomed to destruction,” or “inna ha’ula’i mutabbarun ma hum fihi,” means what these idol devotees are upon will be broken to pieces, shattered, exterminated, like Pharaoh’s constructions that were destroyed and what they established was levelled, and like every society’s monuments whose end the watcher is told to see. “Mutabbar” implies utter smashing, not mere fading, the same uprooting, or “qata‘na dabira,” that ended ’Ad, Thamud, Midian, and Pharaoh.\n\nThe second clause, “and their deeds are in vain,” or “wa batilun ma kanu ya‘maloon,” repeats the verdict after the staff devoured great magic: truth prevailed and their illusions failed, their deeds became batil. The idol worshippers’ deeds, however elaborate the devotion, “ya‘kifoona,” remaining at idols, are null because they violate the first of true prohibitions, associating without sultan, and because they attribute to Allah what they do not know, whose deeds will be in vain like those who deny signs and the meeting in the Hereafter.\n\nThe verse connects succession to criterion: the Children of Israel were honoured above others, given succession of blessed lands for patience, yet honour does not transfer to idol following; following idols destroys honour. The same principle was seen on the Heights where the humble sworn to be denied mercy entered Paradise, while tyrants’ numbers and arrogance proved useless.\n\nA common error is to think devotional intensity sanctifies its object. The Quran says intensity without truth is tabar and batil, smashed and void, because creation and command belong to Him alone, the best clothing is righteousness, and the Book was brought as guide and mercy to those who believe, not to those who beautify taking other guardians. The next verses will add, “Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah while He has honoured you above others?” and remind them of rescue from Pharaoh’s dreadful torment, reinforcing that the only god worthy of being sought is the One who brought them across the sea, not the gods beside that sea whose way is shattered.",
  "140": "“He added, ‘Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah, while He has honoured you above the others?’” continues Moses’ rebuke after declaring what idol devotees follow is doomed and vain. The previous two verses showed the demand for a god like the idols encountered after the sea crossing, the call “you are a people acting ignorantly,” and the verdict that such following is shattered. Now Moses adds a rhetorical question that appeals to their own history of honour: “Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah,” or “a-ghaira Allahi abghikum ilahan,” while He has honoured you above the others,” or “wa huwa faddalakum ala al-alameen.” “Faddala” means favoured, distinguished, elevated, the same favouring by which the Children of Israel were granted successorship of blessed eastern and western lands after enduring oppression, and the same favour that the arrogant on the Heights had sworn the humble would never receive. The honour, “above the others,” or “ala al-alameen,” refers to that generation’s favour above contemporary peoples, with the noble Word fulfilled for what they patiently endured, not an eternal ethnic superiority that survives denial, as earlier those who deny signs and are rebellious are found not true to covenant.\n\nThe question “Shall I seek” shows seeking another god would be seeking another lord besides Allah, the same “a-ghaira Allah abghi Rabban” said earlier when Muhammad said “Shall I seek a lord other than Allah while He is Lord of everything,” and “Shall I seek a judge other than Allah while He revealed the detailed Book.” In each case, seeking another is the abandonment of the straight path for other ways that divide. For the Israelites, to seek another god after being rescued from Pharaoh’s dreadful torment of killing sons and keeping women, after winds and plagues and the split sea, would be to return to the faith of the ’Ad-like tyrants whose constructions were just destroyed.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat favour as license for negligence, as if being honoured above others permits taking a god like theirs and remaining secure. Moses shows the opposite: favour increases responsibility, as fertile land that produces abundantly must be thankful, or it becomes infertile hardly producing and will be varied as lessons for the thankful. The next verse will recall the severe test of Pharaoh’s torment to cement gratitude, before the appointment of forty nights with the Tablets that detail fundamentals, so the honoured successors will remember that the only worthy object of seeking is the One who honoured them, not the shattered gods they asked to imitate.",
  "141": "“And ˹remember˺ when We rescued you from the people of Pharaoh, who afflicted you with dreadful torment—killing your sons and keeping your women. That was a severe test from your Lord” recalls the foundational rescue that makes the demand for another god especially ungrateful. The previous verses rebuked ignorance for asking “Make us a god like theirs” after being honoured above others and declared what such following is doomed and vain. Now the Quran commands remembrance: “And remember when We rescued you from the people of Pharaoh,” or “wa ith anjainakum min ali Fir‘awn,” the same rescue earlier promised as perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy. The rescue is attributed directly to “We,” the Lord who drowned them in the sea for denying signs with heedlessness and made the oppressed successors of blessed lands.\n\nThe torment is detailed again: “who afflicted you with dreadful torment—killing your sons and keeping your women,” or “yasoomoonakum soo’a al-athabi yuqattiloona abna’akum wa yastahyoona nisa’akum,” afflicting, tasting through perpetual harm, the systematic killing of newborn males for fear of a prophesied deliverer and the sparing of females for servitude, as earlier recalled. This is not abstract history; it was their lived terror before and after Moses came, the complaint “We have always been oppressed,” now answered with deliverance.\n\nThe evaluation closes, “That was a severe test from your Lord,” or “wa fi thalikum bala’un min Rabbikum azeem,” a tremendous trial. “Bala’” means test that reveals, as earlier the splitting of the term into affliction and prosperity was bala’ so they might be humbled, and as succession itself is bala’ to see what you will do. Both oppression and rescue are tests: oppression tested patience, rescue tests gratitude.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next appointment of forty nights for Tablets, because the rescued who quickly request a god like others show the rescued still need the fundamentals written and the best-of-it counsel to stay mindful. A common error is to treat past deliverance as guarantee against future testing. The Quran shows deliverance intensifies the next test, just as increase from few to many tested Midian and ’Ad. Remembering dreadful torment and the severe test should produce the humble secret calling with hope and fear, dress properly for worship, eat with thanks without waste, and follow what was sent down, rather than return to the smashing, vain way whose end is drowning while heedless and whose abode is the bed of Hell with flames as cover for the rebellious whose seal prevents hearing.",
  "142": "“We appointed for Moses thirty nights then added another ten— completing his Lord’s term of forty nights. Moses commanded his brother Aaron, ‘Take my place among my people, do what is right, and do not follow the way of the corruptors’” shifts from rescue and honour to preparation for law, after the reproach for demanding a god like theirs and the reminder of severe test. The phrase “We appointed for Moses thirty nights then added another ten—completing his Lord’s term of forty nights,” or “wa wa‘adna Moosa thalathina lailatan wa atmamnahum bi-‘ashrin fa-tamma meeqatu Rabbihi arba‘eena lailah,” shows divine dating of revelation. Nights, or “layali,” are counted because nights mark the lunar calendar and were times of devotion, as earlier Moses’ appointment was at a time and his Lord spoke to him. Forty nights is the meeqat, appointed meeting, a term like every community’s appointed term that cannot be delayed, but here it is a blessed appointment for guidance, not for punishment. The period echoes earlier forty and completes the word of the Lord for the Children of Israel after their endurance.\n\nMoses’ instruction to his brother Aaron, or “Haroon,” is then quoted: “Take my place among my people, do what is right, and do not follow the way of the corruptors,” or “ukhlufnee fi qawmi wa aslih wa la tattabi‘ sabela al-mufsideen.” “Ukhlufnee” means succeed me, be my khalifah among the people, the same succession language used for making you successors after former residents and after Pharaoh. “Aslih” means set right, rectify, act righteously, the same islah after which corruption is forbidden. “Do not follow the way of the corruptors,” or “la tattabi‘ sabela al-mufsideen,” is the straight-path warning not to follow other ways that divide, not to hinder on every road, not to spread corruption after order, and not to take devils as masters.\n\nThe command anticipates the calf incident that will occur in his absence, where the people take a lowing calf as god that neither speaks nor guides, and where deeds will be in vain for denying the meeting, showing that even appointment by divine promise needs vigilant stewardship.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think appointment alone guarantees the community’s steadfastness. This verse shows the leader must arrange a trustworthy successor and explicitly warn against corruptors, because succession is a test to see what you will do, and the best clothing of righteousness must be consciously kept while the appointed term is fulfilled. For the living, forty nights of devoted waiting before receiving fundamentals mirrors the earlier call to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, hoping mercy that is close to good-doers arrives as Tablets.",
  "143": "“When Moses came at the appointed time and his Lord spoke to him, he asked, ‘My Lord! Reveal Yourself to me so I may see You.’ Allah answered, ‘You cannot see Me! But look at the mountain. If it remains firm in its place, only then will you see Me.’ When his Lord appeared to the mountain, He levelled it to dust and Moses collapsed unconscious. When he recovered, he cried, ‘Glory be to You! I turn to You in repentance and I am the first of the believers’” narrates the encounter at the end of the forty-night term, following the appointment and the instruction to Aaron not to follow corruptors. The phrase “When Moses came at the appointed time and his Lord spoke to him,” or “wa lamma ja’a Moosa li-meeqatina wa kallamahu Rabbuhu,” marks the fulfilment of the meeqat, the meeting that parallels every appointed term, but here it is communion, not destruction. “Kallamahu Rabbuhu,” His Lord spoke to him, is a direct, honoured speech without intermediary, as earlier Moses was elevated by messages and speech.\n\nMoses then asks, “My Lord! Reveal Yourself to me so I may see You,” or “Rabbi arinee anzur ilaika.” The request arises not from doubt but from longing after proximity, the natural desire of the one who turned his face to the Originator and whose sincere advice sought the tremendous Day’s mercy. The answer, “You cannot see Me!” or “lan taranee,” denies visibility in this worldly state, where no vision can encompass Him though He encompasses all vision, the same principle that makes the Lord unseen to Pharaoh’s eyes that demanded signs on demand. The test is given: “But look at the mountain. If it remains firm in its place, only then will you see Me,” a mountain far firmer than human flesh, an image of the creation that belongs to Him alone.\n\nThe outcome, “When his Lord appeared to the mountain, He levelled it to dust and Moses collapsed unconscious,” or “fa-lamma tajalla Rabbuhu lil-jabali ja‘alahu dakkan wa kharra Moosa sa‘iqa,” shows that even the firm mountain, whose palaces Thamud carved, cannot remain firm before the faintest self-disclosure, crumbling to dust, a preview of how the earth’s mountains will be levelled on the tremendous Day. Moses falls, or “sa‘iqa,” thunderstruck, showing human createdness cannot bear direct vision in this life.\n\nUpon recovery, he says, “Glory be to You! I turn to You in repentance and I am the first of the believers,” or “subhanaka tubtu ilaika wa ana awwalu al-mu’mineen.” “Subhanaka” declares Allah free from the limitation of being seen as creatures are seen, “tubtu” I repent from asking beyond what was given, and “awwalu al-mu’mineen” first among his people in belief, as earlier Muhammad is first to submit. The episode teaches that the best clothing after such awe is repentant glorification and renewed eagerness to hold firmly to what was given, as the next verses command.",
  "144": "“Allah said, ‘O Moses! I have ˹already˺ elevated you above all others by My messages and speech. So hold firmly to what I have given you and be grateful’” follows Moses’ awe-struck repentance after the mountain was levelled and he fell unconscious. The previous scene ended with his glorification and declaration as first of the believers after asking to see the Lord. Now Allah addresses him with reassurance and instruction. The opening, “O Moses! I have already elevated you above all others by My messages and speech,” or “ya Moosa inni istafaytuka ala al-nasi bi-risalati wa bi-kalami,” recalls that elevation is by divine choice, as earlier “We elevate in rank whoever We please” and as Moses was made to be among those whose Lord has encompassed everything in knowledge. Elevation is not claimed by saying “I am better” like Iblis, but granted by being chosen to carry risalah, messages, and kalam, direct speech, the two honours that make his Lord’s term of forty nights complete.\n\nThe command that follows is practical: “So hold firmly to what I have given you,” or “fa-khuth ma ataituka,” take what I have given you with strength, with firm grasp, like giving full measure and weighing with justice, like holding to the straight path and not following other ways that divide. What was given will be detailed next as Tablets with fundamentals of everything, commandments and explanations. Holding firmly means adherence without the crooked-making that hinders the Way and without the corruption that spreads after order.\n\nThe final instruction, “and be grateful,” or “wa kun min al-shakireen,” ties back to the fertile-infertile parable where lessons were varied for the thankful, and to the earlier “how seldom are you thankful” after establishment on earth and provisioning. Gratitude here is not mere words but keeping the best clothing of righteousness, remembering favours after being successors, and not demanding a god like theirs or making a lowing calf, both acts of ingratitude after immense honour.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates elevation with license to demand more, as Pharaoh demanded signs on demand and as earlier chiefs demanded signs to deceive. Allah tells Moses elevation is already given through messages and speech; the proper response is firm holding and thankfulness, which will be enabled by the Tablets that detail everything as guide and mercy, so perhaps you will be certain of the meeting, and which will soon instruct to take the best of it and be shown the home of the rebellious to avoid, rather than to ask to see what cannot be seen in this life.",
  "145": "“We wrote for him on the Tablets ˹the fundamentals˺ of everything; commandments and explanations of all things. ˹We commanded,˺ ‘Hold to this firmly and ask your people to take the best of it. I will soon show ˹all of˺ you the home of the rebellious’” details the content of what Moses was told to hold firmly and be grateful for. The previous verse said he was elevated by messages and speech and should take what was given with strength. This verse says, “We wrote for him on the Tablets the fundamentals of everything; commandments and explanations,” or “wa katabna lahu fil-alwahi min kulli shay’in maw‘izatan wa tafsilan li-kulli shay’.” “Alwahi” are the stone tablets inscribed by the divine command, the same creation-and-command belonging to Him alone. “Maw‘izah,” admonition, and “tafsil,” detailed explanation for everything needed to judge, parallel the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy and the Word perfected in truth and justice that cannot be changed, showing continuity between Moses’ fundamentals and the later blessed Book.\n\nThe command to apply it is then given: “Hold to this firmly and ask your people to take the best of it,” or “fa-khuthha bi-quwwatin wa’mur qawmaka ya’khuthoo bi-ahsaniha.” Strength, or “quwwah,” repeats the firm grasp, and “ahsaniha,” the best, the most beautiful of it, indicates there are levels of taking, with the best being the most complete implementation, like giving full measure, not coming near indecencies, fulfilling the covenant, all of the straight path’s items. This mirrors the earlier call to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians, and to be among the thankful whose varying lessons produce abundantly.\n\nThe warning closes, “I will soon show all of you the home of the rebellious,” or “sa-ureekum dara al-fasiqeen,” the abode of those who step out, the rebellious, or “fasiqeen,” who were found not true to covenant and most rebellious after the stories, whose home is the end seen after corruption. “I will show you” means you will travel and see ruins, like those destroyed at night or midday, as empirical “see the end of the wicked” and “see the end of the corruptors.” The home of the rebellious, whether ’Ad’s uprooted, Thamud’s lifeless homes, Lot’s brimstone-rained, Midian’s as if never there, or Pharaoh’s drowned constructions, is the visible end that those who disbelieve in meeting denied.\n\nA common error is to treat the Tablets as relics rather than as living fundamentals to be held with strength and with best practice, whose neglect leads to calf-worship that neither speaks nor guides and to the sealing of hearts that will not hear even every sign.",
  "146": "“I will turn away from My signs those who act unjustly with arrogance in the land. And even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe in them. If they see the Right Path, they will not take it. But if they see a crooked path, they will follow it. This is because they denied Our signs and were heedless of them” explains the divine law behind showing the home of the rebellious, linking back to why varied lessons for the thankful produce while the same water leaves the infertile hardly producing. The opening, “I will turn away from My signs those who act unjustly with arrogance in the land,” or “sa-asrifu an ayati allatheena yatakabbaroona fil-ardi bi-ghairi al-haqq,” uses the same “sarf” as “We will turn their hearts and eyes” and “We vary Our signs.” Turning away from signs is the consequence of acting arrogantly without right, like Pharaoh calling Moses a magician, the arrogant chiefs asking the lowly if Salih is sent, and Iblis saying I am better. Arrogance without right is the quality for which gates of heaven do not open and for which a needle’s eye remains too small for a camel until impossible.\n\nThe effect is then described: “And even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe in them,” echoing the repeated verdict “Even if We sent angels and the dead spoke and gathered every sign they would not believe unless Allah willed” and “Even if they see every sign they will not believe.” The reason is not lack of signs but the turning away that follows their own arrogant injustice.\n\nThe next sentence sharpens the choice: “If they see the Right Path, they will not take it. But if they see a crooked path, they will follow it,” or “wa in yaraw sabila al-rushdi la yattakhithoohu sabila wa in yaraw sabila al-ghayyi yattakhithoohu sabila.” “Rushd,” right, mature guidance, is the straight path made clear to the mindful, the best of the Tablets; “ghayy,” crooked error, is the other ways that divide, the devils’ ambush on every road making it seem crooked. Their preference reveals the heart’s seal caused by prior denial.\n\nThe cause is then named: “This is because they denied Our signs and were heedless of them,” or “thalika bi-annahum kathaboo bi-ayatina wa kanu anha ghafileen,” the same denial with arrogance and heedlessness for which Pharaoh’s people were drowned. A common misunderstanding attributes the turning to arbitrary predestination. The verse shows the turning follows acting arrogantly without right, denying signs already come, and being heedless of them, while the fertile alternative remains to take the best of the Tablets with strength, remember the severe test, and be grateful, keeping the path straight so the home shown is not their home.",
  "147": "“The deeds of those who deny Our signs and the meeting ˹with Allah˺ in the Hereafter will be in vain. Will they be rewarded except for what they have done?” connects directly to the law of turning away from signs for arrogance without right. The previous verse warned that Allah will turn away from His signs those who act arrogantly, and even if they see every sign they will not believe, choosing the crooked path over the right one because they denied and were heedless. This verse states the consequence for that choice in terms of deed and recompense. The phrase “The deeds of those who deny Our signs and the meeting in the Hereafter will be in vain,” or “allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina wa liqaa al-akhirah habit at a‘maluhum,” uses “habitat,” to become vain, null, as earlier had they associated others their deeds would have been wasted and as magicians’ illusions became batil. “Liqa’ al-akhirah,” meeting in the Hereafter, is the same meeting the losers denied until the Hour surprised them and which the barrier’s dialogue confirmed as true.\n\nTwo denials combine: denying signs, or “ayat,” which includes the Book explained with knowledge, the winds and fertile-infertile lessons, the staff-snake and shining hand, the she-camel and the measure, and denying the meeting, which removes the motive to keep those signs. Together they make the scale light, because deeds done without acknowledging the Giver and without anticipation of weighing have no weight that the Just weighing will preserve, just as the gates of heaven do not open for the arrogant until an impossibility.\n\nThe rhetorical question, “Will they be rewarded except for what they have done?” or “hal yujzawna illa ma kanu ya‘maloon,” answers that recompense matches earning, as earlier they will each have ranks according to deeds and “none will be wronged” and “will they be rewarded except for what they have done” closes the circle. This is the law of ranks: the vain deeds cannot earn the Home of Peace where bitterness is removed and rivers flow.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think good worldly actions suffice without signs and without belief in meeting. The Quran says signs give direction and meeting gives purpose; denying either empties deeds of weight. For successors who will soon inherit blessed lands and for those who will make a calf that neither speaks nor guides, the verse warns that ignoring signs and forgetting the meeting, as those who took faith as play did, guarantees that what seems like thriving is actually producing hardly anything, until the home of the rebellious is shown and the barrier stands.",
  "148": "“In the absence of Moses, his people made from their ˹golden˺ jewellery an idol of a calf that made a lowing sound. Did they not see that it could neither speak to them nor guide them to the ˹Right˺ Path? Still they took it as a god and were wrongdoers” gives the concrete example of denying signs and meeting that makes deeds vain, immediately after stating that law. The phrase “In the absence of Moses,” or “wa ittakatha qawmu Moosa min ba‘dihi min huliyyihim ijlan jasadan lahu khuwar,” refers to the forty-night appointment during which Moses was told to take what was given firmly and ask his people to take the best of it. His people, left with Aaron’s counsel not to follow corruptors, used their “huliyy,” ornaments, jewellery, likely borrowed from Egyptians, to fashion a calf, or “ijlan,” a body, or “jasad,” with a lowing, or “khuwar,” sound produced by wind through a hollow form, as classical reports explain via Samiri’s craft.\n\nThe Quran then asks the question that should have prevented it: “Did they not see that it could neither speak to them nor guide them to the Right Path?” or “a-lam yaraw annahu la yukallimuhum wa la yahdihim sabila.” Speech and guidance are minimal expectations of divinity, yet the calf could do neither, just as earlier idols could not benefit or harm, could not restore hearing and sight, and could not rescue from darkness on land and at sea. A god that cannot speak cannot convey Lord’s messages or give sincere advice as Moses did, and cannot guide to the straight path made clear to the mindful, unlike the winds that usher mercy and the fertile land that produces abundantly.\n\nDespite that obvious deficiency, “Still they took it as a god and were wrongdoers,” or “wa ittakathoohu wa kanoo zalimeen,” they adopted it as “ilah” and thus placed worship where it does not belong, the definition of zulm. This is the same “it lacked speech and guidance” test that exposed the ’Ad’s invented names and the cattle taboos as without sultan.\n\nThe episode links to the earlier demand “Make for us a god like their gods” after crossing the sea; the same ignorance now builds one from jewellery, showing succession is tested quickly. A common error is to think idolatry requires believing the object is the ultimate creator. Here the people knew Allah rescued them from Pharaoh’s dreadful torment, yet they treated an ornament as a god alongside Him, proving that shirk can be attachment to a lifeless thing for mediation or beauty despite knowing the Lord of all worlds rescued them. The next verses will show remorse when they realized they went astray, illustrating the fertile possibility of return before the home of the rebellious becomes theirs.",
  "149": "“Later, when they were filled with remorse and realized they had gone astray, they cried, ‘If our Lord does not have mercy on us and forgive us, we will certainly be losers’” shows the immediate aftermath of calf-worship, turning the vain-deeds warning into a scene of repentance. The previous verse showed the calf could neither speak nor guide yet they took it as god and were wrongdoers. This verse describes the hearts that turn after that wrong: “when they were filled with remorse,” or “wa lamma suqita fi aidihim,” literally when it was dropped into their hands, an Arabic idiom for intense regret where hands are bitten, and “realized they had gone astray,” or “wa ra’aw annahum qad dalloo,” they saw they had strayed from the straight path just praised as “take the best of it” on the Tablets, the path they had been made successors to keep.\n\nTheir cry, “If our Lord does not have mercy on us and forgive us, we will certainly be losers,” or “la-in lam yarhamna Rabbuna wa yaghfir lana la-nakoonanna min al-khasireen,” repeats exactly the prayer of their parents Adam and his wife after the tree, “We have wronged ourselves, if You do not forgive us and have mercy we will be losers,” and the magicians’ prayer for perseverance and death as submitters. The words join rahmah and maghfirah, the two mercies whose fullness was earlier said to encompass everything and to be ordained for those who shun evil, pay alms, and believe. “Khasireen” losers is the same losers who felt secure from Allah’s planning, whose scales are light, and who were wiped as if never there, but here the speakers fear becoming them and seek to be moved instead to the saved by mercy like Noah’s Ark and the heirs of blessed lands.\n\nThis remorse contrasts with the arrogant who even if they see every sign will not believe and who prefer crooked paths. The calf worshippers’ remorse shows the heart, though turned away for a time, can still be turned back before sealing, unlike those who are turned away from signs as penalty for unjust arrogance.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think that serious shirk like calf worship closes the door permanently. The verse shows the door remains open for those filled with remorse who recognize they have strayed, as long as it occurs before the term that cannot be delayed and before belief after signs would not benefit. Their words become the model for every successor who inherits Scripture and indulges in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven,” that the next passages warn about, teaching that the correct response to realizing straying is not to claim forgiveness while persisting, but to cry for mercy and forgiveness with the humble secret calling that keeps the Direct Path intact.",
  "150": "“Upon Moses’ return to his people, ˹totally˺ furious and sorrowful, he said, ‘What an evil thing you committed in my absence! Did you want to hasten your Lord’s torment?’ Then he threw down the Tablets and grabbed his brother by the hair, dragging him closer” portrays the human intensity of a prophet whose people made the vain deed just warned about. The previous verse showed some worshippers filled with remorse crying for mercy lest they be losers. This verse shows Moses returning after the meeting with his Lord, the mountain levelled, the Tablets given to be held firmly. The description “totally furious and sorrowful,” or “ghadban asifa,” combines anger for Allah’s right violated and sorrow for his people’s self-ruin, the same paired emotions of hope and fear commanded for calling upon Him, and the same grief Shu‘ayb felt when turning away from the rejected. His rebuke, “What an evil thing you committed in my absence!” or “bi’sama khalaftumoonee min ba‘dee,” means how evil is what you left behind as successors after me, echoing being made successors to see what you will do. The question, “Did you want to hasten your Lord’s torment?” or “a-‘ajiltum amra Rabbikum,” accuses them of rushing the appointed term, as earlier they demanded to bring the threat if you are truthful, and as Pharaoh’s people said whatever sign you bring we will never believe. They hastened punishment by embracing the calf that cannot speak.\n\nThe act that follows is striking: “Then he threw down the Tablets and grabbed his brother by the hair, dragging him closer,” or “wa alqa al-alwaha wa akhatha bi-ra’si akhihi yajurruhu ilaih.” Throwing the Tablets is not disrespect for revelation but shock that the Fundamentals of everything were seemingly abandoned while he held them; the Tablets would be taken up again when anger subsided, containing guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe. Grabbing Aaron shows misdirected anger at the appointed guardian who was told not to follow corruptors, but Aaron’s next defense will explain he was overpowered.\n\nThe verse links to the broader succession theme: even a people honoured above others and given blessed lands can, in a few nights, take a lowing calf as god, proving that firm holding and taking the best of the Tablets is not automatic inheritance but requires vigilant remembrance. A common error is to read Moses’ anger as harshness toward Aaron. The Quran shows it is zeal for truth that subsides into mercy, as he immediately prays “My Lord, forgive me and my brother and admit us into Your mercy,” modeling that even great anger for Allah must resolve into supplication for forgiveness and mercy, the only way losers avoid becoming losers after grave error.",
  "151": "“Moses prayed, ‘My Lord! Forgive me and my brother! And admit us into Your mercy. You are the Most Merciful of the merciful’” is the supplication that follows Moses’ furious return, throwing the Tablets and seizing Aaron, and Aaron’s plea not to make enemies rejoice nor count him among wrongdoers. The previous verse left the scene tense with anger and sorrow. This verse resolves it with mercy-seeking that mirrors the magicians’ prayer and the calf-remorseful’s cry. Moses says, “My Lord! Forgive me and my brother!” or “Rabbi ighfir lee wa li-akhi,” forgiving his own haste in anger and any shortfall, and forgiving Aaron, whose stewardship was challenged. The request “And admit us into Your mercy,” or “wa adkhilna fi rahmatika,” asks to be enveloped in the rahmah earlier said to be taken upon Himself, close to good-doers, encompassing everything, and ordained for those who shun evil, pay alms, and believe, especially the mercy poured like winds before rain.\n\nThe closing, “You are the Most Merciful of the merciful,” or “wa anta arhamu al-rahimeen,” appeals to the attribute that makes perseverance pourable and death as submitters achievable, the same mercy that saved Noah’s Ark companions, Hud and Salih’s few, and Lot’s family except the doomed. This du‘a shows that even the elevated, chosen by messages and speech, need forgiveness, linking to the earlier “My Lord has elevated you above others, so hold firmly and be grateful” where elevation was followed by command to be thankful, not to claim immunity.\n\nThe prayer also heals community. By praying for his brother, Moses models that leadership disputes are resolved through joint seeking of mercy, not division into sects whose judgment rests only with Allah. The verse prepares for the next: those who worshipped the calf will be afflicted with wrath and disgrace, but those who repent after evil and believe, your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, showing the same mercy requested here extends to the repentant worshippers.\n\nA common misunderstanding sees prophetic anger as flawless righteousness. Moses’ prayer shows even righteous anger is followed by seeking forgiveness, teaching successors who inherit Scripture not to persist in claiming “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains, but to actually seek forgiveness with the humble, secret, hopeful fear that keeps the Tablets’ guidance alive after anger subsides.",
  "152": "“Those who worshipped the calf will certainly be afflicted with Allah’s wrath as well as disgrace in the life of this world. This is how We reward those who invent falsehood” announces the consequence for the specific vain deed of calf worship after Moses’ prayer for mercy for himself and his brother. The previous verses showed remorseful worshippers crying for mercy lest they be losers and Moses seeking forgiveness for both brothers, yet the divine law remains: deeds have recompense matching their weight. The phrase “Those who worshipped the calf will certainly be afflicted with Allah’s wrath as well as disgrace in the life of this world,” or “inna allatheena ittakhadhoo al-ijla sa-yanaluhum ghadabun min Rabbihim wa thillatun fil-hayati al-dunya,” combines two punishments already seen. Wrath, or “ghadab,” is the same rijs and ghadab said to have fallen on ’Ad for disputing names without sultan and on Pharaoh’s people after plagues, the divine anger whose planning only losers feel secure from. Disgrace, or “thillah,” is smallness, humiliation, the same saghir for Iblis and for those who feel secure, and for magicians threatened with cutting and crucifixion, now realized as communal abasement after the calf that made a lowing sound was taken as god that could not speak or guide.\n\nThe timing, “in the life of this world,” emphasizes that not all recompense is delayed until the weighing just or the barrier’s heights; some disgrace arrives promptly, as the calf was burned, its makers scattered, and the community ordered to slay the transgressors among them as later detailed.\n\nThe principle, “This is how We reward those who invent falsehood,” or “wa kathalika najzi al-muftareen,” generalizes the case. “Iftira’” is fabricating lie against Allah, the greatest wrong for which gates do not open, taking a calf as god being an invention that the idol devotees’ following was already called doomed and void. The same reward formula appeared for the wicked whose illusions failed and for those who will be shown the home of the rebellious.\n\nBut the next verse immediately opens the door still: those who commit evil then repent and believe, your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, distinguishing between persistence in invention and remorse that led to “If our Lord does not have mercy we will be losers.” A common mistake is to think worldly disgrace proves no mercy remains. The verse shows disgrace is the reward for invention, yet mercy remains reachable through repentance, the fertile alternative to infertile persistence in arrogance after seeing every sign and choosing crooked paths while heedless of signs.",
  "153": "“But those who commit evil, then repent and become ˹true˺ believers, your Lord will certainly be All-Forgiving, Most-Merciful” is the mercy that follows the wrath and disgrace threatened for the calf inventors, balancing the law that deeds are vain for deniers with the door that remains for the remorseful. The previous verse said those who worshipped the calf will be afflicted with wrath and disgrace in this world as reward for inventing falsehood. This verse immediately adds the exception that defines the Lord whose creation and command are His and whose mercy the winds herald: “But those who commit evil, then repent and become true believers,” or “wa allatheena amiloo al-sayyi’aat thumma taboo min ba‘diha wa amanoo.” “Sayyi’at” covers the calf worship, the demand for a god like theirs, and the taking of faith as play, the same evil earlier said to be done then repented after ignorance with mending, after which He is Forgiving, Merciful. “Thamma taboo” then repent, turn back, and “wa amanoo” and believe, shows both turning away from the lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides and turning toward the Lord whose Book explained with knowledge is guide and mercy.\n\nThe promise, “your Lord will certainly be All-Forgiving, Most-Merciful,” or “inna Rabbaka min ba‘diha la-Ghafoorun Raheem,” uses the same pair, Ghafur, covering, and Raheem, constantly merciful, that described the Patient who was also forgiving after the she-camel’s hamstringing and after the famine’s humbling. “Min ba‘diha” after it emphasizes after the evil, not before repentance, so mercy is not a blanket that allows claiming “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains again, as later generations who inherited Scripture do.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next scene where Moses’ anger subsides and he takes up the Tablets whose text contains guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe. The Tablets were thrown in anger but retrieved for the very purpose of guiding the repentant, showing that the Fundamentals of everything remain after invention’s disgrace.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads divine mercy as canceling consequences altogether. Here mercy follows repentance and belief, not persistence; the calf’s disgrace still occurred, but the individuals’ scales can be made heavy again. Another error despairs after great evil like calf worship. The verse answers despair with the same prayer the remorseful cried, that without mercy and forgiveness they would be losers, and assures that the Most Merciful of the merciful, whose mercy encompasses everything, has ordained mercy for those who shun evil, pay alms, and believe in Our revelations, as the next verses detail for the followers of the unlettered Prophet.",
  "154": "“When Moses’ anger subsided, he took up the Tablets whose text contained guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe of their Lord” returns to the Tablets thrown in fury and shows their proper place after wrath and after the promise of forgiveness for the repentant. The previous verse assured that those who do evil then repent and believe find the Lord All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. The verse before that threatened wrath and disgrace for calf inventors. This verse displays mercy’s instrument: the Tablets. The phrase “When Moses’ anger subsided,” or “wa lamma sakata an Moosa al-ghadab,” personifies anger as something that speaks and then falls silent, the same anger that made him “ghadban asifa,” furious, sorrowful. Its subsiding shows prophetic forbearance, as earlier patience until the best judge judges was commanded, and as Shu‘ayb and Salih turned away without lingering grief for disbelievers.\n\nHe then “took up the Tablets,” or “akhatha al-alwaha,” retrieving what was thrown, the same Tablets written with fundamentals of everything, commandments and explanations, to be held firmly and to be taken in its best. The text inside is described: “whose text contained guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe of their Lord,” or “wa fi nuskhatiha hudan wa rahmah lillatheena hum li-Rabbihim yarhaboon.” Guidance, or “huda,” is the straight path, the light that lets dead hearts walk among people, and mercy, or “rahmah,” is the closeness to good-doers and the winds that produce every fruit. They are for those who are “yarhaboon,” stand in awe, fearful reverence, the same fear enjoined when calling humbly with hope and fear, and the fear Hud asked “Will you not then fear Him?”\n\nThe link to earlier fertile-infertile is clear: Tablets’ guidance and mercy are the rain; those who stand in awe are fertile land producing abundantly, while those who act arrogantly without right are turned away from signs and even if they see every sign will not believe, preferring crooked paths.\n\nA common error treats scripture as relic to be respected only when calm. The verse shows the Tablets were thrown in anger yet retrieved for purpose: to guide the very people who had just invented falsehood and then felt remorse. For successors who will inherit Scripture and indulge in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven,” the Tablets model is to abide firmly by Scripture and establish prayer, whose reward is never discounted for the righteous, keeping guidance and mercy active, not as a calf that lows but as words that guide to the Lord who encompasses everything in knowledge and in whom trust is placed.",
  "155": "“Moses chose seventy men from among his people for Our appointment and, when they were seized by an earthquake, he cried, ‘My Lord! Had You willed, You could have destroyed them long ago, and me as well. Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done? This is only a test from You—by which You allow whoever you will to stray and guide whoever You will. You are our Guardian. So forgive us and have mercy on us. You are the best forgiver’” recounts the next trial after the Tablets’ guidance and mercy were reclaimed. The phrase “Moses chose seventy men from among his people for Our appointment,” or “wa ikhtara Moosa qawmahu sab‘eena rajulan li-meeqatina,” shows selection for the same forty-night meeqat, the appointed meeting, the same term language used for societies. The seventy represent the community’s elite, the “mala’” who opposed before, now chosen for nearness, testing whether elite status will now produce thankfulness. When they are “seized by an earthquake,” or “akhathathum al-rajfah,” the same rajfah that struck Thamud and Midian for denying signs and hamstringing the camel, it is not final destruction but a shaking that exposes the foolish among them who have done some folly, perhaps demanding to see Allah openly as later generations will imply by asking for a visible god.\n\nMoses’ cry combines theology and supplication. First, “Had You willed, You could have destroyed them long ago, and me as well,” acknowledges the Lord who if He wills can punish or make successors, who can seal hearts so they will not hear, yet who延迟 punishment while they were unaware. Second, “Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done?” distinguishes the community from the foolish minority, the “sufaha,” who invented the calf, a plea that not all be counted among the rebellious whose home is shown.\n\nThe key statement, “This is only a test from You—by which You allow whoever you will to stray and guide whoever You will,” or “in hiya illa fitnatuka tudillu biha man tasha’ wa tahdi man tasha’,” names the earthquake as fitnah, trial, the same word for the wind-driven rain test and the adversity-prosperity cycle, where the same event is guidance for the fertile and misguidance for the infertile who choose arrogance. The closing, “You are our Guardian. So forgive us and have mercy on us. You are the best forgiver,” or “anta waliyyuna fa-ighfir lana wa irhamna wa anta khairu al-ghafireen,” returns to the Guardian, or “Wali,” alone besides whom no guardians are taken, and to forgiveness that is best, matching his earlier prayer “Forgive me and my brother, You are the Most Merciful of the merciful.”\n\nA common misunderstanding sees the earthquake as mere punishment. Moses frames it as fitnah that reveals who will stray and who will be guided, inviting the successors who will inherit Scripture to remain among those who shun evil and are guided, rather than among those turned away from signs for arrogance.",
  "156": "“Ordain for us what is good in this life and the next. Indeed, we have turned to You ˹in repentance˺’ Allah replied, ‘I will inflict My torment on whoever I will. But My mercy encompasses everything. I will ordain mercy for those who shun evil, pay alms-tax, and believe in Our revelations’” continues Moses’ prayer after the seventy were seized and his plea that the earthquake is a test where You allow to stray and guide as You will, You are our Guardian, best forgiver. The first sentence, “Ordain for us what is good in this life and the next. Indeed, we have turned to You,” or “wa uktub lana fi hathihi al-dunya hasanatan wa fil-akhirati inna hudna ilaika,” is the request to “write for us,” or “uktub,” good, or “hasanah,” in both abodes, the same hasanah that earlier gave tenfold and rivers beneath and removal of bitterness. “Hudna ilaika” we have returned, turned, repented, the same return the magicians promised and the calf-remorseful cried, now formalized as the community’s tawbah.\n\nThe divine reply divides justice and mercy with precision. “I will inflict My torment on whoever I will,” or “qala athabee useebu bihi man asha’u,” affirms the planning that none feel secure from except losers, the plagues, famines, and drownings that took those who denied with heedlessness, the punishment that cannot be delayed when its term comes. “But My mercy encompasses everything,” or “wa rahmati wasi‘at kulla shay’,” is the sweeping principle that earlier appeared as “My Lord has taken mercy upon Himself” and “Your Lord is infinite in mercy” and “mercy close to good-doers.” “Wasi‘at” vast, like the night for rest and the winds that usher rain to lifeless land producing every fruit, covering even the foolish among the seventy if they turn.\n\nThe specification then narrows: “I will ordain mercy for those who shun evil, pay alms-tax, and believe in Our revelations,” or “fa-sa-aktubuha lillatheena yattaqoona wa yu’toona al-zakata wa allatheena hum bi-ayatina yu’minoon.” Three acts: taqwa, guarding as best clothing, zakat, the due of harvest and the just measure’s counterpart—giving what is due—and belief in ayat, the signs the arrogant are turned away from. This triplet becomes the profile of the unlettered Prophet’s followers described next, who enjoin good, forbid evil, permit tayyibat, forbid impurity, and are relieved of burdens, showing that hasanah in both worlds is written for that path.\n\nA common error claims mercy without conditions. The verse shows mercy’s vastness is universal in offer, yet its ordaining, or “katb,” as permanent possession is for the guarded, the giving, the believing, guiding the successor inheritors of Scripture away from saying “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains toward actually abiding firmly by Scripture and establishing prayer where reward is never discounted.",
  "157": "“˹They are˺ the ones who follow the Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, whose description they find in their Torah and the Gospel. He commands them to do good and forbids them from evil, permits for them what is lawful and forbids to them what is impure, and relieves them from their burdens and the shackles that bound them. ˹Only˺ those who believe in him, honour and support him, and follow the light sent down to him will be successful” defines more fully those for whom mercy was just ordained as shunning evil, paying zakat, and believing. The verse identifies them as followers of the Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, or “al-rasool al-nabiyy al-ummi,” the Prophet Muhammad, unlettered in the sense not taught by human writing, a sign that his knowledge comes from Him who knows from Allah what others do not. The description, “whose description they find in their Torah and the Gospel,” or “allathee yajidoonahu maktooban indahum fit-Tawrati wal-Injeel,” recalls those to whom We gave the Scripture recognize him as they recognize their children, confirming that the People of the Book know the blessed, detailed Book’s truth, removing the excuse that Scriptures were only for two groups before us and we were unaware.\n\nHis mission is then characterized in five actions: commanding good, or “ma‘roof,” forbidding evil, or “munkar,” permitting tayyibat, lawful good things, and forbidding khaba’ith, impure things, the same tayyibat earlier asked “Who has forbidden adornments and tayyibat Allah brought forth?” and the same khaba’ith forbidden as open and secret indecencies, sin, and waste. The final freeing, “relieves them from burdens and shackles,” or “wa yada‘u anhum israhum wa al-aghlala,” refers to the heavy yokes imposed by earlier laws, like the stringent restrictions on the Children of Israel, and the self-imposed burdens of fabricated cattle taboos and expulsion threats, all lifted by the mercy that encompasses everything.\n\nSuccess is then conditioned on four responses: believing in him, honouring and supporting him, and following the light sent down to him, or “al-noor allathee unzila ma‘ahu,” the Quran explained with knowledge as guide and mercy. These four replace the arrogant turning away from signs and choosing crooked paths; they are fertile soil that produces abundantly.\n\nA common misunderstanding separates the Prophet from the Tablets’ fundamentals. The verse shows his light confirms those fundamentals and makes them easily bearable, taking the best of them, so the hasanah written for this world and the next becomes attainable. For the inheritor of Scripture who indulges in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven,” the way to true forgiveness is to follow the unlettered Prophet whose description was already written, whose burdens’ removal is mercy ordained for the truly mindful.",
  "158": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘O humanity! I am Allah’s Messenger to you all. To Him ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him. He gives life and causes death.’ So believe in Allah and His Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, who believes in Allah and His revelations. And follow him, so you may be ˹rightly˺ guided” universalizes the previous description of the unlettered Prophet after defining his followers. The command “Say, O Prophet, O humanity! I am Allah’s Messenger to you all,” or “qul ya ayyuha al-nasu inni rasoolu Allahi ilaikum jamee‘an,” declares the scope long prepared: the Book sent down to warn the Mother of Cities and around it, the blessed Book reminder to whole worlds, the mercy ordained for the peoples after Moses, all culminating in a messenger to all, not to one town like Midian or to one people like ’Ad. This universality answers the earlier excuse “Scriptures were only for two groups before us.” Now a messenger to all stands with a light sent down to him.\n\nThe creed that follows, “To Him alone belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. There is no god worthy of worship except Him. He gives life and causes death,” or “allathee lahu mulku al-samawati wal-ard la ilaha illa huwa yuhyee wa yumeet,” is the same kingdom earlier said to belong to Him whose creation and command are His, who created heavens and earth in six Days, established on the Throne, makes night and day overlap, and subjects sun, moon, and stars. Giving life and causing death ties directly to winds producing every fruit and raising the dead, and to the sea that drowned Pharaoh and saved the oppressed. This is the Lord whose throne is above Pharaoh’s, whose earth is inherited by whom He chooses.\n\nThe call that follows completes the circle: “So believe in Allah and His Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, who believes in Allah and His revelations. And follow him, so you may be rightly guided,” or “fa-aminoo billahi wa rasoolihi al-nabiyyi al-ummiyyi allathee yu’minu billahi wa kalimatihi wa ittabi‘oohu la‘allakum tahtadoon.” Belief is in both the Sender and the sent, and following, or “ittiba‘,” the same following earlier commanded for what was sent down without taking other guardians, is the path that is perfectly straight made clear to the mindful, where the best clothing is righteousness and where hasanah is written for this world and the next.\n\nA common error limits the Prophet to tribal scope. The verse makes him humanity’s messenger, whose light relieves burdens and whose shackles’ removal proves mercy encompassing everything, inviting every successor who inherits land to choose the fertile guidance that produces abundantly over the infertile that hardly produces, and to be among those on the heights greeted with peace because they believed and did good within capacity, to be told “Enter Paradise, no fear for you.”",
  "159": "“There are some among the people of Moses who guide with the truth and establish justice accordingly” provides a balanced note after the universal call to follow the unlettered Prophet, showing that among the former community not all were lost to calf worship and rebellion. The previous verses traced the Children of Israel from rescue, through demand for a god, calf invention, remorse, wrath and disgrace, then repentance and mercy, Tablets with guidance, the seventy’s earthquake and prayer for hasanah, and the promise to follow the unlettered Prophet whose light relieves burdens. Now the Quran acknowledges, “There are some among the people of Moses who guide with the truth and establish justice accordingly,” or “wa min qawmi Moosa ummatun yahdoona bil-haqqi wa bihi ya‘diloona.” “Ummah” means a community, a group, not merely individuals, and “yahdoona bil-haqq,” guide with truth, is the same truth whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds. “Ya‘diloona” establish justice, or “adl,” accordingly, is the same justice commanded as uprightness, or qist, and as giving full measure and weighing with justice.\n\nThis commendation mirrors earlier commendations of the lowly who believed among Thamud and of the magicians who believed after seeing the devouring staff, showing that within every people there is fertile soil producing abundantly while other soil hardly produces. It prepares for the next details where twelve tribes, each with community, are guided to springs and shaded with clouds and given manna and quails, and where some among them guide with truth while others change words and break Sabbaths, illustrating the varied lessons for the thankful versus the heedless.\n\nThe verse also links to the broader theme of succession: those who take over land after former residents include both righteous and less so, dispersed through land and tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they return. Recognizing a truth-guiding, justice-establishing community among the People of Moses prevents generalization and shows that the noble Word fulfilled for those who patiently endured extends to individuals who remain true to covenant while most were found rebellious.\n\nA common misunderstanding paints an entire community as uniformly condemned after the calf. The verse corrects that: among the same people there remains an ummah that guides by the Book that was explained with knowledge, a guide and mercy for those who believe, holding firmly to the best of the Tablets and abiding firmly by Scripture and establishing prayer where reward is never discounted, whose example the new universal community is invited to follow by following the unlettered Prophet.",
  "160": "“We divided them into twelve tribes— each as a community. And We revealed to Moses, when his people asked for water, ‘Strike the rock with your staff.’ Then twelve springs gushed out. Each tribe knew its drinking place. We shaded them with clouds and sent down to them manna and quails, ˹saying˺, ‘Eat from the good things We have provided for you.’ They ˹certainly˺ did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves” details Allah’s meticulous care for the twelve communities hinted at as guiding with truth but also many who were rebellious, showing providence after succession to blessed lands. The phrase “We divided them into twelve tribes—each as a community,” or “wa qatta‘nahum ithnatai ‘ashrata asbatan umaman,” shows divine ordering, twelve distinct umam, like the varied lessons for the thankful, where each knew its boundary, preventing strife over shares, unlike the earlier unjust division of crops for associates where the associate’s share was not shared with Allah but Allah’s was shared with associates.\n\nThe next miracle, “We revealed to Moses, when his people asked for water, ‘Strike the rock with your staff.’ Then twelve springs gushed out. Each tribe knew its drinking place,” provides water in a desert where famine and shortage had been a plague for Pharaoh, now turned to mercy. The staff that was a snake and that devoured illusions now strikes rock to produce twelve eyes of water, the same staff that struck the sea to part it, demonstrating that the One who sends winds with heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit can also bring water from stone, and that creation and command belong to Him. Each tribe knowing its spring mirrors the just measure without defrauding, as earlier “give just measure and weight, do not defraud people” was the test for Midian.\n\nThe further bounties, “We shaded them with clouds and sent down to them manna and quails,” adds shade in scorching desert, like garments sent down to cover and adorn, and heavenly food, manna with gentle sweetness and quails for meat, saying “Eat from the tayyibat We have provided,” the same tayyibat earlier asked “Who has forbidden adornments and tayyibat,” now freely given. The verdict, “They did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves,” or “wa ma zalamona wa lakin kanoo anfusahum yazlimoon,” shows the self-dooming principle: light scales doom themselves for wrongfully denying signs, not the Lord who is never absent and whose mercy encompasses everything.\n\nA common error sees provision as entitlement to change words, as the next verse shows they changed “Absolve us” and were punished from heaven. The verse teaches that twelve-fold provision with known drinking places is a mercy test to be eaten thankfully and justly, producing abundant fruit for the thankful rather than hardly anything for the rebellious.",
  "161": "“And ˹remember˺ when it was said to them, ‘Enter this city ˹of Jerusalem˺ and eat from wherever you please. Say, ‘Absolve us,’ and enter the gate with humility. We will forgive your sins, ˹and˺ We will multiply the reward for the good-doers’” recalls the next merciful invitation after provision in the wilderness. The previous verse detailed twelve springs, shaded clouds, manna and quails, with the reminder they wronged themselves not Us. This verse shows the next stage of succession: entry into a blessed city, or “qaryah,” often understood as Jerusalem, the holy land whose eastern and western tracts were blessed. The command, “Enter this city and eat from wherever you please,” or “uskunoo hathihi al-qaryata wa kuloo minha haithu shi’tum,” mirrors the generosity of “We brought you a Book which We explained” and the earlier “eat from tayyibat” and “dress for worship, eat without waste,” an abundance with few conditions.\n\nThe conditions are deceptively small but revealing: “Say, ‘Absolve us,’ and enter the gate with humility,” or “wa qooloo hittatun wa udkhuloo al-baba sujjadan.” “Hittatun” means forgive, relieve, drop our burdens, the same burden-relief offered by the unlettered Prophet who relieves shackles, and “sujjadan” prostrating, bowing with humility, the posture the magicians took when truth prevailed and the posture of the righteous. The promise, “We will forgive your sins, and We will multiply the reward for the good-doers,” or “naghfir lakum khatee’atikum sa-nazeedu al-muhsineen,” links forgiveness to humility, as earlier the heights hoped to enter Paradise and the repentant after calf worship were forgiven with rahmah, and increase for muhsin is the tenfold and more promised, the mercy close to good-doers.\n\nThe verse connects to the larger theme of succession after Pharaoh: you were made successors to see what you will do, and here the doing is tested by a simple entry rite, just as Thamud were tested by leaving the she-camel to graze and Midian by giving just measure. The same humility, or “sujud,” was asked of Iblis for Adam and refused as “I am better.” Successors are now asked to prostrate to enter.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats such rites as mere ritual. The Quran shows the small request measures the heart’s willingness to be mindful, to be among the thankful who produce abundantly versus the infertile who change words, as the next verse shows the wrongdoers did, incurring punishment from heaven for their wrongdoing, while the gate of forgiveness remains open for the humble who say hittah and enter bowed.",
  "162": "“But the wrongdoers among them changed the words they were commanded to say. So We sent down a punishment from the heavens upon them for their wrongdoing” records the failure at the city gate that follows the merciful invitation to enter, say “Absolve us,” enter humbly, be forgiven, and be increased for good-doing. The previous verse set the test: eat wherever you please, but speak one word of repentance and assume one posture of humility. This verse says, “But the wrongdoers among them changed the words they were commanded to say,” or “fa-baddala allatheena zalamoo minhum qawlan ghaira allathee qeela lahum,” they substituted another saying, often reported as a word mocking or distorting “hittah,” turning repentance into jest, the same “baddala” used for changing a covenant and for the earlier “We did not find most of them true to covenant.” The change was not mere mispronunciation but deliberate alteration of revelation after it was explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, paralleling those turned away from signs for arrogance who even if they see every sign will not believe and who prefer crooked paths.\n\nThe response is swift and from above: “So We sent down a punishment from the heavens upon them for their wrongdoing,” or “fa-arsalna alaihim rijzan mina al-sama’i bima kanu yazlimoon.” “Rijs” here is torment, plague, the same rijs and wrath said to have fallen on ’Ad and the dreadful torment threatened for the calf worshippers, now from the sky as earlier floods, locusts, lice, frogs, blood, and the drowning sea came from above and below. “Mina al-sama’i” emphasizes heavenly authority, the Lord who created heavens and earth in six Days and established on the Throne, whose creation and command are His, sends recompense from the same heaven that earlier shaded them with clouds and sent manna. “Bima kanu yazlimoon” for their wrongdoing links to ranks according to deeds, that none are wronged, that deeds of those who deny signs and the meeting are vain.\n\nThis episode links directly to the succession test “to see what you will do” after being made heirs of eastern and western blessed lands; entry with humble “hittah” would have shown thankful fertility, but changing the word showed infertile hardness that hardly produces, like the town by the sea that broke the Sabbath next described. A common error is to minimize word-changing as trivial. The Quran shows altering a commanded word while entering a blessed city is betrayal of the Tablets’ fundamentals to be held firmly and to take the best of, inviting a heavenly rijs, whereas the humble who say “Hittah” and enter bowed are forgiven and increased, teaching successors to keep words as revealed without substitution before the term that cannot be delayed.",
  "163": "“Ask them ˹O Prophet˺ about ˹the people of˺ the town which was by the sea, who broke the Sabbath. During the Sabbath, ˹abundant˺ fish would come to them clearly visible, but on other days the fish were never seen. In this way We tested them for their rebelliousness” introduces the Sabbath town story as another example of word-changing and rebellious response to a simple test, continuing the city-entry failure just before. The previous verse told of wrongdoers who changed “Hittah” at the gate and were punished from heaven. Now the town by the sea, often identified as Ayla or a coastal town among the Israelites, is presented as a parallel Sabbath test. The command to Sabbath, or “sabtihim,” a day made sacred for rest and devotion, like night for rest and worship adornment, was a clear limit: no fishing, no worldly work, the single tree not to be approached amid abundance where they could eat from wherever they pleased.\n\nThe test design is detailed: “During the Sabbath, abundant fish would come to them clearly visible, but on other days the fish were never seen,” or “ith ta’tihim heetanuhum yawma sabtihim shurra‘an wa yawma la yasbitoona la ta’tihim.” Fish appearing as “shurra‘an,” surfacing, abundant, conspicuous, on the forbidden day and hiding on permitted days shows the trial, like winds bringing heavy clouds to lifeless land producing fruit versus infertile land hardly producing. The abundance on the forbidden day tempted them to craft legalistic tricks, setting nets before Sabbath to collect after, breaking the spirit while keeping form, the same corruption after order warned against in “do not spread corruption after its reform.”\n\nThe purpose, “In this way We tested them for their rebelliousness,” or “kathalika nabloohum bima kanu yafsuqoon,” names “fisq,” rebellious stepping out, as earlier “most of them truly rebellious” and “We did not find most true to covenant.” The trial exposes whether successors will be among the thankful whose varying lessons produce abundantly or among the infertile whose ruse hardly produces but invites dreadful punishment.\n\nA common misunderstanding reduces Sabbath-breaking to minor ritual lapse. The Quran frames it as appointed-term disobedience, a test of whether a community will honor the Creator’s command over immediate gain, like the she-camel left to graze, the just measure, and the “Hittah” gate. The next verses will show the community’s split into breakers, warners, and the silent, and the rescue of warners versus the dreadful punishment for the wrongdoers and transformation into disgraced apes for persistent violation, making the town-by-sea a mirror for every successor who inherits Scripture and then indulges in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven,” while hunting fish with tricks or words with changes.",
  "164": "“When some of ˹the righteous among˺ them questioned ˹their fellow Sabbath-keepers˺, ‘Why do you ˹bother to˺ warn those ˹Sabbath-breakers˺ who will either be destroyed or severely punished by Allah?’ They replied, ‘Just to be free from your Lord’s blame, and so perhaps they may abstain’” presents the internal debate among the Sabbath town’s three groups, clarifying the duty of reminding despite apparent hopelessness. The previous verse set the test: fish abundant on Sabbath, hidden otherwise, We tested them for rebelliousness. Now two responses within the town are contrasted. One group are warners against evil, the same role as messengers conveying Lord’s messages with sincere advice, calling humbly without transgressing. Another group questions the usefulness of warning the breakers who will either be destroyed or severely punished, echoing the earlier “Should we warn those who will be uprooted?” This question reflects a natural doubt: why bother when the appointment seems set and when the rebellious even if they see every sign will not believe? It mirrors the chiefs’ logic that numbers and arrogance prove truth, suggesting warning the doomed is wasted effort.\n\nThe warners’ answer gives two motives that define the ethic of da‘wah taught throughout this surah. First, “Just to be free from your Lord’s blame,” or “ma‘thiratan ila Rabbikum,” to have an excuse before your Lord, to establish proof that the message was delivered, as earlier “This is because your Lord would never destroy a society while its people are unaware” and “so that We may question those who received messengers.” Excuse before Allah is the same as “so perhaps you will be mindful” and “so you may be warned.” Failure to warn would leave the warners themselves liable for not following what was sent down. Second, “and so perhaps they may abstain,” or “wa la‘allahum yattaqoon,” so perhaps they will become mindful, guarded, the same “perhaps” used for winds raising dead so you will be mindful and for the seventy’s prayer for hasanah. This hope reflects mercy close to good-doers and the fertile expectation that even hard soil may yet produce.\n\nThe exchange links to the next verse where warners are rescued and wrongdoers overtaken with dreadful punishment for rebelliousness, while the middle group that questioned is not named with rescue, teaching that the hopeful warning is itself the path to being among the heavy scales. A common error is to silence warnings because outcome seems fixed. The warners show that warning is worship that frees from blame and keeps the door of “perhaps they may abstain” open, which is the very purpose of the blessed Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy for the believing, to be held firmly and taken in its best.",
  "165": "“When they ignored the warning they were given, We rescued those who used to warn against evil and overtook the wrongdoers with a dreadful punishment for their rebelliousness” gives the term’s fulfilment for the Sabbath town, matching the pattern of every story from Noah to Midian and now the sea town. The previous verse showed warners answering why they bother: to be excused before the Lord and so perhaps the breakers may abstain. This verse shows the result when that “perhaps” is refused: “When they ignored the warning they were given,” or “fa-lamma nasoo ma thukkiroo bihi,” when they forgot, neglected, deliberately put behind what they were reminded, the same “nasoo” used for those who take faith as play and are then forgotten as they forgot the meeting, and for those who forgot that night and day belong to Him. Forgetting the reminder after the fish test is the heedlessness, or “ghafleen,” for which Pharaoh’s people were drowned.\n\nThe division is then stated: “We rescued those who used to warn against evil,” or “anjaina allatheena yanhawna ani al-soo’i,” those who forbade evil, as earlier “do not spread corruption after order” and “call humbly without transgressing” enjoined. Their rescue mirrors Noah’s Ark, Hud’s mercy, Salih’s mercy, Lot’s family except the doomed, and the oppressed made successors of blessed lands. “And overtook the wrongdoers with a dreadful punishment for their rebelliousness,” or “wa akhathna allatheena zalamoo bi-athabin ba’eesin bima kanu yafsuqoon,” uses “ba’ees,” dreadful, severe, the same severe that makes the home of the rebellious shown, and “bima kanu yafsuqoon” for their fisq, rebellious stepping out, the same word for most found rebellious and not true to covenant.\n\nThe verse distinguishes fate between warners and wrongdoers at the same term that cannot be delayed, as earlier the barrier distinguished Paradise and Hell and the weighing distinguished heavy from light. The middle group that questioned warning but did not warn is left unnamed in this rescue, a silent warning about silence.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat “warners rescued” as automatically saved by role. The Quran shows they were rescued because they warned against evil, the same “sincere advice” Moses gave, the same “give just measure” Shu‘ayb gave, the same “do not harm the she-camel” Salih gave, all variations of the Fundamentals written on the Tablets to be taken firmly. For successors inheriting Scripture and tempted to indulge in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven,” the lesson is to be among those who warn against evil with the excuse of hope, not among the silent, before the dreadful wound arrives for the rebellious who persisted despite being reminded.",
  "166": "“But when they stubbornly persisted in violation, We said to them, ‘Be disgraced apes!’” follows the rescue of warners and the overtaking with dreadful punishment for the Sabbath town, intensifying the recompense for persistent rebelliousness. The previous verse already said We rescued warners and overtook wrongdoers with dreadful punishment for their fisq. This verse adds the specific transformation for stubborn persistence. The phrase “But when they stubbornly persisted in violation,” or “fa-lamma ataw amma nuhoo anhu,” when they exalted themselves, crossed the limit in what they were forbidden, as earlier “act unjustly with arrogance in the land” and “spread corruption after order,” the limit was the Sabbath’s “do not fish,” a single tree-like prohibition amid abundant other days’ fishing. Persisting, or “ataw,” means they exceeded, were obstinate, hardened, like Pharaoh who even if they see every sign will not believe and who took devils as masters.\n\nThe sentence “We said to them, ‘Be disgraced apes!’” or “qulna lahum koonoo qiradatan khasi’een,” is a creative command from the Lord whose creation and command belong to Him alone, who created heavens and earth in six Days, made day and night overlap, and subjected sun, moon, and stars. “Khoonoo” be, like the “Be!” that brings dead to life and the “Be!” that levels mountains, and “qiradatan khasi’een,” apes driven away, humiliated, the same “khasieen” and “saghiroon” used for Iblis and Pharaoh’s shamed defeat and for those put to shame. Apes are chosen as image because the violators used legalistic trickery to hunt fish with nets, an outwardly human cunning that inwardly mimics animal mimicry without understanding, and because their community had requested a god like theirs after the sea and now mimics transgression.\n\nThe transformation matches the earlier threat to show the home of the rebellious and the sealing for arrogance that makes crooked paths appealing. It is the visible end of being turned away from signs after denying and being heedless, where deeds become vain.\n\nA common error is to read this as unrelated harshness. The Quran links it to persistent violation after warning, after being given heavy clouds’ rain and fertile lessons, after being told to take the best of the Tablets, after being given excuse and hope of abstaining, yet choosing stubbornness until the dreadful widened to disgrace. For inheritors of Scripture who indulge in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven” and who repeat juridical tricks, the ape metaphor warns that legalism without taqwa makes the outer human while the inner is animal cunning, inviting the dread of being among the disgraced, while the luminous alternative remains to firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, whose reward the righteous never lose.",
  "167": "“And ˹remember, O Prophet,˺ when your Lord declared that He would send against them others who would make them suffer terribly until the Day of Judgment. Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” follows the transformation into disgraced apes and shows that the Sabbath violation was only one station in a longer succession. The previous verses described Sabbath testers who broke despite warning and were made apes, and warned successors not to feel secure by day while at play. This verse widens the lens to the subsequent history of the Children of Israel after inheritance of blessed lands. The phrase “when your Lord declared,” or “wa ith ta’aththana Rabbuka,” means He announced and made known firmly, that He would send against them “others who would make them suffer terribly until the Day of Judgment,” or “la-yab‘athanna alaihim ila yawmi al-qiyamati man yasoomuhum soo’a al-athab.” This declaration was fulfilled through various dominators— Babylonians, Romans, and others—who subjugated and exiled them, a reminder that succession as blessed land heirs does not grant immunity from the term that cannot be delayed.\n\nThe verse then balances attributes, as has become a refrain after each major story: “Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “inna Rabbaka la-saree‘u al-iqab wa innahu la-Ghafoorun Raheem.” This pair appeared at the close of the previous surah for the same reason: succession is test, the earth belongs to Allah who grants it to whom He wills, and swift punishment is for the persistent wicked who spread corruption, violate Sabbath with trickery, and sell Scripture for unlawful gains as the next verses describe, while all-forgiving mercy awaits those who shun evil, pay zakat, and follow the unlettered Prophet.\n\nThe declaration links to the earlier “We dispersed them through the land in groups—some righteous, others less so” and to testing with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return. It shows that dispersion itself is part of the send-against pattern, a living reminder that being successors after former residents is a loan, not ownership.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this declaration as collective condemnation without hope. The verse’s second half corrects that: the same Lord who is swift for the rebellious is certainly forgiving, merciful for those who turn, remain mindful, and firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted. For contemporary heirs of Scripture, the lesson is to read the declaration as warning to remain among the righteous group rather than the less so, lest suffering until the Day become their share while mercy encompasses others who return.",
  "168": "“We dispersed them through the land in groups—some were righteous, others were less so. We tested them with prosperity and adversity, so perhaps they would return ˹to the Right Path˺” explains the form of the declaration just made about sending others to make them suffer until the Day, turning it into a principle of dispersion and trial. The phrase “We dispersed them through the land in groups,” or “wa qatta‘nahum fil-ardi umaman,” means We cut them, divided them, like the twelve tribes divided as communities with twelve springs, now scattered as many umam across lands, not one consolidated empire like Pharaoh’s constructions. The division, “some were righteous, others were less so,” or “minhum al-salihoon wa minhum doona thalika,” shows diversity within dispersion: not all are condemned, as earlier “among the people of Moses a community guides with truth and justice,” and among the dispersed some will be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, while others indulge in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven.” The dispersion preserves both the book and the test.\n\nThe purpose, “We tested them with prosperity and adversity, so perhaps they would return,” or “wa balawnahum bil-hasanati wa al-sayyi’ati la‘allahum yarji‘oon,” repeats the adversity-prosperity cycle described for earlier societies: afflicted with suffering so perhaps they would be humbled, then changed to prosperity until they flourished and said forefathers also had cycles, so seized while unaware. Here the two tests are named “hasanat” good things and “sayyi’at” bad, the same pair that produces the fertile land’s abundant produce versus infertile hardly producing. Both are tests, not mere fortunes, like winds producing every fruit versus floods that drown.\n\nThis verse links succession after destruction to ongoing examination. Being made successors to see what you will do is not a single exam at entry but a repeated course of ease and hardship, each calling to return, or “yarji‘oon,” to the Right Path, the straight way taken by those who firmly abide and establish prayer, versus the crooked that the rebellious prefer.\n\nA common error interprets dispersion as abandonment. The Quran frames it as mercy’s last school: scattered groups still carry Scripture, still have examples of righteous among them, still experience prosperity and adversity as teachers, so perhaps they will return before the home of the rebellious is shown and hearts are sealed for seeing every sign yet not believing. For those who inherit Scripture as later generations do, the call is to be the righteous group that guides with truth, not the less so that barters Scripture for petty gain.",
  "169": "“Then they were succeeded by other generations who inherited the Scripture. They indulged in unlawful gains, claiming, ‘We will be forgiven ˹after all˺.’ And if similar gain came their way, they would seize it. Was a covenant not taken from them in the Scripture that they would not say anything about Allah except the truth? And they were already well-versed in its teachings. But the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter is far better for those mindful ˹of Allah˺. Will you not then understand?” portrays the successors of the dispersed. The previous verse said they were tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return; this verse shows what happened when new generations inherited the book. The phrase “They indulged in unlawful gains,” or “fa-khalafa min ba‘dihim khalfun warithoo al-kitaba ya’khuthoona arada hatha al-adna,” means they took the near, lowly worldly gain, “arad,” a passing advantage like bribe money for distorting judgment, like selling “hittah” at the gate or trading Sabbath fish, treating Scripture as a commodity as earlier the chiefs traded faith as play. Their claim, “We will be forgiven after all,” or “wa yaqooloona sa-yughfaru lana,” is the same false security earlier condemned where some said adornments are forbidden fabrications and then claimed Allah commanded shame, assuming forgiveness without repentance. The proof of insincerity, “And if similar gain came their way, they would seize it,” shows claiming forgiveness did not stop seizing; the infertile land remained infertile despite varied waters.\n\nThe reproach, “Was a covenant not taken from them in the Scripture that they would not say anything about Allah except the truth?” or “a-lam yu’khadh alaihim meethaq al-kitabi an la yaqooloo ala Allahi illa al-haqq,” recalls the mithaq, binding pledge, like the primordial covenant “Am I not your Lord? They said Yes,” and like the prophet-specific covenants, that they would speak only truth about Allah, not “What they follow is doomed” yet followed, not “money is ours” while claiming mercy. “And they were already well-versed in its teachings,” or “wa darasoo ma fihi,” they studied what is in it, so ignorance cannot excuse.\n\nThe evaluation closes, “But the eternal Home of the Hereafter is far better for those mindful. Will you not then understand?” or “wa al-daru al-akhiratu khairun lillatheena yattaqoon a-fa-la ta‘qiloon,” the same Home of Peace and better Home earlier contrasted with play, showing the mindful choose lasting gain over lowly “arad.”\n\nA common misunderstanding treats inheritance of Scripture as guarantee. The verse shows inheritance without abiding firmly produces the “We will be forgiven” illusion, while only those who firmly abide and establish prayer have reward never discounted, teaching the living to be of the mindful who understand, not the well-versed who seize.",
  "170": "“As for those who firmly abide by the Scripture and establish prayer—surely We never discount the reward of those acting righteously” provides the counter-example to the unlawful-gain successors just condemned. The previous verse showed generations who inherited the Book, seized lowly gain, claimed “We will be forgiven,” broke the covenant to speak only truth, though well-versed. Now the Quran praises the opposite group. The phrase “As for those who firmly abide by the Scripture,” or “wa allatheena yumassikoona bil-kitab,” uses “yumassikoona,” to hold fast, to cling firmly, to grip with strength as earlier Moses was told “Hold firmly to that Scripture which We have given you” when the mountain was raised over them like a cloud and as Moses was told “Hold to this firmly and ask your people to take the best of it” regarding the Tablets. Firm abiding contrasts with taking the Book lightly to trade for a price.\n\nThe second trait, “and establish prayer,” or “wa aqamoo al-salah,” is the same establishment earlier linked to being mindful and not taking other guardians, to calling humbly and secretly with hope and fear, and to the twelve springs where each tribe knew its drinking place in a well-ordered community. Establishing prayer is the daily holding fast that keeps covenant alive.\n\nThe promise, “surely We never discount the reward of those acting righteously,” or “inna la nudee‘u ajra al-musliheen,” uses “musliheen,” those who set right, reform, act righteously after the admonition not to spread corruption after order. This mirrors “We never discount the reward of those acting righteously” elsewhere and links to the tenfold reward and the rivers beneath where bitterness is removed. It also contrasts with deeds that become vain for those who deny signs and the meeting; here deeds are preserved because they are built on firm holding.\n\nA common error imagines that being among the righteous group dispersed or among the successors automatically secures reward. The verse conditions reward on firm holding and established prayer, not mere inheritance or knowledge. For inheritors of the Quran explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the instruction is to be like the fertile land that produces abundantly by the Lord’s leave, taking the Scripture with strength, observing its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful, rather than like those turned away from signs for arrogance who, even if they see every sign, will not believe and will choose the crooked path while heedless of signs.",
  "171": "“And ˹remember˺ when We raised the mountain over them as if it were a cloud and they thought it would fall on them. ˹We said,˺ ‘Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful ˹of Allah˺’” recalls the covenant-taking scene that underlies firm abiding and the unlawful-gain successors. The previous verse praised those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer; this verse shows how that firmness was first demanded. The phrase “when We raised the mountain over them as if it were a cloud,” or “wa ith nataqna al-jabala fawqahum ka-annahu zullah,” describes the mountain, often identified as Mount Sinai, lifted and suspended above the Children of Israel, looming like a shaded cloud, the same mountain that earlier, when Moses asked to see his Lord, was levelled to dust at a self-disclosure, showing its own powerlessness before the Lord who created heavens and earth in six Days. The reaction, “and they thought it would fall on them,” or “wa zannoo annahu waqi‘un bihim,” means certainty of collapse, the imminent dread that should humble the chiefs who felt secure from Allah’s planning by night while asleep or day while at play, and who demanded signs as deception.\n\nThe command given under that awe is quoted: “Hold firmly to that Scripture which We have given you and observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful,” or “khuthoo ma atainakum bi-quwwatin wa uthkuroo ma fihi la‘allakum tattaqoon.” This repeats the Tablets’ “Hold to this firmly and ask your people to take the best of it” and Moses’ “hold firmly to what I have given you and be grateful.” Strength, or “quwwah,” and remembrance, or “uthkuroo,” are the two pillars of the best clothing of righteousness and of the fertile land’s abundant production.\n\nThe scene also explains why the covenant not to say about Allah except truth was so firm: it was taken under a mountain, an unmistakable sign that the creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds. A common misunderstanding views this raised mountain as coercion that negates choice. The Quran shows the mountain created awe, not compulsion of hearts, as later many broke the covenant despite seeing the mountain and the sea, confirming that seeing every sign without humility still leads to choosing the crooked path. The lesson for successors who claim “We will be forgiven” is to recall the mountain’s shadow and take the Scripture with strength before it falls, so perhaps mindfulness keeps the scales heavy.",
  "172": "“And ˹remember˺ when your Lord brought forth from the loins of the children of Adam their descendants and had them testify regarding themselves. ˹Allah asked,˺ ‘Am I not your Lord?’ They replied, ‘Yes, You are! We testify.’ ˹He cautioned,˺ ‘Now you have no right to say on Judgment Day, ‘We were not aware of this’” moves from the mountain covenant with the Children of Israel to the primordial covenant of all humanity, rooting every later covenant in an original testimony. The previous verse showed the mountain raised like a cloud to make successors hold firmly so perhaps they will become mindful. This verse goes deeper to the loins, or “min zuhoorihim,” from the backs of the children of Adam, bringing forth every descendant, a scene of all future humanity gathered as seeds before being placed as successors on earth where winds usher mercy and tests alternate. The question, “Am I not your Lord?” or “a-lastu bi-Rabbikum,” asks for acknowledgment of lordship that was earlier described as the Lord who creates in six Days, subjects sun, moon, and stars, sends provision, and alone deserves worship, the same Lord whose Word is perfected.\n\nThe answer, “Yes, You are! We testify,” or “qaloo bala shahidna,” is the innate recognition that later generations suppress when they take other guardians, claim Allah commanded shame, or demand a god like the idol devotees’ gods. The caution that follows explains the purpose of this testimony: “Now you have no right to say on Judgment Day, ‘We were not aware of this,’” or “an taqooloo yawma al-qiyamati inna kunna an hatha ghafileen.” Heedlessness, or “ghafleen,” was the excuse offered by those who said Scriptures were only for two groups before us and we were unaware of their teachings. This primordial bearing of witness removes that excuse universally, establishing that fitrah, innate knowledge of the Lord, was sown before any society’s term.\n\nThe verse links to the next, which anticipates the other excuse, “It was our forefathers who associated and we followed,” showing both personal unawareness and ancestral imitation are nullified by this testimony. A common misunderstanding thinks covenant is only historical for the Israelites under the mountain. This verse universalizes it: every child of Adam testified, so every successor who inherits Scripture and indulges in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven” cannot claim unawareness, nor can those turned away from signs for arrogance claim they never knew the Right Path. The signs varied for the thankful are reminders, not first introductions, calling the heart back to its first “Yes, You are our Lord,” before the Day fulfilment leaves only self-ruin and vanished fabrications.",
  "173": "“Nor say, ‘It was our forefathers who had associated others ˹with Allah in worship˺ and we, as their descendants, followed in their footsteps. Will you then destroy us for the falsehood they invented?’” continues the cautions attached to the primordial testimony. The previous verse removed the excuse “We were not aware” by recalling the testimony “Am I not your Lord? Yes, we testify.” This verse removes the second excuse: ancestral responsibility. The imagined plea, “It was our forefathers who had associated and we followed in their footsteps,” or “innama ashraka aba’una min qablu wa kunna thurriyyatan min ba‘dihim,” is the classic justification used by ’Ad, Thamud, Midian, and Mecca when told to abandon what forefathers worshipped. They asked “Have you come to make us abandon what our forefathers used to worship?” and claimed forefathers’ cycles explain affliction and prosperity. Here the Quran preempts that line before it can be offered on the Day the barrier stands and scales weigh.\n\nThe question, “Will you then destroy us for the falsehood they invented?” or “a-fa-tuhlikuna bima fa‘ala al-mubtiloon,” suggests that if ancestors innovated the divide of crops for associates, the calf’s lowing, or the Sabbath trickery, the descendants who merely inherited it are victims. The covenant and the varied signs answer that each generation is made successors to see what it will do, tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they will return, and given a Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy. Inheritance without examination is itself a choice, as earlier those who took faith as play were deluded, not compelled.\n\nThe verse connects to the next principle, “This is how We make our signs clear, so perhaps they will return,” showing that signs are clarified precisely to prevent blind following. The mubtileen, falsifiers, are those who invent, and the followers who persist become inventors themselves when they repeat the invention without truth, as with the town by the sea that invented Sabbath fishing tricks.\n\nA common error today is to excuse current practice solely because family or culture did it. The primordial covenant teaches that each soul testified independently, so following forefathers in shirk does not shift burden; as earlier “No one will reap except what they sow. No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another,” and ranks are according to deeds, yet alliance of wrongdoers as allies for misdeeds shows followers still earn their own share. The call is to be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, not among those who, well-versed in teachings yet indulging in lowly gain, claim “We will be forgiven” while repeating forefathers’ falsehood.",
  "174": "“This is how We make our signs clear, so perhaps they will return ˹to the Right Path˺” follows the two removed excuses for ignorance and ancestral imitation, giving the purpose of the primordial covenant and all the narrated stories from Noah to the Sabbath town. The previous verses detailed the testimony “Am I not your Lord?” and the warnings not to claim unawareness or forefather association as excuse. This verse states the divine method: “This is how We make our signs clear,” or “wa kathalika nufassilu al-ayat,” the same phrase used for the fertile-infertile parable, for the varied lessons for the thankful, and for the Book explained with knowledge. “Nufassilu” means We detail, distinguish, elaborate from different angles, as with winds, staff, springs, and Tablets, so the Way of the wicked becomes distinct and the straight path is made clear to the mindful.\n\nThe hopeful purpose, “so perhaps they will return to the Right Path,” or “wa la‘allahum yarji‘oon,” repeats the “perhaps they will return” that motivated testing with prosperity and adversity after dispersion, the “perhaps they may abstain” of the warner’s excuse, and the “so perhaps you will become mindful” under the raised mountain. “Raja‘a” here means to turn back, to revert to the original testimony “Yes, You are our Lord,” after wandering like the disoriented by devils in the wilderness whom companions call “Come to us.”\n\nThis verse bridges the universal covenant and the upcoming individual story of the one given signs who abandoned them and was seized by Satan, showing that clear signs alone do not guarantee return; the infertile soil that clings to this life and follows desires must also choose to be among the thankful.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to expect signs to be hidden or overly mystical. The Quran says signs are made clear, varied, and detailed, from the mountain over heads to the calf’s inability to speak to the Springs gushing for twelve tribes, leaving excuses void. The next story of the dog-like denier who abandoned signs after being given them will illustrate that even a single soul given many signs can deviate if he chooses this life, confirming that perhaps they will return is not passive waiting but active invitation to reflect on stories of the past, as the following verse commands to narrate stories so perhaps they will reflect, keeping the barrier’s hope of “Enter Paradise, no fear for you” still attainable before the home of the rebellious is finally shown.",
  "175": "“And relate to them ˹O Prophet˺ the story of the one to whom We gave Our signs, but he abandoned them, so Satan took hold of him, and he became a deviant” shifts from collective covenants and communities to an individual test after stating signs are made clear so perhaps they will return. The command “And relate to them the story,” or “wa utlu alaihim naba’a allathee atainahu ayatina,” is the same “relate” used for narrating tidings of destroyed societies to the Prophet so hearts are sealed for disbelievers, now directed at a single figure. The description, “to whom We gave Our signs, but he abandoned them,” or “aatainahu ayatina fa-insalakha minha,” uses “insalakha,” to slip out, to slough off, like a snake shedding skin, implying he had been clothed with signs yet stripped himself, as earlier the calf worshippers stripped themselves of the best clothing of righteousness and took the lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides, and as later generations inherited Scripture then indulged in unlawful gains.\n\nThe consequence, “so Satan took hold of him, and he became a deviant,” or “fa-atba‘ahu al-shaytanu fa-kana min al-ghaween,” shows the sequence already described: those who take devils as masters instead of Allah thinking they are guided become deviants, or “ghaween,” wanderers like the disoriented in the wilderness whose companions call “Come to us.” The same Satan who swore “I am your sincere advisor” to expose nakedness and who lies in ambush on the Straight Path now finds an unclothed target.\n\nThe figure has been identified in classical reports as Balaam son of Beor, a learned man among the Israelites given knowledge of divine names, and also more generally as any learned person given the Book who trades it for worldly gain, matching the successor who inherits Scripture and says “We will be forgiven” while seizing gain. The specificity is less important than the pattern: being given ayat does not guarantee staying with them; abandoning them is a choice, as earlier those who act arrogantly without right are turned away from signs even if they see every sign.\n\nA common error imagines that knowledge alone protects. The verse shows knowledge abandoned becomes a skin shed, leaving the person exposed to Satan’s hold. The next verse will deepen the image with the dog that pants whether chased or left, showing clinging to this life and following desires after elevation would have been possible, inviting listeners to hold firmly to what was given, as under the raised mountain, so perhaps they will reflect before being among those turned away and shown the home of the rebellious.",
  "176": "“If We had willed, We would have elevated him with Our signs, but he clung to this life—following his evil desires. His example is that of a dog: if you chase it away, it pants, and if you leave it, it ˹still˺ pants. This is the example of the people who deny Our signs. So narrate ˹to them˺ stories ˹of the past˺, so perhaps they will reflect” explains why the sign-bearer became a deviant and what he resembles. The opening, “If We had willed, We would have elevated him with Our signs,” or “wa law shi’na la-rafa‘nahu biha,” echoes “We elevate in rank whoever We please” and “We have elevated you above others by messages and speech,” showing elevation is by divine will through signs, not by birth or by inheriting Scripture alone. The condition “if We had willed” does not mean Allah arbitrarily withheld elevation, but that the person’s choice to cling made the will for elevation not apply, as earlier He opens the chest to Islam for whoever He wills and makes tight whom He wills, based on response.\n\nThe cause of non-elevation, “but he clung to this life—following his evil desires,” or “wa lakinna hu akhlada ila al-ardi wa ittaba‘a hawahu,” uses “akhlada,” to cling, to incline heavily and remain, like the fertile expectation that should produce abundantly but instead the soil clings to earthiness, heaviness, refusing to rise. Following “hawa,” whim, is the same following of desires that led inventors of cattle taboos to mislead without knowledge and that made devils allies of those who disbelieve.\n\nThe similitude that follows is stark: “His example is that of a dog: if you chase it away, it pants, and if you leave it, it still pants,” or “fa-mathaluhu ka-mathali al-kalbi in tahmil alaihi yal hath aw tatrukhu yal hath.” Panting in both states shows constant restlessness, never at ease, like the tight chest as if climbing into the sky, and like the loser who feels secure by day at play and by night asleep, never grateful. This is the example of the people who deny Our signs, generalizing the single deviant to all who deny after being given clarity, whose deeds become vain.\n\nThe command, “So narrate to them stories of the past, so perhaps they will reflect,” or “fa-uqsus al-qasasa la‘allahum yatafakkaroon,” returns to the purpose of varying lessons for the thankful, the mindful, the knowing, so perhaps they will turn. Reflecting, or “tafakkur,” is the fertile reception that turns the dog’s panting into the believer’s calm remembrance under the raised mountain. The lesson for successors who claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing lowly gain is to not cling to the earth with desire, but to let signs elevate before the home of the rebellious is shown and the barrier stands.",
  "177": "“What an evil example of those who deny Our signs! They ˹only˺ wronged their own souls” evaluates the dog similitude just given, turning the image into a moral judgment. The previous verse said if We had willed We would have elevated him with signs, but he clung to the earth following desires, like a dog panting whether chased or left, an example of those who deny. Now the Quran says, “What an evil example of those who deny Our signs!” or “sa’a mathalan al-qawmu allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina.” “Sa’a,” evil, foul, wretched, is the same word used for the evil judgment of dividing shares unfairly and for the evil example of fabricated lies. The example is not merely the dog, but the reality behind it: a person given ayat, the staff, the Tablets, the Book explained with knowledge, yet denying them and clinging to lowly gain, is lower than cattle, as the next verse will say many jinn and humans destined for Hell are like cattle, even less guided.\n\nThe second clause redirects blame: “They only wronged their own souls,” or “wa anfusahum kanoo yazlimoon.” Wronging, or “zulm,” placing things where they do not belong, here is self-wrong, as earlier those whose scales are light doomed themselves, those who rejected Shu‘ayb were the true losers, and those who worshipped the calf were wrongdoers. The deniers imagine they harm the messenger or the Book that was made firm; in reality they harm the soil that could have produced abundantly, sealing themselves as those turned away from signs for arrogance, even if they see every sign they will not believe, choosing the crooked path.\n\nThis self-wrong links to the earlier law that We did not wrong them but they wronged themselves provisioned with twelve springs, shade, manna, and quails, yet changed words at the city gate and broke the Sabbath with fish tricks. The same pattern applies to the successor who inherits Scripture and indulges, claiming “We will be forgiven” while again seizing gain, repeating the dog’s panting for more.\n\nA common misunderstanding externalizes blame to tempters. The verse says the dog-figure was not merely taken by Satan; he abandoned signs first, then Satan followed, then he clung, then followed desire, so the wrong reverts to the soul. For the living, the remedy is to hold firmly to what was given under the raised mountain, to observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful, to establish prayer and abide by Scripture where reward is never discounted, rather than clinging to earth and panting for more until the appointed term that cannot be delayed makes the vain deeds manifest at the weighing that will be just.",
  "178": "“Whoever Allah guides is truly guided. And whoever He leaves to stray, they are the ˹true˺ losers” states the principle behind elevation with signs versus clinging to earth and panting like a dog, directly after calling the deniers’ example evil and self-wronging. The previous verses showed that one given signs who abandons them is not elevated and becomes deviant. This verse abstracts the law: “Whoever Allah guides is truly guided,” or “man yahdi Allahu fa-huwa al-muhtadi.” “Yahdi” is the same guidance praised as opening the chest to Islam, as the straight path perfectly straight, as the light that lets the dead walk among people, and as the guide and mercy in the Tablets for those who stand in awe. True guidance is the one from Allah, not from taking devils as masters thinking one is guided, nor from following the crooked path that seems appealing.\n\nThe counterpart, “And whoever He leaves to stray, they are the true losers,” or “wa man yudlil fa-ula’ika humu al-khasiroon,” uses the same “khasiroon” for losers who feel secure from Allah’s planning, who denied the meeting until the Hour surprised them, and who were wiped as if never there. To be left to stray, or “yudlil,” is not arbitrary; the previous verse showed the straying follows denying signs and wronging own souls, and the wider context shows those who act arrogantly without right are turned away from signs even if they see every sign. The sealing that makes successors unable to hear is the leaving to stray.\n\nThis verse balances earlier statements that If We had willed We would have elevated him, that Allah knows best where to place His message, and that He has guided some while others are destined to stray because they took devils as allies, all showing that will and earnings intertwine: Allah guides the thankful fertile soil that produces abundantly and leaves the unthankful that hardly produces after refusing at first.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this as fatalism. The Quran pairs it with constant invitation to seek guidance: follow what was sent down without taking other guardians, call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, remember favours as successors, hold firmly to Scripture, and narrate stories so perhaps they will reflect. The “true losers” are not those who lacked a chance but those who, like the dog panting for more gain and the heir who claims “We will be forgiven” while seizing again, chose clinging and desire over elevation, thereby confirming the law that deeds become vain and gates remain closed until an impossibility.",
  "179": "“Indeed, We have destined many jinn and humans for Hell. They have hearts they do not understand with, eyes they do not see with, and ears they do not hear with. They are like cattle. In fact, they are even less guided! Such ˹people˺ are ˹entirely˺ heedless” expands the losers just mentioned into a vivid anatomy of heedlessness, showing why guidance and straying are truly distinguishable. The opening, “Indeed, We have destined many jinn and humans for Hell,” or “wa laqad thara’na li-Jahannama katheeran mina al-jinni wal-ins,” uses “thara’na,” to create, scatter, destine, as earlier “We have made for every prophet enemies—devilish humans and jinn.” The destiny is not without cause; the description that follows gives the reason: hearts, eyes, and ears given for understanding, seeing, and hearing are left unused for the purpose they were created, like the fertile land that produces hardly anything despite rain.\n\nThe three disabilities are then listed: “They have hearts they do not understand with,” or “lahum quloobun la yafqahoona biha,” they do not comprehend signs varied for the thankful; “eyes they do not see with,” or “wa lahum a‘yunun la yubsiroona biha,” they do not see the mountain raised like a cloud, the sea split, the she-camel left to graze; “and ears they do not hear with,” or “wa lahum athanun la yasma‘oona biha,” they do not hear the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the “Am I not your Lord?” testimony, or the warners who said “perhaps you will return.” The same triad appeared for the heedless whose punishment comes at night while asleep or day while at play.\n\nThe similitude, “They are like cattle. In fact, they are even less guided!” or “ula’ika ka-al-an‘am bal hum adall,” says they are like grazing livestock that follow pasture without reflection, but even less guided because cattle at least follow instinct beneficially, while these reject the best clothing of righteousness, forbid adorning for worship, and change “Hittah” at the gate. This is the same ranking as “evil example” of dog panting for desire.\n\nThe seal, “Such people are entirely heedless,” or “ula’ika humu al-ghafiloon,” is the heedlessness for which Pharaoh’s people were drowned after denying and being heedless of signs. A common error thinks Hell’s destiny is mere predestination. The verse shows the destined share the same unused faculties; destiny follows the heedless use of hearts, eyes, and ears that were turned away from signs for arrogance, while the path remains to be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, or among those who guide with truth and justice, opening the chest to Islam before the weighing that will be just.",
  "180": "“Allah has the Most Beautiful Names. So call upon Him by them, and keep away from those who abuse His Names. They will be punished for what they used to do” turns from the anatomy of heedlessness to the remedy of remembrance, directly after saying many jinn and humans destined for Hell are like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless. The previous verses diagnosed hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, like the dog panting and the evil example of deniers. Now the Quran offers the names that give those faculties work: “Allah has the Most Beautiful Names,” or “wa lillahi al-asma’u al-husna.” “Al-husna” means most beautiful, most excellent, surpassing all, like the names Ar-Rahman, Ar-Raheem, Al-Hakeem, Al-Khabeer, Al-Wali, the Best Forgiver, the Best of Judges, the Lord of all worlds, the Blessed, the Most Merciful of the merciful, all of which have varied through this surah as winds, springs, and Tablets.\n\nThe command, “So call upon Him by them,” or “fa-id‘oohu biha,” echoes “Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly” and “Call upon Him with hope and fear,” but now with specified means: by the husna names. Calling by Al-Ghafoor when seeking forgiveness after calf remorse, by Ar-Raheem when seeking hasanah in both lives, by Al-Hadi when chest is tight as if climbing into the sky, makes supplication precise and humble, opposite to abusing names.\n\nThe prohibition, “and keep away from those who abuse His Names,” or “wa tharoo allatheena yulhidoona fi asma’ihi,” means to leave those who deviate, distort, incline away in names, as earlier “do not sit with those who ridicule Our revelations” and “leave them and their deceit.” Abuse includes naming idols with divine names, assigning sons and daughters out of ignorance, claiming “the Merciful has a child,” or inventing names without sultan, like the fabricated shares for associates. Such abuse was the theological root of demanding a god like theirs after the sea.\n\nThe warning, “They will be punished for what they used to do,” ties to ranks according to deeds and the home of the rebellious shown, showing that distorting the most beautiful names is not trivial wordplay but rebellious fisq for which dreadful punishment overtook Sabbath violators and for which hearts are sealed.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats names as mere poetry. The verse shows they are tools for guidance: by them the fertile land produces, by abuse the infertile hardly produces. For the dispersed successors who claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains, calling by husna names with hope and fear while abandoning abusers keeps the firm hold on Scripture and establishes prayer where reward is never discounted, before the appointed term.",
  "181": "“And among those We created is a group that guides with the truth and establishes justice accordingly” offers the counterpart to those who deny and those destined for Hell as cattle, showing that not all are heedless. The previous verse taught to call upon Allah by His most beautiful names and to keep away from those who abuse them, whose punishment awaits. This verse then says, “And among those We created is a group that guides with the truth and establishes justice accordingly,” or “wa mimman khalaqna ummatun yahdoona bil-haqqi wa bihi ya‘diloona.” This repeats verbatim the earlier praise of a community among the people of Moses that guides with truth and establishes justice, now universalized to among those We created, indicating that in every era there exists an ummah that holds to the straight path, takes the best of the Tablets, firmly abides by Scripture, and establishes prayer where reward is never discounted.\n\n“Yahdoona bil-haqq,” guides with truth, echoes the earlier “truth prevailed and illusions failed” when the staff devoured great magic, and “ya‘diloona” accordingly means they judge, balance, give just measure as Shu‘ayb commanded, not defrauding people, not spreading corruption after order. This group is the fertile land that produces abundantly by its Lord’s leave, the thankful for whom lessons are varied, the mindful for whom the Home of the Hereafter is far better, the heaviness on the scale that will be successful.\n\nThe verse links directly to promises of mercy ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, and who follow the unlettered Prophet whose light relieves burdens. Such a group does not feel secure from Allah’s planning as losers do, but lives with hope and fear calling humbly and secretly, remembering the mountain over them and the primordial “Am I not your Lord?”\n\nA common error despairs that guidance is extinct after widespread calf worship, Sabbath breaking, and dispersal. The verse asserts a guiding ummah persists, like the Heights’ hopeful entry and the magicians’ prostration, so the narrative of stories is told so perhaps they will reflect, and is not told to induce despair. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing gain, the alternative is to join this ummah that guides with the truth of the Book explained with knowledge and establishes justice accordingly, keeping the barrier’s peace and the rivers’ flow within reach before the term that cannot be delayed, and before the hour that only the Lord knows arrives by surprise.",
  "182": "“As for those who deny Our signs, We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend” returns to the fate of deniers after affirming that a truth-guiding, justice-establishing community persists. The previous verse praised that ummah; this verse shows the opposite trajectory. The phrase “As for those who deny Our signs,” or “wa allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina,” is the same deniers who rejected the mountain covenant, the she-camel, the Springs, and the Book, who deny Our signs and the meeting so deeds are vain, the wicked whose end was shown, the arrogant turned away from signs even if they see every sign. The recompense, “We will gradually draw them to destruction,” or “sa-nastadrijuhum,” means We will lead step by step, by degrees, like the wind-driven clouds that seem gentle before the flood. “Istidraj” is to draw gradually by giving respite that feels like blessing, as earlier “We changed adversity to prosperity until they flourished and said forefathers also had cycles, so We seized them by surprise while unaware,” the same pattern where adversity was meant to humble but prosperity after it was misread as “This is what we deserve.”\n\nThe qualifier, “in ways they cannot comprehend,” or “min haithu la ya‘lamoon,” stresses subtlety; the draw is from where they do not perceive, as the winds usher mercy invisibly before heavy clouds, and as creation and command belong to Him whose planning only losers feel secure from. This mirrors the dispersion test with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return: prosperity itself can be the gradual draw if not met with thankfulness.\n\nThe verse links to the earlier “We afflicted them with suffering so perhaps they would be humbled, then changed to prosperity,” and to the successor who seizes lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven” and would seize again if it came, showing the istidraj of repeated gain without immediate penalty.\n\nA common misunderstanding mistakes continued prosperity after denying as approval. The Quran calls it istidraj, a slow leading to the home of the rebellious that will soon be shown and to the fire whose bed and cover await the arrogant. For those who call with hope and fear, the antidote is to remain among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, where reward is never discounted, and to vary lessons thankfully so that prosperity becomes opening to Islam rather than tightening of the chest as if climbing into the sky, before the gradual drawing ends in being overtaken by surprise.",
  "183": "“I ˹only˺ delay their end for a while, but My planning is flawless” continues the explanation of gradual drawing, clarifying that the delay is intentional and the plan is perfect. The previous verse said as for those who deny Our signs, We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend. Now Allah says, “I only delay their end for a while,” or “wa umlee lahum,” I give them rope, respite, extension, as earlier Pharaoh’s people had famine then removal until a term they must reach then broke, and as Iblis was given delay until the appointed Day. The word “umlee” means to extend, to grant time, the same respite that earlier seemed like prosperity until they flourished and were seized while unaware.\n\nThe second clause, “but My planning is flawless,” or “inna kaidi mateen,” uses “kaid,” planning, scheme, stratagem, the same term used for Allah’s planning that none feel secure from except losers, and for the scheming of the wicked whose large numbers and arrogance are of no use. “Mateen” means firm, strong, unbreakable, not flimsy like the deceivers’ elegant words that are devoured. The contrast is deliberate: their makr is decorated speech that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter and then fails, while His kaid is the encompassing knowledge that was never absent, that knows what is in land and sea, that writes not a leaf falls without a record, that makes the weighing just.\n\nThis verse links to the earlier “We will gradually draw them” and to “Had the people of those societies been faithful We would have opened blessings from heaven and earth, but they disbelieved so We seized them.” The delay is not abandonment but flawless arrangement where each community’s appointed term is kept, where the fertile and infertile are both given rain before harvest is assessed, and where the Book explained with knowledge remains as guide and mercy for those who believe.\n\nA common error treats delay as weakness or forgetfulness. The Quran says delay is mateen, firm, with purpose: to let the warners warned so as to be excused, to let perhaps they may abstain remain possible, and to let the guiding ummah that establishes justice continue. For the living, the lesson is to use the umlah, respite, not as license to claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing again, but as time to hold firmly to the Scripture raised over like a cloud, to observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful, before the flawless plan reaches its term that can neither be delayed nor advanced.",
  "184": "“Have they not ever given it a thought? Their fellow man is not insane. He is only sent with a clear warning” turns the critique from the deniers’ being gradually drawn to their accusation against the warner himself, as the previous verses described the flawless plan that delays the wicked. The question, “Have they not ever given it a thought?” or “awa lam yatafakkaroo,” asks about thinking, or “tafakkur,” the reflecting that stories are narrated so perhaps they will reflect, and that fertile production requires thankful variation. The subject of thought is the messenger: “Their fellow man is not insane,” or “ma bi-sahibihim min jinnah.” “Sahib” means companion, fellow, one among them, like Moses as brother, Hud as brother, the unlettered Prophet from among yourselves, not a stranger. “Jinnah” is madness, possession by jinn, the same slur the chiefs used when they called Hud a fool and Noah misguided and the Meccan chiefs called Muhammad poet and magician. The Quran says their companion is not mad; his behaviour shows the opposite of madness: he conveys Lord’s messages with sincere advice, knows from Allah what they do not, commands uprightness and sincere devotion, and asks no reward.\n\nThe correction, “He is only sent with a clear warning,” or “in huwa illa natheerun mubeen,” defines the role as “natheer,” warner, the same role given to every prophet and to the Book sent down to warn the disbelievers and remind the believers. “Mubeen” clear, manifest, as the staff was a real snake, the hand shining white, the she-camel not to be harmed, the gate’s “Hittah” to be said humbly. The warner’s clarity is the sign that arrogance turns away from even if they see every sign, preferring the crooked path.\n\nThis verse links to the next invitation to reflect on wonders of heavens and earth and that perhaps their end is near, showing madness accusation is a substitute for that reflection, as earlier the disbelieving chiefs saw the messenger as misguided while themselves blind.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates intensity of warning with insanity. The Quran shows the warner’s intensity is mercy, like winds ushering rain, and his companionship is the most intimate proof he seeks their good. For successors who inherited Scripture and say “We will be forgiven” while taking gain, diagnosing the warner as insane is the crooked path that keeps hearts sealed; diagnosing him as clear warner opens the chest to Islam, where calling humbly and secretly with hope and fear keeps mercy close to good-doers before the hour that only the Lord knows arrives by surprise.",
  "185": "“Have they ever reflected on the wonders of the heavens and the earth, and everything Allah has created, and that perhaps their end is near? So what message after this ˹Quran˺ would they believe in?” expands the invitation to think from the warner to the world, after asking whether their fellow is insane and affirming he is a clear warner. The question, “Have they ever reflected on the wonders of the heavens and the earth,” or “awa lam yanzuroo fi malakooti al-samawati wal-ardi,” uses “malakoot,” the kingdom, dominion behind heavens and earth shown to Abraham so he would be certain, the same heavens and earth created in six Days, Throne, overlapping night and day, subjected sun, moon, and stars, winds with heavy clouds producing every fruit. This reflection is the fertile production that earlier hardly produced in the infertile; it is the looking that earlier communities refused when they worshipped a lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides.\n\nThe second object, “and everything Allah has created,” generalizes beyond sky to every leaf whose fall is written and every spring among twelve for each tribe, encompassing the Book explained with knowledge as well. The third reflection, “and that perhaps their end is near?” or “wa an asa an yakoona qad iqtaraba ajaluhum,” uses “ajal,” the appointed term for each community that when it comes cannot be delayed or advanced, that seized societies at night while asleep or day while at play, that made Midian as if never there, that drowned Pharaoh after famine and plagues. The term’s nearness is uncertain to humans but certain to the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who was never absent, making perhaps near a call to urgency, like “You will soon know who will fare best in the end.”\n\nThe closing challenge, “So what message after this Quran would they believe in?” or “fa-bi-ayyi hadeethin ba‘dahu yu’minoonoon,” says if the Quran that is the Fundamentals of everything explained, that holds the best of the Tablets, that is the light relieving burdens, does not move them, what other hadeeth, discourse, will? This echoes “This is how We make our signs clear so perhaps they will return” and “Have they not reflected on wonders,” showing reflection is the antidote to sport.\n\nA common illusion is that wonders are obvious and will always be there. The verse joins wonders above, around, and the nearness of end to provoke mindful urgency before the barrier and the weighing, inviting the heedless who are like cattle, even less guided, to use hearts to understand, eyes to see, ears to hear, before the Gradual drawing while unaware reaches its term.",
  "186": "“Whoever Allah allows to stray, none can guide, leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance” gives the immutable law after two reflection questions about the warner’s sanity and the heavens and earth’s wonders with the near term. The previous verse asked what message after the Quran they would believe, showing that without reflection even wonders do not guide. This verse states the outcome: “Whoever Allah allows to stray, none can guide,” or “man yudlil Allahu fa-la hadiya lahu.” The same law appeared as “Whoever Allah guides is truly guided, and whoever He leaves to stray are the true losers,” and as “We will gradually draw those who deny Our signs.” To be left to stray, or “yudlil,” is to be abandoned to the crooked path chosen when one acts arrogantly without right, is turned away from signs even if seeing every sign, sees the right path and does not take it but sees the crooked and follows it, denies and is heedless, as the rebellious whose home will be shown.\n\nThe consequence, “leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance,” or “wa yatharuhum fi tughyanihim ya‘mahoona,” uses “tughyan,” exceeding bounds, transgression, like spreading corruption after order, taking the calf as god, breaking the Sabbath with fish tricks, claiming “We will be forgiven” while seizing. “Ya‘mahoona” is to wander blindly, to be confused, like the disoriented by devils in the wilderness whom companions call “Come to us,” and like those whose hearts and eyes are turned as they refused to believe at first, leaving them to wander. This wandering is the opposite of the guided ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice, and of those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer.\n\nThis verse links the gradual drawing and flawless planning to personal responsibility: the drawing is of those who deny signs and whose hearts, eyes, and ears do not understand, see, or hear, so the straying is both divine leaving and self-chosen.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this as arbitrary. The Quran’s sequence shows the leaving follows the chain: denying signs and meeting, making deeds vain, acting arrogantly without right, preferring crooked, being heedless. For the successor who still has the Most Beautiful Names to call upon and the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the remedy is to recall the primordial “Am I not your Lord? Yes, we testify,” to hold firmly to the Scripture under the raised mountain, and to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, lest the wander become permanent and the dog’s panting become the final example.",
  "187": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding the Hour, ‘When will it be?’ Say, ‘That knowledge is only with my Lord. He alone will reveal it when the time comes. It is too tremendous for the heavens and the earth and will only take you by surprise.’ They ask you as if you had full knowledge of it. Say, ‘That knowledge is only with Allah, but most people do not know’” addresses the same fixation on timing that earlier communities used to challenge prophets, now focused on the Hour itself. The previous verse warned that whoever Allah allows to stray will wander blindly in defiance after being gradually drawn. Now the question about the Hour, or “as-Sa‘ah,” is quoted, “When will it be?” or “ayyana mursaha,” when will it anchor, when will it occur. This was the same “When will the Hour be if you are truthful?” asked to hasten punishment, like Pharaoh’s “Bring what you threaten” and the Meccan “Bring the torment” and the modern “If this Quran is truth let the sky fall.”\n\nThe commanded answer repeats the earlier creed that signs are only with Allah and that creation and command belong to Him. Twice it is said, “That knowledge is only with my Lord” and “That knowledge is only with Allah,” or “innama ilmuha inda Rabbi” and “innama ilmuha inda Allah.” The hour’s knowledge is one of the keys of the unseen, the same unseen whose keys are with Him alone as sunset to dawn is His to split. “He alone will reveal it when the time comes,” or “la yujalliha li-waqtiha illa huwa,” means He alone will manifest it at its time, like the mountain levelled when He appears, not a prophet’s estimate. The reason it cannot be known is then given: “It is too tremendous for the heavens and the earth and will only take you by surprise,” or “thaqulat fi al-samawati wal-ard la ta’teekum illa baghtah.” “Thaqulat” heavy, tremendous, too weighty for heavens and earth to bear easily; if its weight is heavy on creation, asking for its date as amusement is treating the tremendous as play, the same taking of faith as amusement for which the Fire’s water is forbidden. “Illa baghtah” only by surprise echoes every term that came at night while asleep or day while at play, and the surprise seizure while unaware after prosperity.\n\nThe Quran notes their false impression: “They ask you as if you had full knowledge of it,” or “ka-annaka hafiyyun anha,” as if you are eager, well-informed, intimate with its news, expecting the warner to be a soothsayer. The reply again, “That knowledge is only with Allah, but most people do not know,” mirrors “most of them did not know” when they blamed omens on Moses, and “most people do not know” after the Hour’s heaviness, showing ignorance is the root of the question itself.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats the Hour’s unknown timing as reason to postpone preparation. The verse teaches the opposite: because it is heavy and surprising, the near term, perhaps your term is near, should keep you reflecting on heavens and earth and calling humbly with hope and fear, before the gradual drawing while unaware ends in wandering.",
  "188": "“Say, ‘I have no power to benefit or protect myself, except by the Will of Allah. If I had known the unknown, I would have benefited myself enormously, and no harm would have ever touched me. I am only a warner and deliverer of good news for those who believe’” follows the Hour’s unknown timing with a personal disclaimer that defines prophethood. The previous verse said the Hour’s knowledge is only with the Lord and is too tremendous for heavens and earth. This verse shows the warner’s own limits before that tremendousness. The opening, “I have no power to benefit or protect myself, except by the Will of Allah,” or “qul la amliku li-nafsi naf‘an wa la darran illa ma sha’a Allah,” mirrors Adam’s parents’ dependence, the magicians’ return to the Lord, and Moses’ prayer for hasanah, all acknowledging that the One whose creation and command are His alone grants benefit and harm. A warner who cannot benefit himself without Allah’s will certainly cannot advance or delay the Hour for others.\n\nThe counterfactual, “If I had known the unknown, I would have benefited myself enormously, and no harm would have ever touched me,” or “wa law kuntu a‘lamu al-ghayba la-istakthartu min al-khair wa ma massaniya al-soo’,” clarifies the keys of the unseen are with Him alone. “Ghaib” is the unseen, the same unseen whose keys no one knows except Him, the Hour, the provision of children, the tomorrow’s rain. If the Prophet knew it, he would have amassed every good and avoided every harm, but his life shows both: victory at Badr and wound at Uhud, proving humanity. This is the same humanness as eating after the staff-snake and needing the mountain’s awe.\n\nThe role definition that follows, “I am only a warner and deliverer of good news for those who believe,” or “in ana illa natheerun wa basheerun li-qawmin yu’minoonoon,” repeats the messenger limits given since Noah’s “I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds, conveying messages and giving sincere advice.” Warmer, or “natheer,” and bringer of good news, or “basheer,” are the two faces of the Book sent down to warn and remind, of the winds that usher mercy and the plagues that are clear signs.\n\nA common error expects a prophet to be angelic controller of fate or economist of the unseen who can time the Hour. The verse corrects that expectation, aligning with “Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly, He does not like transgressors” and “He has the Most Beautiful Names, so call upon Him by them.” The warner’s humanity makes his warning more merciful, not less, inviting listeners to reflect on wonders and nearness of term, to follow the unlettered Prophet who believes in Allah and His words, and to prepare with prayer and firm abiding before harm touches.",
  "189": "“He is the One Who created you from a single soul, then from it made its spouse so he may find comfort in her. After he had been united with her, she carried a light burden that developed gradually. When it grew heavy, they prayed to Allah, their Lord, ‘If you grant us good offspring, we will certainly be grateful’” returns to the origin of humanity after defining the warner’s limits. The previous verses left the Hour as unknown and the warner as human; now the Quran roots humanity in a single soul and in pairhood, showing the Lord whose Hour is unknown is the same Lord who creates and gives life. The phrase “He is the One Who created you from a single soul, then from it made its spouse so he may find comfort in her,” or “huwa allathee khalaqakum min nafsin wahidatin wa ja‘ala minha zawjaha li-yaskuna ilaiha,” echoes “We have established you on earth” and “created you then shaped you” and “from a single soul, then assigned a place to live,” emphasizing unity of origin that removes the ground for arrogance that said “I am better than he is from fire, him from clay.” The spouse, or “zawj,” made from the same soul, finds “sakan,” tranquility, covering, as clothing covers nakedness and night is made for rest.\n\nThe next scene is intimate: “After he had been united with her, she carried a light burden that developed gradually,” or “fa-lamma taghashshaha hamalat hamlan khafeefan fa-marrat bihi.” “Taghashsha” covering as a garment, “hamlan khafeefan,” light burden, early pregnancy not yet heavy, that passes, or “marrat bihi,” she continues, moves lightly. “When it grew heavy, they prayed to Allah, their Lord, ‘If you grant us good offspring, we will certainly be grateful,’” or “fa-lamma athqalat da‘awaa Allaha Rabbahuma la-in ataitana salihan la-nakoonanna min al-shakireen.” “Athqalat,” heavy, near term, both prayed together, the same joint prayer Adam and his wife prayed after the tree, “If You do not forgive and have mercy we will be losers.” “Salehan” here means upright, sound, righteous child, body and faith, and “shakireen,” grateful, the fertile soil that produces abundantly and for whom lessons are varied.\n\nThis origin story links to the next verse where, when good offspring is granted, some associate false gods in what He gave, forgetting the prayer. It also links to the earlier mountain covenant and the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” where all testified “Yes, we testify,” showing gratitude was the natural vow before birth. A common misunderstanding treats family creation as secular. The Quran frames pair and pregnancy as signs among wonders of heavens and earth to reflect upon, whose near term may be the child’s own term, inviting thankfulness that dresses for worship and eats without waste, rather than later association that makes deeds vain like the dog’s panting and the calf’s lowing that neither speaks nor guides.",
  "190": "“But when He granted their descendants good offspring, they associated false gods in what He has given them. Exalted is Allah above what they associate ˹with Him˺!” continues the pair’s story from a grateful prayer to a subsequent association, showing how quickly the fertile vow can turn infertile. The previous verse described the light burden growing heavy and the joint supplication “If You grant us good offspring we will be certainly grateful,” a vow made in the vulnerability of pregnancy that mirrors the call “He alone you call upon humbly openly and secretly, If You rescue us from this we will be ever grateful” at sea. Now the outcome, “But when He granted their descendants good offspring, they associated false gods in what He has given them,” or “fa-lamma atahuma salihan ja‘ala lahu shurakaa’a fima atahuma,” records that after the gift of a sound, righteous child, some among their descendants, or broader humanity, “ja‘ala lahu shurakaa’,” make for Him partners in what He gave, naming children with names that serve others, dedicating them to others, or believing others granted the child, like the she-camel made a partner’s share unfairly or the calf taken as god after the sea. The phrase “in what He has given them” emphasizes the very gift prayed for becomes the occasion for ungratefulness, as earlier “Is it not clear to those who take over land” and “We tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return” showed prosperity itself is a test.\n\nThe exclamation, “Exalted is Allah above what they associate with Him!” or “fa-ta‘ala Allahu amma yushrikoon,” is the same glorification uttered after they associated jinn though He created them and falsely attributed sons and daughters out of ignorance, and after “Exalted is He above what they claim.” “Ta‘ala” high, far above, like the Throne established after creation in six Days, the One who makes night and day overlap and subjects sun, moon, stars, whose creation and command belong to Him alone.\n\nThis verse also warns against the common practice of naming children “servant of” other than Allah or attributing a healthy child to a shrine, a spirit, or a star, the same attribution without sultan that invented cattle taboos and mountain carvings. The remedy taught throughout is to remain among those who call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, who firm abide by Scripture and establish prayer, and who remember “We have certainly brought them a Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy,” so gratitude for good offspring remains gratitude to the One who created the single soul and its spouse for tranquility, not to a partner whose idols will soon be shown to have no feet to walk, hands to hold, eyes to see, or ears to hear.",
  "191": "“Do they associate ˹with Allah˺ those ˹idols˺ which cannot create anything, but are in fact created” begins the systematic dismantling of the association just condemned as exalted above. After saying when good offspring was granted they associated in what He gave and that Allah is exalted above that, the Quran now asks about the nature of the associates. The question, “Do they associate those idols which cannot create anything, but are in fact created,” or “a-yushrikoona ma la yakhluqu shay’an wa hum yukhlaqoon,” contrasts inability to create, “ma la yakhluqu shay’an,” anything at all, not a leaf, not a spring among twelve, not a wind that ushers mercy, with being themselves created, “wa hum yukhlaqoon,” fashioned by human hands, carved from stone, cast from golden jewellery into a lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides. The One who created you from a single soul and made earth in six Days and subjected celestial bodies and sends heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit is the Creator, “who created the heavens and earth in six Days” and “who created all things,” while idols are the inverts, possessed, needing creation.\n\nThis contrast echoes the earlier “How could He have children when He has no mate? He created all things and has perfect knowledge” and “That is Allah your Lord, Creator of all things, so worship Him alone, and He is Maintainer of everything,” and directly answers the successor who demanded “Make for us a god like theirs,” showing that like theirs means like the created, not like the Creator.\n\nThe rhetorical “Do they associate?” implies astonishment, as earlier “Do you find it astonishing that a reminder comes through one of your own,” that the mind should find association astonishing given the evidence of winds and fertile land. The next verses will continue, they cannot help them or even themselves, cannot respond, are created beings like yourselves, have no feet, hands, eyes, ears, and if called for guidance cannot hear and appear to face you but do not see.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats idols as metaphorical only. The Quran treats any thing taken as partner that cannot create yet is created, whether wood, stone, gold calf, or desire followed, as falling under this critique. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains, the reminder is that the gain cannot create, the idol cannot create, only the Lord whose mercy encompasses everything and whose punishment is swift can create and command, so worship belongs to Him alone before the Hour that is too tremendous for heavens and earth takes by surprise.",
  "192": "“which cannot help them, or even help themselves?” continues the audit of the associates that cannot create and are themselves created. The previous verse established creative impotence; this verse establishes helplessness. The phrase “which cannot help them, or even help themselves,” or “wa la yastatee‘oona lahum nasran wa la anfusahum yansuroon,” says the idols are double-helpless: they cannot grant “nasr,” help, victory, rescue, to those who call upon them as guardians besides Him, nor can they provide it to themselves when harmed. The lowing calf could not speak or guide; the idol devotees’ gods after the sea could not provide; the associate-gods for whom crops were divided could not share or withhold; the fabricated shares did not help when famine, blood, or earthquake came. The same helplessness was seen when the staff devoured illusions, when the sea drowned Pharaoh while his gods remained silent, and when the Fire’s residents begged for water that the disbelievers’ provision could not give.\n\nThis double inability contrasts with the Lord who rescues from the darkest times on land and at sea, who rescues from every distress, who sends winds, who gives twelve springs, shade, manna, and quails, who writes on Tablets guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe, and who is the Guardian forgiving and most merciful to those who repent after evil. He helps them and Himself needs no help, as earlier He provides for all and is not in need of provision.\n\nThe verse also mirrors the earlier test of the Heights where tyrants’ large numbers and arrogance are of no use; idol helpers are of no use either, because the creation belongs to Him alone and the command belongs to Him. Their inability to help themselves proves they cannot help others; a helper who cannot self-preserve cannot preserve followers on the Day the weighing will be just and the barrier stands.\n\nA common error imagines that at least idols provide psychological comfort. The Quran shows comfort without help is self-deception, like the dog’s panting that persists whether chased or left, and like garments that neither cover nor adorn. The proper Protector is then named two verses later, “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book, for He alone protects the righteous,” the same Protector to call upon humbly and secretly with hope and fear, whose mercy is close to good-doers and whose most beautiful names are to be called upon while keeping away from those who abuse them.",
  "193": "“And if you ˹idolaters˺ call upon them for guidance, they cannot respond to you. It is all the same whether you call them or remain silent” deepens the helplessness audit from creating and helping to hearing and responding. The previous verses established the associates cannot create and cannot help themselves or others; this verse adds they cannot hear and answer. The condition, “And if you call upon them for guidance,” or “wa in tad‘oohum ila al-huda la yattabi‘ookum,” imagines idolaters sincerely seeking huda, the guidance that is the Book explained with knowledge, the straight path made clear to the mindful, the Tablets’ fundamentals and the light of the unlettered Prophet. The result, “they cannot respond to you,” or “la yattabi‘ookum,” they will not follow you, will not answer, just as earlier the calf could not speak or guide, and as later “Those idols you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves, so call upon them and see if they will answer” challenges. The idols lack the hearing that was just praised as “He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing” and the “We were never absent” that makes a full account with sure knowledge, so they cannot be the guardians besides Him warned against.\n\nThe equivalence, “It is all the same whether you call them or remain silent,” or “sawaa’un alaikum a-da‘awtumoo hum am antum samitoon,” shows futility: calling with humble secret hope and calling not at all produce identical nothing, unlike calling upon your Lord humbly and secretly where mercy is close to good-doers and winds usher rain. The same equivalence was seen when the magicians’ great display stunned people but did not guide; stun without guidance is calling without answer.\n\nThis verse links directly to the later “If you call them to guidance, they cannot hear, and you may see them facing towards you but they cannot see,” extending deafness to blindness, and to the challenge “Do they have feet to walk with? hands to hold? eyes to see? ears to hear?” where the idols’ lack of organs is mocked.\n\nA common misunderstanding assigns subtle agency to idols, thinking silence is still presence. The Quran says silence is absence, and presence that cannot guide is not presence at all. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing gain and the student of the calf that lows, the call is to redirect calling to the One whose creation and command are His, blessed Lord of all worlds, who gives life and causes death and who, through the unlettered Prophet, relieves burdens and shackles, so that following him becomes the following that is rightly guided, rather than calling upon the deaf.",
  "194": "“Those ˹idols˺ you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves. So call upon them and see if they will answer you, if your claims are true!” issues the practical challenge after establishing that associates cannot create, cannot help, and cannot respond. The verse states humanity: “Those idols you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves,” or “inna allatheena tad‘oona min dooni Allahi ibadun amthalukum.” “Ibad,” servants, beings like you, as earlier you were created from a single soul, established then shaped, given hearts, eyes, and ears, and as earlier the jinn were created and the mountain raised like a cloud, all are servants, not lords. Idols are servants doubly: they are created by humans and they are created by the Creator who created the humans who made them, the same chain that makes the calf a jasad with lowing and the Sabbath fish a test.\n\nThe word “amthalukum,” like you, removes the aura of mysterious superiority that makes the straight seem crooked in the eyes of those who take devils as masters thinking they are guided. The idol is not a hidden power watching from where you cannot see them; the devils do that, but the idol itself is a servant like you, standing among the twelve tribes who received springs but did not wrong Us but wronged themselves.\n\nThe challenge, “So call upon them and see if they will answer you, if your claims are true!” or “fa-id‘oohum fa-l-yastajeebu lakum in kuntum sadiqeen,” mirrors Moses’ challenge to Pharaoh, “If you have come with a sign bring it if you are truthful,” and Hud’s “Remember He made you successors,” and Shu‘ayb’s “give just measure.” It is an empirical test parallel to “See what was the end of the wicked,” “See how We vary lessons,” and “Have they reflected on the kingdom of heavens and earth.” If invocation is true, let the servant answer, as the All-Hearing answers the humble secret caller and as the winds answer the command to drive heavy clouds.\n\nThe verse prepares the limb-mockery next: feet, hands, eyes, ears, and the Prophet’s counter-challenge “Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay—my Protector is Allah Who revealed the Book.” A common error treats idols as necessary mediators to the Lord of all worlds. The verse says mediators who are servants like you cannot replace the Lord whose beautiful names are to be called upon directly, whose mercy is close to good-doers, and whose guidance via the unlettered Prophet relieves burdens. Testing the call now, while the term remains, shows silence before the silence of the Fire where residents cry for water and are refused.",
  "195": "“Do they have feet to walk with? Or hands to hold with? Or eyes to see with? Or ears to hear with? Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay!’” continues the empirical challenge, now dissecting the idols’ lack of faculties that were earlier said to be unused in many jinn and humans destined for Hell. After saying idols are servants like you, so call and see if they answer if you are truthful, the Quran asks four anatomical questions: “Do they have feet to walk with? Or hands to hold with? Or eyes to see with? Or ears to hear with?” or “a-lahum arjulun yamshoona biha am lahum aidin yabtishoona biha am lahum a‘yunun yubsiroona biha am lahum athanun yasma‘oona biha.” Each question expects “No” in the truest sense. They may have carved feet, hands, eyes, ears, but none function: feet do not walk to help, hands do not grasp to withhold or give, eyes do not see the mountain raised like a cloud or the sea split, ears do not hear the humble secret call with hope and fear. This mirrors the earlier anatomy of heedlessness, hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, who are like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless, and contrasts with the Lord who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing, Most Subtle, All-Aware, never absent, whose knowledge encompasses everything and who hears the magicians’ prayer for perseverance.\n\nThe second half is the Prophet’s defiance: “Say, O Prophet, Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay!” or “qul id‘oo shuraka’akum thumma keedooni fa-la tunziroon.” This is the same defiance Hud showed, “Do you dispute regarding names without sultan? Then wait, I too am waiting,” and Shu‘ayb’s “in Allah we trust, our Lord, judge between us,” and Noah’s Ark against the flood, all trusting creation-and-command belong to Him. The challenge invites all shuraka, partners, to plot, or “keed,” scheme, the word for the flawless planning whose mateen belongs only to Allah, showing their kid is nothing.\n\nThe verse’s placement before “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who revealed this Book, for He alone protects the righteous” explains the defiance’s basis: the Prophet is not protected by numbers and arrogance that are of no use, but by the Wali who protects the righteous, whose mercy is close to good-doers.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats these questions as mockery without purpose. The Quran’s purpose is diagnostic: idols are servants with decorative limbs, not living guardians, so taking them as guardians besides Him violates “Follow what was sent down and do not take other guardians,” and keeps the scale light. The living are invited to choose feet that walk to worship properly adorned, hands that give just measure, eyes that reflect on heavens and earth, ears that listen to the Quran attentively and silently so mercy may be shown.",
  "196": "“Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book. For He ˹alone˺ protects the righteous” gives the answer to the defiance just issued, “Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay.” After inviting all idols to scheme and proving they lack feet, hands, eyes, ears, the Prophet declares his true reliance. The phrase “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book,” or “inna waliyyi Allahu allathee nazzala al-kitaba,” names the Wali, the protecting ally, guardian, the same Wali whose allies are not devils for those who disbelieve, the same Wali who was best forgiver and Guardian for Moses’ seventy, the same Wali whom no one should take besides Him. His qualification, “Who has revealed this Book,” ties protection to revelation, the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the Tablets’ fundamentals, the light sent down with the unlettered Prophet that relieves burdens. The Book is not an abstract text but the cord of protection, as the staff and the sea were protection for Moses.\n\nThe principle that follows, “For He alone protects the righteous,” or “wa huwa yatawalla al-saliheen,” generalizes. “Saliheen,” righteous, are those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, who guide with truth and establish justice, who give just measure, do not spread corruption, whose hearts understand, eyes see, ears hear, unlike cattle. Protection, or “yatawalla,” is to take as wali, to befriend, to manage affairs, the opposite of the dispersing of the heedless through land and sealing of arrogant hearts.\n\nThis declaration contrasts with the immediate context: the idolaters’ gods cannot protect them or themselves, cannot answer, are servants like you, have no functional limbs, while the Prophet’s Wali, who created you from a single soul, created heavens and earth in six Days, sends winds with heavy clouds producing every fruit, and afflicts and relieves, does protect, as He saved Noah in the Ark, Hud and Salih by mercy, Lot’s family except the doomed, Shu‘ayb’s saved, and the magicians who fell prostrate.\n\nA common error seeks protection in numbers, walls, or status, the same large numbers and arrogance said to be of no use. The verse re-centers protection on the One whose most beautiful names are to be called upon and whose knowledge encompasses everything, the same Lord who declared He would send against the heedless others who would make them suffer until the Day, yet is swift in punishment but certainly all-forgiving, most merciful to the righteous He protects when they call humbly and secretly with hope and fear.",
  "197": "“But those ˹false gods˺ you call besides Him can neither help you nor even themselves’” restates the helplessness audit after the declaration that the Protector is Allah who revealed the Book and protects the righteous. The same sentence appeared earlier as “which cannot help them, or even help themselves,” but now it is placed after the Prophet’s defiance, making the contrast between the two protectors explicit. The phrase “But those false gods you call besides Him can neither help you nor even themselves,” or “wa allatheena tad‘oona min doonihi la yastatee‘oona nasrakum wa la anfusahum yansuroon,” uses “dawna,” besides, indicating a guardian taken instead of Him, the very guardians the Book warned not to take. “Nasr,” help, is the same help Moses told his people to seek from Allah and be patient, whose earth belongs to Him and outcome to the righteous. The false gods provide neither help to callers nor to themselves, unlike the winds that usher mercy and the rain that produces every fruit and the Springs that gush for twelve tribes.\n\nThe repetition is not redundancy but emphasis, closing the circle that began with “Do they associate those idols which cannot create anything but are in fact created,” then “cannot help,” then “if you call for guidance they cannot respond,” then “servants like you, call and see if they answer,” then the limb questions, then the challenge to conspire, then the protector declaration, now the summary. Each repetition adds a layer: creation, self-help, hearing, answering, limbs, conspiracy, guardianship, now the final seal that the helpers are helpless.\n\nThe verse also subtly answers the reminder to the Children of Israel “Make for us a god like their gods,” showing that like theirs means like the helpless, while their rescue from dreadful torment, the split sea, and the twelve springs already demonstrated who truly helps.\n\nA common misunderstanding persists in thinking that at least idols do no harm, so calling them is harmless. The Quran shows calling them is harmful because it makes deeds vain, turns away from signs even if every sign is seen, and leads to the home of the rebellious being shown, to the weighing that is just where light scales doom selves, and to gates not opening. The alternative remains to call upon Him by His most beautiful names and to be among the group that guides with truth and establishes justice, whose Protector, who revealed the Book, is already close to good-doers who call with hope and fear and who will be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer.",
  "198": "“If you ˹idolaters˺ call them to guidance, they cannot hear. And you ˹O Prophet˺ may see them facing towards you, but they cannot see” returns to the sensory failure of idols, now applied to guidance specifically, after summarizing their double helplessness. The phrase “If you call them to guidance, they cannot hear,” or “wa in tad‘oohum ila al-huda la yasma‘oo,” repeats the earlier “if you call them to guidance they cannot respond,” but with “yasma‘oo,” hear, the faculty most needed for “Listen to the Quran attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy,” which will be commanded two verses later. Idols lack the ears that earlier were said many jinn and humans possess but do not hear with, becoming like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless. They cannot hear because they were not given hearing that functions, just as they have no feet to walk, hands to hold, eyes to see, ears to hear.\n\nThe second sentence adds a visual illusion: “And you may see them facing towards you, but they cannot see,” or “wa tarahum yanzuroona ilaika wa hum la yubsiroon.” “Yanzuroona,” looking, facing, gives the appearance of attention, like the magicians’ eyes-deceiving great magic that stunned, or the fish appearing abundantly on Sabbath to test. The idols seem to look, facing the worshipper, carved to appear watchful, yet “la yubsiroon,” they do not see, as earlier “no vision can encompass Him but He encompasses all vision,” the idols encompass nothing. The worshipper feels seen, but the gaze is vacant, the same vacant gaze that led the town by the sea to think tricks would hide Sabbath violation while He is never absent.\n\nThis double failure—deaf to guidance and blind though facing—explains why those turned away from signs for arrogance even if they see every sign will not believe, and why if they see the right path they will not take it but if they see a crooked path they will follow it, as earlier described. The human faculties of the worshippers themselves are also dulled by mirroring: calling to the deaf makes the caller deaf to the Quran’s recitation, a warning that leads directly to “When the Quran is recited, listen attentively and be silent.”\n\nA common error assumes that a deity’s image facing the devotee implies care. The Quran strips that illusion, recalling the calf that neither speaks nor guides despite being fashioned from jewellery, and recalling the winds that do hear the command to drive heavy clouds. The cure is to turn calling to the Most Beautiful Names of the One who does hear and who does see, whose mercy is close, and to remember that the righteous He protects are those who hear the Quran and see the wonders of heavens and earth and that perhaps their term is near, so what message after this Quran would they believe if not this.",
  "199": "“Be gracious, enjoin what is right, and turn away from those who act ignorantly” gives the prophetic ethic after the long exposure of idol helplessness, the protector declaration, and the deaf-blind diagnosis. The command is threefold, each part balancing the previous warnings not to take other guardians and not to spread corruption. First, “Be gracious,” or “khuth al-afwa,” take the easy, pardon what is within capacity, be lenient with people’s faults, as earlier “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford” and “hold firmly to the Scripture which We have given you” with strength but without harshness. “Afwa” is what is readily given without strain, the same ease that the Tayyibat are for enjoyment in this world without burden. Graciousness is the opposite of Pharaoh’s cutting hands and feet and crucifying, and of the chiefs’ “We will kill their sons” domination.\n\nSecond, “enjoin what is right,” or “wa’mur bil-‘urf,” command the known good, the ma‘roof that the unlettered Prophet commands and the evil he forbids, the uprightness and sincere devotion earlier commanded, the just measure, the covering of nakedness with best clothing, the remembrance under the raised mountain. “‘Urf” is what is recognized as good by sound fitrah and revelation combined, the guidance that varies for the thankful and produces abundantly, not the elegant deception that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter.\n\nThird, “and turn away from those who act ignorantly,” or “wa a‘rid an al-jahileen,” is the same turning away commanded for those who ridicule revelations unless they change topics, for leaving them and their deceit, for not sitting with mockers, and for Moses’ “you are the best of those who judge.” Ignorant here are not merely uninformed but those who act on ignorance, like those who demanded a god like theirs, who made the calf, who changed “Hittah” and who abused the most beautiful names.\n\nTogether the three create a community like the ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice among those We created, and the group among the People of Moses that does so. A common misunderstanding pits graciousness against commanding right, as if one must be harsh to enjoin. The verse joins them: graciousness makes enjoining attractive, turning away from the ignorant keeps graciousness from being exploited by those turned away from signs who even if they see every sign will not believe. This balance keeps the caller humbly, secretly hopeful yet fearful, while the winds still usher mercy and the term nears, so that when the Quran is recited two verses later, listening attentively and in silence is the natural response rather than the heedless panting of the dog that clings to earth.",
  "200": "“If you are tempted by Satan, then seek refuge with Allah. Surely He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” follows the ethic of graciousness, enjoining right, and turning away from the ignorant with the necessary spiritual protection for that ethic. The previous verse set a demanding balance: to be gracious and to command good while turning from the ignorant who ridicule, change words, and abuse beautiful names. This verse anticipates the inner push that makes that balance wobble. The phrase “If you are tempted by Satan,” or “wa imma yanzaghannaka mina al-shaytani nazghun,” describes a prick, a touch, an incitement, the same nazgh that made forgetfulness about sitting with mockers and that drives the ambush on the Straight Path from front, back, right, and left. Satan’s soldiers who watch from where you cannot see them use such pricks to turn graciousness into resentment or enjoining into harshness.\n\nThe remedy, “then seek refuge with Allah,” or “fa-ista‘ith billah,” is to take shelter, like taking Allah as Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, and like calling upon Him humbly and secretly with hope and fear. Seeking refuge is the practical form of remembering, or “tathakkur,” that fertile land needs to produce abundantly. The reason, “Surely He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” or “innahu Samee‘un Aleem,” repeats the pairing that after “To Him belongs whatever is in day and night and He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” and that guarantees no vision encompasses Him but He encompasses all vision, that We were never absent, and that the most beautiful names belong to Him. He hears the prick’s whisper and the refuge’s utterance, and He knows the temper that would make the caller transgress and thereby become unliked, since He does not like transgressors.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next, where those mindful, when Satan whispers, remember then they start to see clearly, while devils plunge their associates deeper sparing no effort. The heedless are like cattle, even less guided, while the mindful use refuge to restore sight.\n\nA common error believes willpower alone resists the ignorant’s provocation. The Quran teaches refuge is needed, a return to the Guardian who encompasses, to the Lord who subjects winds and writes not a leaf falls without record. Seeking it keeps the heart among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, rather than among those turned away from signs for arrogance who see the crooked path and follow it. In daily practice, before responding to an ignorant act, the gracious, commanding, turning caller whispers “I seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” and the ambush loses its foothold.",
  "201": "“Indeed, when Satan whispers to those mindful ˹of Allah˺, they remember ˹their Lord˺ then they start to see ˹things˺ clearly” describes the successful use of refuge just commanded, contrasting the heedless who are like cattle with the mindful whose hearts understand. The previous verse warned if Satan tempts you seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing. This verse shows what happens when those who are mindful, or “muttaqoon,” the wearers of the best clothing of righteousness, the hopeful-fearful callers whose mercy is close, the thankful fertile soil, are touched. The phrase “when Satan whispers to those mindful, they remember,” or “inna allatheena ittaqaw itha massahum ta’ifun mina al-shaytani tathakkaroo,” uses “massahum ta’if,” a touch, a visitation, a circling, the lightest contact, not possession, showing even the mindful are touched by the ambush from four sides. “Tathakkaroo” they remember, the same remembrance commanded under the raised mountain, “observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful,” and after the winds producing every fruit so perhaps you will be mindful, and after the primordial testimony so perhaps they will return.\n\nThe result, “then they start to see things clearly,” or “fa-itha hum mubsiroon,” they become seeing, insightful, with “basirah,” inner sight, like the insight, or “basa’ir,” from your Lord that came as the Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy. Clear sight is the opposite of the hearts, eyes, ears that do not understand, see, or hear, and of the idols that face you but do not see and are called to guidance but cannot hear. The mindful’s memory restores the faculty the cattle-like lack.\n\nThis verse links the ethic of being gracious, enjoining right, turning from the ignorant, with the practical need for refuge. The ambush that aims to make the gracious turn harsh and the commander turn transgressing is broken by a remembered Name, one of the Most Beautiful Names they are taught to call upon, like Al-Hafiz, the Guardian.\n\nA common misunderstanding expects the mindful to be never touched. The Quran says they are touched, but they remember. Another error expects remembrance without sight. The verse shows remembrance leads to sight, the same sight that makes the Heights people recognize by marks and that makes the magicians fall prostrate. For successors who inherited Scripture and are tempted to seize lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven,” remembering the covenant not to say about Allah except truth restores sight before the gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend reaches its flawless plan, keeping the path perfectly straight visible while the crooked remains enticing but now seen clearly.",
  "202": "“But the devils persistently plunge their ˹human˺ associates deeper into wickedness, sparing no effort” gives the counterpart to the mindful remembering and then seeing clearly, showing what happens without that remembrance. The previous verse described the mindful touched by a visitation who remember and become insightful. This verse describes the associates of devils: “But the devils persistently plunge their human associates deeper into wickedness, sparing no effort,” or “wa ikhwanuhum yamuddoonahum fil-ghayyi thumma la yuqsiron.” “Ikhwanuhum,” their brothers, associates, those who took devils as masters instead of Allah thinking they are guided. “Yamuddoonahum fil-ghayyi,” they extend them in error, in the crooked path, or “ghayy,” the same ghayy that those turned away from signs choose when they see the right path they will not take but the crooked they will follow. “Thumma la yuqsiron,” then they do not fall short, they spare no effort, they do not tire, matching the relentless ambush that promised to come from front, back, right, left to make most ungrateful and the whispering with elegant words that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter to be pleased and persistent.\n\nThe contrast is stark: the mindful are touched lightly then remember and see, the associates are extended in ghayy without curtailment, plunged deeper step by step, as earlier those who deny Our signs are gradually drawn in ways they cannot comprehend and delayed with flawless planning until the appointed term. The devils’ effort is the engine of istidraj, the gradual drawing that feels like ease while being descent, like the Sabbath fish appearing abundantly on the forbidden day to test rebelliousness, like the lowly gain that if it came again they would seize.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats association with devils as dramatic possession. The Quran shows it as persistent, unsparing extension into ghayy, ordinary choices: not giving just measure, lying in ambush on every road, changing “Hittah,” breaking Sabbath, seizing Scripture’s price. Each extension is a choice that invites another, until the heart that could have been turned toward signs for arrogance after seeing every sign still does not believe.\n\nThe remedy remains the previous verse’s pair: refuge with the All-Hearing, All-Knowing and the gracious, commanding, turning ethic that keeps the land from corruption after order and keeps calls humble, hopeful, and fearing. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven,” the warning is that devils do not spare effort in extending the easy excuse into deeper wickedness, until the dreadful punishment for rebelliousness and the disgrace of apes becomes the home of the rebellious shown, while the firm holders of Scripture and establishers of prayer, guided by truth and justice, remain the group that guides and is saved.",
  "203": "“If you ˹O Prophet˺ do not bring them a sign ˹which they demanded˺, they ask, ‘Why do you not make it yourself?’ Say, ‘I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord. This ˹Quran˺ is an insight from your Lord—a guide and a mercy for those who believe’” addresses the demand for signs that has run from Pharaoh’s “Bring it if you are truthful” through the Sabbath tribe’s testing and the gradually drawn deniers. The condition, “If you do not bring them a sign which they demanded,” or “wa itha lam ta’tihim bi-ayatin,” imagines the prophet pausing between signs, as Moses waited with thirty plus ten nights and as winds pause before heavy clouds. The deniers’ retort, “Why do you not make it yourself?” or “law la ijtabaitaha,” why did you not invent it, select it, bring it on your own, assumes revelation is self-authored, like the golden calf’s lowing fashioned from jewellery or the shares divided for associates by claim. It mirrors the chiefs calling Moses a skilled magician who could invent, and the mockers saying “You have studied previous scriptures.”\n\nThe reply defines the prophet’s office: “I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord,” or “qul innama attabi‘u ma yooha ilayya min Rabbi.” This repeats “I only follow what is revealed to me” from earlier, “we only follow what is revealed,” the same following commanded to the community “Follow what was sent down to you from your Lord and do not take other guardians,” the same holding firmly to the Tablets with strength, and the same abiding firmly by Scripture. Following, or “ittiba‘,” replaces invention, or “ijtabaa,” the root of their question, showing the prophet is not an inventor of signs but a follower, the humble secret caller whose staff and hand were given, not fabricated.\n\nThe description of the Quran that follows elevates it above any demanded spectacle: “This Quran is an insight from your Lord—a guide and a mercy for those who believe,” or “hatha basa’iru min Rabbikum wa hudan wa rahmah li-qawmin yu’minoonoon.” “Basa’ir,” insights, is plural of inner sight, the same insight that came as Book explained with knowledge. Guide and mercy are the pair that makes the Book blessed, that the Tablets contained for those who stand in awe, that defines the group that guides with truth, and that makes the fertile land produce abundantly.\n\nA common error deems the Quran insufficient without an extra demanded sign. The verse says the Quran itself is the sign, the insight, sufficient for eyes that refused even though they saw every sign but were turned away for arrogance. The next verses will command listening to it attentively and silently to be shown mercy, remembering inwardly morning and evening with humility not heedlessness, and joining angels who are not too proud to worship and prostrate, rather than persisting in asking why a sign is not self-made.",
  "204": "“When the Quran is recited, listen to it attentively and be silent, so you may be shown mercy” gives the communal ethic for the insight just described as guide and mercy. The previous verse said this Quran is insight from your Lord, a guide and mercy for those who believe, and that the prophet only follows what is revealed, not inventing signs on demand. This verse tells the audience how to receive that insight. The command, “When the Quran is recited, listen to it attentively and be silent,” or “wa itha quri’a al-Qur’anu fa-istami‘oo lahu wa ansitoo,” uses two verbs: “istama‘a” to listen with intent, giving ear, and “ansata” to be silent, to hush, to still. Together they mean not merely hearing sound while devils plunge associates deeper, nor hearing like cattle with ears they do not hear with, but active, quiet, focused listening, as earlier those who intend to understand, see, or hear use hearts, eyes, ears, and as the mindful remember and then see clearly.\n\nThe purpose, “so you may be shown mercy,” or “la‘allakum turhamoon,” is the same “perhaps you will be shown mercy” promised for following the blessed Book and being mindful that winds produce every fruit similarly to raising the dead. Mercy close to good-doers was promised to those who call humbly with hope and fear and do not spread corruption, and now it is promised to those who listen and be silent before the recitation that is insight, guide, and mercy. This ties to the later “Remember your Lord inwardly with humility morning and evening and do not be among the heedless,” showing outward silence and inward remembrance are paired.\n\nThe verse also corrects the demand “Why do you not make a sign yourself?” By listening attentively, the demanded sign is already present: the Quran’s recitation itself is the sign whose wonders of heavens and earth were earlier said to be heedlessly ignored. The silent, attentive listening defeats the ambush from four sides that aims to make most ungrateful, because gratitude begins with hearing.\n\nA common misunderstanding reserves listening for a mosque. The Quran’s recitation occurs in many places; wherever its insight is recited, the same ethic applies: give it ear, make silence, so mercy may descend, as earlier “We have brought them a Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy for those who believe” requires reception. For the righteous community that guides with truth and establishes justice and for those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, this listening is the daily fertile reception that makes the land produce abundantly, before the heedless who do not hear are left wandering blindly in defiance.",
  "205": "“Remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence and in a moderate tone of voice, both morning and evening. And do not be one of the heedless” details the personal counterpart to communal listening. The previous verse commanded when the Quran is recited listen attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy, an outward, collective discipline. This verse gives the inward, constant discipline: “Remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence and in a moderate tone of voice, both morning and evening,” or “wa uthkur Rabbaka fi nafsika tadarru‘an wa kheefatan wa doona al-jahri mina al-qawli bil-ghuduwwi wal-asaal.” “Fi nafsika,” within yourself, inwardly, is the same inwardness praised as best clothing of righteousness and as secret calling that keeps mercy close. “Tadarru‘an,” humbly, lowly, and “kheefatan,” fearfully, reverently, repeat the humble secret calling with hope and fear, opposite to the arrogance for which gates do not open. “Doona al-jahri,” without loudness, moderate, balanced, like the winds not always as flood, suggests voice between silent heart and shouting show, as earlier not transgressing when calling, and as earlier not making faith as play.\n\nThe time, “both morning and evening,” or “bil-ghuduwwi wal-asaal,” is the same morning and evening of those who invoke their Lord seeking His Face, whose scales become heavy, and of the heights’ prayer. The two ends of the day bookend remembrance, as day and night overlap swiftly by His command and He belongs to whatever is in day and night and is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\n\nThe warning, “And do not be one of the heedless,” or “wa la takun min al-ghafileen,” names the heedless, or “ghafileen,” the cattle-like destined for Hell, hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, who are turned away from signs for arrogance and who even if they see every sign will not believe, whose gathering is like the Sabbath town that forgot what they were reminded. Heedlessness is the soil that hardly produces despite winds and Springs.\n\nA common error imagines remembrance only in loud public display. The Quran commends inward, humble, moderate remembrance as the fertile practice that remembers the primordial “Yes, we testify” and the mountain raised like a cloud, keeping the covenant not to say about Allah except truth. Another error defers remembrance to later, but morning and evening mark now, before the near term that perhaps is near and before the Hour that takes by surprise, so perhaps they will return and so stories are narrated to reflect. The next verse shows the model for such remembrance: angels nearest to your Lord not too proud to worship, glorifying and prostrating, whom the mindful join with attentive silence and inward humility.",
  "206": "“Surely those ˹angels˺ nearest to your Lord are not too proud to worship Him. They glorify Him. And to Him they prostrate” closes the chapter with the highest example of the remembrance just commanded. The previous verse taught to remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence in a moderate tone morning and evening and not be among the heedless who are like cattle. This verse shows who does remember without heedlessness: “Surely those angels nearest to your Lord are not too proud to worship Him,” or “inna allatheena inda Rabbika la yastakbiroona an ibadatihi.” The phrase “inda Rabbika,” near your Lord, denotes nearness, the Heights’ nearness hoped for, the Home of Peace with the Guardian because of what they used to do, while “la yastakbiroona,” not too proud, directly answers the arrogance for which gates of heaven are not opened, for which the wicked are turned away from signs even if they see every sign, and for which Iblis refused to prostrate for Adam saying “I am better.” The angels, though near, remain humble, opposite to Pharaoh’s “We will completely dominate” and the chiefs’ swearing humble believers would never receive mercy.\n\nTheir activity is then described: “They glorify Him. And to Him they prostrate,” or “wa yusabbihoonahu wa lahu yasjudoon.” To glorify, or “tasbeeh,” is to declare flawless, exalted above what they associate, as after good offspring were associated and after jinn were associated though He created them, and as Moses cried “Glory be to You” after the mountain crumbled. Prostration, or “sujood,” is the same bowing the magicians fell into, the same sijdah commanded at the city gate with “Hittah,” the same humility those who firmly abide manifest. The angels’ prostration mirrors the believer’s listening and inward remembrance, showing worship is continuous between heavens and earth, day and night, as creation belongs to Him.\n\nThis final verse connects to the opening of the surah where the Book was sent down so you may warn and remind and not let anxiety tighten the chest, and to the principle that every community has a term and the weighing will be just: the angels’ constant glorification prefigures the rivers flowing and bitterness removed for the righteous.\n\nA common misunderstanding imagines nearness to Allah breeds complacency. The angels show nearness increases worship and glorification, not decreases. For the successor who inherited the blessed eastern and western lands, who was given springs, shade, manna, and Tablets to hold firmly, the mountain’s shadow still calls, the primordial “Yes” still testifies, and the Quran’s insight still recites to be heard in silence. The surah that began with Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad ends with prostration, inviting the attentive, grateful, mindful hearer to join the nearest to the Lord, not among those who claim “We will be forgiven” while persisting, but among those who guide with truth and establish justice, whose reward the righteous never lose."
};